"},"6":{"id":"/docs-4","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Key capabilities"},"7":{"id":"/docs-5","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Ecosystem"},"8":{"id":"/docs-6","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"These docs describe the upcoming Apache Iggy 0.10.0 release. Use the source release candidate until the matching packages and images are published. The upgrade guide covers the coordinated cutover and fresh data directory. The 0.9 docs remain available."},"9":{"id":"/docs-7","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#new-in-0100","content":"Partitions distribute their primaries across cluster nodes. Topics can select their replication factor. Consumers can wait for messages and fetch several partitions in one request. Streams have separate encryption keys, and resource updates use set/reset patches."},"10":{"id":"/docs-8","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#new-in-0100","content":"Read about multi-leader partitions, deferred polling, stream options, idempotent producers, metadata changes, external authentication and the Kafka gateway."},"11":{"id":"/docs-9","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#how-iggy-organizes-data","content":"Iggy uses a hierarchical model: streams contain topics, topics contain partitions, and partitions store messages in segments on disk. This gives you flexible multi-tenancy, parallel processing, and efficient storage all in one."},"12":{"id":"/docs-10","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#the-append-only-log","content":"Every partition is an append-only log. Messages are immutable once written, consumers can start from a chosen offset, and retained messages can be replayed. Retention removes old data, and offsets can contain gaps."},"13":{"id":"/docs-11","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Capability"},"14":{"id":"/docs-12","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Details"},"15":{"id":"/docs-13","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Transport"},"16":{"id":"/docs-14","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"TCP, QUIC, WebSocket (custom binary protocol), HTTP (REST)"},"17":{"id":"/docs-15","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Performance"},"18":{"id":"/docs-16","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Thread-per-core + io_uring on Linux, binary message views, configurable memory pool with a default 4 GiB budget"},"19":{"id":"/docs-17","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Security"},"20":{"id":"/docs-18","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"TLS on all transports, Argon2id hashing, AES-256-GCM-SIV encryption at rest, granular permissions with per-stream/per-topic permissions"},"21":{"id":"/docs-19","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Connectors"},"22":{"id":"/docs-20","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"17 sink and 6 source plugins including PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, Apache Iceberg, S3, with data transforms"},"23":{"id":"/docs-21","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"AI Integration"},"24":{"id":"/docs-22","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"MCP server with 40+ tools for LLM-driven message streaming management"},"25":{"id":"/docs-23","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Management"},"26":{"id":"/docs-24","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Web UI dashboard (embedded or standalone), CLI with shell completions, Prometheus metrics. Connector OpenTelemetry export (broker export limits)"},"27":{"id":"/docs-25","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Clustering"},"28":{"id":"/docs-26","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Built on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) consensus. Single node by default, multi-node via [cluster] configuration"},"29":{"id":"/docs-27","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Deployment"},"30":{"id":"/docs-28","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#key-capabilities","content":"Single server process, Docker & Helm, configurable CPU/NUMA affinity on Linux"},"31":{"id":"/docs-29","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Iggy is more than just a server. The project includes a full ecosystem of tools:"},"32":{"id":"/docs-30","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Connectors Runtime - dynamically loaded Rust plugins for data integration: 17 sinks and 6 sources. Ingest from PostgreSQL, Elasticsearch or InfluxDB into Iggy, or forward to MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit, S3 and more. Built-in data transforms and Prometheus metrics."},"33":{"id":"/docs-31","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"MCP Server - Model Context Protocol server exposing 40+ tools for LLM integration. Works with Claude Desktop via stdio and HTTP transports."},"34":{"id":"/docs-32","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Web UI - SvelteKit dashboard for stream/topic management, message browsing with JSON/string/XML decoders, and user management."},"35":{"id":"/docs-33","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"CLI - full-featured command-line interface with named connection contexts, session-based login, and shell completions."},"36":{"id":"/docs-34","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"SDKs - client libraries for 9 languages (Rust, Python, Java, Go, Node.js, C#, C++, PHP, Swift), most with runnable examples."},"37":{"id":"/docs-35","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#ecosystem","content":"Benchmarking - built-in iggy-bench tool with a Yew/WebAssembly dashboard for performance testing."},"38":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Documentation"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting"},"39":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"What the most common first-run failures look like, what causes them, and how to fix them."},"40":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Client hangs with no output"},"41":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Docker container can't be reached"},"42":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Server exits with an io_uring error in a container"},"43":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Kernel too old"},"44":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#missing-root-credentials","content":"Missing root credentials"},"45":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"\"Already exists\" error when running the CLI again"},"46":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-cannot-complete-login","content":"Client cannot complete login"},"47":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/troubleshooting#numa-memory-binding-fails","content":"NUMA memory binding fails"},"48":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"This page covers the problems people most often hit the first time they run Apache Iggy. Each entry shows what you see, what causes it, and how to fix it."},"49":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting","content":"To collect diagnostic data for a bug report instead, see the snapshot command in CLI commands."},"50":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"What you see: the client starts and then prints nothing. It doesn't fail and it doesn't exit."},"51":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Cause: nothing is listening on the address the client connects to, usually 127.0.0.1:8090. The server isn't running, is bound to a different address, or hasn't finished starting. By default the SDKs retry the connection forever, and the retries are only logged if your application has logging set up, so all you see is silence."},"52":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"Fix: check the server has started before starting the client. It logs a server client listeners started line that includes the address, for example tcp=127.0.0.1:8090. You can also check the port directly:"},"53":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-hangs-with-no-output","content":"To make the client fail with an error instead of retrying forever, set reconnection_retries=0 on the connection string, or call .with_reconnection_max_retries(Some(0)) on the Rust client builder. Turning on logging in your application also shows each reconnect attempt."},"54":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-15","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"What you see: the server is running in Docker with -p 8090:8090, but clients on the host can't connect. On Docker Desktop the connection may be accepted and then reset straight away."},"55":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-16","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Cause: the image doesn't override the server's addresses, so it binds 127.0.0.1 inside the container, where the published port can't reach it."},"56":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-17","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#docker-container-cant-be-reached","content":"Fix: set IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090, and the equivalent for any other transport you expose, along with IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS. See the docker run and Compose examples in Docker & Helm."},"57":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-18","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"What you see: the server exits at startup with one of these:"},"58":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-19","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"On a machine with more cores, the same problems can show up as failed to create io_uring runtime for shard N instead."},"59":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-20","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Cause: Docker's default seccomp profile blocks the io_uring system calls the server needs, or the container's locked-memory limit is too small for the server's io_uring rings."},"60":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-21","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#server-exits-with-an-io_uring-error-in-a-container","content":"Fix: run the container with --security-opt seccomp=unconfined and --ulimit memlock=-1:-1, or with a custom seccomp profile and a locked-memory limit big enough for the server. See Why these capabilities? and Linux tuning."},"61":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-22","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"What you see: the server exits with an io_uring Invalid Argument (EINVAL) report that includes a line like:"},"62":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-23","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Cause: on Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer for the io_uring features it uses. WSL2 kernels can be missing io_uring features even on a newer version."},"63":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-24","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#kernel-too-old","content":"Fix: upgrade the host kernel to 5.19 or newer. Under WSL2, run wsl --update. Containers use the host's kernel, so it's the host that needs upgrading. See System requirements."},"64":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-25","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#missing-root-credentials","content":"A fresh standalone server refuses to start without root credentials. Set both IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD before first boot, or use --with-default-root-credentials for a disposable development instance. The server no longer generates a password."},"65":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-26","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#missing-root-credentials","content":"A cluster replica without credentials waits for the root account to arrive through replication. Provision the same initial credentials on all replicas so any promoted node can initialize it. Once root is stored, environment changes do not reset its password. Do not delete a data directory to change an existing password."},"66":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-27","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"What you see: running iggy stream create dev a second time fails:"},"67":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-28","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"The name is missing from the message because of a known bug where error details are lost between the server and the client (#3735)."},"68":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-29","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"Cause: the stream was created the first time. The CLI reports the server's error rather than skipping streams that already exist."},"69":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-30","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#already-exists-error-when-running-the-cli-again","content":"Fix: nothing needs fixing. The stream exists and can be used as it is. Check with iggy stream list before creating it if you're running a setup script more than once."},"70":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-31","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-cannot-complete-login","content":"Make sure that the client matches the server's protocol. Server 0.10.0 uses protocol 0.12.0 and rejects incompatible clients during login. A malformed old client can report a disconnect rather than a useful compatibility error."},"71":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-32","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#client-cannot-complete-login","content":"Use Rust SDK 0.12.0 or Python SDK 0.10.0 with this server. See Server compatibility for the other languages. Also make sure that credentials match the stored account, not just the current bootstrap environment."},"72":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-33","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#numa-memory-binding-fails","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding when the detected topology has one NUMA node. On a multi-node topology, container policy or host restrictions can still reject memory binding."},"73":{"id":"/docs/troubleshooting-34","page_id":"/docs/troubleshooting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/troubleshooting#numa-memory-binding-fails","content":"Set IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding fits the deployment. Inspect the host topology and allowed CPU set before changing production placement. See Linux tuning."},"74":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","AI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/introduction"},"75":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"The AI-related components of Apache Iggy: the MCP server that lets LLM applications work with a running Iggy server."},"76":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"Apache Iggy includes an MCP server, so LLM applications can work with a running Iggy server. The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs."},"77":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"Through the MCP server, an MCP client can check server health and statistics, create and manage streams, topics and partitions, send and poll messages, manage consumer groups and offsets, and administer users and personal access tokens. It connects to Iggy over TCP with its own credentials, and supports HTTP (the default) and stdio transports, with stdio suited to clients running on the same machine."},"78":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"Access is limited in two layers. The [permissions] section of the MCP server's configuration can restrict it to read-only operations, and the Iggy user it connects as keeps its own permissions on the server. For production use, connect as a dedicated user with the minimum permissions it needs."},"79":{"id":"/docs/ai/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/ai/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/introduction","content":"See MCP Server for how to run and configure it, the full list of tools, and client setup."},"80":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"page","content":"MCP Server","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","AI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp"},"81":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-0","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp","content":"Run and configure the Apache Iggy MCP server, including transport, Iggy credentials and TLS."},"82":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-1","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"83":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-2","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"84":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-3","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#available-tools","content":"Available tools"},"85":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-4","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Server"},"86":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-5","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#operation-recovery","content":"Operation recovery"},"87":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-6","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#streams","content":"Streams"},"88":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-7","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#topics","content":"Topics"},"89":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-8","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#partitions--segments","content":"Partitions & Segments"},"90":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-9","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#messages","content":"Messages"},"91":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-10","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer Groups"},"92":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-11","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#consumer-offsets","content":"Consumer Offsets"},"93":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-12","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#users--access-tokens","content":"Users & Access Tokens"},"94":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-13","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#resource-options","content":"Resource options"},"95":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-14","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#permissions","content":"Permissions"},"96":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-15","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#claude-desktop-integration","content":"Claude Desktop integration"},"97":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-16","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#docker","content":"Docker"},"98":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-17","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/ai/mcp#systemd-integration","content":"Systemd integration"},"99":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-18","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp","content":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The Apache Iggy MCP Server is an implementation of the MCP protocol for the message streaming infrastructure. It is built using the rmcp crate and supports both stdio and HTTP transports."},"100":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-19","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"Start an Iggy 0.10.0 or edge broker using the getting started guide. From the matching apache/iggy checkout, run:"},"101":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-20","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"These are the development credentials configured in that guide. Use your broker's credentials or a PAT when connecting to an existing installation."},"102":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-21","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#getting-started","content":"The release uses the Docker image apache/iggy-mcp:0.6.0. Until it is published, build the matching source release candidate."},"103":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-22","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"The minimal viable configuration requires at least the Iggy credentials, used to create the TCP connection to the running Iggy server that the MCP server talks to. You can choose between HTTP (the default) and STDIO transports (e.g. for the local usage with tools such as Claude Desktop choose stdio)."},"104":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-23","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Set telemetry.enabled = true to export to a running collector. For HTTP export, set transport = \"http\" and use complete signal URLs: http://localhost:4318/v1/logs for logs and http://localhost:4318/v1/traces for traces. The MCP server does not append those paths."},"105":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-24","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"The configuration file must be in the toml format. By default, the server looks for core/ai/mcp/config.toml relative to its working directory. Set IGGY_MCP_CONFIG_PATH to use another path. Embedded defaults are loaded first, then the file if it exists, then environment overrides."},"106":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-25","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Each setting can also be overridden using IGGY_MCP_<SECTION>_<KEY>, for example IGGY_MCP_IGGY_USERNAME or IGGY_MCP_HTTP_ADDRESS. Nested settings use the same underscore convention, such as IGGY_MCP_IGGY_TLS_ENABLED. Set IGGY_MCP_ENV_PATH to load a particular dotenv file. Otherwise .env is searched for in the current directory and its parents. Existing environment variables take precedence over dotenv values."},"107":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-26","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"Run iggy-mcp --list-config-env-vars to print every supported MCP configuration variable and exit before loading dotenv or configuration files or creating the Tokio runtime. The output is sorted and contains names only. If a configuration field is an indexed vector, its template uses <N> for indices from 0 through 255. Whole-value vectors are listed once without a placeholder."},"108":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-27","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#configuration","content":"The token value can be either a literal PAT or a file: reference such as token = \"file:/run/secrets/iggy_pat\", in which case the token is read from the given file and surrounding whitespace is removed (~/ is expanded to the home directory). A non-empty token takes precedence over the username and password."},"109":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-28","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#available-tools","content":"The MCP server exposes these 41 tools:"},"110":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-29","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Tool"},"111":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-30","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Description"},"112":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-31","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"ping"},"113":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-32","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Health check"},"114":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-33","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_stats"},"115":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-34","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Server statistics (streams, topics, partitions, OS info)"},"116":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-35","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_me"},"117":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-36","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Current client info"},"118":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-37","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_client"},"119":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-38","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"Get specific client details"},"120":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-39","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/ai/mcp#server","content":"get_clients"},"121":{"id":"/docs/ai/mcp-40","page_id":"/docs/ai/mcp","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs