[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Ping. Code: 1. Empty payload. The only command accepted before login."},"827":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-23","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get stats. Code: 10. Empty payload."},"828":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-24","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get snapshot. Code: 11."},"829":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-25","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Compression codes: 1 = Stored, 2 = Deflated, 3 = Bzip2, 4 = Zstd, 5 = Lzma, 6 = Xz. Snapshot type codes: 1 = FilesystemOverview, 2 = ProcessList, 3 = ResourceUsage, 4 = Test, 5 = ServerLogs, 6 = ServerConfig, 100 = All."},"830":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-26","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get cluster metadata. Code: 12. Empty payload."},"831":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-27","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Describe options. Code: 13."},"832":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-28","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Scope: 1 = topic, 2 = stream, 3 = user. Returns the option catalog for the scope: keys, value kinds, defaults, and descriptions. See Options block."},"833":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-29","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get me. Code: 20. Empty payload."},"834":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-30","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get client. Code: 21."},"835":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-31","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#system","content":"Get clients. Code: 22. Empty payload."},"836":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-32","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Get stream. Code: 200."},"837":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-33","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Get streams. Code: 201. Empty payload."},"838":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-34","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Create stream. Code: 202."},"839":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-35","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"The stream catalog accepts a create-only, write-only encryption_key. An omitted key uses the server creation default. See Stream options."},"840":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-36","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#streams","content":"Delete stream. Code: 203."},"841":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-37","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Get topic. Code: 300."},"842":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-38","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Get topics. Code: 301."},"843":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-39","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Create topic. Code: 302."},"844":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-40","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"The fixed fields are the shape of the operation: which stream, how many partitions, what name. Every topic setting (compression_algorithm, message_expiry, max_topic_size, segment_size, durability, consumer_offset_durability, messages_required_to_save, size_of_messages_required_to_save, preallocate_segments, partition_resize_policy, replication_factor) rides the options block. See Topic options. partitions_count is an argument, not a setting: it is consumed at admission and never persisted as an option."},"845":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-41","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#topics","content":"Delete topic. Code: 303."},"846":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-42","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partitions","content":"Create partitions. Code: 402."},"847":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-43","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partitions","content":"Delete partitions. Code: 403."},"848":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-44","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#segments","content":"Delete segments. Code: 503."},"849":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-45","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#segments","content":"Deletes up to segments_count oldest sealed segments of the partition. The active segment is retained."},"850":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-46","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Poll messages. Code: 100."},"851":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-47","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"strategy is a polling strategy. count is the requested number of messages. auto_commit = 1 asks the server to advance the consumer offset. A primary submits a quorum offset write before replying, but the poll does not wait for that write to commit. Partition reads route to their current primary. See Client failover."},"852":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-48","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"The response body:"},"853":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-49","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"The 40-byte prefix is followed by a stream of batch records: each record's header carries the stamped base_offset and base_timestamp, and each frame's deltas resolve against them. A record may be a server-sliced view of a larger stored batch, so the first polled offset is base_offset + offset_delta of the first frame, not necessarily base_offset itself. current_offset is the partition's newest offset at poll time. The server rebuilds records when decrypting encrypted payloads."},"854":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-50","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Send messages. Code: 101."},"855":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-51","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"metadata_length counts the bytes from stream_id through messages_count inclusive, so a reader can skip straight to the batch. The producer leaves partition_id, base_offset, and base_timestamp zero in the batch header, and the server stamps them. Every checksum is producer-computed and verified at admission. The reserved regions must be zero."},"856":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-52","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"A committed append returns offset confirmations without a metadata result section:"},"857":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-53","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#messages","content":"Each confirmation is 20 bytes. The server reports one partition per append. A zero count or an empty successful body supplies no offset information. A retry can return the retained result rather than append again. Retry coverage is bounded, and crash durability depends on the topic's durability policy."},"858":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-54","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Get consumer offset. Code: 120."},"859":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-55","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"When an offset is stored, the response body is 20 bytes. When none is stored, a successful reply has an empty body."},"860":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-56","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Store consumer offset. Code: 121."},"861":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-57","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Delete consumer offset. Code: 122."},"862":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-58","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"The trailing ack byte is mandatory on both write commands. A payload without it fails to decode. Ack levels:"},"863":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-59","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Value"},"864":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-60","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Level"},"865":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-61","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Meaning"},"866":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-62","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"0"},"867":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-63","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"NoAck"},"868":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-64","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Local write on a single-replica partition. Replicated partitions use the same consensus path as Quorum"},"869":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-65","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"1"},"870":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-66","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Quorum"},"871":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-67","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-offsets","content":"Replicate through partition consensus. Respond after a quorum commit (default for explicit writes)"},"872":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-68","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Get consumer group. Code: 600."},"873":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-69","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Get consumer groups. Code: 601."},"874":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-70","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Create consumer group. Code: 602."},"875":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-71","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Delete consumer group. Code: 603."},"876":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-72","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Join consumer group. Code: 604."},"877":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-73","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Leave consumer group. Code: 605."},"878":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-74","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Sync consumer group. Code: 606."},"879":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-75","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#consumer-groups","content":"Read-only: a member asks for its current partition assignment and the group generation, so it can select partitions to poll client-side."},"880":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-76","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Get user. Code: 31."},"881":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-77","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Get users. Code: 32. Empty payload."},"882":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-78","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Create user. Code: 33."},"883":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-79","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"permissions_len and the permissions bytes are present only when has_permissions = 1. When it is 0 the options block follows immediately. The user catalog includes username and status for updates. Creation carries those values in its fixed fields. status: 1 = active, 2 = inactive."},"884":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-80","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Delete user. Code: 34."},"885":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-81","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Update permissions. Code: 36."},"886":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-82","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"As in CreateUser, the length field is conditional: with has_permissions = 0 the payload ends at the flag."},"887":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-83","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#users","content":"Change password. Code: 37."},"888":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-84","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login and logout are part of the connection lifecycle:"},"889":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-85","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login register. Code: 40. Operation::Register. The body is ClientVersionInfo + credentials, described in Login-register."},"890":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-86","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Login register with PAT. Code: 45. Operation::Register. The body is ClientVersionInfo + token."},"891":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-87","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Logout user. Code: 39. Operation::Logout. Empty payload."},"892":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-88","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#authentication","content":"Codes 38 and 44 are the pre-register login shapes. The server rejects both with a MalformedLogin eviction."},"893":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-89","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Get personal access tokens. Code: 41. Empty payload."},"894":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-90","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Create personal access token. Code: 42."},"895":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-91","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"expiry is a duration in microseconds. Two sentinel values exist: 0 (server default, which currently means no expiry) and u64::MAX (never expires)."},"896":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-92","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#personal-access-tokens","content":"Delete personal access token. Code: 43."},"897":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-93","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#resource-patches","content":"UpdateResource is code 16, operation 150:"},"898":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-94","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#resource-patches","content":"Scope 1 selects a topic with stream and topic identifiers. Scope 2 selects a stream. Scope 3 selects a user. Empty patches, duplicate keys and keys that are both set and reset are rejected. The catalog defines update and reset permissions for each key."},"899":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-95","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partition-routes","content":"GetPartitionRoute is code 105. Its request contains stream and topic identifiers followed by a u32 partition ID. The response is exactly 25 bytes:"},"900":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-96","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#partition-routes","content":"The primary value is a configured replica ID, not a roster position. Retain the incarnation and metadata operation when constructing a request. Group ownership adds its own generation."},"901":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-97","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#deferred-polls","content":"PollMessagesDeferred is code 106. It extends the PollMessages body:"},"902":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-98","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#deferred-polls","content":"The response uses the same 40-byte prefix as PollMessages. Waiting requests need a positive count, min_count from 1 through count and a byte budget of at least 344. A positive wait requires at least one second of reply margin. Waits cannot exceed 600 seconds, and request timeouts cannot exceed 630 seconds. The server can shorten waits through its configuration."},"903":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-99","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#deferred-polls","content":"CancelDeferredRequest is code 108. It has an empty body and identifies the original request through the header request number on the same authenticated connection. The cancel command has no success reply. A committing deferred read still has one terminal reply that tells the caller whether the cancel won or an offset was stored."},"904":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-100","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#grouped-fetches","content":"FetchMessages is code 109:"},"905":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-101","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#grouped-fetches","content":"Targets have unique partition IDs and use offset or next strategies. The request applies its count, minimum count and byte limit across all targets. Each target also has a byte limit no greater than the request limit. A fetch requires the one-second reply margin even when its wait is zero."},"906":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-102","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#grouped-fetches","content":"The response starts with a u32 section count, followed by a table of 56-byte section headers:"},"907":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-103","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#grouped-fetches","content":"All section headers precede the records. Records follow in table order. A failed section has no records. Inspect every section status before accepting progress. See Deferred polling for SDK use."},"908":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands-104","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/commands#metadata-changes","content":"GetMetadataChanges is code 17. It supports Earliest, Latest, After and Current starts, group selection, bounded result counts and optional time bounds. The request codec defines its flags and position encodings. See Metadata changes for the follow and rebuild contract."},"909":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Connection lifecycle","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle"},"910":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","content":"How the protocol version is packed and checked, and the login-register handshake that authenticates a connection."},"911":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"Protocol version"},"912":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"Login-register"},"913":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#bind-a-retained-session","content":"Bind a retained session"},"914":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","content":"Server 0.10.0 uses binary protocol 0.12.0. Clients must use a compatible release."},"915":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"The protocol version is a semver packed into one u32, 10 bits per component (each must be below 1024):"},"916":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#protocol-version","content":"Integer order equals semver order. The value tracks the iggy_binary_protocol crate release. Under 0.x the compatibility gate is minor-scoped: the server accepts a client whose packed version is at least the server's minimum and whose major.minor is at most the server's. Patch releases never change the wire, so the upper bound ignores patch. The implemented minimum is the current version with its patch component zeroed. Compatibility across minor versions after 1.0.0 requires a future change to that calculation."},"917":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"A new logical session authenticates through the register handshake: command code 40 (LOGIN_REGISTER, username and password) or 45 (LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT, personal access token). Both ride operation = 1 (Register) with session = 0, request = 0, and a non-zero client id. Mint a fresh id for a new logical client. A reconnect retains its request identities and binds the existing session. A matching registration retry preserves its epoch."},"918":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"Both request bodies begin with the ClientVersionInfo prefix, so the server can gate on the version before touching credentials:"},"919":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"protocol_version is the packed version of the protocol the client was built against. sdk_name identifies the SDK (for example rust-sdk, go-sdk). sdk_version is the SDK's own build version."},"920":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"After the prefix:"},"921":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"LOGIN_REGISTER (code 40):"},"922":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"LOGIN_REGISTER_WITH_PAT (code 45):"},"923":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"An incompatible protocol version is answered with a 256-byte Eviction frame, reason 14 (IncompatibleProtocol), carrying the accepted window at bytes 144 (max) and 148 (min) as packed u32 versions. A body without a decodable ClientVersionInfo prefix gets reason 15 (MalformedLogin) with a zero window. Bad credentials get reasons 9-11. See EvictionHeader for the frame layout."},"924":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"A successful login is a normal Reply with status = 0. Its body includes an empty result section before the login payload:"},"925":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The client stores session and echoes it in every subsequent request header. Logout is command code 39 (LOGOUT_USER), operation = 3 (Logout), empty body."},"926":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#login-register","content":"The legacy login commands 38 (LOGIN_USER) and 44 (LOGIN_WITH_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN) are refused: the server answers them with an Eviction frame, reason 15 (MalformedLogin). Before authentication, a connection can ping or use the registration and binding flows. Protected commands require an authenticated session."},"927":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#bind-a-retained-session","content":"BindSession, code 15, authenticates another connection for the same logical session without another Register:"},"928":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#bind-a-retained-session","content":"The client creates and protects the random binding secret for its session. The server stores a verifier. Session zero can resolve a lost registration reply using this retained secret. Binding supports connections to different partition primaries while preserving one session identity."},"929":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/connection-lifecycle#bind-a-retained-session","content":"Transport loss alone does not end a retained session. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation do. A password or PAT change does not revoke an already established session. Once the original session ends, unresolved operations cannot recover their old receipts through a new login."},"930":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"page","content":"Shared encodings","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Binary Protocol"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings"},"931":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-0","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","content":"The primitives command payloads are built from: names, identifiers, consumers and the other shared encodings."},"932":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-1","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#name","content":"Name"},"933":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-2","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#identifier","content":"Identifier"},"934":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-3","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"935":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-4","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#optional-partition-id","content":"Optional partition id"},"936":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-5","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partitioning","content":"Partitioning"},"937":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-6","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#polling-strategy","content":"Polling strategy"},"938":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-7","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#header-kinds","content":"Header kinds"},"939":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-8","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#user-headers-block","content":"User headers block"},"940":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-9","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#options-block","content":"Options block"},"941":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-10","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#compression","content":"Compression"},"942":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-11","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partition-context","content":"Partition context"},"943":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-12","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#option-catalog-flags","content":"Option catalog flags"},"944":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-13","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","content":"Primitives the command payloads are built from."},"945":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-14","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#name","content":"Length-prefixed UTF-8 string, used for stream, topic, group, user, and token names:"},"946":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-15","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#identifier","content":"Polymorphic identifier of a stream, topic, user, or consumer group:"},"947":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-16","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#identifier","content":"Numeric: kind = 1, length = 4, value is a u32."},"948":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-17","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#identifier","content":"String: kind = 2, length = 1..255, value is UTF-8 bytes."},"949":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-18","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#consumer","content":"Identifies a consumer or a consumer group in message and offset commands:"},"950":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-19","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#consumer","content":"kind = 1: consumer."},"951":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-20","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#consumer","content":"kind = 2: consumer group."},"952":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-21","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#consumer","content":"kind = 3: externally managed group offsets. This kind cannot poll or join native consumer groups."},"953":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-22","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#optional-partition-id","content":"Several commands take an optional partition id encoded as 5 fixed bytes:"},"954":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-23","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#optional-partition-id","content":"flag = 1 means the id is set. flag = 0 means none (the value bytes are then zero). Group polls and group offset writes require an explicit assigned partition. Plain-consumer polls and consumer-offset reads use partition 0 when the id is omitted."},"955":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-24","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partitioning","content":"How SendMessages picks the target partition:"},"956":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-25","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partitioning","content":"Balanced: kind = 1, length = 0, no value. The binary client resolves it to an explicit partition before sending."},"957":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-26","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partitioning","content":"PartitionId: kind = 2, length = 4, value is a u32 partition id."},"958":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-27","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partitioning","content":"MessagesKey: kind = 3, length = 1..255, value is a routing key hashed to a partition."},"959":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-28","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#partitioning","content":"The Rust binary SDK resolves balanced and key routing client-side and sends kind = 2. The binary server requires explicit partition routing. The HTTP API can resolve the other strategies."},"960":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-29","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings#polling-strategy","content":"Fixed 9 bytes:"},"961":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/encodings-30","page_id":"/