ed and rejected. The 64..127 range is reserved for server-internal operations  and every value in it is refused from clients."},"1260":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-224","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#operation-discriminants","content":"The metadata plane orders resource and session changes. Each partition plane orders messages and offset changes independently. Partition requests retain their incarnation, ownership generation and metadata requirement across retries. Refreshing a route does not authorize a request against a replacement history."},"1261":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-225","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"A retry keeps the original client, session, request number, logical request stamp and arguments. New mutations use new request numbers. Registration uses request zero. Do not infer that a low-numbered request succeeded because a later request committed."},"1262":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-226","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"Session-qualified retry protection is retained by the metadata and partition state machines. A full partition table rejects new writers instead of evicting live protection. Ordered retirement releases ended sessions. Receipt recovery is bounded by the session lifetime and retained state. It is not an unlimited exactly-once guarantee."},"1263":{"id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing-227","page_id":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/binary-protocol/framing#request-numbering","content":"A reconnect uses BindSession. Ending the session loses access to unresolved receipts. A fresh operation can duplicate an earlier uncertain write. SDKs retain uncertain operations and block conflicting work until resolution or aba