JSON page per line:"},"1775":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-117","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#metadata","content":"--after resumes from a returned cursor. --limit accepts 1 through 1000 and defaults to 100. --interval defaults to one second while following. --since and --until use microseconds since the epoch. A follower rebuilds from current state when its saved history expires."},"1776":{"id":"/docs/cli/commands-118","page_id":"/docs/cli/commands","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/commands#resource-update-examples","content":"Updates preserve omitted fields. Compression and storage options remain fixed at creation. topic get displays partition details and explicit and derived options. See Topic options."},"1777":{"id":"/docs/cli/start","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"page","content":"Iggy CLI","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","CLI"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start"},"1778":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-0","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start","content":"Install the Iggy CLI, connect to a server, authenticate, and manage connection contexts."},"1779":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-1","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#installation","content":"Installation"},"1780":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-2","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"Cargo"},"1781":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-3","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"Docker"},"1782":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-4","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Connecting to a server"},"1783":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-5","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"TLS and encryption"},"1784":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-6","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"Authentication"},"1785":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-7","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#login-sessions","content":"Login sessions"},"1786":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-8","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#personal-access-tokens","content":"Personal access tokens"},"1787":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-9","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Connection contexts"},"1788":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-10","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cli/start#shell-completions","content":"Shell completions"},"1789":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-11","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start","content":"The Iggy CLI is a command-line interface for managing an Iggy server: streams, topics, partitions, messages, users, tokens and more. It lives in the core repository under the core/cli directory. The crate is named iggy-cli, the installed binary iggy."},"1790":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-12","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start","content":"This page covers installing the CLI, connecting to a server, authenticating, and managing connection contexts. The full command tree is documented in the command reference."},"1791":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-13","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"These docs target server 0.10.0 and CLI 0.15.0, prepared for the same release. The CLI has its own version number, so use the version that matches your server. Install it from crates.io with:"},"1792":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-14","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"This builds and installs the iggy binary. With cargo-binstall, you can download a prebuilt binary instead. It builds from source if there isn't one for your platform:"},"1793":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-15","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#cargo","content":"To build it from source instead, use the same checkout as your server and run this from its root:"},"1794":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-16","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"The official apache/iggy image ships the CLI alongside the server, installed as /usr/local/bin/iggy. The image entrypoint is the server, so override it to run the CLI:"},"1795":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-17","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"--network host works on Linux. On macOS and Windows, point the CLI at the host instead: --tcp-server-address host.docker.internal:8090."},"1796":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-18","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#docker","content":"Once installed, run iggy without arguments to print an overview of the available commands, or see the command reference."},"1797":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-19","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"The CLI talks to the server over one of four transports, selected with --transport. Accepted values are tcp, quic, http and ws (not websocket). The default is tcp."},"1798":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-20","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Transport"},"1799":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-21","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Address flag"},"1800":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-22","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"Default"},"1801":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-23","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"tcp"},"1802":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-24","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--tcp-server-address"},"1803":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-25","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"127.0.0.1:8090"},"1804":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-26","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"quic"},"1805":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-27","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--quic-server-address"},"1806":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-28","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"127.0.0.1:8080"},"1807":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-29","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"http"},"1808":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-30","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--http-api-url"},"1809":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-31","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"http://localhost:3000"},"1810":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-32","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"ws"},"1811":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-33","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"--websocket-server-address"},"1812":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-34","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connecting-to-a-server","content":"127.0.0.1:8092"},"1813":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-35","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Flag"},"1814":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-36","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Description"},"1815":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-37","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--tcp-tls-enabled"},"1816":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-38","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Enable TLS for the TCP transport"},"1817":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-39","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--tcp-tls-domain"},"1818":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-40","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"TLS domain for the TCP transport (default: localhost)"},"1819":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-41","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--quic-validate-certificate"},"1820":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-42","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Enable certificate validation for QUIC"},"1821":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-43","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"--encryption-key"},"1822":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-44","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Base64-encoded AES-256-GCM key for client-side message payload encryption"},"1823":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-45","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#tls-and-encryption","content":"Each transport also exposes reconnection and tuning flags (retry counts, intervals, QUIC window sizes and MTU). Run iggy --help for the complete list with defaults."},"1824":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-46","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"Broker commands require credentials, except for ping. For commands other than login, the CLI resolves credentials in this order, first match wins:"},"1825":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-47","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"A cached login session token (created by iggy login)."},"1826":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-48","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-n, --token-name <name>: a personal access token stored in the platform keyring under that name."},"1827":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-49","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-t, --token <token>: a personal access token passed directly."},"1828":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-50","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-u, --username <user> with -p, --password <password>. When -p is omitted, the CLI prompts for the password interactively (or reads one line from stdin when piped)."},"1829":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-51","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"A nonempty IGGY_TOKEN environment variable."},"1830":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-52","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"The IGGY_USERNAME and IGGY_PASSWORD environment variables (both must be set)."},"1831":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-53","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"iggy login tries the supplied credentials before a cached session, so it can replace an expired login. Other commands fail if their cached token is rejected. They do not retry with the next credential source in the same invocation."},"1832":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-54","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#authentication","content":"-u, -t and -n are mutually exclusive. Avoid passing the password inline with -p: it lands in your shell history. Prefer iggy login, the interactive prompt, or the environment variables."},"1833":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-55","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#login-sessions","content":"iggy login authenticates once and stores a session token in the platform's secure credential store: Secret Service on Linux and the BSDs, Keychain on macOS, Credential Manager on Windows. Subsequent commands use the cached token automatically. This requires the default login-session feature and an available credential-store backend. Linux and BSD need a running Secret Service provider on the D-Bus session."},"1834":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-56","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#login-sessions","content":"iggy session status only checks whether a token exists in the local keyring. An expired token still reports as active. On TCP, QUIC and WebSocket, run iggy me to verify the session against the server. HTTP does not support me. Use an authenticated command such as iggy stream list with the required permissions."},"1835":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-57","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#personal-access-tokens","content":"Create a token once, then authenticate with it instead of a username and password:"},"1836":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-58","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#personal-access-tokens","content":"--store-token is mutually exclusive with an expiry: stored tokens never expire. They're also namespaced per server address, so a token stored for one server isn't visible when connecting to another. Alternatively, create a token with an expiry and pass its value with -t:"},"1837":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-59","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Contexts are named connection profiles (transport, addresses, TLS settings, credentials) for working with multiple environments. Subcommands: list, use, create, delete, show."},"1838":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-60","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Creating and activating a context are two separate steps:"},"1839":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-61","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"There's no --context flag: the active context is persistent state, switched with iggy context use <name>. Flags passed on the command line override the corresponding values from the active context. Credential fields merge independently: supplying --username does not clear a context token, which still has higher authentication priority."},"1840":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-62","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#connection-contexts","content":"Contexts are stored in contexts.toml, and the active context name in .active_context, both under the Iggy home directory: ~/.iggy by default, overridable with the IGGY_HOME environment variable. The default context always exists and cannot be deleted or recreated through context commands. Deleting the currently active context switches back to default. Context names may contain letters, digits, hyphens and underscores. Passwords and raw tokens supplied to context create are stored as plaintext in contexts.toml (owner-only permissions on Unix). Use a stored token name when the credential should stay in the keyring."},"1841":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-63","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#shell-completions","content":"Generate completions for bash, zsh, fish, elvish or powershell with --generate:"},"1842":{"id":"/docs/cli/start-64","page_id":"/docs/cli/start","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cli/start#shell-completions","content":"For zsh, add ~/.zfunc to fpath before running compinit in your shell configuration."},"1843":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"page","content":"Client Failover","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover"},"1844":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","content":"Route requests to partition primaries and handle reconnects without losing an uncertain operation's identity."},"1845":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"Where requests are served"},"1846":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#reconnect-and-bind","content":"Reconnect and bind"},"1847":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retain-uncertain-mutations","content":"Retain uncertain mutations"},"1848":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#consumer-progress","content":"Consumer progress"},"1849":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#http-forwarding","content":"HTTP forwarding"},"1850":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","content":"A cluster has a metadata primary and separate partition primaries. SDKs discover both. Reaching the metadata primary does not mean that the same node owns every partition request."},"1851":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"Metadata reads can use a replica's local state. A caller that needs a known metadata change can carry a minimum metadata operation and wait for that node to catch up. Counters on other nodes can lag a successful write until a later prepare or commit broadcast."},"1852":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"Partition requests use the partition route. It identifies the primary, view, partition incarnation and metadata progress. An incarnation identifies one history of a partition. Recreating a partition changes that identity. Consumer group operations also carry an ownership generation, so a former member cannot store progress for a new owner."},"1853":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#where-requests-are-served","content":"The SDK retains this context with a request. A route refresh does not replace the history of an already uncertain operation. HistoryUnavailable requires an application decision about the new history. Readers expose a seek operation to choose a new start."},"1854":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#reconnect-and-bind","content":"Binary login registers a logical session and a binding secret. Additional connections and reconnects bind that session. The client can therefore keep connections to several partition primaries without creating an unrelated identity for every link."},"1855":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#reconnect-and-bind","content":"A stale link does not always mean the logical session ended. The SDK closes that link and tries to bind again. A terminal session refusal requires new registration. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end the session. A password or PAT change does not revoke a session already established with those credentials."},"1856":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#reconnect-and-bind","content":"Advertised endpoints must be reachable from the client network. The SDK uses the endpoints returned by cluster metadata. See Configuration for network selectors and TLS names."},"1857":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retain-uncertain-mutations","content":"A request rejected before admission can be retried within its deadline. A lost reply after sending is different: the operation can already be committed. Retained-operation SDKs preserve its original identity and payload instead of submitting a fresh mutation."},"1858":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retain-uncertain-mutations","content":"Resolution requires a matching final committed-result proof. A successful status or a nonzero log position alone is insufficient. A committed business rejection is also a definitive result. An uncertain operation remains retained when resolution times out."},"1859":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retain-uncertain-mutations","content":"resolve_operation and its language equivalents retry a retained mutation. abandon_operation frees local state without undoing or determining the server result. OperationNotRetained does not prove that nothing committed. Keep the same client for recovery and handle the error before retrying application work."},"1860":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#retain-uncertain-mutations","content":"Once the original session ends, the client cannot recover its receipt through a new login. Bounded client retention can also prevent recovery. The SDK guides describe each language's error types and limits. Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution."},"1861":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#consumer-progress","content":"Use checkpoint commits after processing. A checkpoint retains the partition history and owner that delivered the data. Committing only a numeric offset loses that context."},"1862":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#consumer-progress","content":"A committing deferred read can lose its reply after storing an offset. The client reports uncertain fetch progress and blocks affected progress calls until recovery or explicit abandonment. Stateless reads and offset inspection remain available. Abandonment does not roll back a stored offset."},"1863":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#consumer-progress","content":"Readers group targets by primary and refresh routes after changes. They can stop a partition on a history change or an undecodable message. Inspect the reported error before seeking past it."},"1864":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#http-forwarding","content":"A contacted HTTP node can forward to the correct primary in one hop. The route and request carry a signed relay proof, and the destination authenticates the bearer independently. Configure shared JWT key material and synchronize clocks. Proofs expire after ten seconds."},"1865":{"id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/client-failover","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/client-failover#http-forwarding","content":"Forwarding does not make every ambiguous operation safe to replay. HTTP ack=none confirms dispatch only. A deferred read that commits uses POST, and a lost reply can leave its progress uncertain. See Cluster security."},"1866":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"page","content":"Cluster Configuration","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration"},"1867":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration","content":"Reference for the [cluster] settings, consensus timing, and node roster"},"1868":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"Top-level settings"},"1869":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Consensus timing"},"1870":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"Validation rules"},"1871":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#default-replication-factor","content":"Default replication factor"},"1872":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#superblock-failure-handling","content":"Superblock failure handling"},"1873":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"Coordinator placement"},"1874":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#node-roster","content":"Node roster"},"1875":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#name","content":"name"},"1876":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#ip","content":"ip"},"1877":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#replica_id","content":"replica_id"},"1878":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#ports","content":"ports"},"1879":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_address","content":"advertised_address"},"1880":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"advertised_addresses"},"1881":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#authentication-and-tls","content":"Authentication and TLS"},"1882":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration","content":"All clustering settings live in the [cluster] section of the server configuration. The authoritative defaults with detailed comments are in core/server/config.toml. Every value can also be set through an IGGY_-prefixed environment variable, for example IGGY_CLUSTER_ENABLED=true or IGGY_CLUSTER_NODES_0_IP=10.0.1.5."},"1883":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration","content":"With clustering enabled, the server validates the section at boot and refuses to start on an invalid value, printing the rule that failed. The repair chunk limit and superblock failure timeout are validated even when clustering is disabled."},"1884":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"Setting"},"1885":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"Default"},"1886":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"Purpose"},"1887":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"enabled"},"1888":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"false"},"1889":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-22","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"Enables cluster mode. When disabled, the node runs single-node and the rest of the section is mostly inert."},"1890":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-23","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"name"},"1891":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-24","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"\"iggy-cluster\""},"1892":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-25","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"Unique cluster name shared by every node. Prevents accidental cross-cluster communication."},"1893":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-26","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#top-level-settings","content":"cluster.name is a permanent on-disk identity, not a label. It's hashed into the cluster id stamped into every metadata and partition superblock on first boot, and the consensus layer derives its cluster id from the name unconditionally. Nodes with different names cannot connect to each other, and changing the name later makes the server refuse to start against existing data."},"1894":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-27","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Consensus runs on a fixed 10ms tick. Every duration below is converted to whole ticks: values are rounded down to a multiple of 10ms, and anything under 10ms is raised to a single tick rather than firing sooner. repair_gap_debounce_interval has an additional 500ms floor."},"1895":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-28","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Setting"},"1896":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-29","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Default"},"1897":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-30","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Purpose"},"1898":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-31","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"heartbeat_timeout"},"1899":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-32","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"5s"},"1900":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-33","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Backup liveness window: a replica that sees no primary traffic for this long starts a view change"},"1901":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-34","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"commit_broadcast_interval"},"1902":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-35","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"500ms"},"1903":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-36","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"How often the primary broadcasts its commit point. This broadcast is the cluster's liveness signal"},"1904":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-37","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"prepare_retransmit_interval"},"1905":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-38","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"250ms"},"1906":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-39","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Retry prepares that backups have not acknowledged"},"1907":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-40","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"view_change_retransmit_interval"},"1908":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-41","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"500ms"},"1909":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-42","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Retry StartViewChange / DoViewChange while a view change is in progress"},"1910":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-43","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"view_change_status_timeout"},"1911":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-44","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"5s"},"1912":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-45","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Escalate a view change that does not conclude within this window to a fresh election"},"1913":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-46","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"request_start_view_retransmit_interval"},"1914":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-47","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"1s"},"1915":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-48","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"How often a recovering replica re-requests the current view's StartView"},"1916":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-49","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"view_probe_attempts_max"},"1917":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-50","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"5"},"1918":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-51","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Unanswered RequestStartView probes a recovering replica tolerates before falling back to an election"},"1919":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-52","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"repair_retry_interval"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-53","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"1s"},"1921":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-54","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"How long a stalled journal-repair stream waits before re-requesting its remaining window"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-55","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"repair_gap_debounce_interval"},"1923":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-56","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"1s"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-57","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"How long a committed-history gap waits before opening a repair session. Floored at 500ms"},"1925":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-58","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"repair_chunk_max"},"1926":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-59","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"128"},"1927":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-60","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Prepares a peer serves per repair round"},"1928":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-61","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#consensus-timing","content":"Raise heartbeat_timeout on oversubscribed hosts where scheduling stalls fake primary death. Otherwise keep the defaults unless tests show a specific scheduling or network problem."},"1929":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-62","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"Boot-time validation enforces these constraints:"},"1930":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-63","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"Every duration must be nonzero. The 0, disabled, and unlimited sentinels all parse to a zero duration and are rejected. There's no way to switch the liveness window off."},"1931":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-64","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"heartbeat_timeout must be at least 2s and at least 4x commit_broadcast_interval, so one delayed broadcast never trips an election on a healthy primary."},"1932":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-65","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"view_change_status_timeout must be at least 4x view_change_retransmit_interval, so a few dropped view-change messages retransmit instead of escalating into a fresh cluster-wide election."},"1933":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-66","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"view_probe_attempts_max must be between 1 and 100."},"1934":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-67","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#validation-rules","content":"repair_chunk_max must be between 1 and 1024, and strictly below message_bus.peer_queue_capacity: each repair frame rides the per-peer message-bus queue, and a full round above that capacity would overrun the queue and drop frames."},"1935":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-68","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#default-replication-factor","content":"cluster.default_replication_factor defaults to \"all\". It applies to new topics whose creation request omits replication_factor. An explicit value must be from 1 through 32 and no greater than the cluster node count. The resolved value is stored with the topic. Changing this default does not change existing topics."},"1936":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-69","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#default-replication-factor","content":"See Multi-leader partitions for primary placement and the availability consequences of each factor."},"1937":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-70","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#superblock-failure-handling","content":"superblock_wedged_fatal_timeout defaults to 2m. It controls how long repeated superblock persistence failures can continue before the process exits. The affected consensus group stays fenced while persistence is failing. 0, disabled, or unlimited disables the process exit, leaving the group fenced. A nonzero value must be at least 30s."},"1938":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-71","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"These settings live under [cluster.coordinator] and apply when the server has more than one shard:"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-72","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"Setting"},"1940":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-73","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"Default"},"1941":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-74","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"Purpose"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-75","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"skip_shard_zero_for_replicas"},"1943":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-76","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"true"},"1944":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-77","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"Places replica connections on peer shards instead of shard 0"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-78","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"replica_connections_per_peer"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-79","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"1"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-80","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"Replica links per peer. Every node must use the same value."},"1948":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-81","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"skip_shard_zero_for_clients"},"1949":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-82","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"false"},"1950":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-83","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#coordinator-placement","content":"When enabled, excludes shard 0 from client connection placement"},"1951":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-84","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#node-roster","content":"cluster.nodes is the full roster of cluster members and is intended to be byte-identical on every node. The running node finds its own entry through the --replica-id CLI flag. Environment variables replace the entire cluster.nodes array rather than merging individual fields into TOML entries. If you configure any roster entry through environment variables, provide the complete roster that way."},"1952":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-85","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#name","content":"Node name, unique within the roster and non-empty."},"1953":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-86","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#ip","content":"The node's roster address. Replica-to-replica consensus traffic and follower-to-primary HTTP forwarding dial it, so it must be a literal IPv4 or IPv6 address. Hostnames and unspecified addresses (0.0.0.0 or ::) are rejected when the server resolves the roster at startup."},"1954":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-87","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#ip","content":"ip is not the bind interface for the client transports. tcp, quic, http, and websocket bind whatever their own address settings say. The roster supplies only their ports. The defaults bind loopback, so a cluster spread across hosts needs each transport's address set to 0.0.0.0 or the routable interface. The server warns at startup when a bind cannot serve the advertised ip."},"1955":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-88","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#replica_id","content":"Numeric replica id for VSR consensus, 0-based. Ids must be unique and strictly less than the roster size, which makes them contiguous from 0. Rosters with more than 32 replicas are rejected at startup."},"1956":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-89","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#ports","content":"Per-node listener ports: tcp, quic, http, websocket, and tcp_replica. In cluster mode ports is the single source of listener ports: every enabled transport needs an explicit per-node port, otherwise the server refuses to start. tcp and tcp_replica are always required, even when client TCP is disabled. tcp_replica carries consensus traffic. Port 0 is rejected, and no two roster entries may claim the same ip:port."},"1957":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-90","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_address","content":"Optional client-facing address handed out in cluster metadata and leader redirects. Set it when ip is a private replica-network address unreachable by clients (Docker, Kubernetes, NAT). Accepts a literal IP or an RFC 1123 DNS hostname: ASCII letters, digits, - and ., no port, no trailing dot. When unset, clients receive ip."},"1958":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-91","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Per-client-network overrides for split networks, where one catch-all advertised_address would route in-VPC clients out through the public side:"},"1959":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-92","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Clients whose transport-level peer IP falls inside client_cidr are handed address instead of the catch-all. The longest matching prefix wins. Clients matching no selector fall back to advertised_address, then ip. address takes the same forms as advertised_address (literal IP or hostname, never a port)."},"1960":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-93","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Rules and caveats:"},"1961":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-94","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"At most 16 selectors per node. Boot rejects duplicate client_cidr entries on one node (compared truncated, so 10.0.1.0/16 duplicates 10.0.0.0/16) and any two nodes advertising one host:port to overlapping client sets."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-95","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Matching is per address family: 0.0.0.0/0 matches no IPv6 client and ::/0 matches no IPv4 client, so covering both families takes one selector per family. IPv4-mapped IPv6 CIDRs (::ffff:10.0.0.0/104) match like their IPv4 form only at prefix length 96 or longer."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-96","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Matching sees the transport-level peer address, so clients behind a proxy or load balancer match the proxy's network, not their own."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-97","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Every address must be routable from inside its own client_cidr: leader-aware clients redial whatever address metadata advertises, and a selector pointing at an unreachable host strands them mid-redirect."},"1965":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-98","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Prefer literal IPs over hostnames. SDKs differ in how they compare an advertised hostname against the address they dialed, and a mismatch costs a reconnect on every fresh connect."},"1966":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-99","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#advertised_addresses","content":"Every node must run the same server version. Do not mix 0.9 and 0.10 nodes during an upgrade."},"1967":{"id":"/docs/clustering/configuration-100","page_id":"/docs/clustering/configuration","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/configuration#authentication-and-tls","content":"[cluster.auth] configures the pre-shared-key replica handshake and [cluster.tls] wraps replica connections in TLS. Both are covered in Security."},"1968":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"page","content":"Deploy a Cluster","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy"},"1969":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy","content":"Build the binaries and run a three-node Apache Iggy cluster"},"1970":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#build-from-source","content":"Build from source"},"1971":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-one-development-node","content":"Run one development node"},"1972":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"Run a three-node cluster"},"1973":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#spanning-multiple-hosts","content":"Spanning multiple hosts"},"1974":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#containers","content":"Containers"},"1975":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#check-cluster-metadata","content":"Check cluster metadata"},"1976":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#reset-a-replica","content":"Reset a replica"},"1977":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-the-cluster-test-suites","content":"Run the cluster test suites"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy","content":"Build iggy-server and the iggy CLI, run a single development node, then bring up a three-node cluster on one machine. See Configuration for the full [cluster] reference."},"1979":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#build-from-source","content":"Build the server and CLI from the repository root:"},"1980":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#build-from-source","content":"The binaries are:"},"1981":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#build-from-source","content":"The server uses Linux io_uring. Use a recent Linux kernel and a sufficient locked-memory limit."},"1982":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-one-development-node","content":"The default core/server/config.toml has clustering disabled, so a plain run starts a single node:"},"1983":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-one-development-node","content":"--with-default-root-credentials sets the root user to iggy/iggy unless IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD are already exported. These values initialize the root user only on its first creation. Later starts recover the stored user. Supplied environment values are still validated at boot."},"1984":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-one-development-node","content":"When clustering is disabled, --replica-id 0 is still accepted. Any other id is rejected because it would have to match a cluster.nodes entry."},"1985":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"Every node loads the same configuration. Nodes are told apart only by --replica-id, which selects that node's own entry in the cluster.nodes roster."},"1986":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"Save this as /tmp/iggy-vsr.toml:"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"cluster.name is a permanent on-disk identity. It is hashed into the cluster id stamped into every metadata and partition superblock on first boot. Changing the name later makes the server refuse to start against the existing data, so renaming a cluster means starting from an empty data directory."},"1988":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"Export the settings shared by all three processes:"},"1989":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"Root credentials are mandatory on the first boot of each replica when clustering is enabled. Restarts recover the stored root user. The shared secret must be at least 32 bytes. See Security for how it's used and rotated."},"1990":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"Start each replica in a separate terminal. Use a different data path for every process:"},"1991":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-22","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"The exported settings must be present in all three terminals."},"1992":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-23","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"In cluster mode:"},"1993":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-24","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"--replica-id is required and must match exactly one cluster.nodes entry"},"1994":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-25","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"replica_id values must be unique and contiguous from 0"},"1995":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-26","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"ports is the single source of listener ports: every enabled transport needs an explicit per-node port, otherwise the server refuses to start"},"1996":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-27","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"tcp and tcp_replica ports are always required, even when client TCP is disabled. tcp_replica carries replica-to-replica consensus traffic"},"1997":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-28","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"ip must be a literal IP address. Use advertised_address when clients can't reach it (see Configuration)"},"1998":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-29","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-a-three-node-cluster","content":"use a different root path for each process on the same host"},"1999":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-30","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#spanning-multiple-hosts","content":"The roster ip is the address other replicas dial. It's not the bind interface for the client transports: tcp, quic, http, and websocket bind whatever their own address settings say, and the roster supplies only the port. The defaults listen on loopback, so a cluster spread across hosts needs each transport's address set to 0.0.0.0 or the routable interface. The server warns at startup when a bind cannot serve the advertised ip."},"2000":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-31","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#containers","content":"Every roster field can be provided through typed environment variables instead of TOML, which suits container deployments:"},"2001":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-32","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#containers","content":"Give each container a static IP and put that IP in the roster: cluster.nodes[*].ip does not accept hostnames or Docker service names. The repository ships a working reference in bdd/docker-compose.cluster.yml, a real two-node cluster used by the BDD suites."},"2002":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-33","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#check-cluster-metadata","content":"Ask any node for the cluster state with the CLI:"},"2003":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-34","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#check-cluster-metadata","content":"The response lists the cluster name, nodes, client endpoints, and current roles. cluster m is a shorthand for cluster metadata."},"2004":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-35","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#reset-a-replica","content":"iggy-server --fresh deletes the configured data directory before boot. In cluster mode this wipes that replica only: it rejoins the cluster and refills its state by transfer from the other replicas. Wiping a quorum of replicas at the same time destroys committed data, and a service unit that always passes --fresh re-transfers the whole dataset on every restart."},"2005":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-36","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-the-cluster-test-suites","content":"The cross-SDK BDD suites use a standalone server for basic features and add the two-node cluster from bdd/docker-compose.cluster.yml for leader_redirection or all:"},"2006":{"id":"/docs/clustering/deploy-37","page_id":"/docs/clustering/deploy","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/deploy#run-the-cluster-test-suites","content":"The first argument selects the SDK (rust, python, php, go, go-race, node, csharp, java, cpp, swift, or all) and the optional second argument selects one feature (basic_messaging, leader_redirection, raw_command, session_contract, stream_crud, stream_options, resource_options_patch, metadata_changes, deferred_polling, or all). leader_redirection is the clustered feature. Coverage depends on the selected SDK and feature. Use --help for the runner's current selections."},"2007":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","type":"page","content":"Cluster Durability","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/durability"},"2008":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability","content":"Quorum completion, persisted prepare history, and the failures each topic policy covers."},"2009":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"What an acknowledgement means"},"2010":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/durability#replicated-completion","content":"Replicated completion"},"2011":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"Persisted completion"},"2012":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/durability#recovery-and-failure-limits","content":"Recovery and failure limits"},"2013":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/durability#choosing-the-policies","content":"Choosing the policies"},"2014":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability","content":"Iggy uses Viewstamped Replication to commit partition operations. The topic's durability and consumer_offset_durability options select the storage guarantee required for message production and explicit offset changes, respectively. Both default independently to replicated. Both continue to write data to disk."},"2015":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability","content":"For the creation options and single-node behavior, see Durability."},"2016":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"For an operation that waits for completion:"},"2017":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"Policy"},"2018":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"Required before success"},"2019":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"replicated"},"2020":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"VSR quorum commit and local application, without an additional stable-storage barrier"},"2021":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"persisted"},"2022":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"VSR quorum commit backed by recoverable stable-storage copies at the required quorum, followed by local application"},"2023":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"The policy changes what a replica must retain before its acknowledgement can count toward commit. It does not replace quorum commit with a primary-only disk write."},"2024":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"Iggy's replication quorum is not a strict majority for every group size:"},"2025":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"Replicas"},"2026":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"Replication quorum"},"2027":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"View-change quorum"},"2028":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"1"},"2029":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"1"},"2030":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-22","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"1"},"2031":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-23","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"2"},"2032":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-24","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"2"},"2033":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-25","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"2"},"2034":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-26","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"3"},"2035":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-27","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"2"},"2036":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-28","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"2"},"2037":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-29","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"4"},"2038":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-30","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"2"},"2039":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-31","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"3"},"2040":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-32","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"5"},"2041":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-33","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"3"},"2042":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-34","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"3"},"2043":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-35","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"6"},"2044":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-36","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"3"},"2045":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-37","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"4"},"2046":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-38","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#what-an-acknowledgement-means","content":"Except for two replicas, the replication quorum is min(ceil(n / 2), 3) and the view-change quorum is n - replication_quorum + 1. Two replicas require both for either quorum. The quorums therefore intersect. These are the implemented quorum rules, not a configurable acknowledgement count."},"2047":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-39","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#replicated-completion","content":"With both policies set to replicated, partition prepares stay off the disk prepare-WAL path. Replicas retain the prepares in memory, and committed messages reach segment files through ordinary flush scheduling."},"2048":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-40","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#replicated-completion","content":"Success therefore does not prove that the operation reached stable storage on any replica. Replication protects against failures while sufficient peers retain the history and can form the quorums needed for recovery and progress. For example, a healthy three-replica group can continue after one replica fails."},"2049":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-41","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#replicated-completion","content":"Failure independence matters. A shared power loss, an OOM cascade, or a crash triggered on every replica can destroy multiple volatile copies at once. A process-only failure can lose messages still in the processes' journals, even while the kernels and their page caches remain alive. Replication count alone does not establish a bound on that loss."},"2050":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-42","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"When either policy is persisted, each multi-replica partition uses a bounded on-disk prepare WAL. Message prepares contain headers and segment references. Their payloads live in segment files retained by hard links until the WAL history can be reclaimed. Other operations are stored inline. A prepare requiring persistence cannot release its PrepareOk until its message bodies, WAL history, and durable frontier are recoverable."},"2051":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-43","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"Prepares can be forwarded while local persistence is pending. Once enough replicas have met the required barrier, the operation can commit and the primary can apply it and reply. An acknowledged message can be recovered through the WAL before ordinary segment materialization and index updates finish. Recovery does not depend on first reaching a segment flush threshold."},"2052":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-44","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"The WAL also retains predecessors across message and offset operations. Consequently:"},"2053":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-45","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"durability=persisted does not make an offset response persisted when consumer_offset_durability=replicated."},"2054":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-46","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"consumer_offset_durability=persisted with replicated messages still retains message payloads through WAL segment references. A durable offset's predecessor history must remain recoverable."},"2055":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-47","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"A shared barrier can also persist co-batched operations with the weaker policy. That incidental persistence does not strengthen what their earlier replicated acknowledgements promised."},"2056":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-48","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"Only the replicated / replicated combination avoids the disk prepare WAL entirely."},"2057":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-49","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#persisted-completion","content":"WAL history is reclaimed only after the materialized segment and offset state needed to replace it has been synchronized. The server's [partition] wal_bytes_max setting, default 256 MiB, bounds retained and queued/in-flight prepare bytes per partition. The accounting includes message bodies even when the WAL stores only references to them. Capacity pressure causes checkpointing and backpressure. It does not downgrade a persisted operation. Temporary rewrites need additional disk space."},"2058":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-50","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#recovery-and-failure-limits","content":"A restarting replica loads segment and offset state and, when enabled, reconciles the durable prepare WAL with that state. It also restores durable consensus state and follows the recovery protocol before serving as a healthy replica. It does not treat every locally recovered prepare as committed."},"2059":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-51","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#recovery-and-failure-limits","content":"Missing required history, checksum failures, or contradictory materialized state must not become an empty healthy partition. Recovery can fence a partition and repair it from peers. Storage errors withhold successful completion. A locally failed application of a committed operation fences the partition and initiates server shutdown."},"2060":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-52","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#recovery-and-failure-limits","content":"persisted protects acknowledged operations across process and power failures when the required storage copies remain intact and the storage honors synchronization. Recovery and availability still require the protocol's quorums. It cannot protect against destruction of all durable copies, nor does it prevent configured retention or explicit deletion."},"2061":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-53","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#choosing-the-policies","content":"Use replicated when the workload accepts the risk of losing acknowledged operations after correlated failures in exchange for avoiding a required storage barrier at completion. Isolate replicas across failure domains. See Deployment."},"2062":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-54","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#choosing-the-policies","content":"Use durability=persisted when acknowledged messages must be recoverable after volatile copies are lost. Select consumer_offset_durability=persisted independently when explicit offset stores and deletes need the same guarantee. Ordinary flush thresholds can remain at their defaults."},"2063":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-55","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#choosing-the-policies","content":"Neither policy turns HTTP ack=none or a poll's auto-commit into an awaited durable result. See Which responses prove completion."},"2064":{"id":"/docs/clustering/durability-56","page_id":"/docs/clustering/durability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/durability#choosing-the-policies","content":"The storage mechanisms are implemented in the partition persistence worker and prepare journal. The crash-recovery tests exercise acknowledged persisted messages and offsets below ordinary flush thresholds, on both a singleton and a three-replica cluster."},"2065":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","type":"page","content":"Multi-Leader Partitions","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader"},"2066":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","content":"How partition primaries, replication factors and client routing distribute work across Iggy nodes."},"2067":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#place-partition-primaries","content":"Place partition primaries"},"2068":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#select-copies-per-topic","content":"Select copies per topic"},"2069":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#route-clients","content":"Route clients"},"2070":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#operate-the-cluster","content":"Operate the cluster"},"2071":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","content":"A primary orders writes for one consensus group. Iggy has one metadata group and a separate group for each partition. Multi-leader partitions let different nodes lead different partitions at the same time."},"2072":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#place-partition-primaries","content":"New partitions spread their primaries across eligible nodes. The metadata primary also rebalances existing primaries one at a time. This restores the distribution after changes such as a failed node returning. Each partition still has one primary at a time, and its messages retain a partition-local order."},"2073":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#place-partition-primaries","content":"Metadata leadership does not tell a client where every message write belongs. Clients discover a partition's current primary and send partition requests there. A route also identifies the partition's history and ownership generation, so an old request cannot act on a replacement partition."},"2074":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#select-copies-per-topic","content":"replication_factor selects how many nodes store each partition of a topic. The value is fixed at topic creation. It must be from 1 through 32 and no greater than the cluster node count."},"2075":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#select-copies-per-topic","content":"When the request omits it, cluster.default_replication_factor applies. Its default is all. The metadata group always uses the full cluster roster. A topic with fewer copies does not reduce the metadata quorum requirement."},"2076":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#select-copies-per-topic","content":"This configuration requires at least three cluster nodes. A topic can override it at creation. Increasing the number of partitions does not change the factor."},"2077":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#select-copies-per-topic","content":"A factor of one provides no replica redundancy. A factor of two stops writes when either copy is unavailable. With three copies, the partition can tolerate one unavailable replica. See Durability for the quorum rules and persistence policies."},"2078":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#route-clients","content":"Rust, Go, Java, C# and Node.js have native partition routing. Python, C++ and PHP use the Rust SDK through bindings. Swift has its own TCP client. Follow the individual SDK guides for supported recovery operations."},"2079":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#route-clients","content":"HTTP can forward a request to the correct primary in one hop. It needs the cluster authentication and shared JWT configuration described in Security. Keep clocks synchronized because forwarding proofs expire after ten seconds."},"2080":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#operate-the-cluster","content":"Every node must run the same server version and the same cluster.coordinator.replica_connections_per_peer. There is no rolling upgrade between 0.9 and 0.10. The roster supports at most 32 nodes."},"2081":{"id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/multi-leader#operate-the-cluster","content":"More replica connections per peer can spread replication traffic across shards. This does not change the replication factor or quorum. Retain separate data directories for nodes and advertise addresses reachable by clients."},"2082":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","type":"page","content":"Cluster Security","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/security"},"2083":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security","content":"Replica authentication, shared-secret rotation, and TLS for replica traffic"},"2084":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-authentication","content":"Replica authentication"},"2085":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/security#rotating-the-shared-secret","content":"Rotating the shared secret"},"2086":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"Replica TLS"},"2087":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"HTTP tokens across the cluster"},"2088":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security","content":"Replica-to-replica security has two layers, both in the [cluster] configuration: a pre-shared-key handshake that authenticates peers ([cluster.auth]) and optional TLS that encrypts the replica connection ([cluster.tls])."},"2089":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-authentication","content":"Replica authentication uses a cluster-wide pre-shared key (PSK) and a BLAKE3 keyed-MAC handshake. With cluster.auth.enabled = true, every replica peer must complete the authenticated handshake or be rejected:"},"2090":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-authentication","content":"The secret must be at least 32 bytes of CSPRNG output and byte-identical on every node."},"2091":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-authentication","content":"Prefer the IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET environment variable over storing the secret in TOML. The value is masked in logs and never written into the runtime configuration snapshot."},"2092":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-authentication","content":"Authentication is off by default, which leaves replica traffic unauthenticated. Enabling it is a coordinated-restart change: flip every node in one restart."},"2093":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-authentication","content":"The 32-byte minimum is enforced whenever the cluster is enabled, even with auth.enabled = false: a configured non-empty secret shorter than 32 bytes fails boot."},"2094":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#rotating-the-shared-secret","content":"previous_shared_secret enables a rolling key rotation without an authentication outage. The retiring key is accepted for verification only. Every MAC a node produces uses shared_secret. Rotate in three rolls, restarting every node after each step:"},"2095":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#rotating-the-shared-secret","content":"shared_secret = old, previous_shared_secret = new on every node"},"2096":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#rotating-the-shared-secret","content":"shared_secret = new, previous_shared_secret = old on every node"},"2097":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#rotating-the-shared-secret","content":"shared_secret = new alone, closing the window"},"2098":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#rotating-the-shared-secret","content":"Leave previous_shared_secret empty outside a rotation. It has the same 32-byte floor and the same environment-variable preference as the primary key (IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_PREVIOUS_SHARED_SECRET), and it must differ from shared_secret. An identical value is refused at boot because the rotation would be a no-op."},"2099":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"[cluster.tls] wraps every replica connection on the tcp_replica port in TLS 1.3 (ALPN iggy-replica) before the replica handshake runs:"},"2100":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"TLS requires cluster.auth.enabled = true, and the server refuses to start otherwise. The reason: replica TLS carries no client certificates, so it authenticates the acceptor only. The PSK handshake authenticates the peer. TLS supplies confidentiality. Without the PSK requirement, any TLS-capable host could register as a replica."},"2101":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"Enabling TLS is also a coordinated-restart change: a TLS dialer can't talk to a plaintext acceptor or vice versa."},"2102":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"Two certificate modes exist:"},"2103":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"CA mode (self_signed = false, the default): cert_file, key_file, and ca_file are all required. Each node presents cert_file on its acceptor side, and dialers verify peer certificates against the ca_file trust anchors. The dialer verifies each peer against the ip string from that peer's cluster.nodes entry, so certificates need matching IP SANs."},"2104":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#replica-tls","content":"Self-signed mode (self_signed = true): the node auto-generates a certificate at boot and the dialer accepts ANY peer certificate. This gives you encrypted replica traffic without certificate management, but no acceptor authentication at all: peer authenticity rests entirely on the PSK handshake. Use CA mode when the replica network is not trusted."},"2105":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-22","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"Follower nodes forward control-plane HTTP requests (stream, topic, user, and similar metadata operations) to the current metadata primary, so any node can answer any control-plane request. This requires JWT key material that every node can verify, from either of two sources:"},"2106":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-23","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"explicitly configured http.jwt signing secrets, identical on every node, or"},"2107":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-24","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"the cluster PSK: when HTTP is enabled, cluster auth is enabled, and no http.jwt secrets are configured, the signing key is derived from shared_secret."},"2108":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-25","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"Either way, matching JWT key material lets a bearer token minted on one node verify on another. If JWT keys are derived from the PSK, rotating the PSK also changes the JWT key: previous_shared_secret applies only to replica authentication. Use independently configured, shared JWT keys when HTTP tokens must remain valid across PSK rotation. Forwarded requests dial the primary at its roster ip and ports.http. The follower verifies the caller's bearer locally before forwarding, and the primary re-authenticates the request through its normal stack."},"2109":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-26","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"Partition requests use the current partition route and can be forwarded in one hop to its primary. The contacted node signs the route and request with a relay proof. The destination rejects invalid or stale proofs and authenticates the caller separately. Proofs live for ten seconds, so synchronize node clocks. An uncertain result is returned without treating it as proof that a fresh replay is safe. ack=none confirms dispatch only."},"2110":{"id":"/docs/clustering/security-27","page_id":"/docs/clustering/security","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/security#http-tokens-across-the-cluster","content":"When HTTP TLS is enabled, forwarding uses HTTPS and pins the destination certificate to the forwarding node's own HTTP leaf certificate. All nodes therefore need the same HTTP certificate for forwarding. Distinct per-node HTTP certificates fail verification. Replica TLS is configured separately and does not encrypt the HTTP forwarding hop."},"2111":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"page","content":"Viewstamped Replication","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Clustering"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr"},"2112":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-0","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr","content":"How Apache Iggy replicates data across a cluster with VSR"},"2113":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-1","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Current implementation"},"2114":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-2","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Replication model"},"2115":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-3","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#failure-handling","content":"Failure handling"},"2116":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-4","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#wire-protocol-and-sdks","content":"Wire protocol and SDKs"},"2117":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-5","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#status","content":"Status"},"2118":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-6","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"2119":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-7","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr","content":"Apache Iggy replicates clusters with Viewstamped Replication Revisited (VSR). VSR keeps an ordered state machine consistent across replicas and elects a new primary when the current primary fails."},"2120":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-8","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr","content":"Clustering is built into the standard iggy-server binary. There's no separate build, binary, or Cargo feature: the same server runs single-node or clustered, and cluster.enabled in the configuration decides which one you get. Clustering is disabled by default."},"2121":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-9","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr","content":"Read the VSR paper used by the project for the protocol specification."},"2122":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-10","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr","content":"Iggy hasn't reached a 1.0 production release yet. The cluster protocol and configuration described here can still change between pre-release versions."},"2123":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-11","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"The implementation includes:"},"2124":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-12","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"normal operation with quorum commits"},"2125":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-13","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"view changes and deterministic primary selection"},"2126":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-14","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"metadata and partition replication"},"2127":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-15","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"persistent WAL recovery and snapshots"},"2128":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-16","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"replica rejoin with journal repair"},"2129":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-17","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"client sessions, request fencing, and duplicate detection"},"2130":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-18","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"replica authentication and optional TLS"},"2131":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-19","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"leader redirection in the client SDKs"},"2132":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-20","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"deterministic simulation and cross-SDK BDD coverage"},"2133":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-21","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"The main components are:"},"2134":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-22","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Component"},"2135":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-23","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Source"},"2136":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-24","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Responsibility"},"2137":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-25","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Server"},"2138":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-26","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"core/server"},"2139":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-27","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Sharded runtime, listeners, recovery, and request dispatch"},"2140":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-28","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Consensus"},"2141":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-29","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"core/consensus"},"2142":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-30","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"VSR state machine, quorum, view changes, and timeouts"},"2143":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-31","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Wire protocol"},"2144":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-32","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"core/binary_protocol/src/consensus"},"2145":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-33","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Fixed-size VSR headers and replica messages"},"2146":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-34","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Simulator"},"2147":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-35","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"core/simulator"},"2148":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-36","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Deterministic failures, delays, and network partitions"},"2149":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-37","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"Rust SDK"},"2150":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-38","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"core/sdk"},"2151":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-39","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#current-implementation","content":"VSR framing, sessions, retries, and leader redirection"},"2152":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-40","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Iggy splits replication by namespace:"},"2153":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-41","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Plane"},"2154":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-42","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Consensus group"},"2155":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-43","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Replicated work"},"2156":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-44","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Metadata"},"2157":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-45","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"One group on shard 0"},"2158":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-46","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Streams, topics, users, permissions, consumer groups, and access tokens"},"2159":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-47","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Partition"},"2160":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-48","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"One group per partition"},"2161":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-49","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Messages and consumer offsets"},"2162":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-50","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"New partitions spread their primaries across eligible nodes, and the metadata primary rebalances them one at a time. Each group can have a different primary. Metadata writes route through the metadata primary. Partition writes route through the primary for that partition."},"2163":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-51","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#replication-model","content":"Metadata reads can use local replicated state. Partition requests route to the current partition primary. Writes return after the required VSR commit or return a retryable error when the replica is changing view or catching up. See Client failover for how the SDKs handle both."},"2164":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-52","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#failure-handling","content":"VSR uses three main flows:"},"2165":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-53","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#failure-handling","content":"Normal operation: the primary assigns an operation number, sends Prepare, and commits after a quorum sends PrepareOk."},"2166":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-54","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#failure-handling","content":"View change: replicas exchange StartViewChange and DoViewChange, then the new primary sends StartView."},"2167":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-55","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#failure-handling","content":"Recovery: a restarted or lagging replica requests the current view and repairs missing WAL ranges before serving current state."},"2168":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-56","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#failure-handling","content":"Three replicas tolerate one unavailable replica. Five tolerate two. Larger groups use a capped replication quorum and a growing view-change quorum, so the usual 2f + 1 rule does not apply to every group size. See Cluster durability for the quorum rules. Use at least three replicas for one-node fault tolerance. A two-node cluster is useful for development, but it cannot make progress after either node fails."},"2169":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-57","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#wire-protocol-and-sdks","content":"VSR framing is the only binary wire protocol, used by TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket connections. HTTP uses the REST API. Every SDK (Rust, Go, Java, C#, C++, Node.js, Python, PHP, Swift) speaks it, and every BDD suite runs against the clustered-capable iggy-server. Binary clients built before the VSR migration cannot talk to current servers."},"2170":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-58","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#wire-protocol-and-sdks","content":"The Rust SDK needs no feature flags. Depend on the published crate:"},"2171":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-59","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#wire-protocol-and-sdks","content":"or track the repository directly with iggy = { git = \"https://github.com/apache/iggy\" }."},"2172":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-60","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#wire-protocol-and-sdks","content":"The client API is the same in single-node and cluster mode. This example uses the iggy / iggy credentials explicitly provisioned in Getting started:"},"2173":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-61","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#status","content":"VSR started as a parallel server-ng implementation behind a vsr build feature. That phase is over. The VSR server moved into the mainline core/server crate and the iggy-server binary, the feature flag is gone, and the legacy server and its wire format were deleted. What this page describes is the only server implementation."},"2174":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-62","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#next-steps","content":"Deploy a cluster: build the binaries and run a three-node cluster"},"2175":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-63","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#next-steps","content":"Configuration: every [cluster] setting and its validation rules"},"2176":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-64","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#next-steps","content":"Security: replica authentication, key rotation, and TLS"},"2177":{"id":"/docs/clustering/vsr-65","page_id":"/docs/clustering/vsr","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/clustering/vsr#next-steps","content":"Client failover: reads, writes, and leader redirection from the client side"},"2178":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"page","content":"FAQ","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","FAQ"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq"},"2179":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-0","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq","content":"Common questions about Apache Iggy, including how it compares to Kafka and other message brokers."},"2180":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-1","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-is-the-difference-between-iggy-and-traditional-message-brokers-like-kafka","content":"Q: What is the difference between Iggy and traditional message brokers like Kafka?"},"2181":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-2","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-are-there-plans-to-support-kafka-protocol-in-iggy","content":"Q: Are there plans to support Kafka protocol in Iggy?"},"2182":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-3","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-transport-protocol-should-i-use","content":"Q: What transport protocol should I use?"},"2183":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-4","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-are-the-system-requirements","content":"Q: What are the system requirements?"},"2184":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-5","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-does-consumer-group-rebalancing-work","content":"Q: How does consumer group rebalancing work?"},"2185":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-6","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-does-iggy-support-exactly-once-delivery","content":"Q: Does Iggy support exactly-once delivery?"},"2186":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-7","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-do-i-secure-my-iggy-deployment","content":"Q: How do I secure my Iggy deployment?"},"2187":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-8","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-can-i-use-iggy-with-my-existing-tooling","content":"Q: Can I use Iggy with my existing tooling?"},"2188":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-9","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-happened-to-the-tokio-based-runtime","content":"Q: What happened to the Tokio-based runtime?"},"2189":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-10","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-is-clusteringreplication-available","content":"Q: Is clustering/replication available?"},"2190":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-11","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-examples-are-available","content":"Q: What examples are available?"},"2191":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-12","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-is-the-difference-between-iggy-and-traditional-message-brokers-like-kafka","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform that stores messages in an append-only log format, similar to Kafka. However, Iggy is designed for high performance and low latency using io_uring and a thread-per-core architecture. Iggy uses its own binary protocol rather than the Kafka protocol. Clients can use an Iggy SDK, implement the documented binary protocol, or use the HTTP API."},"2192":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-13","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-are-there-plans-to-support-kafka-protocol-in-iggy","content":"The Kafka gateway supports Kafka producers, consumers and selected admin APIs. Run it separately with the Iggy bridge enabled. It supports a defined protocol subset and does not support transactions."},"2193":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-14","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-transport-protocol-should-i-use","content":"For maximum throughput and lowest latency, use TCP. If you need built-in encryption without configuring TLS separately, QUIC is a good choice. WebSocket works well for browser-based clients. HTTP is the most accessible but has the highest overhead due to JSON serialization. HTTP connections can be reused. Consumer groups can be created, inspected, and deleted, but there is no join/leave membership over HTTP."},"2194":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-15","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-are-the-system-requirements","content":"Iggy runs as a single server process without an external coordination service. Native library requirements depend on the build. The provided Linux images include hwloc and udev libraries. The Linux shard runtime requires io_uring flags introduced in kernel 5.19. The server starts with around 20 MB of RAM. The default 4 GiB memory-pool setting is a budget, with buffers allocated on demand and fallback allocations outside the pool."},"2195":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-16","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-are-the-system-requirements","content":"Docker must allow the required io_uring calls and provide enough locked-memory allowance. The supplied Compose configuration uses SYS_NICE, seccomp:unconfined and unlimited memlock. Those settings are not universal requirements for every host. See Docker and configuration."},"2196":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-17","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-does-consumer-group-rebalancing-work","content":"When consumers join or leave a consumer group, the server triggers cooperative partition rebalancing. Partitions are redistributed among active members. During rebalancing, a pending revocation phase fences new polls from the previous owner. Its configurable timeout defaults to 30s, after which the server can force a transfer. This coordinates polling ownership. It does not prove that application processing has finished."},"2197":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-18","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-does-iggy-support-exactly-once-delivery","content":"Committing an offset before application processing, including poll-time auto-commit, can provide at-most-once processing: a crash can lose unprocessed messages. Committing only after successful processing supports at-least-once processing, with duplicates possible after a crash. Recovery also depends on retention and the configured message and consumer-offset durability. Iggy does not provide an atomic transaction between a consumer offset and an external side effect. Exactly-once application effects require an idempotent operation or an atomic deduplication-and-effect transaction. Unique message IDs alone do not provide that guarantee."},"2198":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-19","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-does-iggy-support-exactly-once-delivery","content":"The Rust producer and Kafka gateway support opt-in idempotent batches. This suppresses retries within bounded producer history. It does not make external side effects atomic with offset commits."},"2199":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-20","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-how-do-i-secure-my-iggy-deployment","content":"Iggy supports TLS on all transport protocols, Argon2id password hashing, granular per-stream/per-topic permissions, Personal Access Tokens for programmatic access, and optional AES-256-GCM-SIV encryption of stored message payloads. For the HTTP API, JWT tokens are used for session management. See the Security documentation for details."},"2200":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-21","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-can-i-use-iggy-with-my-existing-tooling","content":"Iggy provides a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server with 40+ tools for LLM integration, connectors for piping data to/from external systems (e.g. PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, S3, Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit), Prometheus metrics, and OpenTelemetry traces/logs in the connectors runtime. Broker OpenTelemetry export has runtime limitations described in configuration. The HTTP API works with REST clients."},"2201":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-22","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-happened-to-the-tokio-based-runtime","content":"Iggy migrated from Tokio to a thread-per-core architecture with compio (which uses io_uring on Linux) starting with version 0.6.0. The published v0.5.0 versus v0.7.0 comparison reports 92% lower P9999 latency for its 16-producer, 16-stream, 40-million-message workload, and 18% higher throughput for the corresponding fsync workload. These are historical workload results, not a guarantee for 0.10.0. You can read the full story in the thread-per-core io_uring blog post."},"2202":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-23","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-is-clusteringreplication-available","content":"Yes. Replication based on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) is built into the server. Multi-node clustering is configured via the [cluster] section (single-node mode is the default, enabled = false). It covers consensus, view changes, node authentication, and TLS between nodes, and is maturing toward production readiness. See the Clustering documentation for the protocol and Deploy for setting up a cluster."},"2203":{"id":"/docs/faq/faq-24","page_id":"/docs/faq/faq","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/faq/faq#q-what-examples-are-available","content":"Examples are available in the repository for multiple languages: Rust (basic, getting-started, message-envelope, message-headers, multi-tenant, new-sdk, sink-data-producer, stream-builder, tcp-tls), C#, Go, Java, Node.js, PHP, Python and Swift. Docker Compose files and Helm charts are also provided."},"2204":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"page","content":"Introduction","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction"},"2205":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction","content":"The connector runtime: dynamically loaded source and sink plugins, how data flows through them, and what ships today."},"2206":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#architecture","content":"Architecture"},"2207":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#data-flow","content":"Data Flow"},"2208":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"Available Connectors"},"2209":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"Transforms"},"2210":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#supported-schemas","content":"Supported Schemas"},"2211":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Features"},"2212":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Quick Start"},"2213":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"2214":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#runtime","content":"Runtime"},"2215":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#sink","content":"Sink"},"2216":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#source","content":"Source"},"2217":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#building-the-connectors","content":"Building the connectors"},"2218":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transformations","content":"Transformations"},"2219":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction","content":"The highly performant and modular runtime for statically typed, yet dynamically loaded connectors. Ingest the data from the external sources and push it further to the Iggy streams, or fetch the data from the Iggy streams and push it further to the external sources. Create your own Rust plugins by simply implementing either the Source or Sink trait and build custom pipelines for the data processing."},"2220":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction","content":"The release uses the Docker image apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0. Until it is published, build the matching source release candidate."},"2221":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#architecture","content":"Connectors are dynamically loaded shared libraries (.so on Linux, .dylib on macOS, .dll on Windows) loaded at runtime via dlopen2. Message batches and metadata crossing the FFI boundary between the runtime and plugins are serialized using postcard, a compact binary format. Plugin configuration is passed as JSON. The SDK initializes one Tokio runtime per loaded plugin library, shared by its connector instances. All plugins run in the same process."},"2222":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#data-flow","content":"The connector runtime operates in two directions - source (ingest) and sink (egress):"},"2223":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#data-flow","content":"Key details:"},"2224":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#data-flow","content":"Transforms run inside the runtime process, not inside the plugins. They are applied after decoding for sources (before sending to Iggy) and after consuming from Iggy for sinks (before forwarding to the external system)."},"2225":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#data-flow","content":"State persistence applies to source connectors and stores the optional checkpoint bytes supplied by the plugin, using a local file or an HTTP backend. The SDK provides MessagePack helpers. The runtime acknowledges a source batch after forwarding it to Iggy and saving its optional checkpoint. See state storage."},"2226":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#data-flow","content":"Consumer groups track sink offsets automatically. Offsets are committed when messages are polled, before processing by the external sink completes."},"2227":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"Type"},"2228":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"Connectors"},"2229":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"Source"},"2230":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"Elasticsearch, HTTP, InfluxDB, PostgreSQL, Random, MQTT"},"2231":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"Sink"},"2232":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#available-connectors","content":"BigQuery, ClickHouse, Delta Lake, Apache Doris, Elasticsearch, HTTP, Apache Iceberg, InfluxDB, Meilisearch, MongoDB, PostgreSQL, Quickwit, RabbitMQ, Redshift, S3, Stdout, SurrealDB"},"2233":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"The following built-in transforms are available:"},"2234":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"add_fields - add new fields to the message payload."},"2235":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"delete_fields - remove existing fields from the message payload."},"2236":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"filter_fields - keep or drop fields based on key and value patterns."},"2237":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"update_fields - update existing fields with static or computed values such as timestamp_millis or uuid_v7."},"2238":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"proto_convert - convert messages between Protocol Buffers and other formats."},"2239":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"flat_buffer_convert - convert messages between FlatBuffers and other formats."},"2240":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"avro_convert - convert messages between Avro and other formats."},"2241":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"unwrap_envelope - promote a nested field of a JSON envelope to the top-level payload."},"2242":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transforms","content":"See the Transforms documentation for the configuration of each transform."},"2243":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#supported-schemas","content":"Messages can be decoded and encoded using the following formats: JSON, Raw bytes, Text, Protocol Buffers, FlatBuffers, and Avro. For Avro, the schema can be provided per stream via the avro_schema_json or avro_schema_path keys in the [[streams]] section."},"2244":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#supported-schemas","content":"The BigQuery sink writes through the Storage Write API. The MQTT source supports deferred broker acknowledgements."},"2245":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"High Performance: Utilizes Rust's performance characteristics to ensure fast data ingestion and egress."},"2246":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Low memory footprint: Designed with memory efficiency in mind, minimizing the memory footprint of the connectors."},"2247":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Modular Design: Designed with modularity in mind, allowing for easy extension and customization."},"2248":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Dynamic Loading: Supports dynamic loading of plugins, allowing integration with various data sources and sinks at runtime."},"2249":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Statically Typed: Ensures type safety and compile-time checks, reducing runtime errors."},"2250":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Easy Customization: Provides a simple interface for implementing custom connectors, making it easy to create new plugins."},"2251":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Data transformation: Supports data transformation with the help of existing functions."},"2252":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Powerful configuration: Define your sinks, sources, and transformations in the configuration file or fetch them from a remote HTTP API."},"2253":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#features","content":"Flexible configuration providers: Support for local file-based and HTTP-based configuration providers for centralized configuration management."},"2254":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Run these commands from the root of the same Iggy source checkout used for the server and plugins. This guide targets server 0.10.0."},"2255":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Build the server, CLI, runtime and quick-start plugins:"},"2256":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"For a debug build, omit --release and replace target/release with target/debug in both the commands and plugin paths. Make sure that the plugins specified in core/connectors/runtime/example_config/connectors/ directory under path are available. The configuration must be provided in toml format. The example directory also enables connectors for ClickHouse, Delta Lake, Apache Doris, Apache Iceberg, and InfluxDB. Without their backing services (or their compiled plugins) these are reported with the Error status, but they don't block the remaining connectors. Set enabled = false in their files to skip them entirely."},"2257":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Run docker compose -f examples/rust/src/sink-data-producer/docker-compose.yml up -d, which will start the Quickwit server to be used by an example sink connector. At this point, you can access the Quickwit UI at http://localhost:7280 - check this dashboard again later on, after the events index will be created."},"2258":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"In the terminal that will run the connectors, set the runtime configuration path:"},"2259":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Start the Iggy server in a separate terminal with credentials matching the sample connector configuration:"},"2260":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"With the server running, create the example streams and topics using the CLI from this checkout. An existing server must have these credentials, or you must adjust the commands and connector configuration to match it."},"2261":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Execute cargo run --example sink-data-producer --release, which sends 100 batches of messages to previously created qw stream and records topic (this will be used by the Quickwit sink connector)."},"2262":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#quick-start","content":"Start the connector runtime cargo run --bin iggy-connectors --release in the terminal configured in step 3. The Quickwit sink indexes the produced records in the events index. At the same time, you should see the new messages being added to the example_stream stream and example_topic topic by the Random source connector - you can start the Iggy Web UI to browse the data. The messages will have applied the basic fields transformations."},"2263":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#configuration","content":"Overriding the configuration via environment variables follows the same rules as the main Iggy server."},"2264":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-59","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#configuration","content":"You can provide the following envs during the runtime startup:"},"2265":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-60","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#configuration","content":"Supported scalar fields and indexed list entries can be overridden with the IGGY_CONNECTORS_ prefix, followed by section and key names joined by underscores, e.g. IGGY_CONNECTORS_IGGY_USERNAME. Header and URL-template maps are configured in TOML. Local connector settings use a per-connector prefix, described in the runtime configuration guide."},"2266":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-61","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#runtime","content":"All the connectors are implemented as Rust libraries and can be used as a part of the connector runtime. The runtime is responsible for managing the lifecycle of the connectors and providing the necessary infrastructure for the connectors to run. For more information, please refer to the runtime documentation."},"2267":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-62","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#sink","content":"Sinks are responsible for consuming the messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s) and sending them further to the specified destination. For example, the Quickwit sink connector is responsible for sending the messages to the Quickwit indexer."},"2268":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-63","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#sink","content":"Please refer to the Sink documentation for the details about the configuration and the sample implementation."},"2269":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-64","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#sink","content":"When implementing Sink, make sure to use the sink_connector! macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink with the runtime. Each sink should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID via expected new() method."},"2270":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-65","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#source","content":"Sources produce messages to an Iggy stream and topic. Configure one [[streams]] entry per source instance: the runtime currently retains only the last configured producer. For example, the Random source connector generates random messages that are then sent to the configured stream and topic."},"2271":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-66","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#source","content":"Please refer to the Source documentation for the details about the configuration and the sample implementation."},"2272":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-67","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#building-the-connectors","content":"New connector can be built simply by implementing either Sink or Source trait. Please check the sink or source documentation, as well as the existing examples under core/connectors/sinks and core/connectors/sources."},"2273":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-68","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transformations","content":"Field transformations (depending on the supported payload formats) can be applied to the messages either before they are sent to the specified topic (e.g. when produced by the source connectors), or before consumed by the sink connectors. To add a new transformation, implement the Transform trait in a new module under the SDK's transforms directory, add a variant to the TransformType enum, and extend its from_config function - the runtime picks it up without any further changes. Each transform may have its own, custom configuration."},"2274":{"id":"/docs/connectors/introduction-69","page_id":"/docs/connectors/introduction","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/introduction#transformations","content":"To find out more about the transforms, please refer to the Transforms documentation. For the stream decoders and encoders, see the SDK documentation."},"2275":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","type":"page","content":"Observability","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/observability"},"2276":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability","content":"Logs, Prometheus metrics, the stats endpoint, OpenTelemetry export and per-source state files for the connector runtime."},"2277":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/observability#logging","content":"Logging"},"2278":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"Prometheus metrics"},"2279":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/observability#runtime-stats","content":"Runtime stats"},"2280":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/observability#opentelemetry","content":"OpenTelemetry"},"2281":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/observability#state-files","content":"State files"},"2282":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability","content":"The connector runtime exposes its health through logs, Prometheus metrics, a stats endpoint, OpenTelemetry export, and per-source state files."},"2283":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#logging","content":"The runtime logs via the tracing crate. Merge the runtime configuration snippets on this page into the minimal runtime configuration. The output format is configured in the [logging] section:"},"2284":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#logging","content":"Two per-connector flags, both disabled by default, add more detail. Set them at the top level of an existing connector configuration file, before any TOML section headers:"},"2285":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#logging","content":"verbose: logs additional per-batch details for the connector."},"2286":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#logging","content":"benchmark: emits per-batch timing events on the iggy_connectors::benchmark tracing target."},"2287":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"Metrics are served by the runtime HTTP API when the [http.metrics] section is enabled (the HTTP API itself is on by default at 127.0.0.1:8081). Metrics are disabled by default. Change enabled to true to expose them:"},"2288":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"Runtime-level gauges:"},"2289":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connectors_sources_total - configured source connectors."},"2290":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connectors_sources_running - sources in the Running status."},"2291":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connectors_sinks_total - configured sink connectors."},"2292":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connectors_sinks_running - sinks in the Running status."},"2293":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"Per-connector counters, labeled with connector_key and connector_type (source or sink):"},"2294":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connector_messages_produced_total - messages received from a source plugin's poll."},"2295":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connector_messages_sent_total - messages sent to Iggy (source)."},"2296":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connector_messages_consumed_total - messages consumed from Iggy (sink)."},"2297":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connector_messages_processed_total - messages passed to the sink plugin in batches that returned success. A failed batch adds an error and no processed messages. The runtime cannot infer partial delivery inside the plugin."},"2298":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connector_messages_filtered_total - messages intentionally dropped by transforms returning Ok(None), excluding transform errors."},"2299":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"iggy_connector_errors_total - errors encountered."},"2300":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#prometheus-metrics","content":"Per-batch stage timings are recorded in the iggy_connector_stage_duration_seconds histogram independently of the benchmark flag, labeled with connector_key, connector_type, and stage. Both connector types record decode, prepare, and total. Sinks also record ffi, while sources record iggy_send and successful checkpoint writes record state_save. Finite buckets range from 50 microseconds to 5 seconds."},"2301":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#runtime-stats","content":"GET /stats on the HTTP API returns a JSON snapshot of the runtime: version, process ID, CPU and memory usage (cgroup-aware in containers), uptime, the source/sink totals, and a connectors array with per-connector details - key, name, type, plugin version, enabled, message counters, error count, and the current status."},"2302":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#runtime-stats","content":"Memory values are in bytes. run_time is elapsed microseconds, and start_time is microseconds since the Unix epoch."},"2303":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#runtime-stats","content":"The JSON status values are starting, running, stopping, stopped, and error. A connector whose plugin fails to load (for example, a missing library) is reported with error without blocking the remaining connectors. Retrieve its last_error from GET /sources/{key} or GET /sinks/{key}. /stats does not include the failure message."},"2304":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#opentelemetry","content":"The [telemetry] section configures OTLP export of logs and traces. Set enabled = true to export to a running collector:"},"2305":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#opentelemetry","content":"For transport = \"http\", use complete signal URLs, for example http://localhost:4318/v1/logs for logs and http://localhost:4318/v1/traces for traces. The runtime does not append those paths."},"2306":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#state-files","content":"With the default file state backend, source plugins can supply checkpoints (see the SDK documentation) for the directory configured in the [state] section. An HTTP state backend is also available:"},"2307":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#state-files","content":"A source that supplies checkpoint bytes gets a file named source_{key}.state (e.g. local_state/source_random.state) holding the raw state bytes - by convention MessagePack produced by the SDK helpers. Sinks have no state files. Their progress lives in Iggy consumer groups."},"2308":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#state-files","content":"On Unix, the file backend writes and synchronizes a .tmp file, atomically renames it over the previous checkpoint, and synchronizes the parent directory. Newly created files have owner-only permissions (0600), since state may carry cursors or tokens."},"2309":{"id":"/docs/connectors/observability-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/observability","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/observability#state-files","content":"An empty or missing state file supplies no previous checkpoint when the connector starts. Loading fails if the file cannot be read or its parent directory is unavailable at load time. Stop the runtime before deleting a checkpoint to reset that source on its next start. Deleting it while the connector runs does not reset its in-memory position."},"2310":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"page","content":"Runtime","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime"},"2311":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"How the connector runtime loads plugins, resolves its configuration, and manages connector lifecycles."},"2312":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"How configuration is resolved"},"2313":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"How plugins are resolved"},"2314":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#minimal-configuration","content":"Minimal configuration"},"2315":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"State storage"},"2316":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-providers","content":"Configuration Providers"},"2317":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#local-file-provider","content":"Local File Provider"},"2318":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-configuration-provider","content":"HTTP Configuration Provider"},"2319":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"Configuration Options"},"2320":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Default URL Templates"},"2321":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"HTTP API"},"2322":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#deferred-sink-reads","content":"Deferred sink reads"},"2323":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#exactly-once-sources","content":"Exactly-once sources"},"2324":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"Runtime is responsible for managing the lifecycle of the connectors and providing the necessary infrastructure for the connectors to run."},"2325":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"The runtime uses a shared Tokio runtime for its connector-management and forwarding tasks. Each loaded plugin library also has an SDK Tokio runtime shared by its instances. Additionally, it has built-in support for logging via tracing crate."},"2326":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"The connector are implemented as Rust libraries, and these are loaded dynamically during the runtime initialization process."},"2327":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"Internally, dlopen2 loads plugin libraries and resolves their C FFI symbols. Plugins execute inside the runtime process."},"2328":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"By default, runtime will look for the configuration file, to decide which connectors to load and how to configure them."},"2329":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"Set the broker credentials and connector configuration directory before starting the runtime. The embedded default has an empty connector directory and cannot start unchanged. For a complete setup, follow the quick start."},"2330":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime","content":"The release uses the Docker image apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0. Until it is published, build the matching source release candidate."},"2331":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"The configuration file path defaults to core/connectors/runtime/config.toml and can be overridden by the IGGY_CONNECTORS_CONFIG_PATH environment variable."},"2332":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"A default configuration is embedded in the binary and always merged as the base, so omitted settings inherit its values. A local provider still needs a nonempty connectors.config_dir."},"2333":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"On startup the runtime loads environment variables from the first .env file found in the working directory or its parents, or from the file pointed to by IGGY_CONNECTORS_ENV_PATH."},"2334":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"Supported scalar fields and indexed list entries can be overridden by environment variables using the IGGY_CONNECTORS_<SECTION>_<KEY> convention (nested keys joined by underscores), e.g. IGGY_CONNECTORS_IGGY_USERNAME or IGGY_CONNECTORS_HTTP_ADDRESS."},"2335":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"Run iggy-connectors --list-config-env-vars to print the supported names and templates, then exit before dotenv and configuration loading, runtime creation, plugin loading, or other startup activity. The output is sorted and uses these placeholders:"},"2336":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"<N> is a vector index from 0 through 255 for connector stream fields. Whole-value vectors are listed once without a placeholder."},"2337":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"<KEY> is the configured connector key, uppercased. These per-connector overrides are available only with the local configuration provider."},"2338":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-configuration-is-resolved","content":"<FIELD> sets one lowercased top-level plugin configuration key. Underscores are preserved (A_B becomes a_b, not nested a.b), and nested plugin configuration keys cannot be set through environment variables. FORMAT is reserved and is listed separately as ..._PLUGIN_CONFIG_FORMAT. The passthrough template does not guarantee that a plugin accepts every field."},"2339":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"The path field of a connector configuration accepts both plugin.so and plugin - the OS-specific extension (.so / .dylib / .dll) is appended when missing. Absolute paths are checked at the literal location. Relative paths are searched in order:"},"2340":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"Literal path (relative to the working directory)"},"2341":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"Directory of the runtime binary (filename only)"},"2342":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"Current working directory (filename only)"},"2343":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"/usr/lib, /usr/lib64, /lib, /lib64, /usr/local/lib, /usr/local/lib64"},"2344":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#how-plugins-are-resolved","content":"A connector whose plugin cannot be resolved or loaded is surfaced with the Error status via the HTTP API instead of blocking the remaining connectors."},"2345":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#minimal-configuration","content":"The runtime opens two Iggy TCP clients, one for producers and one for consumers. Set credentials matching the broker and a connector configuration provider. Omitted settings use the embedded defaults, including file-based source state storage. Save this example as connectors.toml in the repository root and replace path/to/connectors with your connector configuration directory."},"2346":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#minimal-configuration","content":"Start it from the repository root:"},"2347":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#minimal-configuration","content":"Beyond these sections, the runtime configuration also supports [http] for the HTTP API (see below), and [telemetry], [logging], and [http.metrics] covered on the Observability page."},"2348":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"Source plugins supply optional checkpoint bytes. The default file backend writes them to {state.path}/source_{key}.state using a temporary file, file synchronization and atomic rename. On Unix, it also synchronizes the parent directory. The runtime stores these bytes unchanged. The SDK provides MessagePack serialization helpers."},"2349":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"To use an HTTP state server, replace the [state] section above and add:"},"2350":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"The runtime appends source_{key} as a URL path segment, preserving any query string. URL query values are redacted from runtime configuration logs and transport errors. Static request headers are passed to the state server. Configure this map in TOML. The other state settings support environment overrides such as IGGY_CONNECTORS_STATE_STORAGE and IGGY_CONNECTORS_STATE_HTTP_URL."},"2351":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"The HTTP server must implement this checkpoint contract:"},"2352":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"Load uses GET by default, or POST. Return 200 with the stored bytes and a strong ETag, or 404 when no state exists. An empty 200 body is valid state."},"2353":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"Save uses PUT by default, or POST/PATCH, with Content-Type: application/octet-stream. Atomically enforce If-Match for a known ETag or If-None-Match: * for a new checkpoint. Successful 200, 201 or 204 responses must include the new strong ETag."},"2354":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"Each logical save has an Idempotency-Key, reused across retries and resolution of an uncertain write. The server must replay the original outcome for that key without applying the write again."},"2355":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"If-Match, If-None-Match, Idempotency-Key and Content-Type are managed by the runtime and cannot be overridden in request_headers."},"2356":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"Timeouts, connection failures, 425, 429 and 5xx responses are retried. max_attempts counts retries after the first request, so the default 4 allows up to 5 requests. Integer-seconds Retry-After values are honored up to max_backoff. Other save failures, including version conflicts or missing ETags, latch the provider: further saves fail until the connector restarts. An HTTP state-load failure while an enabled source starts aborts runtime startup."},"2357":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#state-storage","content":"The runtime forwards a source batch to Iggy, saves its optional checkpoint, then acknowledges the batch to the plugin. A failed send or checkpoint save produces a negative acknowledgement. Remote checkpoint durability depends on the state server. Connector lifecycle operations do not delete remote state."},"2358":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-providers","content":"The runtime supports two types of configuration providers for managing connector configurations:"},"2359":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#local-file-provider","content":"The default configuration provider reads connector configurations from local files. Each connector (source or sink) is configured in its own separate file within the directory specified by connectors.config_dir. An empty config_dir is a fatal startup error. A missing directory is created automatically with a warning, and no connectors are loaded from it."},"2360":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#local-file-provider","content":"Additional mechanics of the local provider:"},"2361":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#local-file-provider","content":"Only *.toml files directly inside the directory are loaded - hidden files (starting with .) and Cargo.toml are skipped."},"2362":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#local-file-provider","content":"Multiple files sharing the same connector key form a version history (each file carries its own version). The active version of each connector is persisted in {config_dir}/.active_versions.toml and can be selected via the HTTP API for the next process startup. Without a saved selection, the highest version is used. Changing this selection does not update a running connector. The connector restart endpoint currently loads the highest local version, which can differ from the selected active version."},"2363":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#local-file-provider","content":"Top-level plugin_config fields can be overridden per connector via environment variables using the IGGY_CONNECTORS_{SINK|SOURCE}_{KEY}_PLUGIN_CONFIG_<FIELD> convention, e.g. IGGY_CONNECTORS_SINK_QUICKWIT_PLUGIN_CONFIG_URL."},"2364":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-configuration-provider","content":"The HTTP configuration provider allows the runtime to fetch connector configurations from a remote HTTP/REST API. The provider fetches active configurations at startup and handles configuration operations requested through the runtime API. It does not periodically poll for remote changes."},"2365":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"base_url (required): Base URL of the configuration API endpoint"},"2366":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"timeout (optional): HTTP request timeout (default: 10s)"},"2367":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"request_headers (optional): Custom headers to include in all HTTP requests (e.g., authentication headers)"},"2368":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"url_templates (optional): Custom URL templates for API endpoints. Supports variable substitution with {key} and {version} placeholders."},"2369":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"response.data_path (optional): Dot-separated object keys or numeric array indexes used to extract data (e.g., data.config or data.0)."},"2370":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-59","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#configuration-options","content":"response.error_path (optional): A path with the same syntax. Any non-null value at this path is treated as an error, including false or an empty string."},"2371":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-60","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"If not customized, the HTTP provider uses the following RESTful URL patterns:"},"2372":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-61","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Create sink config: POST {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs"},"2373":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-62","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Create source config: POST {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs"},"2374":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-63","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get active configs: GET {base_url}/configs/active"},"2375":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-64","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get active versions: GET {base_url}/configs/active/versions"},"2376":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-65","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Set active sink version: PUT {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/active"},"2377":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-66","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Set active source version: PUT {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/active"},"2378":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-67","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get sink configs: GET {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs"},"2379":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-68","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get sink config by version: GET {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/{version}"},"2380":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-69","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get active sink config: GET {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/active"},"2381":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-70","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get source configs: GET {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs"},"2382":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-71","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get source config by version: GET {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/{version}"},"2383":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-72","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Get active source config: GET {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/active"},"2384":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-73","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Delete sink config: DELETE {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs"},"2385":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-74","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"Delete source config: DELETE {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs"},"2386":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-75","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#default-url-templates","content":"The HTTP provider expects the remote API to implement these endpoints and return connector configuration data in the same format as used by the local provider."},"2387":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-76","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"Connector runtime has an HTTP API which is enabled by default at 127.0.0.1:8081. It can be disabled by setting the enabled flag to false in the [http] section."},"2388":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-77","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"When api_key is set, every request must carry it in the api-key header - except GET / and GET /health, which stay public. An empty key disables authentication. Configuration endpoints return plugin credentials and accept configuration changes, so set an API key before exposing this API to other hosts. Enable TLS or use a trusted TLS proxy for remote access."},"2389":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-78","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"Currently, it does expose the following endpoints:"},"2390":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-79","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /: welcome message."},"2391":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-80","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /health: process liveness response. It does not check connector health. Inspect /stats, /sources or /sinks for connector status."},"2392":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-81","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /stats: runtime statistics (process info plus per-connector status and message counters)."},"2393":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-82","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /metrics: Prometheus metrics, available when [http.metrics] is enabled (path configurable via its endpoint key)."},"2394":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-83","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks: list of sinks."},"2395":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-84","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks/{key}: sink details."},"2396":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-85","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks/{key}/configs: list of configuration versions for the sink."},"2397":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-86","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"POST /sinks/{key}/configs: add a new configuration version for the sink."},"2398":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-87","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"DELETE /sinks/{key}/configs: delete one configuration version, chosen by the version query parameter or the locally saved active selection."},"2399":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-88","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks/{key}/configs/{version}: configuration details for a specific version."},"2400":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-89","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks/{key}/configs/active: active configuration details."},"2401":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-90","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"PUT /sinks/{key}/configs/active: activate a specific configuration version for the sink."},"2402":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-91","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks/{key}/configs/plugin: sink plugin config, including the optional format query parameter to specify the config format."},"2403":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-92","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sinks/{key}/transforms: sink transforms to be applied to the fields."},"2404":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-93","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"POST /sinks/{key}/restart: restart the sink connector."},"2405":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-94","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources: list of sources."},"2406":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-95","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}: source details."},"2407":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-96","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/configs: list of configuration versions for the source."},"2408":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-97","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"POST /sources/{key}/configs: add a new configuration version for the source."},"2409":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-98","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"DELETE /sources/{key}/configs: delete one configuration version, chosen by the version query parameter or the locally saved active selection."},"2410":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-99","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/configs/{version}: configuration details for a specific version."},"2411":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-100","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/configs/active: active configuration details."},"2412":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-101","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"PUT /sources/{key}/configs/active: activate a specific configuration version for the source."},"2413":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-102","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/configs/plugin: source plugin config, including the optional format query parameter to specify the config format."},"2414":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-103","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"GET /sources/{key}/transforms: source transforms to be applied to the fields."},"2415":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-104","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#http-api","content":"POST /sources/{key}/restart: restart the source connector."},"2416":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-105","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#deferred-sink-reads","content":"Sink stream entries use max_wait and min_count. The default wait is one second and the minimum count is one. batch_length limits returned messages. A wait must be positive, and min_count must be from one through the batch length. The server can cap a requested wait."},"2417":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-106","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#deferred-sink-reads","content":"The sink starts processing when the read is ready, its byte budget is reached or the wait ends. Replace the old poll_interval field. A sink that still supplies it fails to start, even when max_wait is also present."},"2418":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-107","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#deferred-sink-reads","content":"The runtime selects CommitPolicy::OnRead. Offsets can advance before a plugin finishes its callback. A failed sink callback is recorded and does not cause the runtime to replay that batch. Deferred reads change waiting and batching, not this delivery guarantee."},"2419":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-108","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#exactly-once-sources","content":"exactly_once=true enables producer batch identity for a source. The plugin must reproduce the same messages from its saved state. The runtime stores producer ID, epoch, sequence and partition with the plugin checkpoint before sending."},"2420":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-109","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#exactly-once-sources","content":"All batches go to one partition, zero when omitted. Each plugin batch is one request. batch_length and linger_time do not apply in this mode. Use a topic with persisted durability and upgrade every node and reader before enabling producer-aware batches."},"2421":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-110","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#exactly-once-sources","content":"The guarantee has limits. A partition retains five batches per producer, at most 4096 producers, and expires producers idle for 24 hours. A changed batch count, a changed partition, expired producer state, or more than five unsaved batches can cause duplicates after recovery. The plugin must return new checkpoint state often enough to preserve its replay position."},"2422":{"id":"/docs/connectors/runtime-111","page_id":"/docs/connectors/runtime","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/runtime#exactly-once-sources","content":"Turning the mode off preserves plugin state in the ordinary format after the next save. Before rolling back to an older runtime, disable it and wait for that save. Older runtimes do not understand the producer record. See Idempotent producers."},"2423":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"page","content":"SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk"},"2424":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk","content":"The Sink and Source traits, the connector macros, and the decoders and encoders used to read and write Iggy streams."},"2425":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"Traits"},"2426":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#state","content":"State"},"2427":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#retry-utilities","content":"Retry utilities"},"2428":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transforms","content":"Transforms"},"2429":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#protocol-buffers-support","content":"Protocol Buffers Support"},"2430":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Configuration Example"},"2431":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#key-configuration-options","content":"Key Configuration Options"},"2432":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-encoder-and-decoder-configuration","content":"Programmatic Encoder and Decoder Configuration"},"2433":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"Transform Options"},"2434":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Supported Features"},"2435":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-usage","content":"Programmatic Usage"},"2436":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#dynamic-schema-loading","content":"Dynamic Schema Loading"},"2437":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#creating-converters-with-schema","content":"Creating Converters with Schema"},"2438":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Usage Notes"},"2439":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk","content":"SDK provides the commonly used structs and traits such as Sink and Source, along with the sink_connector and source_connector macros to be used when developing connectors."},"2440":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk","content":"Moreover, it contains both, the decoders and encoders modules, implementing either StreamDecoder or StreamEncoder traits, which are used when consuming or producing data from/to Iggy streams. All the supported schemas - JSON, Raw bytes, Text, Protocol Buffers, FlatBuffers, and Avro - have both a decoder and an encoder."},"2441":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"A source produces messages to the configured stream and topic. A sink consumes messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s):"},"2442":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"Expose the implementation with sink_connector!(MySink) or source_connector!(MySource). The sink constructor has the signature new(id: u32, config: MySinkConfig) -> Self. The source constructor also takes state: Option<ConnectorState>. The sink guide and source guide provide complete plugin implementations and build setup."},"2443":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#traits","content":"The macros generate the C FFI symbols loaded by the runtime: iggy_sink_open, iggy_sink_consume, iggy_sink_close, and iggy_sink_version for sinks, and iggy_source_open, iggy_source_handle_v2, iggy_source_batch_result, iggy_source_close, and iggy_source_version for sources. Configurations cross FFI as JSON, message batches and metadata use postcard, and source checkpoint bytes are opaque. The SDK creates one Tokio runtime per loaded plugin library, shared by that library's connector instances."},"2444":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#state","content":"Source connectors can persist their position between restarts. Each batch of ProducedMessages may carry an optional ConnectorState. After processing and sending the batch to Iggy, the runtime saves that state through the configured file or HTTP backend before acknowledging the batch. The last saved state is passed to new() on the next startup. ConnectorState wraps raw bytes and provides serialize/deserialize helpers backed by MessagePack."},"2445":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#state","content":"Sources with cursors or destructive operations must stage changes during poll() and apply them on SourceBatchResult::Ack. Nack discards staged progress so the batch can be polled again. The SDK allows one batch in flight and stops polling if on_batch_result() returns an error. Its default no-op is suitable only for sources without staged work."},"2446":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#state","content":"Sinks can keep in-memory state, but the runtime does not persist it. Their consumer-group offsets are tracked by Iggy. See the Observability page for checkpoint storage and the sink guide for offset handling."},"2447":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#retry-utilities","content":"The retry module ships helpers for connectors talking to flaky external systems: a CircuitBreaker, an HttpRetryMiddleware for reqwest-based clients, and a build_retry_client factory that wraps a reqwest client with the retry middleware (the circuit breaker is wired separately)."},"2448":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transforms","content":"Last but not least, the different transforms are available, to transform (add, update, delete etc.) the particular fields of the data being processed via external configuration. It's as simple as adding a new transform to the transforms section of the particular connector configuration file:"},"2449":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transforms","content":"All the available transforms and their configuration options are documented on the Transforms page."},"2450":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#protocol-buffers-support","content":"The SDK includes support for Protocol Buffers (protobuf) format with both encoding and decoding capabilities. Protocol Buffers provide efficient serialization and are particularly useful for high-performance data streaming scenarios."},"2451":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"This example uses the Random source and Stdout sink from the matching checkout. Start a server using the getting-started guide, then build the plugins, runtime, and CLI from the repository root:"},"2452":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"The source's schema = \"proto\" selects the default protobuf encoder, which wraps each JSON record in a google.protobuf.StringValue inside google.protobuf.Any. The sink reads raw bytes and applies proto_convert to expose the Any envelope as JSON. This does not unpack a custom protobuf message schema."},"2453":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Main runtime config (connectors.toml):"},"2454":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Source connector config (connectors/protobuf_source.toml):"},"2455":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Sink connector config (connectors/protobuf_sink.toml):"},"2456":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"Create the stream and topic, then start the runtime from the repository root:"},"2457":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"The source sends 100 records, then continues polling without new messages. Stdout logs message offsets and the serialized JSON envelope bytes, containing type_url and base64 value. The sink's raw schema also determines how the plugin receives those transformed bytes."},"2458":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"The format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults. Every non-optional key shown above must be present, or the configuration fails to deserialize (schema_path, message_type, field_mappings, and descriptor_set are optional)."},"2459":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#configuration-example","content":"The two [[streams]] shapes differ: a source produces to a single topic and can tune batching via batch_length and linger_time, while a sink consumes from a list of topics and can additionally set batch_length, max_wait, min_count, and consumer_group."},"2460":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-encoder-and-decoder-configuration","content":"These are SDK configuration fields, not Random or Stdout plugin_config keys. The runtime's schema = \"proto\" uses the default encoder or decoder."},"2461":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-encoder-and-decoder-configuration","content":"schema_path: Path to the .proto file containing message definitions"},"2462":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-encoder-and-decoder-configuration","content":"message_type: Fully qualified name of the protobuf message type to use"},"2463":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-encoder-and-decoder-configuration","content":"use_any_wrapper: Selects the Any fallback when no message descriptor is loaded. A loaded descriptor takes precedence"},"2464":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"proto_convert: Transform for converting between protobuf and other formats"},"2465":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"source_format / target_format: Formats to convert between - any schema value (json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro)"},"2466":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"preserve_unknown_fields: Accepted by proto_convert, but currently has no effect"},"2467":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"include_paths: Additional directories searched for imported .proto files"},"2468":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"field_mappings: Renames fields in a JSON input object before conversion (e.g., \"old_field\" = \"new_field\")"},"2469":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"conversion_options: pretty_json controls JSON text output and include_metadata enriches supported protobuf-to-JSON paths. validate_messages, type_url_prefix, and strict_mode are accepted but currently have no effect"},"2470":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#transform-options","content":"The schema_registry_url field is reserved and currently not implemented. The SDK never contacts a schema registry, and schemas are loaded only from schema_path or descriptor_set."},"2471":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Encoding: A loaded message descriptor encodes matching JSON fields. The encoder supports booleans, strings, all protobuf integer types, and base64 strings for bytes or already-encoded nested messages. Float, double, and enum fields are unsupported by the encoder. Nested JSON objects, repeated fields, maps, and proto2 groups are not a general-purpose schema conversion path."},"2472":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Decoding: A loaded descriptor extracts present fields. Integer, boolean, and string fields become JSON values. Bytes become base64 and nested messages become metadata with base64 content. Missing fields are not filled with protobuf defaults, and float/double fields produce unsupported_wire_type placeholders. Without a descriptor, the default decoder returns an Any envelope's type_url and base64 value."},"2473":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Transforms: proto_convert supports JSON-to-protobuf schema encoding for scalar fields, including floating-point numbers and numeric enum values. Bytes and already-encoded nested messages use base64 strings. It logs and omits fields it cannot encode. Its protobuf-to-JSON path exposes Any metadata or raw-data metadata rather than decoding a custom message descriptor. Converting protobuf to flat_buffer or avro rewraps bytes without transcoding them."},"2474":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Field Mapping: Encoder/decoder mappings use protobuf field names as keys and JSON field names as values. The encoder applies that mapping in reverse. Transform mappings rename JSON input keys directly."},"2475":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#supported-features","content":"Any Wrapper: The default encoder puts JSON/text in a google.protobuf.StringValue, or binary data in a google.protobuf.BytesValue, inside google.protobuf.Any. The default decoder exposes the envelope without unpacking its inner message."},"2476":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-usage","content":"From the matching repository root, create an example crate and schema directory:"},"2477":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-usage","content":"Save this as connector-sdk-example/Cargo.toml. The path dependency uses the SDK from the same checkout as the runtime:"},"2478":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-usage","content":"Save this as schemas/user.proto:"},"2479":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#programmatic-usage","content":"Each Rust example below is a complete connector-sdk-example/src/main.rs. Run it from the repository root so the relative schema path resolves:"},"2480":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#dynamic-schema-loading","content":"You can load or reload schemas programmatically:"},"2481":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#dynamic-schema-loading","content":"The encoder follows the same pattern:"},"2482":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#creating-converters-with-schema","content":"The loaded schema is used for JSON-to-protobuf conversion. This example maps user_id and full_name to the schema's field names:"},"2483":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-59","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Automatic Loading: Constructors attempt to load schema_path or descriptor_set. schema_path takes precedence when both are set. Constructors log loading errors and return an instance without a loaded schema."},"2484":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-60","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Manual Loading: load_schema() reloads the configured source. A returned error preserves an already-loaded schema. update_config(config, true) also restores the previous configuration on error. false changes the configuration while retaining the cached schema."},"2485":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-61","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Fallbacks: Missing files, compilation failures, absent schema sources, or an unmatched message_type can return Ok(()) without an active message descriptor. Successful reloads into fallback mode clear the previous descriptor. Invalid protobuf syntax and malformed descriptor bytes return errors. Check the actual encoded/decoded result when validating a schema setup."},"2486":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-62","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Encoding Errors: Errors encoding a loaded message descriptor are returned to the caller. The encoder does not retry that message as Any. The decoder attempts Any after a schema decoding error."},"2487":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-63","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Transform Configuration: Create a new converter to change its configuration. load_schema() can reload its existing source. Without a descriptor, JSON-to-protobuf conversion produces JSON text in Payload::Proto, not a schema-encoded binary message."},"2488":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-64","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Format Options: Encoder preserve_unknown_fields, compact_encoding, validate_message, and deterministic_encoding are accepted but have no effect. Decoder preserve_unknown_fields retains unknown varints as numbers and length-delimited data as base64. Fixed-width unknown fields become placeholders. It does not retain the original wire encoding. See the Transforms page for conversion-specific limits."},"2489":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sdk-65","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sdk#usage-notes","content":"Protocol Buffers provide efficient binary serialization compared to JSON"},"2490":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"page","content":"Transforms","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Connectors"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms"},"2491":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-0","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms","content":"Mutate, filter and convert messages as they pass through the connector runtime, and how each transform is configured."},"2492":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-1","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"2493":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-2","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"add_fields"},"2494":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-3","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#delete_fields","content":"delete_fields"},"2495":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-4","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#update_fields","content":"update_fields"},"2496":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-5","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"filter_fields"},"2497":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-6","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#unwrap_envelope","content":"unwrap_envelope"},"2498":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"proto_convert"},"2499":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"flat_buffer_convert"},"2500":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"avro_convert"},"2501":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Adding a new transform"},"2502":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms","content":"Transforms mutate, filter, or convert messages as they flow through the connector runtime. They run inside the runtime process, not inside the plugins. For source connectors they're applied after the plugin's messages are decoded and before producing to Iggy. For sink connectors, after consuming from Iggy and before handing the messages to the plugin."},"2503":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms","content":"A transform receives each decoded message and returns either the (possibly modified) message, or nothing - in which case the message is dropped from the batch. A transform can also return an error. A source rejects the whole batch and its checkpoint on a transform error. A sink drops the affected message and continues processing the batch."},"2504":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"Transforms are configured per connector in the transforms section of the connector configuration file. Each transform type is a table keyed by its name, so a connector can have at most one instance of each type:"},"2505":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"Two rules apply to every transform:"},"2506":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"The enabled key is required. A transform section without it fails to load, and a transform with enabled = false is skipped."},"2507":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"The order of the sections in the file doesn't determine the execution order, so don't rely on one transform seeing the output of another."},"2508":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#configuration","content":"The field-level transforms (add_fields, delete_fields, filter_fields, update_fields, unwrap_envelope) operate on JSON objects only. Other JSON values and other payload formats pass through them unchanged."},"2509":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Adds top-level fields to the message payload, replacing any existing value with the same key. Each field has a key and a value, which is either static or computed at runtime:"},"2510":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"The available computed values are:"},"2511":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Value"},"2512":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Result"},"2513":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"date_time"},"2514":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current date and time as an RFC 3339 string"},"2515":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_nanos"},"2516":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in nanoseconds"},"2517":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-26","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_micros"},"2518":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-27","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in microseconds"},"2519":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-28","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_millis"},"2520":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-29","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in milliseconds"},"2521":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-30","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"timestamp_seconds"},"2522":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-31","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Current Unix timestamp in seconds"},"2523":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-32","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"uuid_v4"},"2524":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-33","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Random UUID (version 4)"},"2525":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-34","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"uuid_v7"},"2526":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-35","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#add_fields","content":"Time-ordered UUID (version 7)"},"2527":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-36","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#delete_fields","content":"Removes the listed top-level fields from the message payload:"},"2528":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-37","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#update_fields","content":"Sets fields to static or computed values, optionally guarded by a condition:"},"2529":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-38","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#update_fields","content":"The condition values are always (the default when omitted - sets the field whether or not it exists), key_exists (update only when the field is already present), and key_not_exists (add only when the field is absent)."},"2530":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-39","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"Keeps or drops fields of the payload based on key and value patterns:"},"2531":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-40","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"keep_fields: fields that are always kept, regardless of the patterns."},"2532":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-41","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"patterns: a list of patterns. A field matches when any pattern matches. Within one pattern, key_pattern and value_pattern must both match when both are set (an omitted one matches everything)."},"2533":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-42","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"include_matching: true (default) keeps the matching fields and drops the rest. false drops the matching fields and keeps the rest."},"2534":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-43","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"An empty keep_fields list together with an empty patterns list leaves the payload unchanged. Invalid regular expressions fail configuration loading. Numeric between comparisons include both endpoints."},"2535":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-44","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"key_pattern variants: exact, starts_with, ends_with, contains, regex - each takes a string, e.g. key_pattern = { contains = \"name\" }."},"2536":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-45","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#filter_fields","content":"value_pattern variants: equals (any JSON value), contains (substring), regex, greater_than, less_than, between (e.g. { between = [1.0, 10.0] }), and the parameterless checks is_null, is_not_null, is_string, is_number, is_boolean, is_object, is_array (written as plain strings, e.g. value_pattern = \"is_number\")."},"2537":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-46","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#unwrap_envelope","content":"Promotes a nested field of a JSON envelope to the top-level payload. Given { \"table\": \"...\", \"operation\": \"...\", \"data\": { ... } }, setting field = \"data\" replaces the whole payload with the contents of data:"},"2538":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-47","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#unwrap_envelope","content":"field must not be empty. Messages that aren't JSON objects, or that don't contain the field, pass through unchanged."},"2539":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-48","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"Converts messages between Protocol Buffers and other formats. This example exposes the type_url and base64 value of an Any envelope received as raw bytes. See the SDK documentation for a complete runtime configuration and the schema loading details:"},"2540":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-49","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"Optional keys: schema_path, message_type, field_mappings (rename JSON input fields, e.g. field_mappings = { \"old\" = \"new\" }), descriptor_set (pre-compiled descriptor bytes instead of a .proto file), and the inactive schema_registry_url."},"2541":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-50","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"A loaded descriptor is used for JSON-to-protobuf encoding. Without one, that direction produces JSON text in Payload::Proto. Protobuf-to-JSON conversion does not decode a custom message descriptor: it parses text as JSON, exposes an Any envelope, or returns metadata containing base64 raw data. Protobuf-to-Avro/FlatBuffers conversion only rewraps the bytes."},"2542":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-51","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#proto_convert","content":"preserve_unknown_fields, validate_messages, type_url_prefix, and strict_mode are accepted but have no effect. pretty_json affects JSON text output, and include_metadata adds fields on the supported protobuf-to-JSON paths."},"2543":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-52","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"Converts JSON to the SDK's generic FlatBuffer representation, or exposes FlatBuffer bytes as metadata, base64 text, or raw bytes. It does not interpret an external .fbs schema. This example can be added to a JSON-producing source with streams.schema = \"flat_buffer\":"},"2544":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-53","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"Optional keys: schema_path, root_table_name, field_mappings. JSON encoding uses a generic key/value representation, so its output is not a buffer for an arbitrary generated table type. FlatBuffer-to-JSON returns buffer metadata and base64 bytes, not decoded table fields."},"2545":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-54","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"schema_path, include_paths, and preserve_unknown_fields have no effect. root_table_name only labels decoding metadata. verify_buffers checks the minimum buffer length (four bytes), not a schema-aware verifier. buffer_size_hint sets the initial encoder capacity. pretty_json, include_metadata, and strict_mode have no effect."},"2546":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-55","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#flat_buffer_convert","content":"The input payload variant must match source_format. The default flat_buffer stream decoder already returns JSON metadata, so it does not supply Payload::FlatBuffer to a following reverse transform."},"2547":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-56","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"Converts JSON to a raw Avro datum and Avro data to JSON. The schema can be given inline (schema_json) or as a file (schema_path). The inline schema takes precedence. This example can be added to the Random source with streams.schema = \"avro\":"},"2548":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-57","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"Optional keys: schema_path, schema_json, field_mappings. JSON encoding and Avro-to-JSON decoding require a schema. Input values must match its field types. The wire payload is one raw Avro datum, not an Avro object container file. Decoding rejects trailing bytes."},"2549":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-58","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"field_mappings renames input JSON fields or fields decoded from Avro. Text input is not field-mapped. Text-to-Avro parses JSON before encoding. Raw-to-Avro rewraps existing bytes without validating them. Avro-to-Text emits base64 and Avro-to-Raw preserves the bytes. All three conversion_options keys are accepted but have no effect. Unsupported conversion pairs pass their input through unchanged."},"2550":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-59","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"To consume raw Avro records in a sink, configure schema = \"avro\" and avro_schema_json or avro_schema_path in its [[streams]] entry. The runtime decodes the datum into JSON before running transforms. A following avro_convert with source_format = \"avro\" would therefore receive the wrong payload variant."},"2551":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-60","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#avro_convert","content":"Unlike the field-level transforms, the three format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults: every key shown in their examples (except the ones listed as optional) must be present, or the connector configuration fails to load. The source_format and target_format keys accept any schema value: json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro. The FlatBuffers transform additionally restricts the pair: only JSON to/from FlatBuffers, FlatBuffers to Text or Raw, and identity conversions are accepted. Any other combination fails the configuration load."},"2552":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-61","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"The extension point is the SDK (core/connectors/sdk/src/transforms/):"},"2553":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-62","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Add a new module implementing the Transform trait."},"2554":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-63","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Add a variant to the TransformType enum."},"2555":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-64","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"Add a match arm to the from_config function deserializing your config."},"2556":{"id":"/docs/connectors/transforms-65","page_id":"/docs/connectors/transforms","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/transforms#adding-a-new-transform","content":"The runtime loads transforms generically by TransformType, so no runtime changes are needed."},"2557":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"page","content":"Kafka Gateway","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Gateways"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka"},"2558":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-0","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka","content":"Run Kafka producers, consumers and admin clients against Iggy through the Kafka gateway."},"2559":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-1","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#enable-the-bridge","content":"Enable the bridge"},"2560":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-2","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#create-produce-and-consume","content":"Create, produce and consume"},"2561":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-3","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#supported-operations","content":"Supported operations"},"2562":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-4","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#delivery-and-durability","content":"Delivery and durability"},"2563":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-5","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#authentication-and-authorization","content":"Authentication and authorization"},"2564":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-6","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Listener and bridge configuration"},"2565":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-7","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#run-several-gateways","content":"Run several gateways"},"2566":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-8","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka","content":"The Kafka gateway 0.2.0 is a separate service named iggy-gateway-kafka. It accepts Kafka wire requests and uses the Rust SDK to store and read data in Iggy. Kafka clients connect to the gateway port, 9093 by default. Native Iggy clients continue to use Iggy listeners."},"2567":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-9","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#enable-the-bridge","content":"The bridge is the gateway's connection to Iggy. It is disabled by default. Without it, data and topic APIs do not store data. Producer ID allocation and in-memory group coordination alone do not prove that storage is enabled."},"2568":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-10","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#enable-the-bridge","content":"Start the Iggy server from the release source with development credentials. Then run the gateway from the same repository root:"},"2569":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-11","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#enable-the-bridge","content":"Use these credentials only for local development. In a deployment, provision a separate bridge account with the required permissions. A transient connection failure retries at startup for up to 60 seconds. Bad credentials or missing required permissions stop startup. The gateway does not silently switch back to storage-free responses."},"2570":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-12","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#enable-the-bridge","content":"The Docker Compose example starts a local Iggy server and gateway. Review its image selection and development credentials before running it. The gateway image is apache/iggy-kafka-gateway."},"2571":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-13","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#create-produce-and-consume","content":"With Kafka command-line tools installed, use the gateway as the bootstrap server:"},"2572":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-14","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#create-produce-and-consume","content":"Enter a message in the producer, then read it from another terminal:"},"2573":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-15","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#create-produce-and-consume","content":"By default, Kafka topic orders maps to Iggy stream kafka, topic orders. IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_STREAM changes the default stream. IGGY_KAFKA_TOPIC_MAP_PATH selects a TOML mapping file:"},"2574":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-16","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#create-produce-and-consume","content":"Topic auto-creation is disabled by default. Enable IGGY_KAFKA_AUTO_CREATE_TOPICS_ENABLED only when Metadata requests are allowed to create missing topics. IGGY_KAFKA_AUTO_CREATE_TOPICS_PARTITIONS defaults to one and cannot exceed 1000."},"2575":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-17","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#supported-operations","content":"The gateway supports Produce, Fetch, ListOffsets, Metadata, topic creation and deletion, partition additions, and topic retention changes. Consumer group APIs support membership, rebalances, static membership, offset commits, group inspection and offset deletion. It also supports ApiVersions, InitProducerId and DescribeCluster."},"2576":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-18","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#supported-operations","content":"Topic configuration changes support retention.ms. The gateway is not a complete Kafka broker implementation. Unsupported API keys and most unsupported versions close the connection. ApiVersions has a discovery error response for unsupported versions. Use the protocol coverage for exact API version ranges."},"2577":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-19","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#supported-operations","content":"Kafka offsets identify the next record to read. The gateway stores externally managed group offsets in Iggy. These are separate from native Iggy consumer group membership. Deleting a Kafka topic deletes its backing Iggy topic, but retains the stream."},"2578":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-20","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#delivery-and-durability","content":"Without gateway idempotence, delivery is at-least-once. A retry after an uncertain write can append records again. Set IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS=true after every Iggy node, gateway and reader uses a compatible release. Idempotent batches then retain their producer ID, epoch and sequence through the gateway."},"2579":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-21","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#delivery-and-durability","content":"Deduplication is bounded. Each partition retains five batches per producer and at most 4096 producers. It expires producers after 24 hours without writes. A retry outside retained history can duplicate a batch. Message IDs do not provide this guarantee."},"2580":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-22","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#delivery-and-durability","content":"Transactions are unsupported. Transactional producer initialization and Produce requests are refused. Do not configure transactional.id."},"2581":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-23","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#delivery-and-durability","content":"The Iggy topic's durability policy controls storage guarantees. The development stack uses replicated, so an acknowledgement does not prove stable-storage synchronization. Use persisted when acknowledged messages must survive loss of volatile copies. A named Docker volume alone does not strengthen an acknowledgement."},"2582":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-24","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#authentication-and-authorization","content":"Listeners support PLAINTEXT, SSL, SASL_PLAINTEXT and SASL_SSL. SASL uses PLAIN against Iggy credentials. SSL listeners can map verified client certificates to Iggy users. Configure TLS for both the Kafka listener and the separate gateway-to-Iggy connection when both hops need encryption."},"2583":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-25","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#authentication-and-authorization","content":"Data operations run as the bridge's Iggy account. Authentication does not provide complete per-principal data authorization in this release. In particular, CreateTopics does not yet apply the principal management gate. Other topic management operations and offset deletion add principal permission checks when the listener identifies a user. Do not expose an unauthenticated listener as a tenant-isolation boundary."},"2584":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-26","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#authentication-and-authorization","content":"Configure metadata.partitions_max on Iggy to bound topic creation. Its default is zero, which means no cap. DescribeAcls presents Iggy permissions, but CreateAcls and DeleteAcls are not supported. The authentication reference describes the exact limits."},"2585":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-27","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Variable"},"2586":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-28","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Default"},"2587":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-29","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Purpose"},"2588":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-30","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_BIND_ADDR"},"2589":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-31","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"127.0.0.1:9093"},"2590":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-32","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Single-listener bind address"},"2591":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-33","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST"},"2592":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-34","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Bind IP"},"2593":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-35","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Address clients can reach, required for a wildcard bind"},"2594":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-36","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_ADVERTISED_PORT"},"2595":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-37","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Bind port"},"2596":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-38","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Client-facing port"},"2597":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-39","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_LISTENERS"},"2598":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-40","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Unset"},"2599":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-41","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Comma-separated PROTOCOL://ip:port listeners"},"2600":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-42","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS"},"2601":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-43","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Listener addresses"},"2602":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-44","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Client-facing endpoint for each protocol"},"2603":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-45","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_MAX_CONNECTIONS"},"2604":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-46","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"1024"},"2605":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-47","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Limit per listener"},"2606":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-48","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_MAX_FRAME_SIZE"},"2607":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-49","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"8388608"},"2608":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-50","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Request frame cap in bytes"},"2609":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-51","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_BRIDGE_ENABLED"},"2610":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-52","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"false"},"2611":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-53","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Enable Iggy storage"},"2612":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-54","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_ADDR"},"2613":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-55","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"127.0.0.1:8090"},"2614":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-56","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Initial Iggy endpoint"},"2615":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-5