ion","content":"4"},"2626":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-68","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Concurrent Iggy fetch clients"},"2627":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-69","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_PRODUCE_SESSIONS"},"2628":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-70","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"256"},"2629":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-71","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Dedicated producer clients. Zero shares one client"},"2630":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-72","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS"},"2631":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-73","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"false"},"2632":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-74","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Pass producer batch identity to Iggy"},"2633":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-75","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"Do not mix LISTENERS with the single-listener advertised host/port or SASL toggle. Use matching ADVERTISED_LISTENERS. TLS needs IGGY_KAFKA_TLS_CERT_FILE and IGGY_KAFKA_TLS_KEY_FILE. The bridge hop uses IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_TLS_ENABLED, IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_TLS_DOMAIN and IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_TLS_CA_FILE."},"2634":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-76","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#listener-and-bridge-configuration","content":"The gateway configuration reference also lists timeouts, TLS reload, client-certificate mapping, authentication capacity and producer session expiry."},"2635":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-77","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#run-several-gateways","content":"Give every gateway the same IGGY_KAFKA_BROKERS list and a unique IGGY_KAFKA_NODE_ID. Put each gateway beside an Iggy node and point its bridge at that node. Clients list multiple gateways in bootstrap.servers. Each gateway can route requests to any Iggy partition primary."},"2636":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-78","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#run-several-gateways","content":"With the bridge and broker list configured, the shared coordinator log is enabled by default. It stores group generations, offset commits and deletions in Iggy. The bridge account needs manage_servers for metadata changes. Explicitly enabling the log with insufficient permissions stops startup. An implicitly enabled log can fall back with a warning, so inspect startup logs."},"2637":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-79","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#run-several-gateways","content":"The coordinator log uses 16 partitions. The owner of partition zero is the controller for topic management. Other gateways return NOT_CONTROLLER, and the Kafka admin client follows it. After a gateway fails, another gateway takes over its assigned groups once the Iggy session expires and ownership moves."},"2638":{"id":"/docs/gateways/kafka-80","page_id":"/docs/gateways/kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/gateways/kafka#run-several-gateways","content":"Use consistent coordinator-log configuration across gateways. With it disabled, the gateway with the lowest node ID coordinates all groups, and groups wait while it is down. See the multi-gateway deployment reference for permissions and takeover timing."},"2639":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"page","content":"About","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about"},"2640":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"What Apache Iggy is: a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, with QUIC, TCP, WebSocket and HTTP transports."},"2641":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Features"},"2642":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Supported languages SDK"},"2643":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#cli","content":"CLI"},"2644":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"Web UI"},"2645":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#connectors","content":"Connectors"},"2646":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#model-context-protocol","content":"Model Context Protocol"},"2647":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Docker"},"2648":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#helm-charts","content":"Helm Charts"},"2649":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/about#versioning","content":"Versioning"},"2650":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, supporting QUIC, TCP, WebSocket (custom binary specification) and HTTP (regular REST API) transport protocols, capable of processing millions of messages per second at ultra-low latency."},"2651":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy provides exceptionally high throughput and performance while utilizing minimal computing resources."},"2652":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"This is not yet another extension running on top of existing infrastructure, such as Kafka or SQL database."},"2653":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming log built from the ground up using low-level I/O with thread-per-core shared nothing architecture, io_uring and compio for maximum speed and efficiency."},"2654":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"The name is an abbreviation for the Italian Greyhound - small yet extremely fast dogs, the best in their class. See the lovely Fabio & Cookie ❤️"},"2655":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about","content":"Historical startup screenshot from server 0.5.0. See configuration for the 0.10.0 settings."},"2656":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Highly performant, persistent append-only log for message streaming"},"2657":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Very high throughput for both writes and reads"},"2658":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Low latency and predictable resource usage thanks to the Rust compiled language (no GC) and io_uring"},"2659":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Thread per core shared nothing design together with io_uring guarantee the best possible performance on modern Linux systems"},"2660":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"NUMA-aware sharding with CPU affinity and memory binding per NUMA node for optimal hardware utilization"},"2661":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"User authentication and authorization with granular permissions, Personal Access Tokens (PAT) and Argon2id password hashing"},"2662":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for multiple streams, topics and partitions"},"2663":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Support for multiple transport protocols (QUIC, TCP, WebSocket, HTTP)"},"2664":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Fully operational RESTful API which can be optionally enabled"},"2665":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Available client SDK in multiple languages"},"2666":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Works directly with binary data, avoiding enforced schema and serialization/deserialization overhead"},"2667":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Custom zero-copy (de)serialization, which greatly improves the performance and reduces memory usage"},"2668":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-28","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Custom memory pool with 28 buckets (buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB) for on-demand allocation, buffer reuse and sharing without copying buffer contents"},"2669":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-29","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Configurable server features (e.g. caching and transport protocols), plus per-topic segment size, durability and flush thresholds"},"2670":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-30","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Server-side storage of consumer offsets"},"2671":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-31","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Multiple ways of polling the messages:"},"2672":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-32","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"By offset (using the indexes)"},"2673":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-33","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"By timestamp (using the time indexes)"},"2674":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-34","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"First/Last N messages"},"2675":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-35","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Next N messages for the specific consumer"},"2676":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-36","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Optional poll auto-commit, with processing and failure semantics explained in concepts"},"2677":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-37","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Consumer groups distributing partitions across connected clients for horizontal scaling. Ordering is per partition, with cooperative partition rebalancing"},"2678":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-38","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Message expiry with auto deletion based on the configurable retention policy"},"2679":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-39","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Multi-tenant support via abstraction of streams which group topics"},"2680":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-40","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"TLS support for all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTPS)"},"2681":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-41","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Connectors - sinks, sources and data transformations based on the custom Rust plugins loaded dynamically at runtime"},"2682":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-42","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Model Context Protocol - provide context to LLM with MCP server exposing 40+ tools for streaming management"},"2683":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-43","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Optional data encryption: server-side at rest with AES-256-GCM-SIV, and client-side with AES-256-GCM"},"2684":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-44","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Optional metadata support in the form of message headers"},"2685":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-45","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Prometheus metrics and OpenTelemetry logs & traces in the connector runtime. Server export has known limitations."},"2686":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-46","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built-in CLI to manage the streaming server installable via cargo install iggy-cli --version 0.15.0 --locked"},"2687":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-47","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built-in Web UI dashboard (Svelte) that can be embedded directly in the server binary or run as a standalone container"},"2688":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-48","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built-in benchmarking app to test the performance, with published results on the benchmarks dashboard"},"2689":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-49","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Single binary deployment without an external broker or database. Operating-system libraries are still required by dynamically linked builds"},"2690":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-50","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Accept-time connection distribution for plaintext TCP and WebSocket across shards via file descriptor transfer"},"2691":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-51","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#features","content":"Built on Viewstamped Replication (VSR) consensus: runs as a single node by default, with multi-node clustering available via the [cluster] configuration"},"2692":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-52","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Language"},"2693":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-53","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Package"},"2694":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-54","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Registry"},"2695":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-55","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Rust"},"2696":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-56","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"iggy"},"2697":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-57","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"crates.io"},"2698":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-58","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/