:"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"npm"},"2710":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-70","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Go"},"2711":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-71","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"iggy-go"},"2712":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-72","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"pkg.go.dev"},"2713":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-73","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"PHP"},"2714":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-74","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"apache/iggy-php"},"2715":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-75","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"GitHub (source build)"},"2716":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-76","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"C++"},"2717":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-77","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"iggy-cpp"},"2718":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-78","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"Source archive"},"2719":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-79","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#supported-languages-sdk","content":"| Swift | Iggy | Swift Package Manager |"},"2720":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-80","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#cli","content":"The interactive CLI is implemented under core/cli, to provide the best developer experience. This is a great addition to the Web UI, especially for developers who prefer using the console tools."},"2721":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-81","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#cli","content":"Iggy CLI can be installed with cargo install iggy-cli --version 0.15.0 --locked and then simply accessed by typing iggy in your terminal. It supports named connection contexts (profiles) for managing multiple server connections, shell completions for bash/zsh/fish/elvish/powershell, and session-based login through platform credential stores on Linux, macOS and Windows when the default login-session feature is enabled."},"2722":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-82","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"The Web UI provides a comprehensive dashboard for the Iggy server, built with SvelteKit and TypeScript. It can run in two modes:"},"2723":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-83","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"Embedded - compiled into the server binary (with the iggy-web feature and built static assets), served at the /ui endpoint when http.web_ui = true"},"2724":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-84","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"Standalone - as a separate container via docker pull apache/iggy-web-ui:0.5.0"},"2725":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-85","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#web-ui","content":"Features include stream/topic/partition management, a message browser with JSON/string/XML decoders, and user listing and creation with initial permissions. Editing or deleting existing users and changing their permissions are placeholders. The logs and terminal pages are also placeholders, and the server settings page has a disabled Save action rather than an operational configuration editor."},"2726":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-86","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#connectors","content":"Iggy provides a highly performant and modular runtime for statically typed, yet dynamically loaded connectors. You can ingest data from external sources and push the data to Iggy streams, or fetch data from Iggy streams and forward it to external systems. Create your own Rust plugins by simply implementing either the Source or Sink trait and build custom pipelines for the data processing."},"2727":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-87","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#connectors","content":"The source tree contains 17 sinks and 6 sources. Sinks include PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, Apache Iceberg, Delta Lake, Quickwit and S3. Sources include PostgreSQL, Elasticsearch, InfluxDB, HTTP, MQTT and random data generation. See the connectors documentation for the full catalog."},"2728":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-88","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#connectors","content":"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via docker pull apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0."},"2729":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-89","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#model-context-protocol","content":"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The Iggy MCP Server is an implementation of the MCP protocol for message streaming infrastructure. It exposes 40+ tools covering streams, topics, partitions, messages, consumer groups, users, and more. It supports both HTTP and stdio transports, making it compatible with tools like Claude Desktop and other MCP clients."},"2730":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-90","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#model-context-protocol","content":"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via docker pull apache/iggy-mcp:0.6.0."},"2731":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-91","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"The official Apache Iggy images can be found on Docker Hub. These docs target server 0.10.0. Use docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0, or docker pull apache/iggy:edge for a development image. SDK and tooling versions are independent of the server version."},"2732":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-92","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"You can also find the images for all the different tooling such as Connectors, MCP Server etc. here."},"2733":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-93","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Please note that the images tagged as latest are based on the official, stable releases, while the edge ones are updated directly from latest version of the master branch."},"2734":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-94","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"You can find the development Dockerfile and docker-compose.yml in the root of the repository. Published server images use core/server/Dockerfile. To build and start the server, run: docker compose up."},"2735":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-95","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"For the root Compose build, run the included CLI with docker exec -it iggy-server /iggy. In published images, its path is /usr/local/bin/iggy."},"2736":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-96","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Keep in mind that running the container on operating systems other than Linux, where the Docker is running in the VM, might result in the performance degradation."},"2737":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-97","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Also, when running the container, make sure to include the additional capabilities, as you can find in docker-compose file:"},"2738":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-98","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#docker","content":"Or when running with docker run:"},"2739":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-99","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#helm-charts","content":"Helm charts for Kubernetes deployment are available in the repository. The chart includes templates for Deployment, Service, ServiceAccount, Ingress, PersistentVolumeClaim, ServiceMonitor (for Prometheus), and root user credentials Secret."},"2740":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-100","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#versioning","content":"The official releases follow the regular semver (0.5.0) or have a latest tag applied (apache/iggy:latest)."},"2741":{"id":"/docs/introduction/about-101","page_id":"/docs/introduction/about","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/about#versioning","content":"We also publish edge/dev/nightly releases (e.g. 0.6.0-edge.1 or apache/iggy:edge), for both SDKs and the Docker images. These are typically compatible with the latest changes but are not guaranteed to be stable and, as the name suggests, are not recommended for production use."},"2742":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"page","content":"Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture"},"2743":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture","content":"Inside the Iggy server: how work is scheduled across cores, how requests are routed, and how a message reaches disk."},"2744":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#how-a-message-flows-through-iggy","content":"How a message flows through Iggy"},"2745":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#thread-per-core-shared-nothing--io_uring","content":"Thread per core (shared nothing) + io_uring"},"2746":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#how-it-works","content":"How it works"},"2747":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#shard-roles-and-connection-distribution","content":"Shard roles and connection distribution"},"2748":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#request-routing","content":"Request routing"},"2749":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#iggynamespace","content":"IggyNamespace"},"2750":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"CPU allocation modes"},"2751":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"io_uring and compio"},"2752":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#performance-tokio-vs-thread-per-core","content":"Performance: Tokio vs Thread-per-Core"},"2753":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#left-right-metadata","content":"Left-right metadata"},"2754":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#clustering","content":"Clustering"},"2755":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#storage-structure","content":"Storage structure"},"2756":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#memory-pool","content":"Memory pool"},"2757":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Write pipeline"},"2758":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture","content":"This page covers the internals of the Iggy server: how work is scheduled across CPU cores, how requests are routed, and how data ends up on disk. If you're looking for the domain model (streams, topics, partitions, consumer groups, message format), see concepts first."},"2759":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#how-a-message-flows-through-iggy","content":"Before diving into the architecture details, here's an overview of a message's journey from client to disk:"},"2760":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#thread-per-core-shared-nothing--io_uring","content":"Iggy uses a thread-per-core shared nothing architecture combined with io_uring for maximum performance. This design has been proven by systems like ScyllaDB and Redpanda, both built on the Seastar framework."},"2761":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#how-it-works","content":"Each configured shard (an instance of IggyShard) has its own single-threaded compio async runtime. With pin_cores = true, Linux shard threads are pinned to their selected CPUs via sched_setaffinity. NUMA allocation modes also bind memory via hwlocality. Setting pin_cores = false disables both bindings. Shared metadata and inter-shard communication still require synchronization."},"2762":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#shard-roles-and-connection-distribution","content":"Shard 0 has a special role: it binds every listener - the replica plane and all client transports (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTP). Connections are then spread across shards at accept time:"},"2763":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#shard-roles-and-connection-distribution","content":"TCP and WebSocket connections, plaintext or TLS, are handed off round-robin across the shards. Shard 0's coordinator duplicates the socket's file descriptor, ships a connection-setup frame to the target shard, and drops its own handle, so the owning shard serves the connection from then on - before a single byte is read. For TLS-wrapped TCP and secure WebSocket (WSS), the target shard runs the TLS handshake. Shard 0 is part of the rotation by default. skip_shard_zero_for_clients = true under [cluster.coordinator] takes it out."},"2764":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#shard-roles-and-connection-distribution","content":"QUIC connections stay on shard 0, installed through its UDP endpoint. HTTP is also served on shard 0."},"2765":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#shard-roles-and-connection-distribution","content":"All shards, including shard 0, own partitions and serve partition requests."},"2766":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#request-routing","content":"Requests are routed between shards using message passing (via crossfire bounded mpsc channels), so partition state remains on its owning shard. The routing logic splits operations into two planes:"},"2767":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#request-routing","content":"Metadata operations (create/delete stream/topic/user etc.) always execute on shard 0 - it is the only shard that commits metadata"},"2768":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#request-routing","content":"Partition operations (send_messages, poll_messages, store_consumer_offset) are routed to the shard owning that partition via a lock-free concurrent map lookup. The request is sent into the owning shard's channel, whichever shard received it"},"2769":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#request-routing","content":"Partition-to-shard assignment is deterministic: the packed IggyNamespace is hashed with Murmur3, and the upper 16 bits of the hash are taken modulo the shard count (the upper bits are used because Murmur3 has weak lower bits for small integer inputs)."},"2770":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#request-routing","content":"Across nodes, the SDK uses a partition route before this shard-local dispatch. Partition primaries are distributed across eligible nodes and rebalanced by the metadata primary. Each topic selects its own replication factor at creation. See Multi-leader partitions."},"2771":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-28","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#iggynamespace","content":"The IggyNamespace packs stream_id (20 bits), topic_id (12 bits), and partition_id (20 bits) into a single u64 for efficient hashing and routing. This gives maximums of 1,048,576 streams, 4096 topics per stream, and 1,000,000 partitions per topic."},"2772":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-29","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"The sharding system supports multiple allocation modes via the cpu_allocation config:"},"2773":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-30","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"\"all\" - one shard per available CPU core"},"2774":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-31","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"A numeric value (e.g. 4) - exactly N shards, pinned to the first N CPUs in the process's allowed CPU set when pinning is enabled"},"2775":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-32","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"A range (e.g. \"5..8\") - shards on CPUs 5, 6 and 7 when pinning is enabled. Those CPUs must be allowed for the process"},"2776":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-33","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"\"numa:auto\" - automatically detect NUMA topology and select physical cores, avoiding sibling hyperthreads"},"2777":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-34","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#cpu-allocation-modes","content":"\"numa:nodes=0,1;cores=4;no_ht=true\" - explicit NUMA nodes, with the same number of cores taken from each listed node and hyperthread avoidance"},"2778":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-35","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"Traditional async runtimes like tokio use epoll which is readiness-based - you ask the kernel \"is this file descriptor ready?\" and then perform the I/O yourself. Regular files cannot be registered with epoll. Tokio runs file I/O on a blocking thread pool that grows on demand (up to 512 threads by default, configurable). This does not scale well."},"2779":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-36","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"io_uring is completion-based - you submit I/O requests to a submission queue (SQ), and the kernel completes them and places results in a completion queue (CQ). Both queues are shared ring buffers between user space and kernel. Submissions and completions can be batched to reduce syscalls. This is fundamentally better for disk I/O."},"2780":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-37","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"Iggy uses compio as its async runtime, which provides a driver-disaggregated architecture on top of io_uring (Linux) and IOCP (Windows). Each shard gets its own compio executor configured with:"},"2781":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-38","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"Capacity: an io_uring submission queue of 4096 entries (by default)"},"2782":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-39","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"Event interval: poll the I/O driver after 128 scheduler ticks (roughly task polls) by default"},"2783":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-40","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"Cooperative task running enabled"},"2784":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-41","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#io_uring-and-compio","content":"IGGY_SHARD_RUNTIME_CAPACITY and IGGY_SHARD_EVENT_INTERVAL override the two numeric defaults. On macOS, compio uses its polling driver and a blocking pool for file I/O."},"2785":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-42","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#performance-tokio-vs-thread-per-core","content":"The historical migration benchmarks compared v0.5.0 with v0.7.0 at approximately 1,000 MB/s per node. The chart shows selected latency reductions for 8, 16 and 32 producers, each using its own stream. The 8-producer case also reported higher P95 and P99 latency. These are not measurements of 0.10.0:"},"2786":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-43","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#left-right-metadata","content":"Shared metadata (streams, topics, consumer groups) uses a left-right concurrent data structure. Shard 0 holds the single write handle and is the only shard that commits metadata mutations, while every shard holds a read handle. This provides:"},"2787":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-44","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#left-right-metadata","content":"Lock-free reads on all shards (no contention on the hot path)"},"2788":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-45","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#left-right-metadata","content":"Strongly consistent writes serialized through shard 0"},"2789":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-46","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#clustering","content":"The server is built on the Viewstamped Replication (VSR) consensus protocol. Metadata operations are committed through the VSR write-ahead log even on a single node, which is the default deployment. Multi-node clustering is enabled via the [cluster] configuration (a node roster plus --replica-id to pick the local entry). See clustering for the details and maturity caveats."},"2790":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-47","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#storage-structure","content":"Having in mind that stream consists of topics, which might have one or more partitions assigned, and each partition consists of segments, we can visualize the structure of the Iggy data directory as follows:"},"2791":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-48","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#storage-structure","content":"This example shows a fresh partition with the default paths. The stream, topic and partition directories are named after their numeric IDs, assigned from 0. The metadata/journal.wal file is the VSR write-ahead log for metadata operations (stream/topic/user creation, etc.). Its index has 1024 slots by default (journal_slots under [metadata]). Before they run out, the server checkpoints the metadata into metadata/snapshot.bin, records the checkpoint in a superblock under metadata/, and drops the checkpointed operations from the log. Root initialization is recorded through metadata replication, and startup checks that journal or checkpoint state proves initialization. The runtime/current_config.toml file is rewritten at every boot with the configuration the server booted with and the addresses it actually bound. Secrets (the JWT secrets, the encryption key and the cluster shared secret) are left out. The state/log file is empty. The server creates it at boot and does not otherwise use it. Segment files are named by their 20-digit start offset. Recovery can create an empty active segment at a reserved offset beyond the last stored message. Partition superblocks record the recovery frontiers. A superblock is written to two files in turn, superblock.a and superblock.b. A fresh partition has only superblock.a. superblock.b appears at the next superblock write, for example after a later persistence operation. The .index file is created automatically and speeds up searches by keeping track of the offsets and timestamps of the records. The offsets/ directory holds the server-side consumer and consumer group offsets."},"2792":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-49","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#memory-pool","content":"Iggy uses a custom memory pool with 28 buckets holding buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB. The default pool size is 4 GiB with up to 8192 buffers per bucket. Buffers are allocated on demand and reused. Requests that exceed the pool budget can allocate outside it. Frozen buffers can be shared between components without copying their contents. The pool is page-aligned (4096-byte multiples) and requires a minimum of 512 MiB."},"2793":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-50","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Messages flow through a multi-stage write pipeline:"},"2794":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-51","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Messages arrive on the owning shard and are buffered in the partition journal."},"2795":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-52","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Partition VSR replicates prepares. With durability=persisted, a multi-replica group requires recoverable prepare-WAL copies at the replication quorum before commit."},"2796":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-53","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Awaited writes apply committed operations before success is returned. A singleton with durability=persisted synchronizes local segment state before replying."},"2797":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-54","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Ordinary segment writes use per-topic count and byte thresholds (defaults: 1024 messages, 1 MiB). Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle work can flush earlier. The MessagesWriter uses vectored I/O in chunks of up to 1024 buffers. Partial writes can require more than one I/O submission."},"2798":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-55","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"When a segment reaches or passes the topic's segment size (default 1 GiB), it is sealed and a new segment is created. The batch that crosses the size is written whole, so a sealed segment can exceed it by up to one batch."},"2799":{"id":"/docs/introduction/architecture-56","page_id":"/docs/introduction/architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/architecture#write-pipeline","content":"Message durability and consumer_offset_durability default independently to replicated. Both policies write data to disk. persisted adds a stable-storage requirement at completion. See Durability."},"2800":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","type":"page","content":"Coming from Apache Kafka","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka"},"2801":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","content":"How Apache Kafka's topics, partitions, consumer groups and offsets map to Apache Iggy, and what works differently."},"2802":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"How the terms map"},"2803":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#what-works-differently","content":"What works differently"},"2804":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","content":"If you already know Apache Kafka, most of Apache Iggy will feel familiar. This page maps the Kafka terms you know to Iggy's and lists what works differently. For when to choose one or the other, see Apache Iggy or Apache Kafka."},"2805":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Kafka"},"2806":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Iggy"},"2807":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"What to know"},"2808":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"No equivalent"},"2809":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Stream"},"2810":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"A container for related topics. For example, a dev stream and a production stream can each have their own orders topic."},"2811":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Topic"},"2812":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Topic"},"2813":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Belongs to one stream."},"2814":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Partition"},"2815":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Partition"},"2816":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Where messages are stored, as in Kafka."},"2817":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Consumer group"},"2818":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Consumer group"},"2819":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Each partition goes to one member of the group, and the server rebalances when members join or leave."},"2820":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Offset"},"2821":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Offset"},"2822":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Starts at 0 in each partition, but can skip numbers after a recovery."},"2823":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Retention"},"2824":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Retention policy"},"2825":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Set per topic. Whole segments are deleted once all their messages have expired."},"2826":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#how-the-terms-map","content":"Concepts explains each of these in more detail."},"2827":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#what-works-differently","content":"Iggy has its own protocol, and the Kafka gateway serves Kafka producers, consumers and supported admin APIs. Run it as a separate service with the Iggy bridge enabled. The gateway has a defined API subset and does not support transactions. Native applications can use an Iggy SDK or the HTTP API."},"2828":{"id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/coming-from-kafka#what-works-differently","content":"There is an extra level above topics. Every topic lives in a stream, so you create a stream before you create its topics."},"2829":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"page","content":"Concepts","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts"},"2830":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts","content":"The domain model behind Iggy, and how an append-only streaming log differs from a message broker."},"2831":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-streaming","content":"Message streaming"},"2832":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#append-only-log","content":"Append-only log"},"2833":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#stream","content":"Stream"},"2834":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#topic","content":"Topic"},"2835":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#partition","content":"Partition"},"2836":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#segment","content":"Segment"},"2837":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#polling-messages","content":"Polling messages"},"2838":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"2839":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Message format"},"2840":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#primaries-copies-and-checkpoints","content":"Primaries, copies and checkpoints"},"2841":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts","content":"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform: messages are stored in a form of an append-only log. You can create multiple streams, consisting of topics, which might have one or more partitions assigned, e.g. to divide consumption among independent consumers. Multi-node replication provides redundancy. You can think of Iggy as an alternative to Kafka or RabbitMQ streams."},"2842":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-streaming","content":"You've probably used RabbitMQ or Kafka already. They look similar at the first glance, and you can achieve the similar results with both (e.g. publishing and consuming the events by the different applications built on top of microservices architecture), but they work differently underneath."},"2843":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-streaming","content":"RabbitMQ is a message broker with both queues and streams. Traditional queues deliver messages to consumers and remove them after acknowledgement, or on delivery when automatic acknowledgement is used, so acknowledged messages cannot be replayed from the queue. Multiple consumers can share a queue and divide its messages. Independent subscribers that each need a copy use separate queues bound to an exchange. Queues normally use FIFO (First In, First Out) order, but priorities, redelivery and competing consumers can affect the observed order. See RabbitMQ's queue semantics. The typical message broker follows the so-called smart pipes and dumb endpoints pattern."},"2844":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-streaming","content":"On the other hand, Kafka is a message streaming platform that stores messages in an append-only log and serves consumer fetch requests. The consumers are responsible for reading the messages from the log and processing them. Multiple consumer groups can read the same retained log without a separate stored copy for each group, although their fetch requests still consume CPU and network resources. The consumers can read the messages from the beginning, or from the specific offset, thus you can replay the messages. The typical message streaming platform follows the so-called dumb pipes and smart endpoints pattern."},"2845":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-streaming","content":"Both approaches have advantages and disadvantages. The message broker is a more mature concept, but the message streaming platform is gaining more and more popularity, especially in the cloud-native world. And you can achieve much higher performance and throughput with the message streaming platform, since it acts as a simple database, being optimized for the append-only operations and can be queried in a very efficient way."},"2846":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-streaming","content":"Iggy is the latter, a message streaming platform."},"2847":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#append-only-log","content":"The append-only log is the core concept of Iggy. It's a simple data structure, which is optimized for the append-only operations. It's a sequence of records, that are being appended to the end of the log. The records are immutable, so that they can't be changed once they are written to the log. Records within a partition follow the order admitted by that partition's primary. There is no global ordering across partitions."},"2848":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#append-only-log","content":"You address the log by offset, the position of the record in the log. Offsets start from 0 and increase within a partition. They are not guaranteed to be contiguous: after recovery, the server can skip reserved offsets to avoid reusing them. When the client reads the records, it specifies the offset to start from and the maximum number of records it wants. Starting from the beginning, or from any earlier offset, is how you replay the messages."},"2849":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#stream","content":"While we could put an equal sign between the log and the stream, they are not the same, at least in a case of Iggy streaming server. The stream is a logical concept, and you might think of it as a namespace. For example, you could have a single stream for the whole system, or multiple streams e.g. representing the different environments, such as dev, staging and production. The stream is identified by its unique ID. The stream can have zero or more topics assigned, which results in the records being published to the specific topics that belong to the particular stream."},"2850":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#topic","content":"The topic is also the logical concept, which is a part of the stream. The topic is identified by its ID, which is unique within its stream. You could think of topic as an entity being responsible for storing the specific type of the records. For example, you could have a topic for the user events, and another topic for the order events, etc."},"2851":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#topic","content":"The messages are not being stored in the topic directly, but rather in the partitions, which are assigned to the topic. The topic can have one or more partitions assigned, that could help achieve higher parallelism and throughput. The topic can also have a retention policy. Expiry removes whole sealed segments after their newest message has expired, subject to the stored consumer-offset barrier. It does not delete each message immediately at its expiry time. See topic options for expiry and size-limit behavior. Topics also support maximum size limits and per-topic storage options (segment_size, durability, consumer_offset_durability, and flush thresholds) set at creation, plus a compression_algorithm option (a placeholder today: no compression is applied yet). Both durability policies independently default to replicated."},"2852":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#partition","content":"The partition has its own unique ID and belongs to the topic. The partition is responsible for storing the records. The records are being distributed between the partitions, therefore the partition acts as a simple database, which is optimized for the append-only operations. The partition is identified by its unique ID, which is an integer. Stream, topic and partition IDs are assigned starting from 0. Deleted stream and topic IDs can be reused. New partitions use IDs above the highest remaining partition ID, so deleting the highest partitions can also allow their IDs to be reused. The partition ID is unique per topic, thus the same partition ID can be used in multiple topics."},"2853":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#partition","content":"Thanks to having multiple partitions, we can achieve the horizontal scalability between many independent consumers, since each consumer can read the messages from the different partitions. This can be achieved by using more advanced concepts such as consumer groups."},"2854":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#partition","content":"Each partition in the thread-per-core architecture is owned by exactly one shard per replica and includes:"},"2855":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#partition","content":"A SegmentedLog with sealed segments and one active segment"},"2856":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#partition","content":"Consumer offsets and consumer group offsets"},"2857":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#segment","content":"The segment, being a part of the partition, is the actual physical layer which stores the records in the binary format in a form of the files. Each segment has a soft size limit (1 GiB unless the topic sets its own segment_size). A segment can exceed it by one whole batch before it is sealed and a new segment is created. The segment name is based on the start offset of the first record in the segment and is unique per partition."},"2858":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-28","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#segment","content":"Each segment consists of:"},"2859":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-29","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#segment","content":".log file - the actual message data"},"2860":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-30","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#segment","content":".index file - positional and time indexes for fast lookups"},"2861":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-31","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#polling-messages","content":"Consumers can poll the messages in multiple ways:"},"2862":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-32","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#polling-messages","content":"By offset - start reading from the specified offset. The client tracks its own position."},"2863":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-33","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#polling-messages","content":"By timestamp - start reading from the first message at or after the given timestamp."},"2864":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-34","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#polling-messages","content":"First / Last - start from the beginning or the end of the partition."},"2865":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-35","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#polling-messages","content":"Next - continue after the consumer offset stored on the server. CommitPolicy::OnRead submits progress with the poll and can advance it before application processing. Use manual checkpoint commits after successful processing when that ordering is required. See Deferred polling."},"2866":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-36","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups provide horizontal scaling for message consumption. When multiple consumers join the same consumer group, the server automatically distributes partitions among group members so that each partition has at most one member permitted to poll it within that group. When members join or leave, the server triggers a cooperative partition rebalancing with a pending revocation phase (configurable timeout, default 30s) to let the previous owner finish processing and commit before handoff. Application processing and offset-commit ordering still determine whether failures cause skips or duplicates."},"2867":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-37","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"The Rust SDK's IggyMessageHeader::to_bytes() representation is 64 bytes, with little-endian fields. The diagram and table describe that SDK representation, not Rust struct memory layout. On the wire and on disk, messages travel inside batch records with a compact 48-byte per-message frame header. See the Binary Protocol section for the exact encodings."},"2868":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-38","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Field"},"2869":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-39","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Bytes"},"2870":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-40","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Type"},"2871":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-41","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Description"},"2872":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-42","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"checksum"},"2873":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-43","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"0-8"},"2874":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-44","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u64"},"2875":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-45","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"xxHash3 integrity checksum"},"2876":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-46","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"id"},"2877":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-47","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"8-24"},"2878":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-48","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u128"},"2879":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-49","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Client-supplied 128-bit ID. Generated as UUIDv4 when omitted by the Rust SDK"},"2880":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-50","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"offset"},"2881":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-51","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"24-32"},"2882":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-52","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u64"},"2883":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-53","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Increasing offset within the partition"},"2884":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-54","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"timestamp"},"2885":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-55","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"32-40"},"2886":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-56","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u64"},"2887":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-57","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Server-assigned timestamp"},"2888":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-58","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"origin_timestamp"},"2889":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-59","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"40-48"},"2890":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-60","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u64"},"2891":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-61","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Client-provided timestamp"},"2892":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-62","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"user_headers_length"},"2893":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-63","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"48-52"},"2894":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-64","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u32"},"2895":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-65","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Length of optional headers"},"2896":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-66","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"payload_length"},"2897":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-67","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"52-56"},"2898":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-68","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u32"},"2899":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-69","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Length of payload"},"2900":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-70","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"reserved"},"2901":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-71","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"56-64"},"2902":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-72","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"u64"},"2903":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-73","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"Reserved (must be 0)"},"2904":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-74","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"In the SDK message representation, the header is followed by payload bytes and then optional user-header bytes."},"2905":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-75","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#message-format","content":"To see how the server schedules these concepts across CPU cores and stores them on disk, head over to architecture."},"2906":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-76","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#primaries-copies-and-checkpoints","content":"A partition primary orders writes for that partition. Different partitions can have different primaries. replication_factor determines how many nodes store each partition. Metadata has its own primary and spans the full cluster."},"2907":{"id":"/docs/introduction/concepts-77","page_id":"/docs/introduction/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/concepts#primaries-copies-and-checkpoints","content":"A checkpoint records a processed position with its partition history and ownership generation. A continuation carries the next read position with the same context. These prevent an old read or commit from silently applying to a recreated partition. See Multi-leader partitions and Deferred polling."},"2908":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"page","content":"Getting started (Python)","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python"},"2909":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","content":"A first program with the Python SDK: create a stream and topic, send messages, then read them back on their own and as a consumer group."},"2910":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"2911":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"2912":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#producer","content":"Producer"},"2913":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"2914":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer-group","content":"Consumer group"},"2915":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#running-it-again","content":"Running it again"},"2916":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#before-we-start","content":"This tutorial uses the Python SDK's low-level API (IggyClient) directly, so you can see each step. It needs Python 3.11 or newer. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer. MacOS has no kernel requirement. See System requirements."},"2917":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#before-we-start","content":"The producer and consumer below match the getting-started examples in the repository."},"2918":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#starting-the-server","content":"IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS and IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS are needed because the server binds 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default, which the published port can't reach. The Rust tutorial's Starting the Iggy server section explains each flag."},"2919":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#starting-the-server","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"2920":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#starting-the-server","content":"Install the SDK in a virtual environment:"},"2921":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#producer","content":"Save this as producer.py:"},"2922":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#producer","content":"Run it with python producer.py:"},"2923":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#producer","content":"from_connection_string takes the transport, host and credentials in one string, iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port. See Connection strings for the other options. Creating a stream or topic that already exists is an error, so the producer checks first and only creates what's missing. That's also what makes it safe to run again."},"2924":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer","content":"Save this as consumer.py:"},"2925":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer","content":"Run it with python consumer.py, after the producer:"},"2926":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer","content":"Consumer.Single(name) is a consumer that polls on its own, and the server stores its offset under that name. With auto_commit=True the offset is stored after the poll returns, without waiting for the store to finish, so a crash straight afterwards can deliver the last batch again."},"2927":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer-group","content":"A consumer group lets several copies of a consumer share a topic's partitions, with each message going to only one of them. Groups need a binary transport (TCP, QUIC or WebSocket). HTTP doesn't support them."},"2928":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer-group","content":"Save this as consumer_group.py:"},"2929":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer-group","content":"Run it with python consumer_group.py. With the producer run twice, so 20 messages on the topic, it prints every offset from 0 to 19 and then stops:"},"2930":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#consumer-group","content":"iter_messages() is the simplest way to read from a group. For a long-running service with its own shutdown signal, use consume_messages(callback, shutdown_event) instead."},"2931":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#running-it-again","content":"Run the producer again and it finds the stream and topic already there, and adds another 10 messages at offsets 10 to 19."},"2932":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#running-it-again","content":"Run consumer.py again and it carries on from its stored offset, printing only the new messages rather than the first ten again."},"2933":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#running-it-again","content":"Run consumer_group.py again and it prints every message from offset 0, because it asks for PollingStrategy.First(). That keeps the example repeatable. A long-running group consumer would leave the polling strategy at its default and keep auto_commit on, so it carries on from the group's stored offset instead."},"2934":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-python#running-it-again","content":"To start again with an empty server, stop the container, which --rm removes, and run docker run again."},"2935":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"page","content":"Getting started (Rust)","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started"},"2936":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","content":"A first program with the low-level Rust SDK: create a stream and topic, send messages, then poll them back."},"2937":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"2938":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#what-we-will-build","content":"What we will build"},"2939":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"Starting the Iggy server"},"2940":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#setting-up-the-project","content":"Setting up the project"},"2941":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-producer","content":"Building the producer"},"2942":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-consumer","content":"Building the consumer"},"2943":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#run-the-applications","content":"Run the applications"},"2944":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"2945":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#before-we-start","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"2946":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#before-we-start","content":"This tutorial uses the low-level Rust SDK to show how things work under the hood - creating streams, topics, sending and polling messages step by step. This is great for understanding the fundamentals."},"2947":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#before-we-start","content":"For real-world applications, you might typically prefer the high-level SDK, which provides IggyProducer and IggyConsumer builders with auto-batching, consumer groups, offset management, reconnection, and retry logic out of the box. Check the Stream Builder docs for the most ergonomic API."},"2948":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#before-we-start","content":"For examples in other programming languages, see the SDK section."},"2949":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#what-we-will-build","content":"We will build a producer (publishing messages to a stream) and a consumer (reading those messages back). In a real system, a service can be both at the same time - publishing its own events while consuming events from other services."},"2950":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#what-we-will-build","content":"The completed sample can be found in the repository. We will go through the implementation step by step so you get a clear understanding of how it all fits together."},"2951":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"For our purpose, we will focus on the basic scenario in order to keep things simple. Before we begin implementing the consumer and producer apps, we need to start the Iggy streaming server."},"2952":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"This guide targets server 0.10.0. Use its official Docker image:"},"2953":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"The container needs a host kernel of 5.19 or newer, because the server uses io_uring. See System requirements if it fails to start. The capabilities, seccomp setting, and memlock limit form a permissive development setup. Production deployments can use narrower syscall permissions and a finite memory budget. See Docker & Helm for the details. IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS is needed because the server binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default, which a published port cannot reach. IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS is needed because that wildcard leaves the server with no address to give clients, and it refuses to start rather than publish one nobody can dial. Here the port is published to the host, so localhost is that address. Setting the root credentials explicitly means the username and password used later in this guide will work."},"2954":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"Alternatively, build from source by cloning the repository and running:"},"2955":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"A bare iggy-server boot does not create the root user with the well-known iggy/iggy pair. A fresh standalone server refuses to start without credentials. A cluster replica without credentials waits for root to be replicated. The --with-default-root-credentials flag switches to the development credentials (username: iggy, password: iggy), and --fresh wipes the data directory first, so the root user is created anew with them. You can also set IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD yourself. The environment takes precedence over the flag. The credentials initialize the stored root account only on first creation. Changing them on a later boot does not reset its password. Supplied environment credentials are still validated on restarts."},"2956":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"All the data used by the server will be persisted under the local_data directory, unless specified differently in the configuration."},"2957":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#starting-the-iggy-server","content":"As long as the Iggy server is running, we're good to go. If you'd like to play with the configuration (e.g. change the addresses, ports, caching or so) you will find config.toml under core/server in the repository."},"2958":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#setting-up-the-project","content":"Create a Rust application with two binaries:"},"2959":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#setting-up-the-project","content":"Until the release packages are published, use the SDK from the same release candidate as the server. Replace the iggy dependency in Cargo.toml with:"},"2960":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#setting-up-the-project","content":"Save the producer in src/bin/producer.rs and the consumer in src/bin/consumer.rs. The programs use resource names, so they do not depend on the IDs assigned to other streams."},"2961":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-25","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-producer","content":"The producer connects, creates missing resources and sends ten messages to partition zero. Resource creation returns errors for existing names, which this example handles separately from other failures."},"2962":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-26","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-producer","content":"StreamCreateOptions configures stream creation. TopicCreateOptions selects partitions, retention and storage policies. Omitted optional fields use server defaults. The default message durability is replicated. Select Durability::Persisted when successful sends must wait for stable storage. See Topic options."},"2963":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-27","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-producer","content":"A topic's first partition is zero. Partitioning::partition_id(0) selects it explicitly. balanced() distributes batches across partitions, and messages_key() selects a partition from a key. Ordering applies within a partition."},"2964":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-28","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-consumer","content":"The consumer starts after its stored offset. It waits up to five seconds for messages and processes the returned batch before storing its checkpoint. An empty reply ends this example."},"2965":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-29","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-consumer","content":"A continuation carries the next position and the partition history that produced it. Do not advance an offset by the number of returned messages because offsets can contain gaps. A checkpoint stores progress under that history. If the partition is recreated, the server refuses a stale checkpoint."},"2966":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-30","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#building-the-consumer","content":"A standalone consumer does not join a group. Two applications using its same identity can change the same stored offset. Use a consumer group to assign partitions among service instances. The high-level consumer manages those assignments and grouped fetches."},"2967":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-31","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#run-the-applications","content":"Run the producer first:"},"2968":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-32","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#run-the-applications","content":"Then run the consumer:"},"2969":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-33","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#run-the-applications","content":"The consumer prints the messages and stores its progress. Running it again waits and exits if no new messages exist. Running the producer again appends ten more messages. A processing failure before checkpoint storage can cause replay on the next run, so application side effects must tolerate retries."},"2970":{"id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started-34","page_id":"/docs/introduction/getting-started","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/getting-started#next-steps","content":"Use deferred polling to tune wait and batch limits. Use the high-level SDK for producers and group consumers. The examples cover headers, serialization, TLS and stream builders."},"2971":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","type":"page","content":"Glossary","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/glossary"},"2972":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Short definitions of the terms used across the Apache Iggy docs, with links to where each one is explained."},"2973":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"A one-line definition of each term, with a link to the page that explains it."},"2974":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Append-only log. An ordered list of messages that only grows at the end. Messages can't be changed once written, and you can read from any earlier point to replay them. See Concepts."},"2975":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Auto-commit. An option on a poll. The server saves the consumer's position as it returns the messages, before the consumer has processed them. See Polling messages."},"2976":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Connection string. A single value such as iggy://user:password@localhost:8090 that tells a client how to connect. All SDKs accept connection strings, with language-specific schemes and options. See Connection strings."},"2977":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Consumer. A client that reads messages on its own rather than as part of a consumer group. Its position is kept separately from every other consumer's."},"2978":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Consumer group. A set of consumers that share a topic's partitions, with each partition read by one member at a time. See Consumer groups."},"2979":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Durability. A topic setting, replicated or persisted, that decides what must be done before a write is reported as successful. See Durability."},"2980":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Offset. The position of a message within a partition, starting at 0. See Concepts."},"2981":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Partition. Where a topic's messages are actually stored. A topic's messages are spread across its partitions. See Partition."},"2982":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Partitioning. How a producer picks the partition for a message: a fixed partition, round robin, or a hash of a message key. See Getting started."},"2983":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Payload. The body of a message. The server stores it as bytes without interpreting it, so the format is up to you. See Serialising messages."},"2984":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Personal access token. A token you create on the server and use in place of a username and password, for example in a scraper or a script. See Connection strings."},"2985":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Primary and replica. In a cluster, the primary orders writes and the replicas copy them. If the primary fails, the replicas elect a new one. See Viewstamped Replication."},"2986":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Retention policy. Topic settings that delete old messages by age or by size. See Topic options."},"2987":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-15","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Segment. Part of a partition, stored on disk as a data file and an index file. Retention deletes old segments whole. See Segment."},"2988":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-16","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Shard. One thread in the server that owns a set of partitions. See Architecture."},"2989":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-17","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Stream. A container for related topics. For example, a dev stream and a production stream can each have their own orders topic. See Stream."},"2990":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-18","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Topic. A named set of partitions for one kind of message, such as orders or user events. See Topic."},"2991":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-19","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"User headers. Optional key and value pairs sent with a message, kept separate from the payload. See Serialising messages."},"2992":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-20","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"VSR. Viewstamped Replication, the protocol Apache Iggy uses to keep replicas in step. See Viewstamped Replication."},"2993":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-21","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Deferred polling. A read that waits on the server for messages or a deadline. A grouped fetch reads several partitions of one primary."},"2994":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-22","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Checkpoint. A position that can be committed after processing. It retains the partition incarnation and consumer ownership generation."},"2995":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-23","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Partition incarnation. The identity of one partition history. Deleting and recreating a partition changes it."},"2996":{"id":"/docs/introduction/glossary-24","page_id":"/docs/introduction/glossary","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/glossary","content":"Replication factor. The number of nodes that store each partition of a topic. Set it at topic creation."},"2997":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","type":"page","content":"Quickstart","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart"},"2998":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","content":"Run the Iggy server and send and receive your first message with the Rust SDK, in one file."},"2999":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#start-the-server","content":"Start the server"},"3000":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#send-and-receive-a-message","content":"Send and receive a message"},"3001":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"3002":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","content":"This page gets a server running and a message sent and received, in one file. To build separate producer and consumer applications step by step, see Getting started. For other languages, see the SDK section."},"3003":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"3004":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#start-the-server","content":"See Docker & Helm for what each option does."},"3005":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#send-and-receive-a-message","content":"Create a project and add the SDK and the async runtime:"},"3006":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#send-and-receive-a-message","content":"Replace src/main.rs with:"},"3007":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#send-and-receive-a-message","content":"Run it with cargo run. Partition IDs are 0-based, so the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"3008":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#next-steps","content":"Build a producer and a consumer as two applications: Getting started."},"3009":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#next-steps","content":"Use the producer and consumer builders with batching and offset management: High-level SDK."},"3010":{"id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/quickstart","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/quickstart#next-steps","content":"Use another language: SDK section."},"3011":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","type":"page","content":"Serialising messages","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Introduction"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages"},"3012":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-0","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","content":"Choose a format for your message payloads, and send more than one kind of message to the same topic."},"3013":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-1","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#one-kind-of-message","content":"One kind of message"},"3014":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-2","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-the-payload","content":"A type field in the payload"},"3015":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-3","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-a-header","content":"A type field in a header"},"3016":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-4","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#size-limits","content":"Size limits"},"3017":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-5","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","content":"The server stores each message payload as bytes without interpreting it, so you choose the format: plain text, JSON, Protobuf or anything else. This page shows how to send more than one kind of message to the same topic, so the consumer knows how to decode each one."},"3018":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-6","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","content":"The examples are in Rust and come from the examples in the Apache Iggy repository."},"3019":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-7","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#one-kind-of-message","content":"If every message on a topic has the same shape, send the encoded value as the payload and decode it the same way on the other side. Getting started does this with plain strings."},"3020":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-8","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-the-payload","content":"Wrap each message in an envelope that names its type, and send the envelope as JSON:"},"3021":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-9","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-the-payload","content":"The consumer decodes the envelope, reads message_type, then decodes the inner payload as that type. This works with any client, but every message is decoded twice, and the inner payload is a JSON string inside the envelope. See the message-envelope example."},"3022":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-10","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-a-header","content":"Messages can carry user headers, which are key and value pairs kept separate from the payload. Put the type in a header and send the payload as it is:"},"3023":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-11","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-a-header","content":"The consumer reads the header and decodes the payload once:"},"3024":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-12","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-a-header","content":"This is the better choice for Protobuf or any other binary format, because the payload doesn't have to fit inside JSON. See the message-type example."},"3025":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-13","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#a-type-field-in-a-header","content":"Header values don't have to be strings. They can also be numbers, booleans or raw bytes, so you can add things like a trace ID next to the type, as the typed-headers example shows. The message-compression example uses a header in the same way to record how the payload was compressed."},"3026":{"id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages-14","page_id":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/introduction/serialising-messages#size-limits","content":"A payload can be up to 64 MB, and a message's user headers up to 100 KB in total. The whole send request also has to fit the server's request limit, which by default is 64 MiB over TCP and 2 MB over HTTP."},"3027":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"page","content":"Connection Strings","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings"},"3028":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","content":"The canonical reference for Iggy connection strings, covering every scheme, credential form and option."},"3029":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#format","content":"Format"},"3030":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"Transport selection"},"3031":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#credentials","content":"Credentials"},"3032":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#duration-values","content":"Duration values"},"3033":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#tcp-options","content":"TCP options"},"3034":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#quic-options","content":"QUIC options"},"3035":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#websocket-options","content":"WebSocket options"},"3036":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#http-options","content":"HTTP options"},"3037":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#usage","content":"Usage"},"3038":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#pending-operation-limits","content":"Pending-operation limits"},"3039":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#native-sdk-differences","content":"Native SDK differences"},"3040":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","content":"A connection string configures an Iggy client in a single line: transport, credentials, server address, and options. This page is the canonical reference for every scheme, credential form, and option key."},"3041":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","content":"The tables below describe the Rust SDK and its Python, C++ and PHP bindings. Native Go, Node.js, C# and Swift clients also accept TCP connection strings. Java supports a smaller subset, described below."},"3042":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#format","content":"The host and port are always required. Use a hostname, IPv4 address or bracketed IPv6 address such as [::1]:8090. Options are appended as key=value pairs separated by &. Scheme names and option keys are case-sensitive."},"3043":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#format","content":"The parser does not percent-decode credentials or option values. Reserved separators such as : and @ cannot be escaped inside a username or password. Use a PAT for credentials that contain them. Duplicate option keys use the last value. Boolean options enable only for the literal true. Use false to disable them."},"3044":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#format","content":"Unknown option keys are hard errors. The parser rejects the whole string with InvalidConnectionString instead of ignoring the key, so a typo fails fast rather than silently falling back to a default."},"3045":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"The scheme picks the transport. Each transport has its own default server port:"},"3046":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"Scheme"},"3047":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"Transport"},"3048":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"Default server port"},"3049":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"iggy://"},"3050":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"TCP (default)"},"3051":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"8090"},"3052":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"iggy+tcp://"},"3053":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"TCP"},"3054":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"8090"},"3055":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"iggy+quic://"},"3056":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"QUIC"},"3057":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transport-selection","content":"8080"},"3058":{"id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/connection-strings#transpo