vent-driven service with a consumer group","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Tutorials"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service"},"3479":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","content":"Build a small order-processing service in Python: key messages by customer, share a topic between two service instances with a consumer group, and see them take over from each other and resume."},"3480":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"3481":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#start-the-server","content":"Start the server"},"3482":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"The producer"},"3483":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-4","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"The service"},"3484":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-5","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Run it"},"3485":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-6","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"Running it again"},"3486":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-7","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","content":"You will build a small order-processing service. A producer writes order events to a topic, keyed by customer. Two copies of the service share the topic through a consumer group. You stop one and watch the other take over, then start it again and see it resume from stored progress."},"3487":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-8","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"You need Docker, Python 3.11 or later, and the Python SDK in a virtual environment. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer, but macOS has no kernel requirement (see System requirements)."},"3488":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-9","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#before-we-start","content":"Consumer groups need a binary transport, so this tutorial uses TCP. HTTP cannot join a group (see Consumer groups and the FAQ)."},"3489":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-10","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#start-the-server","content":"Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."},"3490":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-11","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Save as orders_producer.py."},"3491":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-12","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Partitioning.messages_key hashes the key to pick the partition, so orders for one customer share a partition and stay in order. The connection string comes from the environment, so the same script works against a server on another host (see Connection strings)."},"3492":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-13","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"Run it:"},"3493":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-14","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-producer","content":"The following output illustrates the message shape. Customer choices, offsets and partition scheduling vary."},"3494":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-15","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"Save as order_service.py."},"3495":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-16","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"consume_messages runs until the shutdown event is set. Ctrl-C, or a SIGTERM from a supervisor, sets it, and the current message finishes before the call returns. The instance name is only used in the log."},"3496":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-17","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#the-service","content":"The automatic policy counts a message after its handler succeeds. A handler exception ends the run and leaves that message uncounted. A later run can read it again. Await consumer shutdown to finish pending progress and leave the group."},"3497":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-18","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"One instance. Start the service in a second terminal:"},"3498":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-19","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"It is the only member, so it gets all three partitions and drains what the producer wrote:"},"3499":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-20","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Scale out. In a third terminal start a second instance, then run the producer again:"},"3500":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-21","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"The server rebalances the group. The following example shows svc-b reading partition 2 while svc-a reads 0 and 1. Rebalances and retries can replay messages."},"3501":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-22","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Scale in. Press Ctrl-C in the svc-a terminal. It prints [svc-a] shutting down and exits. Run the producer again. svc-b now owns every partition and receives all 15 orders (output trimmed):"},"3502":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-23","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"Resume. Start svc-a again. It prints only its joined line, because the group's stored offsets mean nothing is replayed. Run the producer once more and svc-a gets partition 2 back:"},"3503":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-24","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#run-it","content":"When a partition moves, the server stops the old owner polling it before handing it to the new one, and waits up to 30 seconds by default (see How does consumer group rebalancing work?)."},"3504":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-25","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"The producer creates the stream and topic only if they are missing, so it can be run any number of times. A restarted service resumes from the group's stored offset. To start from nothing, stop the container, and --rm removes it along with its data."},"3505":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service-26","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/event-driven-service#running-it-again","content":"The service is the same consumer shape as the high-level consumer example in the repository, with a JSON handler and signal handling added."},"3506":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"page","content":"A Grafana dashboard for the broker","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Tutorials"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard"},"3507":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-0","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","content":"Run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana in Docker Compose, and watch the broker on a dashboard."},"3508":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-1","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#before-we-start","content":"Before we start"},"3509":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-2","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#the-stack","content":"The stack"},"3510":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard-3","page_id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard#start-it","content":"Start it"},"3511":{"id":"/docs/tutorials/grafana-dashboard