arp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"publisher.SendAsync(batch) takes ownership - do not dispose the batch yourself. Its buffer returns to the pool once the batch is sent (immediately, or after the background flush)."},"6853":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-111","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"For a direct low-level send, pass batch.Messages to client.SendMessagesAsync(...) yourself, await it, then dispose the batch (using var batch = builder.Build())."},"6854":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-112","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"After disposal, batch.Messages and every payload's Span throw ObjectDisposedException instead of reading recycled pool memory."},"6855":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-113","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#publishing-with-rented-batches","content":"Dispose the builder only when abandoning it before Build(). A using on the builder is safe either way (it becomes a no-op after Build())."},"6856":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-114","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"For automatic object serialization, use IggyPublisherBuilder<T> with an ISerializer<T>:"},"6857":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-115","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"Besides the collection overload, SendAsync also accepts a single item (SendAsync(order, messageId: null, userHeaders: null)) or a collection of (data, messageId, userHeaders) tuples when you need per-message ids or headers."},"6858":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-116","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"For JSON you don't need a custom serializer - the built-in SystemTextJsonSerializer<T> (in Apache.Iggy.Publishers) writes System.Text.Json output directly into the send buffer, with optional JsonSerializerOptions:"},"6859":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-117","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"With background sending enabled, typed values are serialized at flush time. Keep them immutable or pass a snapshot until WaitUntilAllSendsAsync completes."},"6860":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-118","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-publisher","content":"There is no built-in deserializer counterpart - consumers implement IDeserializer<T> themselves (see Typed consumer)."},"6861":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-119","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggyconsumer","content":"The consumer reads through a PartitionReader and yields one message at a time. A standalone consumer requires WithPartitions. A group consumer follows the server assignment."},"6862":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-120","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#iggyconsumer","content":"The fragment assumes a connected client. Manual commits are the default. Commit the checkpoint after processing. DisposeAsync calls ShutdownAsync to close the reader and leave the group."},"6863":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-121","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"Use Consumer.Group(\"my-group\") and WithConsumerGroup(\"my-group\", createIfNotExists: true). Do not supply standalone partitions. Set WithPollOptions(new PollOptions { Commit = CommitPolicy.Auto() }) for automatic commits after processing."},"6864":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-122","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"AutoCommit.Default stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. CommitAfter.Processing counts a message when enumeration advances or a ConsumeAsync handler succeeds. A handler exception ends consumption and leaves that message uncounted. CommitAfter.Marked supports explicit progress marking after concurrent work."},"6865":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-123","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-groups","content":"CommitPolicy.OnRead submits the offset before processing. It cannot be combined with a consumer encryptor. See Deferred polling."},"6866":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-124","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"Use WithPollOptions for count, wait, minimum count, byte budget and commit policy. Use WithPartitions for standalone partition selection, and WithPollingStrategy for the initial position. The previous WithBatchSize, WithPollingInterval, WithPartitionId and WithAutoCommitMode consumer methods are replaced."},"6867":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-125","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consumer-builder-options","content":"A read error that stops a partition ends enumeration with ReadException. Inspect StopsPartition, IsResumable and Skip before selecting a new start with SeekAsync. Automatic retries do not discard an undecodable record without an application decision."},"6868":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-126","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#consuming-with-rented-buffers","content":"ReceiveRentedAsync exposes payloads from a pooled buffer. Dispose each yielded message before moving on, and copy bytes that must survive disposal. Read limits and commit policies are the same as for ReceiveAsync."},"6869":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-127","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"IggyConsumerBuilder<T> accepts an IDeserializer<T> and exposes ReceiveDeserializedAsync. Keep the typed builder in a variable before configuring it because inherited fluent methods return the base builder type. Use WithPollOptions to select the commit policy, and inspect each message status before accepting its progress."},"6870":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk-128","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/high-level-sdk#typed-consumer","content":"See the C# examples for complete producer and consumer programs."},"6871":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"page","content":"C# SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","C#"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro"},"6872":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The async-first C# SDK for .NET applications over TCP and HTTP, from installation to creating a client."},"6873":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6874":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Supported protocols"},"6875":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"Connection semantics"},"6876":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Creating a client"},"6877":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6878":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6879":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6880":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Next steps"},"6881":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The Iggy SDK for C# is a modern, async-first client library for interacting with an Iggy message streaming server from your .NET applications. It supports TCP and HTTP transports. The package is available on NuGet and the source code lives on GitHub."},"6882":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","content":"The SDK is built around the IIggyClient interface, which aggregates every feature (publishing, consuming, stream/topic management, users, offsets, consumer groups, and system operations). For the low-level per-call API and the full configuration reference, see the Guide. For the ergonomic, batteries-included producer/consumer abstractions, see the High-level SDK."},"6883":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"The SDK targets .NET 8 and .NET 10 (net8.0 and net10.0). Version 0.10.0 matches the server 0.10.0 protocol and includes both topic durability options. Version 0.8.0 uses the older TCP framing. When building from source, use the server and SDK from the same checkout."},"6884":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"To use the source SDK with these release-preparation examples, run from the Iggy checkout root:"},"6885":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#installation","content":"Put a sample in IggySample/Program.cs and run dotnet run --project IggySample. For console logging, add its package to that project:"},"6886":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"The SDK supports two transport protocols:"},"6887":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"TCP - binary protocol for optimal performance and lower latency (recommended). Over TCP the SDK speaks the VSR consensus framing, the only wire protocol the server accepts. See Connection semantics"},"6888":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"HTTP - RESTful JSON API for stateless operations. BaseAddress must then be a full URI such as http://127.0.0.1:3000 (the server's default HTTP port is 3000, TCP is 8090)"},"6889":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#supported-protocols","content":"Some operations are TCP-only and throw FeatureUnavailableException on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, GetMeAsync, DeleteSegmentsAsync, and SendBinaryRequestAsync."},"6890":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"TCP uses the current binary protocol and routes partition requests to their primaries. The client discovers the cluster roster and follows metadata leadership separately. Balanced and message-key partitioning are resolved on the client."},"6891":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"A retained session can survive transport loss. Reconnect binds it before registering a new session. Logout, session expiry and account deactivation end the session. An uncertain mutation retains its request identity for recovery. Keep the same client and use ResolveOperationAsync or AbandonOperationAsync when an exception reports a retained operation. Abandonment does not undo the server result."},"6892":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"A committing deferred poll whose reply is lost can throw VsrFetchProgressUncertainException. Later progress calls can throw VsrPartitionLaneBlockedException until the operation is abandoned. Offset reads remain available for inspection. Do not convert every exception into a fresh send."},"6893":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#connection-semantics","content":"The reader and high-level consumer follow group assignments. A one-shot poll names one partition. They default to manual checkpoint commits. See Deferred polling and the Guide."},"6894":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"The examples use iggy / iggy. For a new server data directory, start the matching source server from the Iggy repository root:"},"6895":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"For an existing server, use its configured credentials. Create a client with IggyClientFactory.CreateClient, then call ConnectAsync:"},"6896":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"Optionally, provide an ILoggerFactory for diagnostics (defaults to NullLoggerFactory.Instance). The console logger requires the Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console package:"},"6897":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#creating-a-client","content":"IggyClientConfigurator also exposes TCP socket buffers, TLS, heartbeat, response-frame limits, reconnection with exponential backoff (on by default), auto-login, and message encryption. HTTP clients require explicit login. The TCP connection settings are not applied to HTTP. See Client configuration for the full reference."},"6898":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#quick-start","content":"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the equivalent low-level, per-call flow, see the Guide."},"6899":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#consumer","content":"ReceiveAsync polls indefinitely - pass a CancellationToken or break out of the loop to stop."},"6900":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Guide - client configuration reference and the full API surface: auth, streams, topics, partitions, publishing, consuming, offsets, consumer groups, system operations"},"6901":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"High-level SDK - IggyPublisher / IggyConsumer with background sending, retries, auto-commit, typed (de)serialization, and pooled (rented) buffers to avoid separate payload copies on TCP"},"6902":{"id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/csharp/intro#next-steps","content":"Examples - producer, consumer-group, and typed-message samples, plus links to runnable projects"},"6903":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples"},"6904":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"Runnable Go examples from the core repository, and how to start a server they can connect to."},"6905":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"6906":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"6907":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"TLS"},"6908":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example lives in the examples/go directory of the core repository. Its go.mod carries a replace directive pointing at the in-repo SDK, so the example always builds against the SDK source in the same checkout. The examples are exercised in CI via scripts/run-examples-from-readme.sh --language go."},"6909":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"The Go SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples require Go 1.25 or newer and log in as iggy/iggy. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"},"6910":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#starting-the-server","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory (local_data by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"6911":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"The producer creates sample-stream and sample-topic (if missing) and sends 5 batches of 10 messages to partition 0. The consumer polls them from offset 0 and exits after 5 batches. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based. These repository examples use numeric stream and topic IDs 0, so create no other streams or topics before running the producer on the fresh server. Run the producer first."},"6912":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Run both from the examples/go directory:"},"6913":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Each consumer reads partition 0 independently, starting from offset 0 or the earliest retained message. Running several consumers reads the same messages. These examples do not join a consumer group."},"6914":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"Both binaries accept the same flags:"},"6915":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tcp-server-address - server address, default 127.0.0.1:8090"},"6916":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls - enable TLS"},"6917":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls-ca-file - path to a CA certificate file"},"6918":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#getting-started","content":"--tls-domain - TLS server domain name for SNI"},"6919":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"From the repository root, start a disposable server with TLS enabled and the development certificates:"},"6920":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Run the producer before the consumer with the TLS flags (paths relative to examples/go):"},"6921":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/examples#tls","content":"For the code walkthrough, see the quick start on the intro page, which follows the same example."},"6922":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"page","content":"Go SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Go"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro"},"6923":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Go SDK, a blocking client speaking the VSR wire protocol over TCP, and which servers it pairs with."},"6924":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"6925":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6926":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6927":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6928":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6929":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"TLS"},"6930":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Delivery semantics"},"6931":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"6932":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#connection-strings-and-metadata","content":"Connection strings and metadata"},"6933":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"6934":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","content":"The Iggy Go SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Go applications. It is a blocking client that speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol over TCP, with or without TLS. The package is available on pkg.go.dev and the source code lives in foreign/go."},"6935":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#server-compatibility","content":"The SDK and the server must speak the same wire protocol. Tagged releases up to v0.8.0 predate VSR (and the current API) and pair only with servers of the same era. The current SDK speaks VSR only. This page targets server 0.10.0. Use a compatible SDK release or edge version. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."},"6936":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"The current source requires Go 1.25 or newer. Run these commands from your application module:"},"6937":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"To pin the release that matches server 0.10.0:"},"6938":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#installation","content":"For unreleased changes, use the local SDK replacement shown in the repository examples. A published edge tag need not contain every change in your checkout."},"6939":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"6940":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"Run the server from the repository root with disposable development data and no root credential overrides:"},"6941":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#quick-start","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory. IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD take precedence over the flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials, including credentials recovered from cluster peers. The snippets use iggy/iggy. Run the producer before the consumer."},"6942":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#producer","content":"CreateTopic accepts *iggcon.TopicOptions. A nil value selects server defaults. CreateStream accepts *iggcon.StreamOptions. UpdateTopic, UpdateStream and UpdateUser accept typed patches. DescribeOptions lists the keys and mutability flags."},"6943":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"The consumer reads partition 0 independently and keeps its offset only in memory. Restarting reads from offset 0 again, or the earliest retained message if older messages were removed."},"6944":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer","content":"Uncompressed payloads and user headers alias the reply buffer, so retaining their slices pins the whole reply. Copy those bytes to retain only the message you need. S2 decompression allocates a separate payload buffer."},"6945":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"Replace the quick-start client constructor with the following TCP options. Start the TLS server as shown on the examples page. The development CA path below is relative to examples/go:"},"6946":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#tls","content":"tcp.WithTLSValidateCertificate controls certificate validation. The client also accepts client.WithLogger for a custom *slog.Logger and runs a built-in heartbeat loop (every 5 seconds by default) once connected."},"6947":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"SendMessages returns the placements reported by the server. An uncertain mutation retains its frozen request and original identity. Reconnection binds the existing session before attempting a new registration. ResolveOperation retries the retained identity, and AbandonOperation releases local state without undoing the server result. Keep the same client for recovery. A fresh send after losing receipt access can duplicate work."},"6948":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#delivery-semantics","content":"Crash durability follows the topic's durability policy: replicated confirms replication, while persisted also waits for the required replicas to persist the message data. See Durability. An empty confirmation list is a valid success but does not by itself prove that new messages were appended."},"6949":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"The client exposes CreateConsumerGroup, JoinConsumerGroup, LeaveConsumerGroup, GetConsumerGroups and SyncConsumerGroup. A one-shot PollMessages reads an explicit partition. Use reader.NewPartitionReader or reader.NewIggyConsumer with iggcon.NewGroupConsumer to read the full assignment."},"6950":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#consumer-groups","content":"The reader joins, refreshes assignments and leaves on Close. Its default wait is five seconds. Commit stores a delivered checkpoint after processing. iggcon.AutoCommitPolicy(iggcon.DefaultAutoCommit()) selects automatic commits after processing. See Deferred polling."},"6951":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#connection-strings-and-metadata","content":"client.ParseConnectionString returns TCP options from a connection string. Pass them to client.WithTcp when constructing the client. It accepts credentials, TLS and reconnect options. Use GetMetadataChanges to read resource or activity changes without listing the entire catalog. Raw requests can target a replica with SendBinaryRequestToReplica. See Metadata changes for restart and retention behavior."},"6952":{"id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/go/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/go/intro#examples","content":"A runnable getting-started example with producer, consumer, and TLS flags lives in examples/go. See the examples page for how to run it."},"6953":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples"},"6954":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable Java examples from the core repository, built with Gradle, and how to start a server for them."},"6955":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"Starting the server"},"6956":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"6957":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Blocking vs. async"},"6958":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","content":"Runnable examples live in the examples/java directory of the core repository as a standalone Gradle project. The project builds against the in-repo SDK (via an includeBuild substitution), so the examples always match the SDK source in the same checkout. Java 17 or newer is required. The included gradlew wrapper downloads the pinned Gradle version on first run."},"6959":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"These examples target server 0.10.0 and speak the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples log in as iggy/iggy. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"},"6960":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#starting-the-server","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory (local_data by default). Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. A fresh cluster replica can recover credentials from peers. This setup is intended only for development and testing."},"6961":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Run each example from the examples/java directory with its Gradle task. Run the producer before its consumer. The ordinary consumers keep their cursor in memory and start from offset 0 or the earliest retained message on each run. The multi-tenant consumer uses consumer groups and server-managed offsets."},"6962":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"gettingstarted - basic blocking producer and consumer, the best starting point."},"6963":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"messageheaders - message metadata via custom header keys and values, with a message_type header selecting the application handler."},"6964":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"messageenvelope - JSON envelope pattern for polymorphic message handling (order events wrapped in envelopes for type identification)."},"6965":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"multitenant - multi-tenant isolation: per-tenant streams, users with stream-specific permissions, and concurrent producers and consumers across tenants."},"6966":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"sinkdataproducer - high-volume data generation (100 batches of 1000 to 1099 messages) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."},"6967":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"streambuilder - a combined producer and consumer in a single class. It deletes its test_stream stream after the run."},"6968":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"async - the async client: CompletableFuture chaining, submitting multiple sends without blocking, backpressure, error recovery with exponential backoff, and thread pool separation."},"6969":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"tcptls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with CA certificate verification. Requires a TLS-enabled server. Run this server command from the repository root:"},"6970":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Return to examples/java for the client commands. Their CA path is relative to that directory."},"6971":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/examples#blocking-vs-async","content":"Use the blocking client for scripts, CLI tools, integration tests, and anywhere sequential code is easier to reason about. Use the async client for high throughput, reactive applications, and composing non-blocking requests with CompletableFuture. The async client runs I/O on Netty event loop threads: never block them with .join(), .get(), Thread.sleep(), or blocking I/O inside thenApply/thenAccept. Offload blocking work with thenApplyAsync(fn, executor). The examples/java README covers these async patterns in detail."},"6972":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"page","content":"Java SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Java"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro"},"6973":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Java SDK, with blocking and CompletableFuture clients over TCP, and which servers they pair with."},"6974":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"Server compatibility"},"6975":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"6976":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Maven"},"6977":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Gradle"},"6978":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"6979":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"6980":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"6981":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Client types and configuration"},"6982":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"Deferred reads and recovery"},"6983":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"6984":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","content":"The Iggy Java SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Java applications. It provides a blocking client and an async client (based on CompletableFuture) over TCP, plus a blocking HTTP client. The artifact is published to Maven Central and the source code lives in foreign/java."},"6985":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#server-compatibility","content":"This documentation targets server 0.10.0 and SDK 0.10.0. The TCP transport speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. The Java artifact 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."},"6986":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#installation","content":"Use Java 17 or newer. The SDK is published to Maven Central."},"6987":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#maven","content":"Add the dependency under the project element in pom.xml:"},"6988":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#gradle","content":"Check Maven Central for the latest published version. Snapshot builds are available from the ASF snapshot repository."},"6989":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."},"6990":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#quick-start","content":"Start a development server using the example setup, with iggy/iggy credentials, then run the producer before the consumer. Bootstrap credentials do not replace recovered credentials."},"6991":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#producer","content":"Creation uses StreamOptions and TopicOptions. Updates use StreamUpdate, TopicUpdate and UserUpdate. Their builders send only named fields and explicit reset keys. Compression and other creation-only options cannot change later."},"6992":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#consumer","content":"The consumer reads partition 0 independently and stops on an empty poll. Its cursor starts at offset 0 or the earliest retained message each time it runs. autoCommit is disabled, so this sample does not store its progress on the server."},"6993":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"org.apache.iggy.Iggy is the unified entrypoint for building clients. Iggy.tcpClientBuilder().blocking() returns the same builder as IggyTcpClient.builder() used above."},"6994":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Close clients when finished: client.close(), asyncClient.close().join(), and httpClient.close(). The HTTP close method declares IOException."},"6995":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"The TCP builder also accepts TLS and resilience options. Start the TLS development server and run this fragment with examples/java as the working directory:"},"6996":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#client-types-and-configuration","content":"Beyond streams, topics, and messages, the client exposes consumer groups, partitions, users, and personal access tokens through client.consumerGroups(), client.partitions(), client.users(), and client.personalAccessTokens(). The SDK's custom exception types inherit from IggyException. Failed futures can wrap them in CompletionException when joined."},"6997":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"PollOptions.waitFor(Duration.ofSeconds(5)) enables deferred polling. CommitPolicy.manual() is the default. A reader or consumer also accepts CommitPolicy.auto(...). One-shot polls reject automatic policies. The async client exposes a partition reader through client.reader(...). A blocking wrapper is available as org.apache.iggy.client.blocking.reader.PartitionReader."},"6998":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"TCP retains uncertain mutation identities. Keep the client and use resolveOperation or abandonOperation when an exception reports a retained operation. An uncertain committing poll blocks progress for its partition until abandonment. HTTP supports standalone consumers and deferred reads, but not consumer group membership."},"6999":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"The TCP builders accept fromConnectionString. Only iggy:// and iggy+tcp://, username/password credentials, tls and tls_ca_file are supported. PAT connection strings and additional options are rejected. The builder also exposes shutdown quiet-period configuration for applications that need prompt close."},"7000":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#deferred-reads-and-recovery","content":"getMetadataChanges follows metadata history, and raw requests can target a named replica. See Metadata changes and Client failover."},"7001":{"id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/java/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/java/intro#examples","content":"Runnable examples covering the async client, message headers and envelopes, multi-tenant setups, and TLS live in examples/java. See the examples page for how to run them."},"7002":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples"},"7003":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Build the local Node.js SDK and run its TypeScript examples."},"7004":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"7005":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. They use the SDK from the same checkout:"},"7006":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"getting-started - basic producer and consumer"},"7007":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"basic - producer and consumer with utilities"},"7008":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"message-envelope - JSON message envelope pattern"},"7009":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"message-headers - message-type dispatch using a JSON wrapper in the payload"},"7010":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"multi-tenant - separate streams for tenants, using one client"},"7011":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"tcp-tls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections"},"7012":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"stream-builder - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"},"7013":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","content":"sink-data-producer - bulk data generation for sink connectors"},"7014":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These examples target server 0.10.0. See Getting started for prerequisites:"},"7015":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The default-root flag sets the iggy/iggy credentials used by the examples. --fresh deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables override the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials."},"7016":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"From the repository root, build the local SDK before installing the examples. The stream-builder example creates a stream and topic, sends and consumes three messages, and deletes its resources:"},"7017":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Each example has a script in examples/node/package.json. Set DEBUG=iggy:examples* to see its progress."},"7018":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The separate producer and consumer scripts do not consistently share resources: the envelope and headers consumers create new empty topics, and the tenant producer deletes its streams before exiting. The getting-started and basic producers spread batches over five partitions, while their consumers poll partition 0. Use the self-contained stream-builder command above for a complete send-and-consume walkthrough. The sink-data producer deletes its selected topic and stream when it finishes, including resources that already existed."},"7019":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The TCP/TLS pair needs a TLS-enabled source server. Run this command from the repository root, then the two test:tcp-tls:* scripts from examples/node, where they load ../../core/certs/iggy_ca_cert.pem:"},"7020":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These are repository development certificates."},"7021":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Node.js SDK intro."},"7022":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"page","content":"Node.js SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Node"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro"},"7023":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Node.js and TypeScript SDK, from installation to keeping client and server versions in step."},"7024":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"7025":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"7026":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"7027":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"7028":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"Consumer group"},"7029":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"Running the samples again"},"7030":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"Configuration"},"7031":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"7032":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"7033":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"The Iggy Node.js SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Node.js and TypeScript applications. It communicates with the Iggy server over TCP or TLS using the binary protocol. The package is available on npm and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"7034":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","content":"These docs target server 0.10.0 and Node SDK 0.11.0. The Node package 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. The current SDK speaks only the VSR wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats."},"7035":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below expect an Iggy server on 127.0.0.1:8090. See Getting started for the server prerequisites:"},"7036":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the root credentials the samples log in with. Without --with-default-root-credentials (or the IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME / IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD variables), a fresh server refuses to start. For source runs, --fresh deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables take precedence over the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace credentials already stored in recovered data."},"7037":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#quick-start","content":"Save the following snippets as producer.mjs and consumer.mjs in the project where you installed the SDK. Run node producer.mjs before node consumer.mjs. A consumer group sample follows them."},"7038":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#producer","content":"The client resolves the target partition and routes requests to its primary. A primary can differ from the metadata leader."},"7039":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer","content":"The poll response does not wait for the auto-commit to finish. The sample processes messages after polling, so auto-commit does not confirm that the application handled each message."},"7040":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"A consumer group distributes partitions among its members. The high-level consumer creates and joins the group, then reads its assigned partitions. Save this as consumer-group.mjs:"},"7041":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#consumer-group","content":"This loop waits for messages until it is stopped. It stores checkpoints after printing. The group has its own offsets, separate from a standalone consumer."},"7042":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"All three samples can be run again without changes:"},"7043":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"node producer.mjs finds the existing stream and topic and appends ten more messages."},"7044":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"node consumer.mjs prints only the new messages, because PollingStrategy.Next continues after the stored offset. With nothing new to read, it prints nothing."},"7045":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"node consumer-group.mjs does the same from the group's stored offset."},"7046":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#running-the-samples-again","content":"To start again with an empty server, stop the container and run the docker run command again. --rm removes the container and its data."},"7047":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"The client constructor accepts a few options:"},"7048":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"reconnect: automatic reconnection with { enabled, interval, maxRetries }."},"7049":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"heartbeatInterval: the client pings the server every 5 seconds by default. 0 disables heartbeats. When server heartbeat eviction is enabled, silent consumer-group members can be evicted, so keep it well below the server's heartbeat timeout."},"7050":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"maxResponseFrameSize: response frames larger than this limit (default 64 MiB) are rejected and close the connection. Raise it when polling very large batches."},"7051":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"poolSize: the client currently supports exactly one pooled connection. min/max above 1 throw."},"7052":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#configuration","content":"TLS: set transport: 'TLS' and pass Node tls.ConnectionOptions in options. See the tcp-tls example."},"7053":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer groups: client.group manages groups (including ensure-and-join in one call), and client.message.reader or client.consumerGroup reads the assigned partitions. A one-shot poll names one partition."},"7054":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer streams: A high-level consumer supports async iteration, handler-based consumption and toReadable()."},"7055":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Administration: users, personal access tokens, offsets, partitions, segments, and cluster metadata are all available on the client."},"7056":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Errors: the package exports ResponseError, DeserializeError, ProtocolFrameError, and VsrEvictionError for precise error handling."},"7057":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"See the foreign/node README for the full API walkthrough."},"7058":{"id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/node/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/node/intro#examples","content":"Examples are available in the examples/node directory, written in TypeScript. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"7059":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"page","content":"PHP SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","PHP"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro"},"7060":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The experimental PHP SDK, a native extension wrapping the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source."},"7061":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Requirements"},"7062":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Build and install"},"7063":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"7064":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"Consumer groups"},"7065":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"API notes"},"7066":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"Resource options and recovery"},"7067":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"7068":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The Iggy PHP SDK is a native PHP extension built in Rust with ext-php-rs. It wraps the Rust SDK and exposes a synchronous Iggy\\Client: each call drives the extension's internal Tokio runtime and blocks the calling PHP thread until the operation completes. The source code is available on GitHub."},"7069":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","content":"The extension is experimental. It's defined as the apache/iggy-php composer package (php-ext type). To use it today, build it from the repository source."},"7070":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"Rust and Cargo"},"7071":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"PHP 8.3 or newer with php-config (non-thread-safe builds only, no ZTS)"},"7072":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#requirements","content":"cargo-php"},"7073":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"Alternatively, skip the install and point PHP at the built library per invocation:"},"7074":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#build-and-install","content":"On macOS the library is libiggy_php.dylib."},"7075":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The sample below targets Iggy 0.10.0 on 127.0.0.1:8090. Start the server:"},"7076":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for a new data directory. Existing stored credentials are not replaced. For the source command, ensure IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD are unset or match the sample. Environment values take precedence over the flag. --fresh removes the local replica state, so use it only for disposable development data."},"7077":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#quick-start","content":"For binary transports, credentials in the connection string take effect on connect(). HTTP requires loginUser() explicitly. Alternatively, construct the client with a plain address (new \\Iggy\\Client('127.0.0.1:8090')) and call loginUser('iggy', 'iggy') explicitly."},"7078":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"consumerGroup() creates and joins a group by default. It reads assigned partitions and stores no offsets unless you select a policy or commit checkpoints. This example processes five messages:"},"7079":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"consumeMessages() requires a finite limit. A callback exception ends the run. With automatic commits after processing, the failed message stays uncounted. PollOptions carries the commit policy. Use CommitPolicy::manual(), CommitPolicy::onRead() or CommitPolicy::auto(...)."},"7080":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#consumer-groups","content":"reader() reads batches from explicit partitions. groupReader() reads an existing group's assignment. Close a reader with close(). A timeout on next(timeout_micros) returns null and keeps a later batch available for the next call."},"7081":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Methods are camelCase and classes live in the Iggy namespace, for example Iggy\\Client and Iggy\\SendMessage."},"7082":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Partition ids are 0-based: for a topic with one partition, use 0."},"7083":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PHP strings are passed as named identifiers, including strings that contain only digits. PHP integers are passed as numeric identifiers."},"7084":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"PollingStrategy::timestamp() and timestampMicros() expect microseconds since the Unix epoch. Use PollingStrategy::timestampSeconds() for PHP time() values."},"7085":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Message IDs and checksums are returned as decimal strings. Offset and timestamp getters return PHP integers and are limited to PHP_INT_MAX."},"7086":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"Errors are thrown as exceptions under Iggy\\Exception: AuthenticationException, ConnectionException, NotFoundException, TransientException, and the IggyException base."},"7087":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"TLS uses the Rust SDK connection-string format, for example iggy+tcp://user:pass@host:port?tls=true&tls_domain=localhost&tls_ca_file=/path/to/ca.pem."},"7088":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#api-notes","content":"The extension owns a lazy global Tokio runtime. Don't call pcntl_fork() after the first Iggy call: the child inherits file descriptors but not the runtime's worker threads."},"7089":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"createStream($name, $encryption_key) accepts the standard padded base64 of a 32-byte stream key. createTopic accepts named creation arguments, including replication_factor. Stream and topic updates change only named fields and reset keys. describeOptions() lists each catalog. User and PAT administration are not exposed as typed PHP methods in this release. Reads expose encryption fingerprints, never key material."},"7090":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"getMetadataChanges() follows the metadata log. sendBinaryRequestToReplica() sends supported non-replicated commands to a selected node. See Metadata changes."},"7091":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#resource-options-and-recovery","content":"Retained mutation errors expose an operation ID. Use resolveOperation() on the same client to retry its retained identity, or abandonOperation() to accept the unknown result and release local state. A lost committing poll throws FetchProgressUncertain and blocks progress for the partition until abandonment."},"7092":{"id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/php/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/php/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/php directory. They read IGGY_CONNECTION_STRING for a binary transport with credentials. Otherwise they connect over TCP using IGGY_HOST and IGGY_PORT, then log in with the literal IGGY_USERNAME and IGGY_PASSWORD values:"},"7093":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples"},"7094":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working Python examples from the core repository, covering connection strings, message headers and TLS."},"7095":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"Running the examples"},"7096":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. CI runs the source examples against a source-built server, including a separate TLS pass:"},"7097":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"getting-started - producer and consumer with optional TLS via TcpConfig"},"7098":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"basic - producer and consumer using connection strings"},"7099":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","content":"message-headers - user headers with str, bytes, bool, int, and float values, in two variants: plain-headers (plain dict, the SDK infers wire types) and typed-headers (explicit HeaderKey/HeaderValue)"},"7100":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"These examples target Iggy 0.10.0. Start the server:"},"7101":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced, and environment credentials override the source command's default-credentials flag. Use --fresh only with disposable local replica data."},"7102":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"With Python 3.11 or newer and Rust/Cargo available, run from examples/python. uv uses the local SDK path in pyproject.toml. The pip command below names that path explicitly:"},"7103":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/examples#running-the-examples","content":"For the quick-start code itself, see the Python SDK intro."},"7104":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"page","content":"Python SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Python"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro"},"7105":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Python SDK, a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, and how to install it."},"7106":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Installation"},"7107":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"Quick start"},"7108":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#producer","content":"Producer"},"7109":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"7110":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Beyond the basics"},"7111":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Examples"},"7112":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The Iggy Python SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Python application. It is built as a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, which means it supports TCP, QUIC, HTTP, and WebSocket transports via connection strings. The package is available on PyPI and the source code can be found on GitHub."},"7113":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","content":"The wheel bundles the Rust SDK, whose binary transports speak the current Iggy wire protocol without falling back to older formats. Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build the SDK and server from the same source checkout. The published wheel can lag source API changes."},"7114":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"Python 3.11 or newer is required. Install the published package in a virtual environment:"},"7115":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#installation","content":"For the source version described here, install from the repository root with Rust and Cargo available:"},"7116":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The samples below target Iggy 0.10.0 on 127.0.0.1:8090:"},"7117":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#quick-start","content":"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced. Environment credentials override the default-credentials flag, so unset them or set them to match the sample. --fresh deletes local replica state. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."},"7118":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"TLS: use TcpConfig with tls_enabled and tls_ca_file, or the TLS options in a connection string. The certificate must match tls_domain, which defaults to the dialed host. The getting-started example shows the full setup."},"7119":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Consumer groups: await client.consumer_group(...) returns an IggyConsumer that creates and joins the group by default and commits offsets according to the commit policy in poll_options, which defaults to manual commits. Groups require a binary transport. HTTP supports ordinary consumers only."},"7120":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"User headers: SendMessage(data, user_headers=...) accepts a plain dict with str, bytes, bool, int, or float values. HeaderKey/HeaderValue give explicit control over the wire type. See the message-headers examples."},"7121":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Topic options: create_topic(..., options=TopicOptions(...)) accepts typed options. TopicOptions.raw accepts extra keys as dict[str, str], validated by the server. client.describe_options(\"topic\") lists the keys a server accepts."},"7122":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#beyond-the-basics","content":"Administration: user and permission management. Personal access tokens can be used for login via AutoLogin.personal_access_token(...) in the connection config. PAT management isn't exposed yet."},"7123":{"id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/python/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/python/intro#examples","content":"Working examples are available in the examples/python directory. See Examples for the list and how to run them."},"7124":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples"},"7125":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The Rust SDK examples in the core repository, and how to start a server to run them against."},"7126":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"In the core repository, you can find the following examples using the Rust SDK."},"7127":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. Start the server from the repository root in a separate terminal. The examples use iggy/iggy. For a new local instance with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"},"7128":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. --fresh deletes this replica's local data. Use disposable development data. A new standalone server without explicit credentials generates a root password. A new cluster requires explicit credentials."},"7129":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"The examples are cargo example targets of the iggy_examples crate. Run them from the repository root with cargo run --example <name> so relative certificate paths resolve. Run each producer before its consumer. Some examples create fixed names and require fresh data when repeated. The getting-started consumer also expects stream and topic IDs 0, so run that pair first on a fresh server. Partition IDs are 0-based, but balanced producers and consumer groups can use multiple partitions."},"7130":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Getting started - the basic example which is discussed in the getting started guide."},"7131":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Basic - producer and consumer using the low-level Client trait with CLI args for transport selection (TCP/QUIC/HTTP/WebSocket)."},"7132":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"New SDK - an introduction to the high-level SDK as discussed in the dedicated guide."},"7133":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Stream builder - examples using the IggyStream, IggyStreamProducer, and IggyStreamConsumer builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."},"7134":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Message envelope - the example of how to send a message with a custom envelope e.g. to differentiate between different types of messages."},"7135":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Message headers - the example of how to send a message with custom headers (typed headers, message type discrimination, client-side compression)."},"7136":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Multi-tenant - the larger example of how to structure your application to support multiple tenants (separated by the unique streams) with the different users, permissions, topics etc."},"7137":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"TCP TLS - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with custom CA certificates. Stop the plain server and start a TLS-enabled server from the repository root:"},"7138":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"These certificates are for local testing. Then run:"},"7139":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/examples","content":"Sink data producer - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."},"7140":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"page","content":"High-level SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk"},"7141":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"IggyProducer and IggyConsumer: batching, consumer groups and offset commits without the boilerplate."},"7142":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"7143":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Producer"},"7144":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Background producer configuration"},"7145":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Send confirmations"},"7146":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Consumer"},"7147":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Consumer configuration"},"7148":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"Automatic commits"},"7149":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#read-errors","content":"Read errors"},"7150":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#idempotent-producer","content":"Idempotent producer"},"7151":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"Raw requests"},"7152":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"IggyProducer batches writes and IggyConsumer reads messages through a partition reader. Both share the client's routing, session and recovery behavior. The consumer manages group membership and tracks checkpoints. Select when it stores those checkpoints through PollOptions."},"7153":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","content":"The snippets assume an async function with a Result return type and the iggy, Tokio and futures-util dependencies. Use the Rust SDK setup and a development server with the sample credentials."},"7154":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Instead of providing the configuration for the client, you can use the connection string. It's a string that contains all the necessary information to connect to the Iggy API, and it works with all four transports:"},"7155":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Which can be used to create the client like this:"},"7156":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Consumer groups require a binary transport. Use an ordinary consumer with an explicit partition over HTTP."},"7157":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Options are appended as query parameters. For TCP:"},"7158":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The option keys differ per transport (for example QUIC uses reconnection_max_retries and reconnection_reestablish_after where TCP uses reconnection_retries and reestablish_after). Unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"7159":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"Unless you need client-side encryption, a custom Partitioner, or other settings outside the connection-string options, you should be good to go with the connection string. On the other hand, you can always make use of IggyClientBuilder::from_connection_string() to extend the options on top of the provided connection string."},"7160":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#connection-string","content":"The IggyClient internally wraps its transport in an IggyRwLock, but the client itself does not implement Clone. Share an Arc<IggyClient> across tasks. Cloning the Arc keeps the same client and connection. Typically, it's a good idea to create a separate connection for producing and consuming, but it's not a requirement."},"7161":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The producer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to send messages to the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke client.producer() to get the IggyProducerBuilder allowing you to configure the producer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"},"7162":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-21","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"The direct producer splits each send() call into requests of at most 1000 messages. For sequential calls, the 5 ms linger setting delays a call until at least 5 ms after the previous successful send. It does not buffer messages between calls or delay individual chunks within a call. Choose background to buffer sends on worker tasks. balanced routes requests round-robin across partitions. By default, init() creates a missing stream and topic, requiring the corresponding permissions."},"7163":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-22","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Finally, you can use the send() method to send the messages to the topic. The producer never needs to be a mutable binding: both init() and send() take &self, so a plain let producer is enough for its whole life. Here's how you can send the messages:"},"7164":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-23","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Of course, you can provide the message headers, custom binary serialization etc. as it's the same IggyMessage type as the one used in the low-level API (use IggyMessage::builder() to set headers or a custom ID). The producer will take care of the rest, including the retries, partitioning, batching etc."},"7165":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-24","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#producer","content":"Besides send(), the producer also offers send_one(message), send_with_partitioning(messages, partitioning) to override the partitioning per call, and send_to(stream, topic, messages, partitioning) to target a different stream and topic."},"7166":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-25","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"The background mode buffers messages and sends them from shard workers, trading latency for throughput and backpressure control. BackgroundConfig::builder() exposes:"},"7167":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-26","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Field"},"7168":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-27","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Default"},"7169":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-28","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Description"},"7170":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-29","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"num_shards"},"7171":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-30","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1"},"7172":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-31","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Number of shard workers running in parallel"},"7173":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-32","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"sharding"},"7174":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-33","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"OrderedSharding"},"7175":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-34","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Routes each send to a worker. OrderedSharding keeps a stream/topic pair on one sequential worker. BalancedSharding round-robins across workers for maximum throughput and can reorder sends"},"7176":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-35","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"linger_time"},"7177":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-36","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1ms"},"7178":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-37","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"How long a shard may wait before flushing an incomplete batch"},"7179":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-38","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"batch_size"},"7180":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-39","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1 MiB"},"7181":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-40","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Per-worker flush threshold in bytes. 0 disables this trigger"},"7182":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-41","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"batch_length"},"7183":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-42","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1000"},"7184":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-43","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Per-worker flush threshold in queued sends, not individual messages. 0 disables this trigger"},"7185":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-44","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"failure_mode"},"7186":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-45","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Block"},"7187":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-46","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Behavior when the byte budget is exhausted: Block, BlockWithTimeout(duration), or FailImmediately"},"7188":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-47","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"max_buffer_size"},"7189":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-48","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"32 MiB"},"7190":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-49","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Budget for message bytes buffered or in flight across all workers. 0 disables this budget"},"7191":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-50","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"max_in_flight"},"7192":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-51","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"1"},"7193":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-52","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Maximum concurrent writes across workers. Each worker remains sequential. 0 uses the semaphore's maximum capacity"},"7194":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-53","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"error_callback"},"7195":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-54","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"LogErrorCallback"},"7196":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-55","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Async callback invoked on errors the producer cannot recover from"},"7197":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-56","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"batch_size and batch_length are flush triggers checked after adding a send. The final send can take the buffer past the byte threshold. The linger deadline starts when an empty buffer receives a send. The first trigger reached causes a flush. Each worker also has a bounded queue, which can make dispatch wait independently of failure_mode. A send larger than the entire byte budget fails under every backpressure mode. Nonzero max_buffer_size and max_in_flight values must not exceed tokio::sync::Semaphore::MAX_PERMITS. Constructing the dispatcher with a larger value panics."},"7198":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-57","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#background-producer-configuration","content":"Stop all senders, then call producer.shutdown().await to drain background queues and wait for writes and error callbacks. Dropping the producer without shutdown can lose buffered messages. Shutdown does not turn failed writes into successful ones. The default error callback logs and drops failed sends."},"7199":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-58","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#send-confirmations","content":"Every send method returns a SendMessagesResponse carrying the commit confirmations of the chunks the send was split into. The list is empty whenever the server sends no confirmation payload, and always for a background producer, which hands the messages to a dispatcher and returns before the send happens. Branch on confirmations.is_empty() instead of indexing. A retried chunk may already have been committed by an earlier attempt, so a confirmation offset does not imply uniqueness. Direct-send completion follows the topic's durability policy."},"7200":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-59","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"A consumer group reads its assigned partitions. A standalone consumer requires an explicit .partitions(...) list. The builder uses PollingStrategy::next(), a five-second server wait, up to 1000 messages and a 1 MiB reply. It stores no offsets by default."},"7201":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-60","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer","content":"Import futures_util::StreamExt to use next(). The mutable consumer drives reads and retries when polled. A checkpoint keeps the partition history and ownership that delivered the message. Committing it after recreation or an ownership change is refused. Process messages before committing their checkpoints."},"7202":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-61","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Method"},"7203":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-62","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Default"},"7204":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-63","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Behavior"},"7205":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-64","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"poll_options(...)"},"7206":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-65","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Five-second wait, manual commit"},"7207":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-66","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Read limits and commit policy"},"7208":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-67","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"partitions(...)"},"7209":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-68","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Required for standalone consumer"},"7210":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-69","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Explicit partition IDs. Refused for a group"},"7211":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-70","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"prefetch_bytes(...)"},"7212":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-71","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"16 MiB"},"7213":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-72","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Encoded bytes reserved for buffered and in-flight replies"},"7214":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-73","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"prefetch_messages(...)"},"7215":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-74","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"16000, or the larger poll count"},"7216":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-75","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Reserved messages"},"7217":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-76","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"polling_strategy(...)"},"7218":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-77","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"next()"},"7219":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-78","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Start of a partition without a position"},"7220":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-79","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"create_consumer_group_if_not_exists()"},"7221":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-80","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Enabled"},"7222":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-81","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Create the group before joining"},"7223":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-82","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"polling_retry_interval(...)"},"7224":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-83","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"One second"},"7225":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-84","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Delay after a refused read"},"7226":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-85","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"init_retries(...)"},"7227":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-86","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Disabled"},"7228":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-87","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Retry missing stream or topic during initialization"},"7229":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-88","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"allow_replay()"},"7230":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-89","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Disabled"},"7231":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-90","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Permit reads at or below stored progress"},"7232":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-91","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"offset_drain_timeout(...)"},"7233":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-92","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Five seconds"},"7234":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-93","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Shutdown budget for automatic stores"},"7235":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-94","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#consumer-configuration","content":"Group membership is managed by the reader. The old auto_join_consumer_group, poll_interval, batch_length and enum-based auto-commit consumer configuration are replaced by the current reader and PollOptions API."},"7236":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-95","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"To store progress after successful processing, use:"},"7237":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-96","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"The default automatic policy stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds, after processing. CommitAfter::Delivery counts a yielded message immediately. CommitAfter::Marked counts checkpoints that the application marks. A handler failure leaves its message uncounted when using processing-based commits."},"7238":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-97","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"CommitPolicy::OnRead submits offsets with the read and can advance them before processing. The consumer refuses this policy with a client-side encryptor because decryption can fail after the store. Use manual or automatic commits instead."},"7239":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-98","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#automatic-commits","content":"Always await consumer.shutdown() before dropping it. Shutdown flushes automatic progress, adjusts on-read progress to delivered messages, and leaves the group. It is not a substitute for successful application processing. See Deferred polling for the shared contract."},"7240":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-99","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#read-errors","content":"ReadError identifies the failed partition and contains its underlying error. Recoverable read failures are retried. A history change, uncertain read or undecodable message can stop a partition. Inspect the error before calling seek(partition_id, strategy) to select a new start. A skip continuation lets an application deliberately move past a record it cannot decode."},"7241":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-100","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#read-errors","content":"An uncertain committing read can require abandon_operation before seeking. Abandon does not undo a store. The source consumer example shows explicit recovery choices."},"7242":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-101","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#idempotent-producer","content":"An idempotent producer prevents duplicate appends when the same producer batch is retried. Enable it with .idempotent() before build() and call init() to obtain a producer ID. The default is disabled, and this mode requires a binary transport."},"7243":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-102","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#idempotent-producer","content":"Each chunk carries a producer ID, epoch and per-partition sequence. This is separate from message IDs and session retry protection. Upgrade all topic readers to compatible 0.10 clients before writing these batches. See Idempotent producers for limits."},"7244":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-103","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"send_binary_request(code, payload) sends a raw request through a binary transport. send_binary_request_to_replica(code, payload, replica_id) targets a replica. Session-management commands remain restricted so a raw call cannot replace the client's session state. HTTP returns FeatureUnavailable for binary calls."},"7245":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk-104","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/high-level-sdk#raw-requests","content":"send_http_request(method, path, body) sends an HTTP request and returns its raw body. Binary transports return FeatureUnavailable for that call. Use typed APIs for commands whose route, ownership or recovery identity the SDK must manage."},"7246":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"page","content":"Intro","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro"},"7247":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"The Rust SDK, the primary and most complete Iggy client, and where its source lives."},"7248":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"Prerequisites"},"7249":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"High-level vs low-level API"},"7250":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Connection string"},"7251":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"Quick example"},"7252":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"The Rust SDK is the primary and most feature-complete client for Iggy. It is available on crates.io and the source code is part of the core repository."},"7253":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"Run from your application crate to add the published SDK:"},"7254":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","content":"Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build both from the same checkout. The Getting Started guide shows the source dependency setup."},"7255":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"The examples below log in with iggy/iggy. For a new local development instance, with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides, use:"},"7256":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#prerequisites","content":"--fresh wipes this replica's local data directory. Environment credentials take precedence over the flag, and bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials. In a cluster, a fresh replica can recover credentials from peers. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."},"7257":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"The SDK provides two layers. The snippets below assume an async Tokio context with a Result return type."},"7258":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"High-level API (recommended) - the easiest way to get started. It handles connection management, auto-batching, consumer group lifecycle, offset commits, retry logic, and reconnection out of the box. Use IggyClient, IggyProducer, and IggyConsumer for the best developer experience."},"7259":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"Low-level API - direct access to individual protocol operations. Useful when you need fine-grained control over every request, or when building custom abstractions on top."},"7260":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#high-level-vs-low-level-api","content":"The high-level API is built on top of the low-level one, so you can always drop down when needed."},"7261":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"The connection string is the simplest way to configure the client. It supports all four transport protocols:"},"7262":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials on connect(). HTTP requires an explicit login and supports ordinary consumers only."},"7263":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Options can be appended as query parameters:"},"7264":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-17","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"Personal Access Tokens are also supported. Any credential without a colon is treated as a PAT. Paste the token exactly as the server returned it, with no prefix:"},"7265":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-18","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#connection-string","content":"See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."},"7266":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-19","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"Partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0. The consumer uses the default consumer identity and requests auto-commit. Its offset can advance before application processing finishes. Use explicit offset storage after successful processing when that distinction matters."},"7267":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro-20","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/intro#quick-example","content":"For the full getting started tutorial, see Getting Started. For the high-level producer/consumer builders, see High-level SDK and Stream Builder."},"7268":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"page","content":"Stream Builder","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Rust"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder"},"7269":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"Configure a Rust producer and consumer by stream and topic names."},"7270":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"Build a producer"},"7271":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"Build a consumer"},"7272":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#share-an-existing-client","content":"Share an existing client"},"7273":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"Defaults and buffering"},"7274":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","content":"The stream builder creates an IggyProducer, an IggyConsumer, or both from configuration objects. It uses the same APIs described in High-level SDK. Stream and topic names identify the resources. The 0.10 API does not take stream or topic IDs in these configuration objects."},"7275":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"This complete example uses a local server with the development credentials. The producer creates missing resources during initialization. Existing topics keep their configuration."},"7276":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"batch_length limits messages in a direct request. linger_time sets the minimum gap between sequential direct sends. It does not buffer separate calls. Use the high-level producer's background mode when you need a buffer."},"7277":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-producer","content":"with_topic_durability(Durability::Persisted) selects the policy when the builder creates a topic. It does not change an existing topic. Client-side encryptor configuration is separate from stream encryption."},"7278":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"The consumer uses a group by default. This example creates the group, reads messages and commits each checkpoint after printing its payload. Add futures-util to use StreamExt."},"7279":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"A standalone consumer uses ConsumerKind::Consumer. Select its partitions with with_partitions. The default configuration selects partition zero. Group consumers use the server assignment instead. ExternalGroup represents externally managed offsets and cannot poll."},"7280":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#build-a-consumer","content":"PollOptions defaults to manual commits. CommitPolicy::Auto(AutoCommit::default()) stores progress after processing every 10,000 messages or five seconds. CommitPolicy::OnRead stores with the read, before application processing. Read the commit contract before selecting a policy."},"7281":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#share-an-existing-client","content":"IggyStreamProducer::build(&client, &producer_config) and IggyStreamConsumer::build(&client, &consumer_config) reuse a connected client. To build both, combine the configuration:"},"7282":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#share-an-existing-client","content":"IggyStream::with_client_from_connection_string also creates the client. Its result contains the client, producer and consumer. Resource creation flags remain explicit on the consumer configuration."},"7283":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"IggyConsumerConfig::default() names test_stream, test_topic and test_consumer. It uses a group, next(), a five-second deferred wait and manual commits. It retries initialization five times at three-second intervals. It does not create a missing stream or topic by default."},"7284":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-15","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"The reader reserves up to 16 MiB and 16000 messages for buffered and in-flight replies. If the poll count is larger, that count is the default message reservation. Configure prefetch_bytes and prefetch_messages to bound read-ahead independently of each fetch."},"7285":{"id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder-16","page_id":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/rust/stream-builder#defaults-and-buffering","content":"The stream-builder examples cover producer, consumer and combined configurations. The source consumer configuration defines each builder field."},"7286":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","type":"page","content":"Examples","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Swift"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples"},"7287":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"Run the Swift producer and consumer from the Apache Iggy source release."},"7288":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"The Swift examples use the SDK from the same checkout. Start the server from the repository root with development credentials and a separate data directory:"},"7289":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"From examples/swift, run the producer and then the consumer:"},"7290":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"The producer creates sample-stream and sample-topic if needed and sends five batches of ten messages. The consumer reads from the first message, stores no offset and exits after five batches. Running it again replays retained messages."},"7291":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/examples","content":"Both programs accept --tcp-server-address, --tls, --tls-ca-file and --tls-domain. The example README provides the development TLS commands."},"7292":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"page","content":"Swift SDK","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","SDK","Swift"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro"},"7293":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-0","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","content":"Use Swift concurrency to produce, read and manage Iggy resources over TCP with optional TLS."},"7294":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-1","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"Requirements and installation"},"7295":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-2","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Connect and create resources"},"7296":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-3","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#readers-and-consumers","content":"Readers and consumers"},"7297":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-4","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#options-metadata-and-recovery","content":"Options, metadata and recovery"},"7298":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-5","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","content":"The Swift SDK 0.2.0 provides an async TCP client with optional TLS, resource management, producers, partition readers and consumers. It routes partition requests to their primaries and reconnects with heartbeat support."},"7299":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-6","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"The SDK requires Swift 6.0 or later. Supported platforms are macOS 13, iOS 16, tvOS 16, watchOS 9, visionOS 1 and Linux. The runnable examples require Swift 6.1 or later."},"7300":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-7","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"The repository root contains the Swift Package Manager manifest and exports the Iggy product. For the upcoming release, use the source release candidate. The package dependency below names that release candidate without relying on an unpublished final tag:"},"7301":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-8","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#requirements-and-installation","content":"Add .product(name: \"Iggy\", package: \"iggy\") to your target dependencies. Build the server and client from the same release source."},"7302":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-9","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Start the development server with iggy/iggy credentials. Run these calls from an async Swift context:"},"7303":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-10","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Use new resource names when running this creation example again. disconnect() closes the current connection and permits a later connect(). shutdown() is final."},"7304":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-11","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#connect-and-create-resources","content":"Connection strings accept iggy:// and iggy+tcp://. TLS uses system roots or the configured CA file. tls_domain overrides the name checked in the certificate. The parser accepts pending-operation limits, but does not implement retained mutation limits."},"7305":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-12","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#readers-and-consumers","content":"client.reader returns batches from explicit partitions or a group assignment. client.consumerGroup yields individual messages. A consumer needs initialize() before iteration:"},"7306":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-13","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro#readers-and-consumers","content":"This fragment uses an existing connected client. Commit after processing. Automatic commits use .auto(.default), which counts processing and stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. .onRead stores with the read. Close readers and shut down consumers to finish offset work and leave groups."},"7307":{"id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro-14","page_id":"/docs/sdk/swift/intro","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/sdk/swift/in