erver wait, up to 1000 messages and a 1 MiB reply. It stores no offsets by default."],[6522,"A consumer that creates and joins a consumer group, stores each offset after handling the yielded message and requesting the next one, and surfaces polling errors:"],[1864,"A contacted HTTP node can forward to the correct primary in one hop. The route and request carry a signed relay proof, and the destination authenticates the bearer independently. Configure shared JWT key material and synchronize clocks. Proofs expire after ten seconds."],[2810,"A container for related topics. For example, a dev stream and a production stream can each have their own orders topic."],[2965,"A continuation carries the next position and the partition history that produced it. Do not advance an offset by the number of returned messages because offsets can contain gaps. A checkpoint stores progress under that history. If the partition is recreated, the server refuses a stale checkpoint."],[4401,"A count or byte trigger, or a full active segment, can flush the committed journal prefix. Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle work can flush earlier. These thresholds are not a maximum data-loss guarantee and do not specify a periodic flush interval. A quiet replicated topic can retain messages in process memory while waiting for a trigger."],[3441,"A cursor identifies a position in this log. Save the returned next value after applying the corresponding records. Cursor text starts with mc1_. Current-state page tokens start with cs1_. Treat both as opaque values and use the SDK parsers."],[5041,"A custom query must be a structured object. In TOML that means nested tables (an inline JSON string won't work):"],[5535,"A custom S3 endpoint restricts the default credential chain. Set AWS_FORCE_CREDENTIAL_LOAD=true in the runtime environment when shared profile or SSO credentials are required. Static keys, web identity, container credentials and instance metadata remain available without that override."],[5302,"A custom_query replaces the entire default query, including its ordering, limit and processed-row filter. Its placeholders are $table, $offset, $limit, $now (RFC3339 UTC) and $now_unix (integer seconds). Substitution is textual, with no automatic quoting or escaping. $offset is empty if neither saved state nor initial_offset supplies it. Keep queries trusted and explicitly preserve ascending tracking-column order. The offset comes from the last returned row with a usable tracking value."],[2332,"A default configuration is embedded in the binary and always merged as the base, so omitted settings inherit its values. A local provider still needs a nonempty connectors.config_dir."],[2077,"A factor of one provides no replica redundancy. A factor of two stops writes when either copy is unavailable. With three copies, the partition can tolerate one unavailable replica. See Durability for the quorum rules and persistence policies."],[4960,"A factor of one stores one copy. Replicated durability can acknowledge it before stable storage. A factor of two requires both replicas to make progress. Three copies tolerate one unavailable replica. See Cluster durability for larger quorum rules."],[4613,"A few of these are deliberate rather than arbitrary:"],[4863,"A fingerprint identifies a key without exposing it. It consists of 16 lowercase hexadecimal characters. It cannot replace a key during recovery."],[2936,"A first program with the low-level Rust SDK: create a stream and topic, send messages, then poll them back."],[2909,"A first program with the Python SDK: create a stream and topic, send messages, then read them back on their own and as a consumer group."],[1655,"A frame's absolute position is derived, not stored: offset = base_offset + offset_delta. The server-stamped time is the flat batch base_timestamp (the delta does not apply to it). Producer time resolves as origin_timestamp + timestamp_delta."],[64,"A fresh standalone server refuses to start without root credentials. Set both IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD before first boot, or use --with-default-root-credentials for a disposable development instance. The server no longer generates a password."],[4475,"A fresh standalone server requires IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD, or the development flag. It never generates a password. A cluster replica without credentials waits for the root account to be replicated. Provision credentials on every replica for first cluster boot."],[4789,"A full active segment also triggers an ordinary flush. These are soft limits: a flush writes whole committed batches, so the actual count or size can overshoot. Required persistence, capacity pressure, and lifecycle operations can flush below the thresholds. The settings do not define a periodic flush interval. The MessagesWriter uses vectored I/O with up to 1024 buffers per syscall, so many buffered batches can land in one write."],[6738,"A full snapshot can exceed the default 64 MiB reply-frame bound, in which case the reply is refused and the connection dropped. Raise MaxResponseFrameSize before requesting large snapshots."],[4417,"A graceful shutdown drains work and forces a final flush of committed messages, regardless of the thresholds. This depends on the shutdown finishing and its I/O succeeding. A container or service termination that expires its grace period and sends SIGKILL is a process crash, not a completed graceful shutdown."],[3506,"A Grafana dashboard for the broker"],[3237,"A group consumer uses its assigned partitions. Assignment changes and primary changes require a refresh before further reads. Use the SDK reader or consumer to manage this state. A raw fetch caller must also supply the route and ownership information required by the protocol."],[6482,"A JSON value, parsing other payload variants as JSON. Invalid JSON is rejected"],[4864,"A key-encryption key (KEK) wraps stream data keys. Rotation changes the wraps and does not re-encrypt messages. The metadata primary replicates these changes. Each stream can hold up to three wraps."],[2541,"A loaded descriptor is used for JSON-to-protobuf encoding. Without one, that direction produces JSON text in Payload::Proto. Protobuf-to-JSON conversion does not decode a custom message descriptor: it parses text as JSON, exposes an Any envelope, or returns metadata containing base64 raw data. Protobuf-to-Avro/FlatBuffers conversion only rewraps the bytes."],[5793,"A lookup failure skips the affected message. It does not fail the batch. Each batch loads its destination tables again. The route field is included in the JSON row, but is ignored by the writer if the target schema has no matching column."],[6489,"A malformed record is skipped while valid records in its chunk are still submitted. A failed chunk does not stop later chunks, and the last error is returned. The sink checks HTTP and SQL statement statuses. Its own processed counter counts records submitted in successful statements, including records ignored by INSERT IGNORE. Runtime counters instead reflect whether the whole callback succeeded. Neither is a count of newly inserted rows."],[6716,"A Message takes an id (Guid or UInt128) and a payload:"],[5501,"A message whose payload type does not match the chosen format is skipped with an error log. The rest of the batch is still sent. A batch with no serializable payloads returns success without an insert. In row_binary mode, a JSON row whose values cannot be converted to the column types fails the whole batch before any insert request, so it does not enter the plugin's insert retry loop."],[4793,"A missing or torn sparse index can be rebuilt from valid log data. An incomplete tail can be truncated, but an interior gap, intact records after damage, or a contradiction with durable history can require recovery refusal. Recovery does not blindly truncate at the first invalid batch."],[917,"A new logical session authenticates through the register handshake: command 