ying the failed batch. It auto-commits while polling. A failed callback can therefore coexist with stored documents, and a successful callback can include documents rejected by Quickwit. Runtime processed counts do not prove successful indexing. The sink provides no end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[6027,"An insert error containing only duplicate-key errors (11000) and no write-concern error is treated as success. This covers all unique indexes, not only _id, and does not compare payload contents. Reusing a message ID in one stream/topic/partition preserves the existing document. A conflict on another unique index can also discard a distinct message while runtime reports it processed. Account for this when choosing collection indexes."],[7241,"An uncertain committing read can require abandon_operation before seeking. Abandon does not undo a store. The source consumer example shows explicit recovery choices."],[132,"An uncertain result is not proof that the operation failed. Keep the same MCP process for retained-operation recovery. Abandoning a request does not make a fresh retry safe from duplicates."],[6367,"And finally, each sink should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID via expected new() method."],[5393,"And finally, each source should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID and optional state via expected new() method."],[6387,"And that's all, enjoy using the sink connector!"],[5416,"And that's all, enjoy using the source connector!"],[1059,"Answer to one request"],[1031,"Any other value is rejected. The option is a placeholder today: the value is validated, persisted, and echoed back, but it does not enable automatic payload compression or decompression. To compress today, do it client-side and tag messages via user headers - see the message headers examples in the Iggy repo."],[2475,"Any Wrapper: The default encoder puts JSON/text in a google.protobuf.StringValu