sible after a crash. Recovery also depends on retention and the configured message and consumer-offset durability. Iggy does not provide an atomic transaction between a consumer offset and an external side effect. Exactly-once application effects require an idempotent operation or an atomic deduplication-and-effect transaction. Unique message IDs alone do not provide that guarantee."],[3203,"Committing fetches use a separate pool with the same configured limits. That pool stores target metadata, not reply bodies, and retains unknown progress across sessions. Exhausting a limit rejects or delays further work according to the operation path. See Client failover."],[3273,"Common operations"],[5087,"Common Options"],[5546,"Common Options"],[5666,"Common Options"],[5810,"Common Options"],[5890,"Common Options"],[6248,"Common Options"],[6408,"Common Options"],[2179,"Common questions about Apache Iggy, including how it compares to Kafka and other message brokers."],[4523,"Compare the existing policy with madvise if allocator page promotion or compaction is relevant. Direct reclaim and compaction can stall allocations. On recent kernels, per-size policies and explicit MADV_COLLAPSE requests also matter, so the top-level switch alone does not describe all behavior. See transparent huge-page controls."],[4983,"Compare your configuration with the 0.10 reference. Do not copy obsolete keys into the new deployment."],[3258,"Compatibility and recovery"],[4594,"compio + custom binary protocol"],[4598,"compio-quic (quinn)"],[4606,"compio-ws"],[4416,"Completed buffered file writes normally remain in Linux's page cache after the server process dies. Messages still in the process's journal do not. Page cache alone does not protect against machine or power failure."],[4089,"Completed deferred reads waiting for their owning shard."],[4934,"Completion policy for explicit consumer-offset stores and deletes, independent of message durability."],[2134,"Component"],[3331,"Component"],[3519,"compose.yml:"],[941,"Compression"],[4753,"Compression"],[829,"Compression codes: 1 = Stored, 2 = Deflated, 3 = Bzip2, 4 = Zstd, 5 = Lzma, 6 = Xz. Snapshot type codes: 1 = FilesystemOverview, 2 = ProcessList, 3 = ResourceUsage, 4 = Test, 5 = ServerLogs, 6 = ServerConfig, 100 = All."],[1690,"Compression is a required positional: none or gzip. It is stored as topic metadata, but compression is not applied. Expiry is a human-readable duration (7d, 1day 12h, unlimited). Omitting expiry at creation selects no expiration."],[6737,"Compression methods: Stored, Deflated, Bzip2, Zstd, Lzma, Xz. Snapshot types: FilesystemOverview, ProcessList, ResourceUsage, Test, ServerLogs, ServerConfig, All."],[4919,"compression_algorithm"],[4958,"compression_algorithm, segment_size, both durability policies, flush thresholds, preallocate_segments, partition_resize_policy and replication_factor are create-only. Select them before creating the topic. A finite size limit must still be at least the segment size."],[2829,"Concepts"],[2826,"Concepts explains each of these in more detail."],[1161,"Concrete code for NonReplicated, zero otherwise."],[2626,"Concurrent Iggy fetch clients"],[3661,"Config file: the path in IGGY_CONFIG_PATH, or core/server/config.toml resolved against the current working directory. A missing file is only a warning. The server continues on embedded defaults."],[6045,"Configurable Payload Storage: Store the payload as raw bytes, native JSONB, or text"],[2669,"Configurable server features (e.g. caching and transport protocols), plus per-topic segment size, durability and flush thresholds"],[83,"Configuration"],[2213,"Configuration"],[2492,"Configuration"],[3617,"Configuration"],[4998,"Configuration"],[5054,"Configuration"],[5086,"Configuration"],[5171,"Configuration"],[5189,"Configuration"],[5322,"Configuration"],[5353,"Configuration"],[5420,"Configuration"],[5437,"Configuration"],[5507,"Configuration"],[5545,"Configuration"],[5614,"Configuration"],[5665,"Configuration"],[5