e server's default HTTP port is 3000, TCP is 8090)"],[5081,"HTTP 200 confirms an in-memory enqueue. It does not confirm an Iggy write or durable storage. Process crashes and shutdown can lose accepted events. Sender retries and replayed batches can also create duplicates."],[5735,"HTTP 429 Retry-After: on unsuccessful responses, the header is logged but not honored. Retry timing always uses the computed backoff."],[6193,"HTTP 429, 5xx and network failures can retry. Calculated waits use exponential backoff with jitter and the configured cap. A valid Retry-After on 429 replaces that wait. Other HTTP statuses stop that request's retry loop. Readiness has its separate attempt budget. There is no circuit breaker or sink deduplication key, so retrying an accepted request can produce duplicates. max_retries = 1 disables the sink's HTTP retry loop."],[5883,"HTTP 429, all 5xx responses, and network errors are retried. max_retries = 3 allows the initial attempt plus two retries. 0 and 1 both allow one attempt. Backoff starts at retry_delay, doubles, adds ±20% jitter, and is capped by retry_max_delay. An integer-seconds Retry-After on a 429 overrides that cap. HTTP-date values are ignored. Startup health checks use a separate attempt budget and cap, retrying any failed check."],[2321,"HTTP API"],[4554,"HTTP API requests"],[2079,"HTTP can forward a request to the correct primary in one hop. It needs the cluster authentication and shared JWT configuration described in Security. Keep clocks synchronized because forwarding proofs expire after ten seconds."],[2318,"HTTP Configuration Provider"],[1849,"HTTP forwarding"],[4619,"HTTP is the most accessible protocol but has the highest overhead due to JSON serialization and the stateless nature of HTTP (no persistent connections for consumer groups). You can find request examples in the server.http file."],[5694,"HTTP method: GET, HEAD, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE"],[3036,"HTTP options"],[4009,"HTTP or HTTPS endpoint. Required when enabled and omitted from the effective configuration output."],[3212,"HTTP reads"],[5483,"HTTP request timeout in seconds"],[5652,"HTTP request timeout in seconds, from connection through response body. 0 is treated as 1"],[7156,"HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Consumer groups require a binary transport. Use an ordinary consumer with an explicit partition over HTTP."],[3738,"HTTP sessions hold live server-side session state (they count against metadata.clients_table_max). Consumer group management (create, get, delete) is available over HTTP. Group membership (join, leave) is not and needs a stateful transport."],[5663,"HTTP Sink"],[5052,"HTTP Source"],[5738,"HTTP success is determined by the response status. The sink does not inspect a successful response body for per-item failures."],[3827,"HTTP TLS is file-based only. There's no self_signed option for this listener. Both files must exist when enabled."],[2087,"HTTP tokens across the cluster"],[4957,"HTTP updates use a flat JSON object, not a nested options object:"],[4552,"http_requests_total"],[1809,"http://localhost:3000"],[6149,"HTTP(S) base URL with a host. Path prefixes/trailing slashes work, query strings and fragments are rejected"],[4487,"Huge pages and mimalloc"],[4506,"Huge pages can reduce translation overhead, but they consume memory differently from ordinary pages. Measure both throughput and tail latency. Iggy's default server build uses mimalloc. Check the startup allocator message, since builds that disable mimalloc do not use its environment options."],[5752,"Iceberg Sink"],[800,"ICO1 version 9, readers accept 8 and 9"],[794,"ICT5"],[1626,"id"],[2876,"id"],[6121,"id stores the full unsigned 128-bit message ID as a decimal, without stream, topic, partition or offset in the key. Offset and checksum values are cast to signed 64-bit integers, and partition IDs to signed 32-bit integers. Values above those signed ranges appear negative. Message and origin timestamps are interpreted as Unix microseconds. Zero becomes the Unix epoch. A timestamp outside the date library's range falls back to the current time. created_at is generated by PostgreSQL when inserting."],[7150,"Idempotent producer"],[3253,"Idempotent Producers"],[933,"Identifier"],[6544,"Identifiers"],[949,"Identifies a consumer or a consumer group in message and offset commands:"],[3681,"Identify this node within cluster.nodes. Required when cluster.enabled = true. The value must match exactly one replica_id in the roster."],[3882,"Idle time before a connection is closed. \"0 s\" disables (not recommended)."],[3126,"Idle timeout in milliseconds. 0 leaves Quinn's 30000 ms default"],[4712,"If a stream has no entry in the user's stream permissions, only global permissions apply to it. The same holds for topics within a stream."],[3019,"If every message on a topic has the same shape, send the encoded value as the payload and decode it the same way on the other side. Getting started does this with plain strings."],[5607,"If max_filter_ratio permits malformed rows, Doris can accept a load while dropping those rows. The plugin logs a warning when Doris reports them. The where option deliberately excludes rows before loading."],[2371,"If not customized, the HTTP provider uses the following RESTful URL patterns:"],[1388,"If status is nonzero, this reply reports a pre-commit rejection. It does not settle an earlier uncertain attempt. The body is empty."],[4883,"If the server exits with io_uring was denied locked memory, the container's locked-memory limit is too low for it. Raise it, as the --ulimit memlock=-1:-1 flag does in Docker & Helm."],[3557,"If the server is compiled without the iggy-web feature and web_ui = true, a warning is logged but the server continues to run normally."],[2804,"If you already know Apache Kafka, most of Apache Iggy will feel familiar. This page maps the Kafka terms you know to Iggy's and lists what works differently. For when to choose one or the other, see Apache Iggy or Apache Kafka."],[4457,"If you compile the source code in release mode, linking takes longer because LTO is enabled in the [profile.release] section of the workspace Cargo.toml."],[5788,"If you don't know the names of the Iceberg tables you want to route data to in advance, you can use the dynamic routing feature. Insert a top-level field in your JSON messages with the name of the Iceberg table the message should be routed to. The Iggy connector will parse this field at runtime and route the message to the correct table."],[5803,"If your source emits envelope-wrapped JSON, use the unwrap_envelope transform to extract the inner data field before it reaches the sink:"],[2355,"If-Match, If-None-Match, Idempotency-Key and Content-Type are managed by the runtime and cannot be overridden in request_headers."],[2616,"iggy"],[2696,"iggy"],[2702,"iggy"],[3284,"iggy"],[3294,"iggy"],[2806,"Iggy"],[3324,"Iggy"],[1777,"Iggy CLI"],[2721,"Iggy CLI can be installed with cargo install iggy-cli --version 0.15.0 --locked and then simply accessed by typing iggy in your terminal. It supports named connection contexts (profiles) for managing multiple server connections, shell completions for bash/zsh/fish/elvish/powershell, and session-based login through platform credential stores on Linux, macOS and Windows when the default login-session feature is enabled."],[3588,"Iggy comes with a built-in benchmarking tool, iggy-bench. It's written in Rust and uses the tokio runtime for asynchronous I/O, mimicking the example client applications, so you can use it to estimate the performance of the server in your environment. It is part of the core repository and lives in the core/bench directory."],[4809,"Iggy encrypts message payloads and user headers with a separate AES-256-GCM-SIV key per encrypted stream. They are encrypted before storage and decrypted for polling. Metadata journals, metadata snapshots, and structural record headers remain unencrypted. The key must decode from base64 to 32 bytes. See Security."],[2827,"Iggy has its own protocol, and the Kafka gateway serves Kafka producers, consumers and supported admin APIs. Run it as a separate service with the Iggy bridge enabled. The gateway has a defined API subset and does not support transactions. Native applications can use an Iggy SDK or the HTTP API."],[4877,"Iggy has no embedded, in-process test server. The server ships as a binary, not a library, so a test suite can't start it in-process. To test against a real server, start the published Docker image when your tests start and stop it when they finish. The examples below use each language's testcontainers library to do that."],[2122,"Iggy hasn't reached a 1.0 production release yet. The cluster protocol and configuration described here can still change between pre-release versions."],[4798,"Iggy includes a custom memory pool to reuse buffers on the hot path. The pool has 28 buckets with buffer sizes from 4 KiB up to 512 MiB (non-uniform spacing, denser around common message sizes, with sizes above 2 MiB rounded to hugepage-friendly steps). Components request a buffer from the appropriate bucket and return it when done."],[2653,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming log built from the ground up using low-level I/O with thread-per-core shared nothing architecture, io_uring and compio for maximum speed and efficiency."],[2191,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform that stores messages in an append-only log format, similar to Kafka. However, Iggy is designed for high performance and low latency using io_uring and a thread-per-core architecture. Iggy uses its own binary protocol rather than the Kafka protocol. Clients can use an Iggy SDK, implement the documented binary protocol, or use the HTTP API."],[2650,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform written in Rust, supporting QUIC, TCP, WebSocket (custom binary specification) and HTTP (regular REST API) transport protocols, capable of processing millions of messages per second at ultra-low latency."],[2841,"Iggy is a persistent message streaming platform: messages are stored in a form of an append-only log. You can create multiple streams, consisting of topics, which might have one or more partitions assigned, e.g. to divide consumption among independent consumers. Multi-node replication provides redundancy. You can think of Iggy as an alternative to Kafka or RabbitMQ streams."],[31,"Iggy is more than just a server. The project includes a full ecosystem of tools:"],[2846,"Iggy is the latter, a message streaming platform."],[1833,"iggy login authenticates once and stores a session token in the platform's secure credential store: Secret Service on Linux and the BSDs, Keychain on macOS, Credential Manager on Windows. Subsequent commands use the cached token automatically. This requires the default login-session feature and an available credential-store backend. Linux and BSD need a running Secret Service provider on the D-Bus session."],[1831,"iggy login tries the supplied credentials before a cached session, so it can replace an expired login. Other commands fail if their cached token is rejected. They do not retry with the next credential source in the same invocation."],[6475,"Iggy message IDs, partition IDs, offsets, timestamps and checksums are stored as strings. Timestamps retain Iggy's microsecond values, not SurrealDB datetime values. Record IDs encode stream/topic UTF-8 bytes as hex, then append partition, offset and the 32-digit hexadecimal message ID. Changing the payload or transforms does not change that identity. Changing any identity component creates a different key."],[2201,"Iggy migrated from Tokio to a thread-per-core architecture with compio (which uses io_uring on Linux) starting with version 0.6.0. The published v0.5.0 versus v0.7.0 comparison reports 92% lower P9999 latency for its 16-producer, 16-stream, 40-million-message workload, and 18% higher throughput for the corresponding fsync workload. These are historical workload results, not a guarantee for 0.10.0. You can read the full story in the thread-per-core io_uring blog post."],[2726,"Iggy provides a highly performant and modular runtime for statically typed, yet dynamically loaded connectors. You can ingest data from external sources and push the data to Iggy streams, or fetch data from Iggy streams and forward it to external systems. Create your own Rust plugins by simply implementing either the Source or Sink trait and build custom pipelines for the data processing."],[2200,"Iggy provides a Model Context Protocol (MCP) server with 40+ tools for LLM integration, connectors for piping data to/from external systems (e.g. PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, S3, Delta Lake, Apache Iceberg, Quickwit), Prometheus metrics, and OpenTelemetry traces/logs in the connectors runtime. Broker OpenTelemetry export has runtime limitations described in configuration. The HTTP API works with REST clients."],[2651,"Iggy provides exceptionally high throughput and performance while utilizing minimal computing resources."],[4670,"Iggy provides granular permissions at three levels:"],[4661,"Iggy provides multiple layers of security covering authentication, authorization, transport encryption, and data encryption at rest."],[3277,"Iggy provides official client SDKs in multiple languages. The Rust SDK is the most feature-complete, offering both a high-level API (with auto-batching, consumer groups, offset management, retry logic) and a low-level API for direct protocol operations."],[2194,"Iggy runs as a single server process without an external coordination service. Native library requirements depend on the build. The provided Linux images include hwloc and udev libraries. The Linux shard runtime requires io_uring flags introduced in kernel 5.19. The server starts with around 20 MB of RAM. The default 4 GiB memory-pool setting is a budget, with buffers allocated on demand and fallback allocations outside the pool."],[4443,"Iggy sends a JSON POST:"],[4455,"Iggy server is the most important part of the system as it's responsible for handling all the incoming connections, managing the data and providing the API for the clients. The server is written in Rust. It uses io_uring on Linux and a polling backend on macOS."],[1834,"iggy session status only checks whether a token exists in the local keyring. An expired token still reports as active. On TCP, QUIC and WebSocket, run iggy me to verify the session against the server. HTTP does not support me. Use an authenticated command such as iggy stream list with the required permissions."],[1521,"Iggy speaks one binary protocol over its three binary transports: TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket. Commands, responses, data models, and status codes are the same on all of them. The HTTP transport is separate: it exposes JSON REST endpoints for a subset of these operations, listed in server.http."],[2152,"Iggy splits replication by namespace:"],[4587,"Iggy supports four transport protocols simultaneously, each optimized for different use cases. All stateful protocols (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket) use the same custom binary wire protocol, while HTTP provides a standard REST API."],[4662,"Iggy supports local credentials and personal access tokens. HTTP also accepts configured JWT issuers. An optional external authentication service handles unknown local usernames."],[2199,"Iggy supports TLS on all transport protocols, Argon2id password hashing, granular per-stream/per-topic permissions, Personal Access Tokens for programmatic access, and optional AES-256-GCM-SIV encryption of stored message payloads. For the HTTP API, JWT tokens are used for session management. See the Security documentation for details."],[2792,"Iggy uses a custom memory pool with 28 buckets holding buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB. The default pool size is 4 GiB with up to 8192 buffers per bucket. Buffers are allocated on demand and reused. Requests that exceed the pool budget can allocate outside it. Frozen buffers can be shared between components without copying their contents. The pool is page-aligned (4096-byte multiples) and requires a minimum of 512 MiB."],[11,"Iggy uses a hierarchical model: streams contain topics, topics contain partitions, and partitions store messages in segments on disk. This gives you flexible multi-tenancy, parallel processing, and efficient storage all in one."],[2760,"Iggy uses a thread-per-core shared nothing architecture combined with io_uring for maximum performance. This design has been proven by systems like ScyllaDB and Redpanda, both built on the Seastar framework."],[2780,"Iggy uses compio as its async runtime, which provides a driver-disaggregated architecture on top of io_uring (Linux) and IOCP (Windows). Each shard gets its own compio executor configured with:"],[4497,"Iggy uses io_uring. The kernel must implement the operations required by the server build, and the service or container must be allowed to use them. The server's startup diagnostics identify failed ring creation and unsupported operations. Where the kernel exposes them, inspect:"],[2014,"Iggy uses Viewstamped Replication to commit partition operations. The topic's durability and consumer_offset_durability options select the storage guarantee required for message production and explicit offset changes, respectively. Both default independently to replicated. Both continue to write data to disk."],[3538,"Iggy Web UI"],[3547,"Iggy Web UI provides a comprehensive dashboard for Iggy server. It allows you to monitor the server's health, streams, topics, browse the messages, users and more. The dashboard is built with SvelteKit and TypeScript and is available as open-source as well as the Docker image on Docker Hub."],[5947,"iggy_checksum and iggy_message_id are strings. Offsets and message timestamps remain numbers. Message timestamps are microseconds, while iggy_ingested_at uses current wall-clock milliseconds. Disabling metadata leaves payload fields intact and still injects a missing primary key."],[2299,"iggy_connector_errors_total - errors encountered."],[2296,"iggy_connector_messages_consumed_total - messages consumed from Iggy (sink)."],[2298,"iggy_connector_messages_filtered_total - messages intentionally dropped by transforms returning Ok(None), excluding transform errors."],[2297,"iggy_connector_messages_processed_total - messages passed to the sink plugin in batches that returned success. A failed batch adds an error and no processed messages. The runtime cannot infer partial delivery inside the plugin."],[2294,"iggy_connector_messages_produced_total - messages received from a source plugin's poll."],[2295,"iggy_connector_messages_sent_total - messages sent to Iggy (source)."],[5401,"iggy_connector_sdk"],[6374,"iggy_connector_sdk"],[2292,"iggy_connectors_sinks_running - sinks in the Running status."],[2291,"iggy_connectors_sinks_total - configured sink connectors."],[2290,"iggy_connectors_sources_running - sources in the Running status."],[2289,"iggy_connectors_sources_total - configured source connectors."],[5917,"iggy_id"],[5721,"iggy_id is a 32-character lowercase hex string (no dashes)."],[2591,"IGGY_KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST"],[2600,"IGGY_KAFKA_ADVERTISED_LISTENERS"],[2594,"IGGY_KAFKA_ADVERTISED_PORT"],[2588,"IGGY_KAFKA_BIND_ADDR"],[2609,"IGGY_KAFKA_BRIDGE_ENABLED"],[2624,"IGGY_KAFKA_FETCH_SLOTS"],[2630,"IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS"],[2612,"IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_ADDR"],[2618,"IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_PASSWORD"],[2621,"IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_STREAM"],[2615,"IGGY_KAFKA_IGGY_USERNAME"],[2597,"IGGY_KAFKA_LISTENERS"],[2603,"IGGY_KAFKA_MAX_CONNECTIONS"],[2606,"IGGY_KAFKA_MAX_FRAME_SIZE"],[2627,"IGGY_KAFKA_PRODUCE_SESSIONS"],[5851,"iggy_messages"],[4880,"IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD. A fresh standalone server requires them or the explicit development flag."],[2784,"IGGY_SHARD_RUNTIME_CAPACITY and IGGY_SHARD_EVENT_INTERVAL override the two numeric defaults. On macOS, compio uses its polling driver and a blocking pool for file I/O."],[2918,"IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS and IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS are needed because the server binds 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default, which the published port can't reach. The Rust tutorial's Starting the Iggy server section explains each flag."],[4881,"IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090 and IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost. By default the server listens on 127.0.0.1 inside the container, which a mapped port can't reach. Listening on all addresses also needs an advertised address, or the server won't start. Apache Iggy 0.10.0 skips memory binding on a single-NUMA-node topology. The older Docker Desktop workaround is no longer required for that case. Use IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when you explicitly want CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding."],[5722,"iggy_timestamp is the message timestamp in Unix epoch microseconds. include_checksum and include_origin_timestamp add the corresponding metadata fields. Both default to false."],[3591,"iggy-bench doesn't start a server. It only connects, and fails with a connection error when nothing is listening. For a disposable development server with an empty data directory, use the credentials expected by the benchmark defaults:"],[2717,"iggy-cpp"],[3314,"iggy-cpp"],[2711,"iggy-go"],[3304,"iggy-go"],[2004,"iggy-server --fresh deletes the configured data directory before boot. In cluster mode this wipes that replica only: it rejoins the cluster and refills its state by transfer from the other replicas. Wiping a quorum of replicas at the same time destroys committed data, and a service unit that always passes --fresh re-transfers the whole dataset on every restart."],[3673,"iggy-server accepts these four startup options, plus --help (-h) and --version (-V):"],[6506,"iggy::ffi::from_connection_string() accepts these strings. new_connection() instead takes an IggyClientConfig: {} selects TCP at 127.0.0.1:8090 without automatic login, and its server_address field overrides that address. Binary clients authenticate from their connection string during connect(). HTTP requires an explicit login_user() call. See connection strings for the supported options and credential syntax."],[3049,"iggy://"],[3372,"iggy://"],[2024,"Iggy's replication quorum is not a strict majority for every group size:"],[4756,"Iggy's storage engine is built around the concept of a segmented append-only log. Message data follows a hierarchy: System -> Streams -> Topics -> Partitions -> Segments. This page covers how data is stored, indexed, flushed, recovered, and cleaned up on disk."],[4535,"Iggy's topic durability and consumer_offset_durability independently default to replicated. persisted changes completion guarantees, and huge pages or sysctl settings do not replace it. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous. Record both policies, replication-group size, payload and batch sizes, warmup, CPU allocation, and the exact host changes with each benchmark result."],[3058,"iggy+http://"],[3378,"iggy+http://"],[3055,"iggy+quic://"],[3376,"iggy+quic://"],[3052,"iggy+tcp://"],[3374,"iggy+tcp://"],[3061,"iggy+ws://"],[3380,"iggy+ws://"],[6537,"IggyClientConfigurator"],[6897,"IggyClientConfigurator also exposes TCP socket buffers, TLS, heartbeat, response-frame limits, reconnection with exponential backoff (on by default), auto-login, and message encryption. HTTP clients require explicit login. The TCP connection settings are not applied to HTTP. See Client configuration for the full reference."],[6573,"IggyClientConfigurator exposes the full set of connection options - buffer sizes, TLS, heartbeat, automatic reconnection with exponential backoff, and auto-login:"],[6747,"IggyConsumer"],[6869,"IggyConsumerBuilder<T> accepts an IDeserializer<T> and exposes ReceiveDeserializedAsync. Keep the typed builder in a variable before configuring it because inherited fluent methods return the base builder type. Use WithPollOptions to select the commit policy, and inspect each message status before accepting its progress."],[7283,"IggyConsumerConfig::default() names test_stream, test_topic and test_consumer. It uses a group, next(), a five-second deferred wait and manual commits. It retries initialization five times at three-second intervals. It does not create a missing stream or topic by default."],[6511,"IggyException::OperationId() identifies retained uncertain operations. ResolveOperation retries a mutation with its retained identity, without returning the original payload. AbandonOperation frees local state without undoing the result. A lost committing poll reports fetch_progress_uncertain and blocks partition progress until abandonment."],[2749,"IggyNamespace"],[7141,"IggyProducer and IggyConsumer: batching, consumer groups and offset commits without the boilerplate."],[7152,"IggyProducer batches writes and IggyConsumer reads messages through a partition reader. Both share the client's routing, session and recovery behavior. The consumer manages group membership and tracks checkpoints. Select when it stores those checkpoints through PollOptions."],[6743,"IggyPublisher"],[6742,"IggyPublisher and IggyConsumer for C#, with background batching, retries and automatic offset commits."],[7282,"IggyStream::with_client_from_connection_string also creates the client. Its result contains the client, producer and consumer. Resource creation flags remain explicit on the consumer configuration."],[7281,"IggyStreamProducer::build(&client, &producer_config) and IggyStreamConsumer::build(&client, &consumer_config) reuse a connected client. To build both, combine the configuration:"],[1156,"Ignored on the wire. The server stamps the authenticated user itself. Send zero."],[6616,"ILoggerFactory"],[4336,"Image"],[4348,"Images tagged latest are based on stable releases. Images tagged edge are built from the latest master branch."],[6620,"IMessageEncryptor?"],[4590,"Implementation"],[7201,"Import futures_util::StreamExt to use next(). The mutable consumer drives reads and retries when polled. A checkpoint keeps the partition history and ownership that delivered the message. Committing it after recreation or an ownership change is refused. Process messages before committing their checkpoints."],[4479,"In another terminal at the repository root:"],[3801,"In cluster mode the secret has an extra role: a configured secret, identical on every node, makes bearer tokens valid cluster-wide and activates follower-to-primary HTTP forwarding. When both JWT secrets are empty and cluster.auth is enabled, the JWT key is derived from the cluster PSK. Explicit JWT secrets take precedence. Without either, tokens are node-local and forwarding stays disabled."],[3739,"In cluster mode, HTTP requests use the metadata or partition primary as appropriate. A contacted node can forward in one hop when shared JWT key material enables signed relay proofs. Partition polls also use the partition route. See Client failover."],[4647,"In cluster mode, listener ports come exclusively from each node's cluster.nodes ports entry, including the tcp_replica port for the consensus plane, and nodes can advertise different client-facing addresses per client network. Replica-to-replica traffic can be authenticated with a pre-shared key and wrapped in TLS 1.3. See Clustering configuration and Clustering security."],[4739,"In cluster mode, replica-to-replica traffic can require an authenticated handshake based on a pre-shared key (with a documented rolling rotation procedure) and can be wrapped in TLS 1.3. With cluster.auth enabled and no JWT secrets configured, the PSK also becomes the cluster-wide JWT key source. See Clustering security."],[1992,"In cluster mode:"],[3553,"In embedded mode neither applies: the UI is served from the same origin as the HTTP API and uses relative URLs."],[5725,"In JSON batch modes, raw, FlatBuffer, Protobuf and Avro payload variants are base64-encoded with an explicit marker:"],[5429,"In mapped mode, each payload must be a JSON object. Top-level fields map to table columns by name. Unknown fields are ignored. Required columns without defaults must have non-null values. Missing or null repeated columns become empty arrays."],[4477,"In order to see the detailed logs from the server, run it with RUST_LOG=trace environment variable."],[5309,"In polling mode, an existing payload_column bypasses the envelope. Use bytea or raw for a BYTEA column with schema = \"raw\", text for a text column with schema = \"text\", and json_direct, jsonb or jsonb_direct for JSON/JSONB with schema = \"json\". A null BYTEA/text payload becomes empty bytes, which the Iggy message builder rejects. The entire batch is Nacked. Use non-null, non-empty BYTEA/text payloads. A null JSON/JSONB payload becomes JSON null and can be delivered."],[5430,"In raw mode, payload_column defaults to payload. A JSON column requires valid JSON. A STRING column requires text or UTF-8 bytes. A BYTES column accepts raw bytes from any payload type. Other required columns must have defaults."],[5499,"In row_binary mode the schema is captured once at startup and the insert stream is positional. An ALTER TABLE on the target while the connector runs can silently corrupt inserted rows, so restart the connector after any schema change. The self-describing json_each_row format (including string mode with string_format = \"json_each_row\") maps values by field name, but changed column names, types, or constraints can still make inserts fail. string passthrough with csv or tsv sends plain positional ClickHouse formats mapped by the current table column order."],[3200,"In Rust, construct clients inside a Tokio runtime, which QUIC needs even before connect(). The following snippet assumes an async function returning Result<_, IggyError> and the matching Rust SDK setup."],[4952,"In the CLI, both durability policies have named flags. --set is repeatable and covers other storage options, such as segment_size. compression_algorithm, message_expiry, and max_topic_size also have first-class parameters. Run iggy options topic to discover what the server accepts."],[7126,"In the core repository, you can find the following examples using the Rust SDK."],[2904,"In the SDK message representation, the header is followed by payload bytes and then optional user-header bytes."],[2258,"In the terminal that will run the connectors, set the runtime configuration path:"],[4138,"In-memory index of the metadata WAL, in slots. Larger buys more headroom between forced checkpoints at the cost of memory and bigger WAL rewrites. Boot enforces journal_slots >= 4 x max(64, prepare_queue_depth)."],[4081,"inbox_capacity"],[555,"incarnation"],[570,"incarnation"],[6305,"Include available headers in JSON output, independently of metadata"],[5998,"Include client-provided timestamp"],[5994,"Include message checksum"],[6083,"Include message checksum"],[1737,"Include message headers in the output"],[6079,"Include offset, timestamp, stream, topic and partition columns"],[5990,"Include offset, timestamp, stream, topic and partition metadata"],[6087,"Include original message timestamp"],[6228,"Include producer time"],[6222,"Include stream, topic, partition, offset, timestamp and schema"],[5868,"Include stream/topic/partition metadata as tags or fields. Independent of checksum and origin timestamp"],[6301,"Include stream/topic/partition/offset/timestamp in JSON output"],[6225,"Include the Iggy checksum"],[5991,"include_checksum"],[6080,"include_checksum"],[6223,"include_checksum"],[6302,"include_headers"],[2533,"include_matching: true (default) keeps the matching fields and drops the rest. false drops the matching fields and keeps the rest."],[5147,"include_metadata"],[5249,"include_metadata"],[5699,"include_metadata"],[5865,"include_metadata"],[5927,"include_metadata"],[5987,"include_metadata"],[6076,"include_metadata"],[6220,"include_metadata"],[6298,"include_metadata"],[6467,"include_metadata"],[5872,"include_metadata = false omits those three values. It does not disable the independently co