nverted to Arrow using the target table schema, written to Parquet, then appended in an Iceberg transaction per destination table. Partitioned tables split a batch by the default partition spec. Writers can roll over into multiple files, so there is no fixed one-file-per-batch guarantee for either partitioned or unpartitioned tables."],[1960,"Rules and caveats:"],[1972,"Run a three-node cluster"],[3507,"Run an Iggy server with Prometheus and Grafana in Docker Compose, and watch the broker on a dashboard."],[81,"Run and configure the Apache Iggy MCP server, including transport, Iggy credentials and TLS."],[6912,"Run both from the examples/go directory:"],[2933,"Run consumer_group.py again and it prints every message from offset 0, because it asks for PollingStrategy.First(). That keeps the example repeatable. A long-running group consumer would leave the polling strategy at its default and keep auto_commit on, so it carries on from the group's stored offset instead."],[2932,"Run consumer.py again and it carries on from its stored offset, printing only the new messages rather than the first ten again."],[6075,"Run CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS. Existing tables are not migrated"],[2257,"Run docker compose -f examples/rust/src/sink-data-producer/docker-compose.yml up -d, which will start the Quickwit server to be used by an example sink connector. At this point, you can access the Quickwit UI at http://localhost:7280 - check this dashboard again later on, after the events index will be created."],[6961,"Run each example from the examples/java directory with its Gradle task. Run the producer before its consumer. The ordinary consumers keep their cursor in memory and start from offset 0 or the earliest retained message on each run. The multi-tenant consumer uses consumer groups and server-managed offsets."],[7253,"Run from your application crate to add the published SDK:"],[2335,"Run iggy-connectors --list-config-env-vars to print the supported names and templates, then exit