icit aws_s3_access_key and aws_s3_secret_key are optional and must be supplied together. Without them, credentials come from the supported provider chain. aws_s3_endpoint_url is optional, for S3-compatible stores like MinIO. aws_s3_allow_http is optional and defaults to false."],[4122,"Safety tick for the partition reconciliation loop, which also wakes on every metadata commit."],[3932,"Same load-or-generate behavior as [tcp.tls]: an existing PEM pair wins, otherwise an ephemeral loopback certificate is generated."],[4637,"Same load-or-generate rule as TCP."],[4723,"Same load-or-generate rule as TCP."],[6766,"Same, authenticating with a personal access token instead of a username and password"],[5355,"Sample implementation"],[6331,"Sample implementation"],[3494,"Save as order_service.py."],[3490,"Save as orders_producer.py."],[4889,"Save it as iggy.test.mjs and run it with node --test."],[5446,"Save the configuration below in the runtime's connector directory. Set the endpoint, credentials, database, and table for your deployment."],[4360,"Save the excerpt as my-values.yaml, customize it for your environment, and deploy with:"],[7037,"Save the following snippets as producer.mjs and consumer.mjs in the project where you installed the SDK. Run node producer.mjs before node consumer.mjs. A consumer group sample follows them."],[2960,"Save the producer in src/bin/producer.rs and the consumer in src/bin/consumer.rs. The programs use resource names, so they do not depend on the IDs assigned to other streams."],[1986,"Save this as /tmp/iggy-vsr.toml:"],[2477,"Save this as connector-sdk-example/Cargo.toml. The path dependency uses the SDK from the same checkout as the runtime:"],[2928,"Save this as consumer_group.py:"],[2924,"Save this as consumer.py:"],[2921,"Save this as producer.py:"],[2478,"Save this as schemas/user.proto:"],[5772,"Save this complete connector file in the runtime's connector directory:"],[5681,"Save this configuration in the runtime's connector directory:"],[5006,"Save this connector entry in the runtime's connector directory. Start the runtime from the checkout root so the plugin path resolves."],[5961,"Save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory and replace the MongoDB URI and destination names for your deployment. Startup parses the URI and pings the target database. max_pool_size, when set, overrides the URI's pool setting. Other connection options come from the MongoDB driver and URI."],[5897,"Save this connector file in the runtime's connector directory. Replace the URL and API key with your Meilisearch endpoint and credentials. The key needs permission to inspect the index, submit documents and read tasks, plus create the index when needed. Startup checks health, checks the index, and waits for index creation to succeed even when wait_for_tasks = false. Set primary_key to match an existing index. A mismatch only produces a startup warning. The connector does not change the index key, and document tasks can fail."],[5206,"Save this entry in the runtime's connector directory and start the runtime from the checkout root:"],[2353,"Save uses PUT by default, or POST/PATCH, with Content-Type: application/octet-stream. Atomically enforce If-Match for a known ETag or If-None-Match: * for a new checkpoint. Successful 200, 201 or 204 responses must include the new strong ETag."],[3501,"Scale in. Press Ctrl-C in the svc-a terminal. It prints [svc-a] shutting down and exits. Run the producer again. svc-b now owns every partition and receives all 15 orders (output trimmed):"],[3499,"Scale out. In a third terminal start a second instance, then run the producer again:"],[2545,"schema_path, include_paths, and preserve_unknown_fields have no effect. root_table_name only labels decoding metadata. verify_buffers checks the minimum buffer length (four bytes), not a schema-aware verifier. buffer_size_hint sets the initial encoder capacity. pretty_json, include_metadata, and strict_mode have no effect."],[2461,"schema_path: Path to the .proto file containing message definitions"],[3046,"Scheme"],[3370,"Scheme"],[898,"Scope 1 selects a topic with stream and topic identifiers. Scope 2 selects a stream. Scope 3 selects a user. Empty patches, duplicate keys and keys that are both set and reset are rejected. The catalog defines update and reset permissions for each key."],[832,"Scope: 1 = topic, 2 = stream, 3 = user. Returns the option catalog for the scope: keys, value kinds, defaults, and descriptions. See Options block."],[4655,"Scoped permissions"],[4537,"Scrape the server's Prometheus metrics: the token the endpoint needs, what it exports, and which metrics are worth alerting on."],[2423,"SDK"],[3455,"SDK"],[4049,"SDK clients maintain heartbeats when connected. The Java blocking client delegates to the async TCP implementation and also uses its heartbeat. Keep the client heartbeat interval below the server session expiry threshold. Call the SDK connection method before using a long-lived consumer, and close it through the SDK lifecycle API."],[3428,"SDK methods"],[2439,"SDK provides the commonly used structs and traits such as Sink and Source, along with the sink_connector and source_connector macros to be used when developing connectors."],[36,"SDKs - client libraries for 9 languages (Rust, Python, Java, Go, Node.js, C#, C++, PHP, Swift), most with runnable examples."],[3423,"SDKs also expose metadata changes and raw requests to a selected replica. Recovery APIs differ. Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution or pending mutation limits. It has abandonment for uncertain committing reads. Read each language guide before implementing retries after an uncertain write."],[4953,"SDKs expose typed durability values in their topic creation options. In Rust, set TopicCreateOptions::durability and TopicCreateOptions::consumer_offset_durability to Durability::Replicated or Durability::Persisted. The HTTP API takes \"durability\" and \"consumer_offset_durability\" as string values in the create body's options map."],[4646,"SDKs maintain heartbeats after connection. The Java blocking client delegates to the async TCP client and uses its heartbeat. Reconnect binds a retained session before creating a new one. See Client failover."],[5046,"Search and JSON-decoding failures, timed_out: true, and a nonzero _shards.failed count are returned as poll errors without advancing the watermark. The next polling cycle retries from the previous watermark. Poll errors are logged by the SDK. They do not increment the runtime's forwarding-error counter or change the runtime connector status. There is no per-query retry loop or configured HTTP request timeout. The source stages a candidate watermark and applies it only after runtime Ack. Nack keeps the previous watermark so the next search can fetch the rejected records again. Fetched-document and byte counters include these repeated attempts. This does not remove the timestamp limitations above or provide exactly-once delivery."],[5036,"Search size per poll. 0 returns no hits, and Elasticsearch enforces its own result-window limit"],[4333,"seccomp:unconfined permits io_uring calls blocked by Docker's default seccomp profile. A custom profile allowing the required syscalls is an alternative to disabling filtering. See Docker seccomp profiles."],[4879,"seccomp=unconfined as a security option. Docker's default seccomp profile blocks the io_uring calls the server needs."],[4356,"Secret - root user credentials from server.users.root (default iggy/changeit). Point server.users.root.existingSecret.name at your own Secret in production. usernameKey and passwordKey select its keys."],[3667,"Secret configuration includes: http.jwt.encoding_secret, http.jwt.decoding_secret, encryption.key, encryption.previous_keys, external_auth.url, cluster.auth.shared_secret, and cluster.auth.previous_shared_secret. Prefer setting them through their environment variables (IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET, IGGY_ENCRYPTION_KEY, IGGY_CLUSTER_AUTH_SHARED_SECRET, and so on) instead of storing them in a file. Secret values are masked in logs and never serialized into runtime/current_config.toml."],[3621,"Secrets"],[4630,"Section"],[4716,"Section"],[4599,"Secure connections out of the box"],[19,"Security"],[4648,"Security"],[5549,"Security Notes"],[2176,"Security: replica authentication, key rotation, and TLS"],[4870,"See Configuration for environment variables and Security for transport protection."],[7265,"See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."],[3004,"See Docker & Helm for what each option does."],[4968,"See Durability for single-node failure behavior and Cluster Durability for the quorum and WAL guarantees."],[79,"See MCP Server for how to run and configure it, the full list of tools, and client setup."],[1936,"See Multi-leader partitions for primary placement and the availability consequences of each factor."],[4000,"See Stream options for the rotation procedure. Environment overrides include IGGY_ENCRYPTION_PREVIOUS_KEYS and IGGY_ENCRYPTION_RETIRED_KEK_FINGERPRINTS, each accepting a JSON array."],[6870,"See the C# examples for complete producer and consumer programs."],[7057,"See the foreign/node README for the full API walkthrough."],[2242,"See the Transforms documentation for the configuration of each transform."],[1662,"segment"],[2836,"Segment"],[4814,"Segment archiving / S3 backup placeholders: removed. Delete archive_expired and the old [system.segment] table rather than retaining a false value."],[796,"Segment log"],[6570,"Segment management"],[4398,"segment_size"],[4923,"segment_size"],[4760,"segment_size is a per-topic creation option, bounded to a 512-byte multiple between 1 MiB and 1 GiB. It's a soft limit: rotation fires after the append that crosses it, so the crossing batch lands whole and a sealed segment may run one batch past the configured size. preallocate_segments (also per topic) reserves each segment's bytes up front on filesystems that support it. See Topic options."],[3709,"segment_size topic option"],[4945,"segment_size x partitions at most 64 GiB per create"],[2987,"Segment. Part of a partition, stored on disk as a data file and an index file. Retention deletes old segments whole. See Segment."],[3391,"SegmentClient - delete segments"],[328,"SegmentDelete"],[4743,"Segmented log"],[811,"Segments"],[4761,"Segments store batch records, not individual messages. The record is byte-identical to the wire encoding: the one layout a SendMessages body, the replicated prepare, the persisted segment record, and the poll reply all share. There is no other message encoding."],[3426,"Select a starting point"],[2068,"Select copies per topic"],[4822,"Select encryption at creation"],[5185,"Select the destination topic's durability before creation. An Iggy acknowledgement under replicated does not imply stable-storage persistence. The MQTT source reference includes broker setup and recovery examples."],[4364,"Select the policy when creating the topic"],[4473,"Select this node's entry in cluster.nodes. Required when cluster.enabled = true. See Clustering."],[5162,"Selected-column json: V2 parses the cell as JSON. V3 serializes the column's existing JSON value, so a string containing JSON remains a JSON string."],[5164,"Selected-column raw: the column must contain standard base64 text, which is decoded into raw bytes."],[5163,"Selected-column text: V2 emits the cell as UTF-8. V3 emits strings directly and serializes other JSON values as text."],[3844,"self_signed"],[3887,"self_signed"],[3930,"self_signed"],[3945,"self_signed = true always generates an ephemeral certificate and ignores configured files."],[3943,"self_signed = true generates an ephemeral certificate only while cert_file does not exist. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead."],[4634,"self_signed = true generates an ephemeral certificate only while cert_file does not exist. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead."],[4720,"self_signed = true generates an ephemeral certificate only while cert_file does not exist. An existing PEM pair is loaded instead."],[4704,"Self-service: an authenticated user can always read their own account, change their own password, and manage their own personal access tokens, without any of the user permissions above."],[2104,"Self-signed mode (self_signed = true): the node auto-generates a certificate at boot and the dialer accepts ANY peer certificate. This gives you encrypted replica traffic without certificate management, but no acceptor authentication at all: peer authenticity rests entirely on the PSK handshake. Use CA mode when the replica network is not trusted."],[1019,"Semantics:"],[1706,"Send and poll messages. Subcommands: send and poll. The flush command is removed."],[3000,"Send and receive a message"],[7145,"Send confirmations"],[6196,"Send Iggy messages to RabbitMQ with the connector runtime."],[6206,"Send Iggy messages to Redshift with the connector runtime."],[4700,"Send messages"],[168,"Send messages (partitioning: balanced, key, partition)"],[1716,"Send messages from a binary file written by poll --output-file"],[6132,"Send messages from Iggy streams to a Quickwit index over HTTP, creating the index when it does not exist."],[854,"Send messages. Code: 101."],[3512,"Send some messages"],[1710,"Send to a specific partition"],[3415,"Send to a specific partition"],[3117,"Send window size in bytes"],[1111,"Send zero."],[7244,"send_binary_request(code, payload) sends a raw request through a binary transport. send_binary_request_to_replica(code, payload, replica_id) targets a replica. Session-management commands remain restricted so a raw call cannot replace the client's session state. HTTP returns FeatureUnavailable for binary calls."],[7245,"send_http_request(method, path, body) sends an HTTP request and returns its raw body. Binary transports return FeatureUnavailable for that call. Use typed APIs for commands whose route, ownership or recovery identity the SDK must manage."],[167,"send_messages"],[4701,"send_messages"],[3116,"send_window"],[3868,"send_window"],[6734,"SendBinaryRequestAsync is an escape hatch that sends a raw command code with a prebuilt payload and returns the raw response bytes, for commands the typed API does not cover:"],[6595,"SendBufferSize"],[6758,"Sending and receiving over rented, pooled buffers to avoid separate payload copies on TCP"],[6555,"Sending messages"],[1251,"SendMessages"],[6947,"SendMessages returns the placements reported by the server. An uncertain mutation retains its frozen request and original identity. Reconnection binds the existing session before attempting a new registration. ResolveOperation retries the retained identity, and AbandonOperation releases local state without undoing the server result. Keep the same client for recovery. A fresh send after losing receipt access can duplicate work."],[6836,"SendMessagesAsync accepts messages with ReadOnlyMemory<byte> payloads, which can share backing storage. On hot paths, build a RentedMessageBatch instead - every payload is written into a single buffer rented from the shared array pool - and hand it to SendAsync:"],[6717,"SendMessagesAsync returns a SendMessagesResponse whose Confirmations carry StreamId, TopicId, PartitionId, and BaseOffset (the offset of the first message of the batch) per committed batch. An empty list reports no assigned offsets. A single-message overload is also available: SendMessagesAsync(streamId, topicId, partitioning, message)."],[4086,"Separate per-shard channel for forwarded client replies. Size for peak reply fan-out. Dropped replies have no bus retransmission."],[3949,"Separate section, [cluster.tls]. See Clustering."],[5402,"serde"],[6375,"serde"],[3011,"Serialising messages"],[5407,"serialize_state() encodes the state to MessagePack via ConnectorState::serialize(). generate_messages() samples the half-open messages_range (lower bound included, upper bound excluded), capped by the remaining max_count. Each message carries a JSON-serialized Record with payload_size random alphanumeric characters of text. A non-increasing range returns Error::InvalidConfigValue from poll() without advancing state."],[3820,"Serve HTTPS."],[3737,"Serve the embedded Web UI at /ui. Requires the iggy-web feature and static assets built with npm --prefix web ci and npm --prefix web run build:static before compiling the server. Without the feature, true logs a warning. Missing assets return 404."],[3728,"Serve the HTTP REST API."],[3858,"Serve the QUIC transport."],[3833,"Serve the TCP binary protocol."],[3901,"Serve the WebSocket transport."],[3929,"Serve WSS."],[85,"Server"],[2137,"Server"],[1525,"Server ↔ SDK client, everything a client sends or receives:"],[1531,"Server ↔ server, the replica-to-replica consensus traffic that never appears on a client connection:"],[4339,"Server + CLI"],[3332,"Server 0.10.0"],[914,"Server 0.10.0 uses binary protocol 0.12.0. Clients must use a compatible release."],[3330,"Server 0.10.0 uses binary protocol 0.12.0. Upgrade clients and server together. There is no fallback to the 0.9 protocol. These are the component versions prepared for the upcoming release. Until publication, build the matching source release candidate."],[4980,"Server 0.10.0 uses protocol 0.12.0. The server rejects incompatible binary clients at login. Use the versions in Server compatibility. The 0.9 documentation remains available for the old deployment."],[3333,"Server 0.9.0"],[6582,"Server address. TCP takes host:port (e.g. 127.0.0.1:8090). HTTP needs a full URI (e.g. http://127.0.0.1:3000)"],[4092,"Server cap on a requested wait, at most 600 seconds."],[3213,"Server capacity"],[3270,"Server compatibility"],[6924,"Server compatibility"],[6974,"Server compatibility"],[4829,"Server configuration"],[1324,"Server context."],[4916,"Server default, initially all"],[4353,"Server Deployment - runs apache/iggy with the pod security context the server needs: seccomp profile Unconfined (for io_uring) plus the IPC_LOCK capability (for memory locking). Listener addresses are set to 0.0.0.0 via server.env, and the data volume mounts at /app/local_data. The chart supplies IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS as the in-cluster Service DNS name. Override it with server.advertisedAddress when clients arrive through a LoadBalancer or an Ingress. The server image tag defaults to the chart's appVersion (0.9.0-edge.6 in this source revision). The quick start selects 0.10.0 explicitly."],[4838,"Server encryption key configured"],[42,"Server exits with an io_uring error in a container"],[4836,"Server generates a stream key"],[6639,"Server name for the TLS handshake"],[4050,"Server OTLP export is unavailable in this revision: grpc prevents startup, and http does not deliver logs or traces. Leave enabled = false."],[4354,"Server Service - exposes the http (3000), quic (8080/UDP), tcp (8090), and websocket (8092) ports."],[115,"Server statistics (streams, topics, partitions, OS info)"],[1058,"server to client"],[1062,"server to client"],[1264,"Server to client. 256 bytes, followed by size - 256 bytes of body."],[1395,"Server to client. 256 bytes, never a body. An eviction has no per-request correlation. Close the affected link. A stale-client eviction can occur while the logical session is still live, so try binding the retained session before replacing it. A terminal bind refusal requires a new registration."],[1451,"server_protocol_version"],[1456,"server_protocol_version_min"],[6707,"Server-assigned ids are 0-based: the first stream, topic, partition, or consumer group gets id 0. The root user has id 0 as well."],[1344,"Server-assigned prepare timestamp on committed replies, or the request timestamp on direct replies."],[2887,"Server-assigned timestamp"],[1703,"Server-side option, repeatable. Updates accept only updatable keys."],[2670,"Server-side storage of consumer offsets"],[1547,"Server-stamped at admission. Producers send zero."],[1557,"Server-stamped at journal append (microseconds). Producers send zero."],[1552,"Server-stamped when the batch is appended to the partition journal. Producers send zero."],[222,"Server-to-server"],[1532,"Server-to-server: prepare/ack replication, view changes, and state transfer."],[4057,"service_name"],[4358,"ServiceAccount and Ingress - a shared ServiceAccount and separate optional server/UI ingresses. The chart has no HPA template. Server replicaCount must not exceed 1. Cluster mode uses one release per node, each with its own replica ID and storage."],[4359,"ServiceMonitor - Prometheus scrape config (optional). The /metrics endpoint requires a bearer credential like every other read, so wire a token (e.g. a personal access token stored in a Secret) into server.serviceMonitor.authorization - an unauthenticated scrape gets 401."],[387,"session"],[747,"session"],[1149,"session"],[1195,"session (code 39)"],[1189,"session (login handshake, codes 40 and 45)"],[1496,"Session bound to a different release"],[1493,"Session epoch below the cluster's retained minimum"],[1151,"Session fence epoch from the login reply. Zero on the login-register request itself and on non-replicated operations sent before login (only PING is accepted unauthenticated)."],[3267,"Session retry protection and producer idempotence solve different problems. A client can retain an uncertain request and resolve it using its original identity. Ending that session can remove receipt access. An idempotent producer adds batch identity at the partition level, but does not turn an uncertain application operation into a confirmed success."],[6735,"Session-control codes (login, logout, and the like) are rejected with an invalid-command error, and the operation is TCP-only: HTTP clients throw FeatureUnavailableException."],[1508,"Session-level failure"],[1262,"Session-qualified retry protection is retained by the metadata and partition state machines. A full partition table rejects new writers instead of evicting live protection. Ordered retirement releases ended sessions. Receipt recovery is bounded by the session lifetime and retained state. It is not an unlimited exactly-once guarantee."],[1507,"SessionError"],[1495,"SessionReleaseMismatch"],[322,"SessionRetirementProgress"],[1492,"SessionTooLow"],[2376,"Set active sink version: PUT {base_url}/sinks/{key}/configs/active"],[2377,"Set active source version: PUT {base_url}/sources/{key}/configs/active"],[4519,"Set allocator variables in the launching environment or the service's Environment= entries before process startup. The server can allocate before loading its .env file. Verbose allocator output is useful for checking the experiment, then turn it off for timed runs."],[215,"Set command to the absolute path of the built iggy-mcp executable. This Claude Desktop example uses the broker and development credentials from the getting started guide:"],[5427,"Set either credentials_path or credentials_json, never both. Without either, the connector uses Application Default Credentials. The identity needs table schema read and row append permissions, bigquery.tables.get and bigquery.tables.updateData."],[4865,"Set encryption.key to the new key and add the old key to encryption.previous_keys on every node. Restart nodes with the updated configuration. Retain the old key during this phase."],[5804,"Set field to the envelope key that holds the row data. See the transforms documentation and your source connector's documentation for details on the envelope shape."],[4733,"Set IGGY_ENCRYPTION_KEY to the standard base64 encoding of 32 random bytes before starting this configuration. Enabling encryption does not change existing unencrypted streams. Follow Stream options to create keyed streams and rotate the server key without re-encrypting messages."],[3263,"Set IGGY_KAFKA_IDEMPOTENT_PRODUCERS=true on the Kafka gateway to enable its idempotent producer path. The bridge must also be enabled. The default is false. Without this switch, a Kafka producer can obtain an ID but its retries do not receive the batch deduplication guarantee."],[4984,"Set IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD before first boot. The server no longer generates a root password. Use --with-default-root-credentials only for development."],[3679,"Set IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD to iggy unless they are already present in the environment. These values initialize the root user only at first creation. Development only."],[73,"Set IGGY_SHARDING_CPU_ALLOCATION=all when CPU pinning without NUMA memory binding fits the deployment. Inspect the host topology and allowed CPU set before changing production placement. See Linux tuning."],[6698,"Set IggyClientConfigurator.MessageEncryptor to encrypt message payloads and user headers client-side. They are encrypted on send and decrypted on poll. Routing information and message metadata remain visible to the server. The built-in AesMessageEncryptor uses AES-GCM (confidentiality + authenticity) and takes a 16-, 24-, or 32-byte key for AES-128/192/256:"],[5724,"Set include_metadata = false to send the payload without wrapping (useful when the downstream service expects bare JSON, e.g. Slack webhooks)."],[3957,"Set path before any TOML table header. [runtime] path = \"runtime\" and [logging] path = \"logs\" are relative to it. Data uses the fixed layout {path}/streams/{stream_id}/topics/{topic_id}/partitions/{partition_id}. Stream, topic, and partition path overrides are rejected."],[2825,"Set per topic. Whole segments are deleted once all their messages have expired."],[104,"Set telemetry.enabled = true to export to a running collector. For HTTP export, set transport = \"http\" and use complete signal URLs: http://localhost:4318/v1/logs for logs and http://localhost:4318/v1/traces for traces. The MCP server does not append those paths."],[2329,"Set the broker credentials and connector configuration directory before starting the runtime. The embedded default has an empty connector directory and cannot start unchanged. For a complete setup, follow the quick start."],[6129,"Set the pool limit with max_connections. It does not report pool usage"],[4471,"Set the root credentials to iggy/iggy on first start, unless IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME/IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD are already set. Development only."],[2528,"Sets fields to static or computed values, optionally guarded by a condition:"],[1884,"Setting"],[1895,"Setting"],[1939,"Setting"],[6211,"Setting"],[5346,"Setting max_count = 0 stops production immediately (the connector produces zero messages). To generate messages without a limit, leave the key out."],[4893,"Setting options"],[2940,"Setting up the project"],[5950,"Setting wait_for_tasks = false returns after submission, before Meilisearch confirms indexing. Later task failures are neither observed nor retried by the connector. Index-creation tasks are still awaited during startup. Closing the connector does not wait for outstanding document tasks."],[3572,"Settings - Web UI preferences"],[953,"Several commands take an optional partition id encoded as 5 fixed bytes:"],[3671,"Several sections validate relationships between keys (QUIC windows, sharding shutdown budgets, metadata journal sizing, partition transfer floors). A violation aborts boot with an error naming the keys. The constraints are listed with their sections below."],[2762,"Shard 0 has a special role: it binds every listener - the replica plane and all client transports (TCP, QUIC, WebSocket, HTTP). Connections are then spread across shards at accept time:"],[2747,"Shard roles and connection distribution"],[2988,"Shard. One thread in the server that owns a set of partitions. See Architecture."],[2139,"Sharded runtime, listeners, recovery, and request dispatch"],[7173,"sharding"],[7272,"Share an existing client"],[930,"Shared encodings"],[1529,"Shared encodings: primitives the payloads are built from."],[2786,"Shared metadata (streams, topics, consumer groups) uses a left-right concurrent data structure. Shard 0 holds the single write handle and is the only shard that commits metadata mutations, while every shard holds a read handle. This provides:"],[2097,"shared_secret = new alone, closing the window"],[2096,"shared_secret = new, previous_shared_secret = old on every node"],[2095,"shared_secret = old, previous_shared_secret = new on every node"],[5370,"Shared-library path (String)."],[6346,"Shared-library path (String)."],[1788,"Shell completions"],[2972,"Short definitions of the terms used across the Apache Iggy docs, with links to where each one is explained."],[1762,"Show info about the current connection: client ID, user ID, client address as seen by the server, protocol. Supported on TCP, QUIC and WebSocket. HTTP returns FeatureUnavailable."],[1763,"Show server statistics: streams, topics, partitions, messages, plus host OS details. -o, --output <table|list|json|toml> selects the format (default: table)."],[7234,"Shutdown budget for automatic stores"],[4111,"shutdown_drain_timeout"],[4117,"shutdown_join_timeout"],[4114,"shutdown_poll_interval"],[3769,"Signing algorithm for self-issued tokens."],[3793,"Signing key. If only one secret is configured, it is used for both signing and verification. See the fallback rules below."],[4736,"Signing secrets: encoding_secret and decoding_secret default to empty. Without cluster.auth, the server generates a secure random secret on every start. That's a safe single-node default with two consequences: issued tokens die on restart, and in a cluster each node signs with its own key, so bearers are node-local and follower-to-primary request forwarding stays disabled. For clusters, configure an identical secret on every node (prefer the IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET/IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET environment variables over on-disk config), or enable cluster.auth so the JWT key derives from the cluster's shared PSK."],[2146,"Simulator"],[2689,"Single binary deployment without an external broker or database. Operating-system libraries are still required by dynamically linked builds"],[5766,"Single destination table"],[30,"Single server process, Docker & Helm, configurable CPU/NUMA affinity on Linux"],[2590,"Single-listener bind address"],[2215,"Sink"],[2231,"Sink"],[6326,"Sink"],[2455,"Sink connector config (connectors/protobuf_sink.toml):"],[6360,"Sink connector config (connectors/stdout.toml):"],[6332,"Sink connectors are responsible for writing data from Iggy streams to external systems or destinations. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources and destinations, enabling data flow between Iggy and external systems."],[7139,"Sink data producer - generating random JSON records in bulk batches for sink connector testing."],[2416,"Sink stream entries use max_wait and min_count. The default wait is one second and the minimum count is one. batch_length limits returned messages. A wait must be positive, and min_count must be from one through the batch length. The server can cap a requested wait."],[7013,"sink-data-producer - bulk data generation for sink connectors"],[6966,"sinkdataproducer - high-volume data generation (100 batches of 1000 to 1099 messages) with realistic records, for testing and benchmarking."],[2267,"Sinks are responsible for consuming the messages from the configured stream(s) and topic(s) and sending them further to the specified destination. For example, the Quickwit sink connector is responsible for sending the messages to the Quickwit indexer."],[2446,"Sinks can keep in-memory state, but the runtime does not persist it. Their consumer-group offsets are tracked by Iggy. See the Observability page for checkpoint storage and the sink guide for offset handling."],[1089,"size"],[1287,"size"],[1418,"size"],[4023,"size"],[6284,"size"],[1068,"Size"],[1266,"Size"],[1397,"Size"],[1539,"Size"],[1615,"Size"],[3972,"Size at which a log file rotates. Zero disables size rotation. There is no hourly rotation."],[4499,"Size file-descriptor limits for connections, partitions, open segment files, and runtime overhead. LimitNOFILE=65536 is an example starting budget, not an Iggy requirement. Increase it if measurements justify more. A systemd service can use the following drop-in:"],[3016,"Size limits"],[6285,"size or messages"],[6289,"Size rotation threshold. Must be positive and at most 5GiB, even in message-count mode"],[6324,"Size your rotation thresholds accordingly: smaller files bound the loss window, larger files upload more efficiently."],[4395,"size_of_messages_required_to_save"],[4939,"size_of_messages_required_to_save"],[4786,"size_of_messages_required_to_save (default 1 MiB): byte threshold."],[3707,"size_of_messages_required_to_save topic option"],[4801,"Size-based retention (max_topic_size): the cluster has no single owner of a topic-wide total, so each partition enforces an equal share: max_topic_size / partition_count, counted over sealed bytes. The share is floored at one sealed-segment ceiling (segment_size plus the maximum message-bus frame, since a sealed segment can overshoot by one batch). A cap that divides below that floor is raised to it, so the policy always means at least \"keep the newest sealed segment\". Once sealed bytes exceed the budget, the oldest sealed segments are trimmed. There is no percentage-based early trigger."],[1948,"skip_shard_zero_for_clients"],[1942,"skip_shard_zero_for_replicas"],[4141,"Slots in the VSR client table: how many distinct clients (TCP/QUIC/WebSocket virtual clients and HTTP sessions together) hold live session state. When full, the client with the oldest last commit is evicted and re-registers on its next request. The HTTP session cap tracks this at half. Must be between 2 and 65536."],[5245,"snake_case_columns"],[124,"snapshot"],[1678,"snapshot"],[4806,"snapshot.bin: a MessagePack (rmp-serde) snapshot of the whole metadata state machine, written at checkpoints so the journal can be truncated. Recovery loads the snapshot, then replays journal entries newer than it, rebuilding the client session table alongside."],[6569,"Snapshots"],[4529,"Socket-buffer requirements depend on bandwidth and round-trip time. For example, 10 Gbit/s across 10 ms has a bandwidth-delay product of about 12.5 MB. If the connection is window-limited, inspect net.core.rmem_max, net.core.wmem_max, net.ipv4.tcp_rmem, and net.ipv4.tcp_wmem. Larger ceilings permit larger buffers, but do not force the application to use them or guarantee higher throughput."],[4400,"Soft segment limit. A whole batch can cross it"],[4926,"Soft size limit per segment: a segment may close one whole batch past it."],[6889,"Some operations are TCP-only and throw FeatureUnavailableException on HTTP: joining/leaving a consumer group, GetMeAsync, DeleteSegmentsAsync, and SendBinaryRequestAsync."],[2135,"Source"],[2216,"Source"],[2229,"Source"],[3360,"Source"],[3363,"Source"],[5350,"Source"],[2718,"Source archive"],[5761,"Source Compatibility"],[2454,"Source connector config (connectors/protobuf_source.toml):"],[5384,"Source connector config (connectors/random.toml):"],[5356,"Source connectors are responsible for ingesting data from external sources into Apache Iggy. They provide a way to integrate Apache Iggy with various data sources, such as databases, message queues, or file systems."],[2444,"Source connectors can persist their position between restarts. Each batch of ProducedMessages may carry an optional ConnectorState. After processing and sending the batch to Iggy, the runtime saves that state through the configured file or HTTP backend before acknowledging the batch. The last saved state is passed to new() on the next startup. ConnectorState wraps raw bytes and provides serialize/deserialize helpers backed by MessagePack."],[2348,"Source plugins supply optional checkpoint bytes. The default file backend writes them to {state.path}/source_{key}.state using a temporary file, file synchronization and atomic rename. On Unix, it also synchronizes the parent directory. The runtime stores these bytes unchanged. The SDK provides MessagePack serialization helpers."],[2465,"source_format / target_format: Formats to convert between - any schema value (json, raw, text, proto, flat_buffer, avro)"],[2270,"Sources produce messages to an Iggy stream and topic. Configure one [[streams]] entry per source instance: the runtime currently retains only the last configured producer. For example, the Random source connector generates random messages that are then sent to the configured stream and topic."],[5802,"Sources that wrap row data in an envelope need a transform when the table schema describes the inner row. Otherwise, the Arrow JSON reader maps envelope keys to table columns, producing nulls or schema errors."],[2445,"Sources with cursors or destructive operations must stage changes during poll() and apply them on SourceBatchResult::Ack. Nack discards staged progress so the batch can be polled again. The SDK allows one batch in flight and stops polling if on_batch_result() returns an error. Its default no-op is suitable only for sources without staged work."],[1770,"Space-separated subset of filesystem_overview, process_list, resource_usage, test, server_logs, server_config, all"],[1973,"Spanning multiple hosts"],[3091,"Stable connection duration that resets retry backoff"],[3141,"Stable connection duration that resets retry backoff"],[3157,"Stable connection duration that resets retry backoff"],[1510,"StaleClient"],[2724,"Standalone - as a separate container via docker pull apache/iggy-web-ui:0.5.0"],[3543,"Standalone mode"],[4341,"Standalone Web UI"],[3431,"Start"],[6990,"Start a development server using the example setup, with iggy/iggy credentials, then run the producer before the consumer. Bootstrap credentials do not replace recovered credentials."],[6313,"Start a local MinIO server:"],[6415,"Start a local SurrealDB instance with the credentials used below. This example stores SurrealDB data in memory:"],[100,"Start an Iggy 0.10.0 or edge broker using the getting started guide. From the matching apache/iggy checkout, run:"],[3558,"Start an Iggy server with HTTP enabled, as shown in Getting started, then run the Web UI as a separate container:"],[3404,"Start at or after the given broker append timestamp"],[966,"Start at this offset"],[969,"Start at this timestamp (microseconds)"],[1990,"Start each replica in a separate terminal. Use a different data path for every process:"],[3402,"Start from a specific offset"],[972,"Start from the beginning (value ignored)"],[3406,"Start from the earliest available message"],[975,"Start from the end (value ignored)"],[1723,"Start from the first message in the partition"],[1721,"Start from the given offset"],[1725,"Start from the last message"],[3510,"Start it"],[2346,"Start it from the repository root:"],[7219,"Start of a partition without a position"],[2262,"Start the connector runtime cargo run --bin iggy-connectors --release in the terminal configured in step 3. The Quickwit sink indexes the produced records in the events index. At the same time, you should see the new messages being added to the example_stream stream and example_topic topic by the Random source connector - you can start the Iggy Web UI to browse the data. The messages will have applied the basic fields transformations."],[7302,"Start the development server with iggy/iggy credentials. Run these calls from an async Swift context:"],[2568,"Start the Iggy server from the release source with development credentials. Then run the gateway from the same repository root:"],[2259,"Start the Iggy server in a separate terminal with credentials matching the sample connector configuration:"],[5098,"Start the runtime as described in the source guide. After a polling cycle, read the produced records:"],[5008,"Start the runtime as described in the source guide. After the configured 30-second polling delay, read the produced message:"],[2999,"Start the server"],[3481,"Start the server"],[5395,"Start with the imports, then define the internal state and the public source connector along with its configuration."],[6369,"Start with the imports, then define the state, connector and configuration."],[5142,"Starting RFC3339 cursor when no checkpoint exists"],[2939,"Starting the Iggy server"],[2911,"Starting the server"],[6905,"Starting the server"],[6955,"Starting the server"],[2822,"Starts at 0 in each partition, but can skip numbers after a recovery."],[6312,"Startup writes an empty object at the bucket-root key .iggy-sink-probe, then attempts to delete it. The probe ignores prefix, needs PutObject on that key, and rejects non-success HTTP statuses. ListBucket is not required. Failed probe cleanup does not prevent startup. Reserve this key: an existing object at that name is overwritten, and deleted if the credentials permit cleanup."],[284,"StartView"],[280,"StartViewChange"],[336,"StartViewChange, DoViewChange and StartView establish a new view. DoViewChange includes an untrusted-log flag so a replica without proven history cannot vote as an authoritative empty log. RequestStartView probes for the current view after recovery."],[235,"StartViewChangeHeader"],[237,"StartViewHeader"],[2426,"State"],[5001,"State"],[5090,"State and Failures"],[2281,"State files"],[2225,"State persistence applies to source connectors and stores the optional checkpoint bytes supplied by the plugin, using a local file or an HTTP backend. The SDK provides MessagePack helpers. The runtime acknowledges a source batch after forwarding it to Iggy and saving its optional checkpoint. See state storage."],[2315,"State storage"],[785,"State transfer handles larger gaps. RequestStateTransfer obtains a manifest, then RequestStateChunk and StateChunk transfer artifacts. The receiver checks each complete artifact against its manifest length and checksum before using it. The manifest has magic ISM1, a u32 entry count, a u8 entry length and a trailing XxHash3-64 checksum. Entries contain kind, frontier, length and checksum."],[5050,"state.auto_save_interval and state.tracked_fields do not affect the runtime plugin. Its JSON snapshot is a direct file write, separate from the runtime's atomic checkpoint protocol, and snapshot errors only warn. If enabled, account for this second snapshot when resetting or restoring progress. Removing only the runtime state file does not reset the plugin snapshot."],[310,"StateChunk"],[244,"StateChunkHeader"],[306,"StateTransferTarget"],[242,"StateTransferTargetHeader"],[5757,"Static Routing"],[2249,"Statically Typed: Ensures type safety and compile-time checks, reducing runtime errors."],[1677,"stats"],[4570,"stats_rollup_underflows_total. It goes up when a stream, topic or partition total would have dropped below zero and was held at zero instead. A few during deletes or restores are expected. A steady rise at other times is worth a look."],[1362,"status"],[2117,"Status"],[3443,"Status"],[5064,"Status"],[6389,"Stdout Sink"],[7198,"Stop all senders, then call producer.shutdown().await to drain background queues and wait for writes and error callbacks. Dropping the producer without shutdown can lose buffered messages. Shutdown does not turn failed writes into successful ones. The default error callback logs and drops failed sends."],[5337,"Stop producing after this many messages in total. Omit for unlimited"],[4413,"Storage device lost or irreparably damaged"],[4740,"Storage Engine"],[2755,"Storage structure"],[5527,"storage_backend_type"],[186,"Store consumer offset"],[860,"Store consumer offset. Code: 121."],[6031,"Store messages from Iggy topics in a PostgreSQL table, with optional table creation and configurable payload storage."],[4992,"Store offsets after processing, or select an explicit automatic commit policy. Without stored offsets, a restarted consumer can read retained messages again. Committing on read can advance the offset before application processing. See Deferred polling."],[6470,"Store stream/topic/partition/offset/timestamp/schema fields"],[5782,"store_access_key_id: The optional access key ID of the object storage."],[5785,"store_class: The storage class to use. Currently, only S3-compatible storage is supported."],[185,"store_consumer_offset"],[5786,"store_path_style_access: Use path-style S3 URLs (http://host/bucket/key). Defaults to true. Set to false for stores that require virtual-hosted-style URLs."],[5784,"store_region: The region of the object storage. Required. For S3-compatible stores that ignore it, supply any placeholder value."],[5783,"store_secret_access_key: The optional secret key used to upload data to the object storage. Supply both credential fields or omit both to use the default AWS credential provider chain."],[5781,"store_url: The URL of the object storage for data uploads."],[1254,"StoreConsumerOffset"],[5956,"Stored Documents"],[6478,"Stored payload and encoding"],[6409,"Stored Shape"],[1768,"stored, deflated, bzip2, zstd, lzma, xz"],[3581,"Storing and comparing results"],[1718,"Strategy"],[3399,"Strategy"],[3412,"Strategy"],[851,"strategy is a polling strategy. count is the requested number of messages. auto_commit = 1 asks the server to advance the consumer offset. A primary submits a quorum offset write before replying, but the poll does not wait for that write to commit. Partition reads route to their current primary. See Client failover."],[1659,"stream"],[2809,"Stream"],[2833,"Stream"],[6714,"Stream and topic purge are removed. DeleteTopicAsync deletes the topic and its data. There is no on-demand flush command. Select both durability policies when creating the topic."],[7268,"Stream Builder"],[7133,"Stream builder - examples using the IggyStream, IggyStreamProducer, and IggyStreamConsumer builder APIs as discussed in the stream builder guide."],[5372,"Stream configuration entries."],[6348,"Stream configuration entries."],[4853,"Stream data key"],[4821,"Stream names, per-stream encryption keys and server key rotation."],[4820,"Stream Options"],[4672,"Stream permissions - scoped to one stream and all its topics"],[5187,"Stream rows from PostgreSQL into Iggy topics by incremental table polling or change data capture over logical replication."],[4706,"Stream scope: manage_stream, read_stream, manage_topics, read_topics, poll_messages, send_messages, plus an optional per-topic map. read_stream includes read_topics and poll_messages within that stream."],[4839,"Stream uses the supplied key"],[3874,"stream_receive_window"],[4616,"stream_receive_window is the per-stream limit within the shared connection receive window. Both default to 64 MiB."],[7012,"stream-builder - end-to-end walkthrough: create a stream and topic, produce, and consume"],[4710,"Stream-scoped poll_messages on stream 1 lets the user poll from all topics in stream 1, but not from other streams."],[4758,"Stream, topic, and partition ids are numeric and 0-based. Each partition directory holds pairs of .log and .index files. The filename is the segment's start offset, zero-padded to 20 digits. Recovery can create an empty active segment at a reserved offset beyond the last stored message. Next to the segments, every partition keeps its own superblock pair (replica identity and consensus state for that partition) and an offsets/ tree for consumer offset storage. Multi-replica partitions also keep a prepare WAL when either topic durability policy is persisted."],[2989,"Stream. A container for related topics. For example, a dev stream and a production stream can each have their own orders topic. See Stream."],[6309,"Stream/topic substitutions preserve ASCII letters, digits, ., _ and -, replacing other characters with _. Distinct names can therefore produce the same key. Keep stream/topic separation in the template, use names that remain distinct after replacement, and avoid sharing the same output paths across independent producers. A repeated object key overwrites the current object, subject to bucket versioning. The offset range alone does not provide global deduplication. Extensions are .jsonl, .json and .bin for the three formats."],[6967,"streambuilder - a combined producer and consumer in a single class. It deletes its test_stream stream after the run."],[3388,"StreamClient - create, get, list, update, delete streams"],[2962,"StreamCreateOptions configures stream creation. TopicCreateOptions selects partitions, retention and storage policies. Omitted optional fields use server defaults. The default message durability is replicated. Select Durability::Persisted when successful sends must wait for stable storage. See Topic options."],[5371,"streams"],[6347,"streams"],[87,"Streams"],[808,"Streams"],[6550,"Streams"],[3564,"Streams - list, create, and manage streams"],[4555,"streams, topics, partitions, segments"],[6706,"Streams, topics, users, and consumer groups are referenced by an Identifier, which can be either numeric or a name:"],[2158,"Streams, topics, users, permissions, consumer groups, and access tokens"],[5014,"string"],[5018,"string"],[5022,"string"],[5030,"string"],[5038,"string"],[5104,"string"],[5108,"string"],[5112,"string"],[5116,"string"],[5120,"string"],[5124,"string"],[5128,"string"],[5136,"string"],[5140,"string"],[5144,"string"],[5214,"string"],[5218,"string"],[5226,"string"],[5234,"string"],[5238,"string"],[5254,"string"],[5258,"string"],[5266,"string"],[5270,"string"],[5274,"string"],[5278,"string"],[5286,"string"],[5298,"string"],[5331,"string"],[5453,"string"],[5457,"string"],[5461,"string"],[5465,"string"],[5469,"string"],[5473,"string"],[5477,"string"],[5524,"string"],[5528,"string"],[5561,"string"],[5565,"string"],[5569,"string"],[5573,"string"],[5577,"string"],[5585,"string"],[5589,"string"],[5593,"string"],[5626,"string"],[5630,"string"],[5634,"string"],[5688,"string"],[5692,"string"],[5696,"string"],[5708,"string"],[5826,"string"],[5830,"string"],[5834,"string"],[5838,"string"],[5842,"string"],[5846,"string"],[5850,"string"],[5854,"string"],[5862,"string"],[5904,"string"],[5908,"string"],[5912,"string"],[5916,"string"],[5920,"string"],[5940,"string"],[5968,"string"],[5972,"string"],[5976,"string"],[6000,"string"],[6016,"string"],[6057,"string"],[6061,"string"],[6089,"string"],[6101,"string"],[6263,"string"],[6267,"string"],[6271,"string"],[6275,"string"],[6279,"string"],[6283,"string"],[6287,"string"],[6295,"string"],[6424,"string"],[6428,"string"],[6432,"string"],[6436,"string"],[6440,"string"],[6444,"string"],[6464,"string"],[6580,"string"],[6637,"string"],[6641,"string"],[6686,"string"],[6690,"string"],[6694,"string"],[989,"String"],[6021,"string and its text alias require valid UTF-8 and store a BSON string."],[5538,"String columns: non-null, non-string values are converted to their JSON string representation. Nulls remain null."],[3569,"String decoder (UTF-8 text)"],[5476,"string_format"],[5498,"string: accepts text payloads and appends a newline to each payload that does not already end with one. Set the stream schema = \"text\". Use string_format to tell ClickHouse which format the payload is in (csv, tsv, or json_each_row)."],[948,"String: kind = 2, length = 1..255, value is UTF-8 bytes."],[2788,"Strongly consistent writes serialized through shard 0"],[5151,"stuck_batch_cap_factor"],[4307,"Sub-sections:"],[1685,"Subcommands have single-letter aliases too: c (create), d (delete), g (get), l (list), u (update). So iggy s l is iggy stream list."],[1733,"Submit the current batch's last offset asynchronously before delivering the poll reply. Not an acknowledged offset store"],[6779,"SubscribeOnBackgroundError(handler)"],[6781,"SubscribeOnMessageBatchFailed(handler)"],[2048,"Success therefore does not prove that the operation reached stable storage on any replica. Replication protects against failures while sufficient peers retain the history and can form the quorums needed for recovery and progress. For example, a healthy three-replica group can continue after one replica fails."],[5734,"success_status_codes (default [200, 201, 202, 204]) short-circuits retries: any status code in this set is treated as success and is never retried, even codes that are normally transient. Placing 429 in the set makes the sink accept rate-limited responses as delivered. The sink warns at startup about such overlaps."],[1391,"Success: count = 0, and the typed response payload (if any) follows the 4 count bytes."],[5166,"Successful polls return a versioned MessagePack checkpoint, including empty polls. After forwarding messages, the runtime saves it and sends Ack. Only then does the plugin apply its candidate cursor and row count. Nack discards that candidate so the next poll can fetch the records again. A failure after forwarding but before acknowledgement can produce duplicates."],[1872,"Superblock failure handling"],[4300,"superblock_wedged_fatal_timeout"],[1937,"superblock_wedged_fatal_timeout defaults to 2m. It controls how long repeated superblock persistence failures can continue before the process exits. The affected consensus group stays fenced while persistence is failing. 0, disabled, or unlimited disables the process exit, leaving the group fenced. A nonzero value must be at least 30s."],[4807,"superblock.a / superblock.b: a ping-pong pair of checkpoint records, where the newest valid slot wins by sequence number. The superblock records which checkpoint is current and the snapshot's checksum. Boot cross-checks the pairing: a superblock pointing at a checkpoint newer than the on-disk snapshot, or a snapshot whose checksum doesn't match the record, refuses to boot rather than silently rewinding committed state."],[4703,"Supersets: every manage_* permission includes its read_* counterpart. In addition manage_streams includes manage_topics, read_streams includes read_topics, and read_topics includes poll_messages. manage_topics also includes send_messages, so manage_streams permits sending too."],[4987,"Supply a valid logging.level. An invalid filter stops startup. Log files rotate by size, with retention and total-size cleanup."],[2662,"Support for multiple streams, topics and partitions"],[2663,"Support for multiple transport protocols (QUIC, TCP, WebSocket, HTTP)"],[5765,"Support for REST catalogs"],[5764,"Support for S3-compatible storage"],[2434,"Supported Features"],[2642,"Supported languages SDK"],[2561,"Supported operations"],[6874,"Supported protocols"],[2334,"Supported scalar fields and indexed list entries can be overridden by environment variables using the IGGY_CONNECTORS_<SECTION>_<KEY> convention (nested keys joined by underscores), e.g. IGGY_CONNECTORS_IGGY_USERNAME or IGGY_CONNECTORS_HTTP_ADDRESS."],[2265,"Supported scalar fields and indexed list entries can be overridden with the IGGY_CONNECTORS_ prefix, followed by section and key names joined by underscores, e.g. IGGY_CONNECTORS_IGGY_USERNAME. Header and URL-template maps are configured in TOML. Local connector settings use a per-connector prefix, described in the runtime configuration guide."],[2210,"Supported Schemas"],[6426,"SurrealDB HTTP host and port, e.g. 127.0.0.1:8000. Full http:// / https:// URLs also accepted"],[6405,"SurrealDB Sink"],[6490,"SurrealQL INSERT IGNORE preserves existing records with the same ID, including their old payloads. With the SurrealDB 3.1.4 instance above, IGNORE also silently skips unique-index conflicts and field-assertion failures while accepting valid records from the same chunk. These skips return a successful statement and are included in processed counters. This protects repeated writes of the same identities. It does not ensure that a failed batch will be delivered. There is no end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[4486,"Swapping and writeback"],[3323,"Swift"],[3473,"Swift"],[3205,"Swift accepts the TCP option names, but checks and then ignores pending mutation limits because it has no retained mutation pool. Its reconnect interval is a full pause between endpoint rounds. Node.js accepts reestablish_after for format compatibility without applying a Node equivalent. Do not assume one SDK's parser and timing behavior across all languages."],[7309,"Swift does not expose retained mutation resolution or mutation lanes. It does track uncertain committing reads. fetchProgressUncertain carries an operation ID, and affected progress calls fail with partitionLaneBlocked until abandonOperation releases it. Abandonment does not undo a committed offset. A ReadError can stop a partition until seek selects a new position."],[3325,"Swift Package Manager"],[3355,"Swift SDK"],[7292,"Swift SDK"],[878,"Sync consumer group. Code: 606."],[4332,"SYS_NICE broadens scheduling and NUMA permissions. Pinning a thread owned by the process with sched_setaffinity does not by itself require this capability. Container policies can impose additional restrictions. See Linux affinity permissions."],[3982,"sysinfo_print_interval"],[807,"System"],[6566,"System operations"],[4452,"System requirements"],[3462,"system.getMetadataChanges"],[3716,"system.message_deduplication (whole section)"],[3700,"system.partition.enforce_fsync"],[3704,"system.partition.messages_required_to_save"],[3694,"system.partition.path, partition.path / IGGY_PARTITION_PATH"],[3706,"system.partition.size_of_messages_required_to_save"],[3692,"system.partition.validate_checksum"],[3688,"system.path / IGGY_SYSTEM_PATH"],[3690,"system.runtime, system.logging, system.encryption, system.memory_pool, system.sharding"],[3714,"system.segment.archive_expired, system.recovery.recreate_missing_state"],[3710,"system.segment.preallocate"],[3708,"system.segment.size"],[3712,"system.stream.path, system.topic.path"],[3696,"system.topic.max_size"],[3698,"system.topic.message_expiry"],[3387,"SystemClient - ping, stats, snapshot"],[98,"Systemd integration"],[5026,"table"],[5456,"table"],[5568,"table"],[5642,"table"],[5704,"table"],[6435,"table"],[5798,"Table name: users"],[5197,"Table Polling: Incrementally fetch rows using a tracking column"],[6036,"Table Schema"],[5523,"table_uri"],[5221,"tables"],[5775,"tables: The names of the Iceberg tables you want to statically route Iggy messages to. The name should include the table’s namespace, separated by a dot (.). Ignored when dynamic_routing = true."],[5840,"Target bucket"],[3910,"Target buffer size for batched writes before flush."],[5978,"Target collection name"],[5118,"Target database"],[5844,"Target database"],[5974,"Target database name"],[5567,"Target database, [A-Za-z0-9_]+"],[5632,"Target index name"],[5910,"Target index UID"],[3907,"Target minimum size of the frame read buffer."],[5459,"Target table name"],[5571,"Target table, [A-Za-z0-9_]+"],[6438,"Target table. Must be a safe SurrealQL identifier"],[5690,"Target URL for HTTP requests"],[3163,"Target write buffer size in bytes"],[6060,"target_table"],[905,"Targets have unique partition IDs and use offset or next strategies. The request applies its count, minimum count and byte limit across all targets. Each target also has a byte limit no greater than the request limit. A fetch requires the one-second reply margin even when its wait is zero."],[1801,"tcp"],[3053,"TCP"],[3301,"TCP"],[3306,"TCP"],[3375,"TCP"],[4580,"TCP"],[4592,"TCP"],[4632,"TCP"],[4718,"TCP"],[6887,"TCP - binary protocol for optimal performance and lower latency (recommended). Over TCP the SDK speaks the VSR consensus framing, the only wire protocol the server accepts. See Connection semantics"],[3050,"TCP (default)"],[3373,"TCP (default)"],[1996,"tcp and tcp_replica ports are always required, even when client TCP is disabled. tcp_replica carries replica-to-replica consensus traffic"],[2763,"TCP and WebSocket connections, plaintext or TLS, are handed off round-robin across the shards. Shard 0's coordinator duplicates the socket's file descriptor, ships a connection-setup frame to the target shard, and drops its own handle, so the owning shard serves the connection from then on - before a single byte is read. For TLS-wrapped TCP and secure WebSocket (WSS), the target shard runs the TLS handshake. Shard 0 is part of the rotation by default. skip_shard_zero_for_clients = true under [cluster.coordinator] takes it out."],[6614,"TCP automatic login on connect"],[6705,"TCP clients publish connection state changes, useful for reacting to reconnects. HTTP subscription methods are no-ops:"],[6726,"TCP groups reads by primary. HTTP uses one poll per partition and supports standalone consumers only. A group reader joins and follows its assignment. ReadException reports the partition and any continuation that skips an undecodable record. A stopped partition needs an explicit seek before reading resumes."],[3837,"TCP is the highest-throughput transport. These two keys, plus the TLS block below, are the entire section."],[3033,"TCP options"],[4611,"TCP provides the highest performance as it works directly with binary data without the overhead of HTTP or TLS handshakes (unless TLS is enabled). The full set of keys:"],[6594,"TCP receive buffer override in bytes. Null preserves the OS default"],[6610,"TCP reconnection behavior (see below)"],[6998,"TCP retains uncertain mutation identities. Keep the client and use resolveOperation or abandonOperation when an exception reports a retained operation. An uncertain committing poll blocks progress for its partition until abandonment. HTTP supports standalone consumers and deferred reads, but not consumer group membership."],[6598,"TCP send buffer override in bytes. Null preserves the OS default"],[7137,"TCP TLS - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with custom CA certificates. Stop the plain server and start a TLS-enabled server from the repository root:"],[6606,"TCP TLS configuration (see below). HTTP uses its HTTPS URI and standard certificate validation"],[6890,"TCP uses the current binary protocol and routes partition requests to their primaries. The client discovers the cluster roster and follows metadata leadership separately. Balanced and message-key partitioning are resolved on the client."],[3326,"TCP with optional TLS"],[7011,"tcp-tls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections"],[3296,"TCP, HTTP"],[3311,"TCP, HTTP"],[3070,"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials during connect(). HTTP requires an explicit login, as described below."],[7262,"TCP, QUIC, and WebSocket apply connection-string credentials on connect(). HTTP requires an explicit login and supports ordinary consumers only."],[3286,"TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket"],[3291,"TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket"],[3316,"TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket"],[3321,"TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket"],[16,"TCP, QUIC, WebSocket (custom binary protocol), HTTP (REST)"],[3942,"TCP, WebSocket"],[6946,"tcp.WithTLSValidateCertificate controls certificate validation. The client also accepts client.WithLogger for a custom *slog.Logger and runs a built-in heartbeat loop (every 5 seconds by default) once connected."],[6530,"TcpTls - TLS-encrypted TCP connection"],[6969,"tcptls - TLS-encrypted TCP connections with CA certificate verification. Requires a TLS-enabled server. Run this server command from the repository root:"],[4063,"telemetry.logs.endpoint"],[4060,"telemetry.logs.transport"],[4069,"telemetry.traces.endpoint"],[4066,"telemetry.traces.transport"],[4515,"Tell the allocator which page mechanisms to request. They are not kernel pool sizes."],[4871,"Testing with Iggy"],[6114,"text"],[6483,"text"],[6188,"Text"],[6115,"TEXT"],[5880,"Text carriage returns and newlines become the literal sequences \\r and \\n in the stored string. Use base64 if those original bytes must round-trip. Tabs in measurement names or tag values are rejected. Quoted text fields allow tabs."],[5659,"Text payloads are wrapped as { \"text\": \"...\", \"data_type\": \"text\" }."],[5877,"text: the payload must be valid UTF-8 and is written as a single payload_text string field."],[2853,"Thanks to having multiple partitions, we can achieve the horizontal scalability between many independent consumers, since each consumer can read the messages from the different partitions. This can be achieved by using more advanced concepts such as consumer groups."],[212,"The [permissions] section provides a first layer of access control, checked before any request is forwarded to the Iggy server. This lets you restrict the MCP server to read-only operations, for example:"],[2304,"The [telemetry] section configures OTLP export of logs and traces. Set enabled = true to export to a running collector:"],[4624,"The [websocket] section exposes the frame-tuning knobs (read_buffer_size, write_buffer_size, max_write_buffer_size, max_message_size, max_frame_size, accept_unmasked_frames). See Configuration. TLS for WebSocket has its own [websocket.tls] section."],[4762,"The 256-byte batch header carries partition_id, base_offset, base_timestamp, origin_timestamp, batch_length, batch_checksum (XxHash3-64), and message_count. An optional producer section carries a producer ID, epoch and sequence. Remaining reserved bytes must be zero. Each 48-byte frame header carries checksum (XxHash3-64), id (u128), offset_delta (u32), timestamp_delta (u32), and the two lengths. A message's absolute offset is base_offset + offset_delta. Its server timestamp is the flat base_timestamp, while timestamp_delta resolves producer time against origin_timestamp. All fields are little-endian, and records are stored contiguously with no padding."],[2093,"The 32-byte minimum is enforced whenever the cluster is enabled, even with auth.enabled = false: a configured non-empty secret shorter than 32 bytes fails boot."],[853,"The 40-byte prefix is followed by a stream of batch records: each record's header carries the stamped base_offset and base_timestamp, and each frame's deltas resolve against them. A record may be a server-sliced view of a larger stored batch, so the first polled offset is base_offset + offset_delta of the first frame, not necessarily base_offset itself. current_offset is the partition's newest offset at poll time. The server rebuilds records when decrypting encrypted payloads."],[1656,"The 64-byte IggyMessageHeader (checksum, id, offset, timestamp, origin_timestamp, lengths, reserved) that earlier protocol generations put in front of every message no longer exists on the wire or on disk. It survives only as an in-memory model."],[4259,"The address clients dial, published in cluster metadata. A literal IP or a DNS hostname. Left unset, the server derives it from the bind address of the first enabled listener. That fails when the listener binds a wildcard, and the server refuses to start rather than publish an address no client can use. Declaring the unspecified address (0.0.0.0, ::) is refused for the same reason."],[75,"The AI-related components of Apache Iggy: the MCP server that lets LLM applications work with a running Iggy server."],[5551,"The Apache Doris sink connector consumes JSON messages from Iggy streams and writes them to a pre-created Doris table via Doris's Stream Load HTTP API. Batches are loaded under deterministic labels so that retries deduplicate while Doris retains those labels."],[6572,"The API fragments below reuse a connected, authenticated client (see Creating a client) and the imports in the configuration example. Use existing streams and topics for read, update, delete, publish, and poll operations. The create examples show how to prepare them."],[5,"The append-only log"],[2847,"The append-only log is the core concept of Iggy. It's a simple data structure, which is optimized for the append-only operations. It's a sequence of records, that are being appended to the end of the log. The records are immutable, so that they can't be changed once they are written to the log. Records within a partition follow the order admitted by that partition's primary. There is no global ordering across partitions."],[3241,"The application stores checkpoints after successful processing"],[6872,"The async-first C# SDK for .NET applications over TCP and HTTP, from installation to creating a client."],[3496,"The automatic policy counts a message after its handler succeeds. A handler exception ends the run and leaves that message uncounted. A later run can read it again. Await consumer shutdown to finish pending progress and leave the group."],[2510,"The available computed values are:"],[7166,"The background mode buffers messages and sends them from shard workers, trading latency for throughput and backpressure control. BackgroundConfig::builder() exposes:"],[1534,"The batch layout messages travel and rest in, from the 256-byte batch header down to individual message frames."],[5714,"The batch_mode option controls how messages from one poll cycle are delivered to the endpoint."],[5424,"The BigQuery sink writes Arrow record batches through the Storage Write API _default stream. The target table must already exist. Startup reads its schema and rejects unsupported column types before consuming messages."],[2244,"The BigQuery sink writes through the Storage Write API. The MQTT source supports deferred broker acknowledgements."],[1980,"The binaries are:"],[5712,"The body size limit defaults to 10 MiB and applies after serialization: per message in individual/raw, or to the whole batch in ndjson/json_array. Setting max_payload_size_bytes = 0 disables it. Oversized bodies fail without being sent. Optional startup health checks use the same URL, retry policy and success codes, with HEAD as the default method."],[2567,"The bridge is the gateway's connection to Iggy. It is disabled by default. Without it, data and topic APIs do not store data. Producer ID allocation and in-memory group coordination alone do not prove that storage is enabled."],[5313,"The builtin backend creates (or reuses) a logical replication slot using the test_decoding output plugin. The default slot name is iggy_slot. No publication is created or required, since test_decoding ignores publications entirely."],[6505,"The C++ SDK supports the same connection string format as the Rust SDK:"],[6493,"The C++ SDK, a CXX bridge over the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source with Bazel."],[3028,"The canonical reference for Iggy connection strings, covering every scheme, credential form and option."],[4892,"The catalog"],[1023,"The catalog is discoverable with DescribeOptions, code 13, using scope 1 for topics, 2 for streams and 3 for users. All three catalogs have entries. Stream keys are create-only and secret. Stream and topic names and user identity/status can be updated. Topic retention fields can be reset, while compression and storage policies are fixed at creation. See Topic options and Stream options."],[804,"The catalog of command codes, their operation bytes, and which commands are replicated."],[5884,"The circuit breaker counts at most one failure per consumed batch, based on its first error. A permanent HTTP error does not increment the counter. A fully successful batch resets it. While the breaker is open, incoming batches fail without a write. After the cool-down window, writes resume and the failure counter resets."],[1797,"The CLI talks to the server over one of four transports, selected with --transport. Accepted values are tcp, quic, http and ws (not websocket). The default is tcp."],[5442,"The ClickHouse sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and inserts them into ClickHouse tables over the HTTP interface. It supports three insert formats: json_each_row (default), row_binary, and string passthrough for pre-formatted CSV/TSV/JSON payloads."],[2172,"The client API is the same in single-node and cluster mode. This example uses the iggy / iggy credentials explicitly provisioned in Getting started:"],[7047,"The client constructor accepts a few options:"],[928,"The client creates and protects the random binding secret for its session. The server stores a verifier. Session zero can resolve a lost registration reply using this retained secret. Binding supports connections to different partition primaries while preserving one session identity."],[6949,"The client exposes CreateConsumerGroup, JoinConsumerGroup, LeaveConsumerGroup, GetConsumerGroups and SyncConsumerGroup. A one-shot PollMessages reads an explicit partition. Use reader.NewPartitionReader or reader.NewIggyConsumer with iggcon.NewGroupConsumer to read the full assignment."],[7038,"The client resolves the target partition and routes requests to its primary. A primary can differ from the metadata leader."],[925,"The client stores session and echoes it in every subsequent request header. Logout is command code 39 (LOGOUT_USER), operation = 3 (Logout), empty body."],[3574,"The Clients, Logs and terminal routes are placeholders hidden from navigation. The Server settings page displays information, but saving settings is disabled. Editing and deleting existing users, changing their permissions and bulk user actions are also placeholders. See the roadmap for what is planned."],[2107,"the cluster PSK: when HTTP is enabled, cluster auth is enabled, and no http.jwt secrets are configured, the signing key is derived from shared_secret."],[256,"The command byte is at offset 60. This is the complete registry, including client commands:"],[4387,"The committed batches are flushed even below the ordinary flush thresholds. Setting durability=persisted is sufficient. Setting messages_required_to_save=1 is not required. A single-replica partition does not use the cluster's prepare WAL."],[2950,"The completed sample can be found in the repository. We will go through the implementation step by step so you get a clear understanding of how it all fits together."],[4810,"The compression_algorithm topic option accepts none (default) and gzip, but it is a placeholder today: the value is persisted and reported back, but no message compression is applied. Payload encryption still applies when enabled. To compress today, do it client-side and tag messages via user headers - see the message headers examples in the Iggy repo."],[1024,"The compression_algorithm value used by topic options:"],[4442,"The concurrency cap applies per shard, so total in-flight callouts can reach the shard count multiplied by this value. There is no per-client rate limit in the callout. Apply one at the service or network boundary if required."],[2529,"The condition values are always (the default when omitted - sets the field whether or not it exists), key_exists (update only when the field is already present), and key_not_exists (add only when the field is absent)."],[4817,"The config loader rejects the old [system] table and IGGY_SYSTEM_* environment mappings. The full mapping is in Relocated configuration keys."],[105,"The configuration file must be in the toml format. By default, the server looks for core/ai/mcp/config.toml relative to its working directory. Set IGGY_MCP_CONFIG_PATH to use another path. Embedded defaults are loaded first, then the file if it exists, then environment overrides."],[2331,"The configuration file path defaults to core/connectors/runtime/config.toml and can be overridden by the IGGY_CONNECTORS_CONFIG_PATH environment variable."],[7261,"The connection string is the simplest way to configure the client. It supports all four transport protocols:"],[2326,"The connector are implemented as Rust libraries, and these are loaded dynamically during the runtime initialization process."],[5045,"The connector does not use scroll, search_after or a document-ID tiebreaker. If a timestamp group spans multiple batches, advancing past that timestamp skips the remaining tied documents. Late arrivals and updates at or below the watermark are also skipped. scroll_timeout is accepted but unused. Without a usable timestamp, repeated polls can produce duplicates. The default @timestamp sort still requires a sortable field in the index mapping."],[5728,"The connector doesn't expect the metadata envelope on input. It adds it on the way out. Producers must supply payloads accepted by the configured stream decoder and the receiving endpoint."],[6394,"The connector emits info-level tracing events through the runtime to stdout. The runtime selects text or JSON with logging.format and filters events with RUST_LOG (default INFO). It logs batch metadata on every invocation. print_payload additionally logs each message's offset and decoded payload. The invocation counter is in memory and resets when the plugin is recreated."],[5042,"The connector file remains TOML. plugin_config_format = \"json\" only selects the default format of the HTTP API's plugin-config response. It does not change how the local file is parsed. The upstream config example also uses TOML with a structured [plugin_config] table."],[5609,"The connector preserves the Authorization header across Doris FE-to-BE 307/308 redirects, validating each target first. When fe_url uses HTTPS, an HTTP redirect target is refused unless allow_insecure_redirect = true. Location must be absolute, and at most five redirects are followed per attempt. A nonempty allowed_redirect_hosts list restricts destinations to the listed hosts or host:port endpoints. An unset or empty list does not restrict hosts."],[6026,"The connector retries transient insert failures up to max_retries total attempts, with linear waits of retry_delay, twice that delay, and so on. An invalid delay silently falls back to 1s. Driver-level retryable writes may add retries independently, according to the URI and MongoDB deployment. Startup has no connector-level retry loop."],[2282,"The connector runtime exposes its health through logs, Prometheus metrics, a stats endpoint, OpenTelemetry export, and per-source state files."],[2222,"The connector runtime operates in two directions - source (ingest) and sink (egress):"],[5737,"The connector runtime uses consumer auto-commit before consume() completes. A plugin error is logged and counted, the failed batch adds no processed messages, and polling continues without replaying that batch. Earlier messages or requests from the same batch may already have succeeded. A crash or exhausted retry budget can lose messages. A retry after an ambiguous response can deliver them again. There is no end-to-end at-least-once or exactly-once guarantee."],[2205,"The connector runtime: dynamically loaded source and sink plugins, how data flows through them, and what ships today."],[5347,"The connector stages its count for each batch and commits it only after the runtime acknowledges delivery and saves the checkpoint. Restoring that checkpoint enforces max_count across restarts. Missing or undecodable state starts the count at zero. After reaching the limit, polling continues but returns no messages or new checkpoint."],[5157,"The connector substitutes its RFC3339 cursor into the query. Both versions require ascending timestamps and a stable, unique order within each timestamp group. Include all necessary tag or key columns after the timestamp in the sort. The examples use host because their data model has one point per host and timestamp."],[3021,"The consumer decodes the envelope, reads message_type, then decodes the inner payload as that type. This works with any client, but every message is decoded twice, and the inner payload is a JSON string inside the envelope. See the message-envelope example."],[2969,"The consumer prints the messages and stores its progress. Running it again waits and exits if no new messages exist. Running the producer again appends ten more messages. A processing failure before checkpoint storage can cause replay on the next run, so application side effects must tolerate retries."],[6943,"The consumer reads partition 0 independently and keeps its offset only in memory. Restarting reads from offset 0 again, or the earliest retained message if older messages were removed."],[6992,"The consumer reads partition 0 independently and stops on an empty poll. Its cursor starts at offset 0 or the earliest retained message each time it runs. autoCommit is disabled, so this sample does not store its progress on the server."],[3023,"The consumer reads the header and decodes the payload once:"],[6861,"The consumer reads through a PartitionReader and yields one message at a time. A standalone consumer requires WithPartitions. A group consumer follows the server assignment."],[2964,"The consumer starts after its stored offset. It waits up to five seconds for messages and processes the returned batch before storing its checkpoint. An empty reply ends this example."],[7278,"The consumer uses a group by default. This example creates the group, reads messages and commits each checkpoint after printing its payload. Add futures-util to use StreamExt."],[2953,"The container needs a host kernel of 5.19 or newer, because the server uses io_uring. See System requirements if it fails to start. The capabilities, seccomp setting, and memlock limit form a permissive development setup. Production deployments can use narrower syscall permissions and a finite memory budget. See Docker & Helm for the details. IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS is needed because the server binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default, which a published port cannot reach. IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS is needed because that wildcard leaves the server with no address to give clients, and it refuses to start rather than publish one nobody can dial. Here the port is published to the host, so localhost is that address. Setting the root credentials explicitly means the username and password used later in this guide will work."],[2637,"The coordinator log uses 16 partitions. The owner of partition zero is the controller for topic management. Other gateways return NOT_CONTROLLER, and the Kafka admin client follows it. After a gateway fails, another gateway takes over its assigned groups once the Iggy session expires and ownership moves."],[4548,"The core metrics are below. The gauges are read fresh on every scrape."],[2005,"The cross-SDK BDD suites use a standalone server for basic features and add the two-node cluster from bdd/docker-compose.cluster.yml for leader_redirection or all:"],[6936,"The current source requires Go 1.25 or newer. Run these commands from your application module:"],[4819,"The current storage format is incompatible with 0.9 data directories. Boot checks it before metadata replay. Preserve old files for rollback and use a fresh directory for 0.10. See Upgrading."],[7237,"The default automatic policy stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds, after processing. CommitAfter::Delivery counts a yielded message immediately. CommitAfter::Marked counts checkpoints that the application marks. A handler failure leaves its message uncounted when using processing-based commits."],[2359,"The default configuration provider reads connector configurations from local files. Each connector (source or sink) is configured in its own separate file within the directory specified by connectors.config_dir. An empty config_dir is a fatal startup error. A missing directory is created automatically with a warning, and no connectors are loaded from it."],[1982,"The default core/server/config.toml has clustering disabled, so a plain run starts a single node:"],[5167,"The default file backend stores source_influxdb.state under the runtime's state path (default local_state). The runtime also supports an HTTP state backend. V2 restores versioned state or its legacy unversioned state. V3 requires versioned V3 state. Corrupt checkpoints, invalid saved timestamps and mismatched versions prevent startup. To reset file-backed progress, stop the runtime and remove the source's checkpoint, then restart with the desired initial_offset."],[219,"The default HTTP address is 127.0.0.1:8082, which inside a container is unreachable from the outside, so IGGY_MCP_HTTP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8082 is required for the published port to work."],[5310,"The default payload_format = \"json\" reads a selected column as BYTEA but reports JSON to the runtime, so those bytes must contain valid JSON. Without a selected column, the connector always emits a JSON envelope. If the selected column is absent, it falls back to the envelope while retaining the selected format's schema. Use a column present in every configured table. Type mismatches reject the poll. See the upstream README for sink round-trip recipes."],[5301,"The default query selects rows with tracking_column > last_offset, orders that column ascending, and applies batch_size separately to each table. Use a non-null, unique tracking value that increases in the database's sort order. A timestamp with ties can skip rows at a batch boundary, and inserts or updates at or below the watermark are not revisited. Polling does not capture deletions."],[7015,"The default-root flag sets the iggy/iggy credentials used by the examples. --fresh deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables override the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace recovered credentials."],[5513,"The Delta Lake sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and writes them to Delta Lake tables on the local filesystem, AWS S3, Azure Blob Storage, or Google Cloud Storage. Each successful nonempty batch is flushed and appended in one Delta transaction."],[5412,"The destination schema selects the StreamEncoder. A decode, transform or encoding error rejects the entire source batch, leaves its checkpoint uncommitted and produces Nack. The runtime logs and counts the error. An intentional filter result (Ok(None)) may drop selected messages without rejecting the batch."],[7162,"The direct producer splits each send() call into requests of at most 1000 messages. For sequential calls, the 5 ms linger setting delays a call until at least 5 ms after the previous successful send. It does not buffer messages between calls or delay individual chunks within a call. Choose background to buffer sends on worker tasks. balanced routes requests round-robin across partitions. By default, init() creates a missing stream and topic, requiring the corresponding permissions."],[2570,"The Docker Compose example starts a local Iggy server and gateway. Review its image selection and development credentials before running it. The gateway image is apache/iggy-kafka-gateway."],[2728,"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via docker pull apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0."],[2730,"The docker image is available, and can be fetched via docker pull apache/iggy-mcp:0.6.0."],[2830,"The domain model behind Iggy, and how an append-only streaming log differs from a message broker."],[5618,"The Elasticsearch sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and indexes them into an Elasticsearch index using the bulk API."],[5002,"The Elasticsearch source connector polls documents from an Elasticsearch index and produces them to Iggy streams. With a timestamp field configured, it filters searches using the last acknowledged timestamp. This is a timestamp watermark, with the limitations described below."],[2506,"The enabled key is required. A transform section without it fails to load, and a transform with enabled = false is skipped."],[2481,"The encoder follows the same pattern:"],[6701,"The encryptor applies to every message on the connection - topics mixing encrypted and plaintext messages are not supported."],[3069,"The environment takes precedence over the flag. Stored or recovered root credentials are not replaced by bootstrap settings. --fresh deletes this replica's local data. In a cluster it can recover committed state, including credentials, from another replica. Use this reset only for disposable development data. A fresh standalone server requires credentials. Provision root credentials on every replica for a new cluster."],[7076,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for a new data directory. Existing stored credentials are not replaced. For the source command, ensure IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME and IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD are unset or match the sample. Environment values take precedence over the flag. --fresh removes the local replica state, so use it only for disposable development data."],[7101,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced, and environment credentials override the source command's default-credentials flag. Use --fresh only with disposable local replica data."],[7117,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and bootstrap the iggy/iggy root credentials for new data. Stored credentials are not replaced. Environment credentials override the default-credentials flag, so unset them or set them to match the sample. --fresh deletes local replica state. Use disposable development data. A fresh server requires explicit credentials or the development flag."],[7036,"The environment variables make the server reachable through the published port (it binds to 127.0.0.1 inside the container by default) and set the root credentials the samples log in with. Without --with-default-root-credentials (or the IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME / IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD variables), a fresh server refuses to start. For source runs, --fresh deletes existing local server data. Explicit credential environment variables take precedence over the default-root flag. Bootstrap settings do not replace credentials already stored in recovered data."],[1448,"The evicted client id."],[4961,"The example needs a cluster with at least three nodes. The metadata group still spans the full cluster, independent of each topic's factor."],[5770,"The example requires a REST catalog at http://localhost:8181 and S3-compatible storage at http://localhost:9000, configured with the credentials below. Create the storage bucket, namespace and target table before starting the connector. The sink does not create them."],[5205,"The example uses an existing PostgreSQL database named iggy, with an iggy user and password. Run this SQL in that database:"],[7129,"The examples are cargo example targets of the iggy_examples crate. Run them from the repository root with cargo run --example <name> so relative certificate paths resolve. Run each producer before its consumer. Some examples create fixed names and require fresh data when repeated. The getting-started consumer also expects stream and topic IDs 0, so run that pair first on a fresh server. Partition IDs are 0-based, but balanced producers and consumer groups can use multiple partitions."],[3018,"The examples are in Rust and come from the examples in the Apache Iggy repository."],[7255,"The examples below log in with iggy/iggy. For a new local development instance, with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides, use:"],[3365,"The examples below use the iggy/iggy root credentials. For a new local development instance, with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides, use:"],[4330,"The examples use a permissive syscall profile and unlimited locked memory for development. Production deployments can use a custom seccomp profile and a finite locked-memory budget that permit the server's required operations. See Linux tuning."],[6894,"The examples use iggy / iggy. For a new server data directory, start the matching source server from the Iggy repository root:"],[3068,"The examples use iggy/iggy. For a new local development instance, with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides, use:"],[6525,"The examples/csharp directory in the Iggy repository contains complete, runnable projects:"],[7060,"The experimental PHP SDK, a native extension wrapping the Rust SDK, and how to build it from source."],[1991,"The exported settings must be present in all three terminals."],[7069,"The extension is experimental. It's defined as the apache/iggy-php composer package (php-ext type). To use it today, build it from the repository source."],[7088,"The extension owns a lazy global Tokio runtime. Don't call pcntl_fork() after the first Iggy call: the child inherits file descriptors but not the runtime's worker threads."],[2552,"The extension point is the SDK (core/connectors/sdk/src/transforms/):"],[3556,"The feature alone does not generate these assets. A server binary built without them returns 404 at /ui."],[6508,"The FFI bridge exposes command-level operations for streams, topics, partitions, and segments. Message sending and polling with user headers. Consumer groups and consumer offsets. Stats, client info, and cluster metadata. Snapshots. User create/get/update/delete, permission updates and password changes. Topic option discovery (describe_options). And raw binary requests (send_binary_request)."],[2508,"The field-level transforms (add_fields, delete_fields, filter_fields, update_fields, unwrap_envelope) operate on JSON objects only. Other JSON values and other payload formats pass through them unchanged."],[5305,"The file state backend stores source_postgres-source.state under the runtime's state path (default local_state). Its MessagePack state contains per-table offsets, a last-poll time and a processed-row count. The HTTP state backend is also available. Missing or undecodable plugin state starts fresh. File access errors prevent startup. Stop the runtime before resetting its checkpoint. initial_offset is used only where saved offsets are absent."],[2006,"The first argument selects the SDK (rust, python, php, go, go-race, node, csharp, java, cpp, swift, or all) and the optional second argument selects one feature (basic_messaging, leader_redirection, raw_command, session_contract, stream_crud, stream_options, resource_options_patch, metadata_changes, deferred_polling, or all). leader_redirection is the clustered feature. Coverage depends on the selected SDK and feature. Use --help for the runner's current selections."],[1036,"The fixed 256-byte header every binary-transport message rides in, and the request, reply and eviction shapes it carries."],[844,"The fixed fields are the shape of the operation: which stream, how many partitions, what name. Every topic setting (compression_algorithm, message_expiry, max_topic_size, segment_size, durability, consumer_offset_durability, messages_required_to_save, size_of_messages_required_to_save, preallocate_segments, partition_resize_policy, replication_factor) rides the options block. See Topic options. partitions_count is an argument, not a setting: it is consumed at admission and never persisted as an option."],[4784,"The flush thresholds and completion policies are per-topic creation options:"],[2233,"The following built-in transforms are available:"],[3493,"The following output illustrates the message shape. Customer choices, offsets and partition scheduling vary."],[2458,"The format-conversion transforms define no per-key defaults. Every non-optional key shown above must be present, or the configuration fails to deserialize (schema_path, message_type, field_mappings, and descriptor_set are optional)."],[4967,"The former enforce_fsync option is rejected. Use durability for messages and consumer_offset_durability for explicit offset changes. Poll auto-commit remains asynchronous, and HTTP ack=none confirms dispatch only, regardless of these policies."],[4384,"The former enforce_fsync topic option is rejected. Use durability for messages and consumer_offset_durability for offsets. The old server-wide consumer_offset_enforce_fsync setting has also been removed."],[4576,"The four transports Iggy serves simultaneously, TCP, QUIC, WebSocket and HTTP, and what each is best suited to."],[6862,"The fragment assumes a connected client. Manual commits are the default. Commit the checkpoint after processing. DisposeAsync calls ShutdownAsync to close the reader and leave the group."],[3261,"The fragment assumes a connected IggyClient in an async function. Initialization obtains the producer ID. The producer selects each chunk's partition and sends chunks for that partition in sequence. The default producer does not enable this mode."],[6759,"The fragments below reuse an authenticated client and the imports in the first example. The named streams/topics must already exist unless the snippet creates them. Both abstractions can be built from an existing connected client via IggyPublisherBuilder.Create(...) / IggyConsumerBuilder.Create(...), or equivalently via the client.CreatePublisherBuilder(...) / client.CreateConsumerBuilder(...) extension methods from Apache.Iggy.Extensions."],[4763,"The full byte-level tables live on the Message batches page. Absent at-rest encryption, what lands on disk is exactly what the producer sent, with the server stamping partition_id at admission and base_offset / base_timestamp (plus a batch_checksum recompute) at journal append."],[2634,"The gateway configuration reference also lists timeouts, TLS reload, client-certificate mapping, authentication capacity and producer session expiry."],[3264,"The gateway does not support transactions. Idempotent appends do not make application processing and offset commits one atomic operation. See Kafka gateway."],[2575,"The gateway supports Produce, Fetch, ListOffsets, Metadata, topic creation and deletion, partition additions, and topic retention changes. Consumer group APIs support membership, rebalances, static membership, offset commits, group inspection and offset deletion. It also supports ApiVersions, InitProducerId and DescribeCluster."],[6512,"The generated bridge passes ErrorInfo by reference to mutations, login, polls and recovery calls. Use a separate value for each concurrent call. GetMetadataChanges follows the metadata log, and send_binary_request_to_replica targets a node for supported non-replicated commands."],[6532,"The getting-started producer sends 50 messages to partition 0. Its consumer reads five nonempty batches and then exits. Envelope and header examples also use five batches. NewSdk uses four partitions and its group consumer runs until stopped."],[4675,"The global permissions and the operations they unlock:"],[6909,"The Go SDK speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples require Go 1.25 or newer and log in as iggy/iggy. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"],[6923,"The Go SDK, a blocking client speaking the VSR wire protocol over TCP, and which servers it pairs with."],[2421,"The guarantee has limits. A partition retains five batches per producer, at most 4096 producers, and expires producers idle for 24 hours. A changed batch count, a changed partition, expired producer state, or more than five unsaved batches can cause duplicates after recovery. The plugin must return new checkpoint state often enough to preserve its replay position."],[802,"The header definitions and command registry define the layouts. The replica transport implements preamble admission and authentication."],[1047,"The header shape is identified by the command byte at offset 60. Values a client sends or receives:"],[7260,"The high-level API is built on top of the low-level one, so you can always drop down when needed."],[2219,"The highly performant and modular runtime for statically typed, yet dynamically loaded connectors. Ingest the data from the external sources and push it further to the Iggy streams, or fetch the data from the Iggy streams and push it further to the external sources. Create your own Rust plugins by simply implementing either the Source or Sink trait and build custom pipelines for the data processing."],[2785,"The historical migration benchmarks compared v0.5.0 with v0.7.0 at approximately 1,000 MB/s per node. The chart shows selected latency reductions for 8, 16 and 32 producers, each using its own stream. The 8-producer case also reported higher P95 and P99 latency. These are not measurements of 0.10.0:"],[3042,"The host and port are always required. Use a hostname, IPv4 address or bracketed IPv6 address such as [::1]:8090. Options are appended as key=value pairs separated by &. Scheme names and option keys are case-sensitive."],[4476,"The HTTP API endpoints can be found in server.http file, which can be used with REST Client extension for VS Code."],[4618,"The HTTP API is built on axum and provides a standard REST interface. It includes JWT authentication, CORS configuration, and optional TLS."],[4734,"The HTTP API uses JWT (JSON Web Tokens) for session management. Tokens are signed with HS256 by default and have configurable expiry, clock skew tolerance, and audience/issuer validation:"],[2364,"The HTTP configuration provider allows the runtime to fetch connector configurations from a remote HTTP/REST API. The provider fetches active configurations at startup and handles configuration operations requested through the runtime API. It does not periodically poll for remote changes."],[218,"The HTTP endpoint uses the configured Iggy account for every MCP client. This example publishes it only on the host loopback interface."],[2386,"The HTTP provider expects the remote API to implement these endpoints and return connector configuration data in the same format as used by the local provider."],[3199,"The HTTP scheme constructs a plain http:// API URL. Use the Rust HTTP builder's with_api_url() for HTTPS. HTTP parses the credential field but does not log in with it. In Rust, call login_user() or login_with_personal_access_token() explicitly before protected requests."],[4620,"The HTTP server also hosts:"],[2351,"The HTTP server must implement this checkpoint contract:"],[5677,"The HTTP Sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and delivers them to any HTTP endpoint: webhooks, REST APIs, serverless functions, or SaaS integrations. This is the transport-level, generic HTTP connector. It's distinct from sinks such as Elasticsearch or Quickwit, which use HTTP internally to talk to one specific system. You bring the URL, headers, and batching strategy, and the sink handles transport, retries, and metadata wrapping."],[5729,"The HTTP sink supports authentication via custom headers under [plugin_config.headers]. Custom headers are sent with data requests and health checks, except Content-Type: the sink ignores a configured value and sets it from the batch mode for data requests."],[5058,"The HTTP source accepts webhook POST bodies and produces raw messages to one configured stream and topic. Instances that use the same listener address share the listener. A separate admin address serves management, health details and metrics."],[5763,"The Iceberg Sink Connector allows you to consume messages from Iggy topics and store them in Iceberg tables."],[6500,"The Iggy C++ SDK wraps the Rust SDK through a CXX FFI bridge, so it can use all transport protocols (TCP, QUIC, HTTP, WebSocket) via connection strings. The source code is available at github.com/apache/iggy/tree/master/foreign/cpp."],[1789,"The Iggy CLI is a command-line interface for managing an Iggy server: streams, topics, partitions, messages, users, tokens and more. It lives in the core repository under the core/cli directory. The crate is named iggy-cli, the installed binary iggy."],[4351,"The iggy CLI is on PATH in the official image. With the compose example above (container iggy, password Secret123):"],[6934,"The Iggy Go SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Go applications. It is a blocking client that speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol over TCP, with or without TLS. The package is available on pkg.go.dev and the source code lives in foreign/go."],[5789,"The Iggy Iceberg Connector will skip messages in the following cases:"],[6984,"The Iggy Java SDK is a client library for interacting with the Iggy server from Java applications. It provides a blocking client and an async client (based on CompletableFuture) over TCP, plus a blocking HTTP client. The artifact is published to Maven Central and the source code lives in foreign/java."],[7033,"The Iggy Node.js SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Node.js and TypeScript applications. It communicates with the Iggy server over TCP or TLS using the binary protocol. The package is available on npm and the source code can be found on GitHub."],[7068,"The Iggy PHP SDK is a native PHP extension built in Rust with ext-php-rs. It wraps the Rust SDK and exposes a synchronous Iggy\\Client: each call drives the extension's internal Tokio runtime and blocks the calling PHP thread until the operation completes. The source code is available on GitHub."],[7112,"The Iggy Python SDK is a client library that allows you to interact with the Iggy API from your Python application. It is built as a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, which means it supports TCP, QUIC, HTTP, and WebSocket transports via connection strings. The package is available on PyPI and the source code can be found on GitHub."],[6881,"The Iggy SDK for C# is a modern, async-first client library for interacting with an Iggy message streaming server from your .NET applications. It supports TCP and HTTP transports. The package is available on NuGet and the source code lives on GitHub."],[3552,"The Iggy server must allow cross-origin requests from the Web UI origin. CORS is enabled for all origins by default ([http.cors] in the server config). If you restricted allowed_origins, add the Web UI origin."],[2581,"The Iggy topic's durability policy controls storage guarantees. The development stack uses replicated, so an acknowledgement does not prove stable-storage synchronization. Use persisted when acknowledged messages must survive loss of volatile copies. A named Docker volume alone does not strengthen an acknowledgement."],[3539,"The Iggy Web UI dashboard, for monitoring server health and browsing streams, topics, messages and users."],[1830,"The IGGY_USERNAME and IGGY_PASSWORD environment variables (both must be set)."],[7160,"The IggyClient internally wraps its transport in an IggyRwLock, but the client itself does not implement Clone. Share an Arc<IggyClient> across tasks. Cloning the Arc keeps the same client and connection. Typically, it's a good idea to create a separate connection for producing and consuming, but it's not a requirement."],[2771,"The IggyNamespace packs stream_id (20 bits), topic_id (12 bits), and partition_id (20 bits) into a single u64 for efficient hashing and routing. This gives maximums of 1,048,576 streams, 4096 topics per stream, and 1,000,000 partitions per topic."],[4327,"The image bakes in no address overrides, so the server binds the loopback defaults (127.0.0.1) and is unreachable from outside the container even with published ports. Set IGGY_TCP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0:8090 (and the equivalent for every other transport you expose) alongside the -p flags."],[5405,"The implementation also uses humantime, rand, simd-json, and uuid. The reference crate uses rmp-serde directly in its tests. The production state helpers come from the SDK."],[2123,"The implementation includes:"],[4433,"The implementation references are the topic policy definitions, partition completion paths, and HTTP response handling."],[1033,"The incarnation identifies the partition history. The owner generation identifies group ownership and is zero for ordinary consumers. The metadata operation establishes the minimum metadata needed to interpret the context. Poll replies, continuations and checkpoints preserve these fields."],[5869,"The include_checksum, include_origin_timestamp, include_stream_tag, include_topic_tag, and include_partition_tag flags also default to true."],[6471,"The include_headers, include_checksum, and include_origin_timestamp flags also default to true and work independently of include_metadata. Retry and timeout options are query_timeout (30s per HTTP request), max_retries (3 total write attempts, with 0 raised to 1), retry_delay (100ms), and max_retry_delay (5s). Invalid duration strings log a warning and fall back to 1s. Zero durations are accepted. The maximum delay is raised to the base delay if configured smaller. verbose_logging defaults to false and raises per-poll submission logs from debug to info. See the upstream surrealdb_sink README for the full table."],[5814,"The InfluxDB sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and writes them to InfluxDB as line-protocol points. It supports both InfluxDB V2 (OSS 2.x / Cloud) and InfluxDB V3 (Core / Enterprise), selected with the version option."],[5091,"The InfluxDB source connector polls InfluxDB on an interval and produces the resulting rows as messages into an Iggy stream. It supports both InfluxDB V2 (OSS 2.x / Cloud, Flux queries) and InfluxDB V3 (Core / Enterprise, SQL queries), selected with the version option. An acknowledged timestamp cursor and row offset are stored in connector state. Restarts resume from that checkpoint, subject to the ordering and late-arrival limits below."],[2546,"The input payload variant must match source_format. The default flat_buffer stream decoder already returns JSON metadata, so it does not supply Payload::FlatBuffer to a following reverse transform."],[2720,"The interactive CLI is implemented under core/cli, to provide the best developer experience. This is a great addition to the Web UI, especially for developers who prefer using the console tools."],[6973,"The Java SDK, with blocking and CompletableFuture clients over TCP, and which servers they pair with."],[2303,"The JSON status values are starting, running, stopping, stopped, and error. A connector whose plugin fails to load (for example, a missing library) is reported with error without blocking the remaining connectors. Retrieve its last_error from GET /sources/{key} or GET /sinks/{key}. /stats does not include the failure message."],[2566,"The Kafka gateway 0.2.0 is a separate service named iggy-gateway-kafka. It accepts Kafka wire requests and uses the Rust SDK to store and read data in Iggy. Kafka clients connect to the gateway port, 9093 by default. Native Iggy clients continue to use Iggy listeners."],[2192,"The Kafka gateway supports Kafka producers, consumers and selected admin APIs. Run it separately with the Iggy bridge enabled. It supports a defined protocol subset and does not support transactions."],[926,"The legacy login commands 38 (LOGIN_USER) and 44 (LOGIN_WITH_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN) are refused: the server answers them with an Eviction frame, reason 15 (MalformedLogin). Before authentication, a connection can ping or use the registration and binding flows. Protected commands require an authenticated session."],[2482,"The loaded schema is used for JSON-to-protobuf conversion. This example maps user_id and full_name to the schema's field names:"],[2443,"The macros generate the C FFI symbols loaded by the runtime: iggy_sink_open, iggy_sink_consume, iggy_sink_close, and iggy_sink_version for sinks, and iggy_source_open, iggy_source_handle_v2, iggy_source_batch_result, iggy_source_close, and iggy_source_version for sources. Configurations cross FFI as JSON, message batches and metadata use postcard, and source checkpoint bytes are opaque. The SDK creates one Tokio runtime per loaded plugin library, shared by that library's connector instances."],[2133,"The main components are:"],[109,"The MCP server exposes these 41 tools:"],[5894,"The Meilisearch sink connector consumes messages from Iggy streams and indexes them as documents in a Meilisearch index through the official Rust SDK. It can create the index on startup and waits for Meilisearch indexing tasks to complete by default, so task failures surface as connector errors."],[5792,"The message does not contain the field specified in the dynamic_route_field configuration option, or is not a JSON object."],[2851,"The messages are not being stored in the topic directly, but rather in the partitions, which are assigned to the topic. The topic can have one or more partitions assigned, that could help achieve higher parallelism and throughput. The topic can also have a retention policy. Expiry removes whole sealed segments after their newest message has expired, subject to the stored consumer-offset barrier. It does not delete each message immediately at its expiry time. See topic options for expiry and size-limit behavior. Topics also support maximum size limits and per-topic storage options (segment_size, durability, consumer_offset_durability, and flush thresholds) set at creation, plus a compression_algorithm option (a placeholder today: no compression is applied yet). Both durability policies independently default to replicated."],[3430,"The metadata change log records public results of committed metadata operations. It includes resource changes, permissions, sessions and consumer group membership. Reading it requires root or the global manage_servers permission."],[334,"The metadata group orders resources, users and sessions. Every partition has its own group for messages and offsets. The group field identifies the target. The metadata group uses 1 << 63, while partition groups use packed stream, topic and partition IDs."],[1260,"The metadata plane orders resource and session changes. Each partition plane orders messages and offset changes independently. Partition requests retain their incarnation, ownership generation and metadata requirement across retries. Refreshing a route does not authorize a request against a replacement history."],[3814,"The metrics route requires authentication: a missing or invalid bearer credential is rejected with 401. Any authenticated user may scrape. There's no extra RBAC rule. Scrapers present a JWT or a personal access token as the bearer:"],[103,"The minimal viable configuration requires at least the Iggy credentials, used to create the TCP connection to the running Iggy server that the MCP server talks to. You can choose between HTTP (the default) and STDIO transports (e.g. for the local usage with tools such as Claude Desktop choose stdio)."],[99,"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The Apache Iggy MCP Server is an implementation of the MCP protocol for the message streaming infrastructure. It is built using the rmcp crate and supports both stdio and HTTP transports."],[2729,"The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open protocol that standardizes how applications provide context to LLMs. The Iggy MCP Server is an implementation of the MCP protocol for message streaming infrastructure. It exposes 40+ tools covering streams, topics, partitions, messages, consumer groups, users, and more. It supports both HTTP and stdio transports, making it compatible with tools like Claude Desktop and other MCP clients."],[5959,"The MongoDB sink connector writes messages from Iggy streams to a MongoDB database. Each message is inserted as a document into the configured collection."],[5175,"The MQTT source subscribes to an MQTT broker and sends payloads to one Iggy stream and topic. It supports MQTT 3.1.1 and MQTT 5, TLS, optional client certificates and per-filter QoS. MQTT 5 is the default."],[2654,"The name is an abbreviation for the Italian Greyhound - small yet extremely fast dogs, the best in their class. See the lovely Fabio & Cookie ❤️"],[67,"The name is missing from the message because of a known bug where error details are lost between the server and the client (#3735)."],[4567,"The names have no iggy_ prefix, so watch for clashes if Prometheus scrapes other exporters with similar names."],[7023,"The Node.js and TypeScript SDK, from installation to keeping client and server versions in step."],[1953,"The node's roster address. Replica-to-replica consensus traffic and follower-to-primary HTTP forwarding dial it, so it must be a literal IPv4 or IPv6 address. Hostnames and unspecified addresses (0.0.0.0 or ::) are rejected when the server resolves the roster at startup."],[4448,"The normal-status metadata primary performs callouts. Other nodes and view changes can produce retryable errors. Timeouts, network failures, overload, HTTP 429 and HTTP 5xx map to retryable failures. Other unsuccessful HTTP responses and invalid decisions terminate the login."],[6181,"The object itself"],[2731,"The official Apache Iggy images can be found on Docker Hub. These docs target server 0.10.0. Use docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0, or docker pull apache/iggy:edge for a development image. SDK and tooling versions are independent of the server version."],[1794,"The official apache/iggy image ships the CLI alongside the server, installed as /usr/local/bin/iggy. The image entrypoint is the server, so override it to run the CLI:"],[3269,"The official Iggy client SDKs, their packages and registries, and the transports each one supports."],[2740,"The official releases follow the regular semver (0.5.0) or have a latest tag applied (apache/iggy:latest)."],[801,"The offset artifact also retains partition history, retry state and producer state. These internal readability windows do not provide migration from a 0.9 data directory. Use the upgrade procedure."],[3670,"The old [system] table and its environment mappings are rejected. Removed placeholders such as archive_expired and recreate_missing_state must be removed, not set to false."],[1182,"The operation byte tells the server which state-machine operation the request carries. Replicated operations are identified by operation alone. Non-replicated operations (reads, ping) all use operation = 2 and carry their concrete u32 command code in header bytes 196..200."],[1141,"The Operation discriminant."],[7158,"The option keys differ per transport (for example QUIC uses reconnection_max_retries and reconnection_reestablish_after where TCP uses reconnection_retries and reestablish_after). Unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables and defaults."],[3385,"The option keys differ per transport and unknown keys are rejected as hard errors. See Connection Strings for the full per-transport option tables, defaults, and default ports."],[5049,"The optional [plugin_config.state] enables a separate JSON snapshot loaded during open() and saved during close(). Its loaded values can override the runtime checkpoint. It defaults to ./connector_states/elasticsearch_source_<numeric_id>.json. state_id and storage_config.base_path customize that location. Only file storage is implemented. Selecting elasticsearch, redis or an unknown storage type warns and falls back to ./connector_states, ignoring the supplied backend location."],[5428,"The optional emulator_endpoint and emulator_grpc_endpoint must be supplied together. They disable credentials and use plaintext connections. Use them only for local test services."],[2507,"The order of the sections in the file doesn't determine the execution order, so don't rely on one transform seeing the output of another."],[3043,"The parser does not percent-decode credentials or option values. Reserved separators such as : and @ cannot be escaped inside a username or password. Use a PAT for credentials that contain them. Duplicate option keys use the last value. Boolean options enable only for the literal true. Use false to disable them."],[2852,"The partition has its own unique ID and belongs to the topic. The partition is responsible for storing the records. The records are being distributed between the partitions, therefore the partition acts as a simple database, which is optimized for the append-only operations. The partition is identified by its unique ID, which is an integer. Stream, topic and partition IDs are assigned starting from 0. Deleted stream and topic IDs can be reused. New partitions use IDs above the highest remaining partition ID, so deleting the highest partitions can also allow their IDs to be reused. The partition ID is unique per topic, thus the same partition ID can be used in multiple topics."],[2339,"The path field of a connector configuration accepts both plugin.so and plugin - the OS-specific extension (.so / .dylib / .dll) is appended when missing. Absolute paths are checked at the literal location. Relative paths are searched in order:"],[6104,"The payload_format option determines the type of the payload column and how the message payload is stored:"],[6752,"The per-call IIggyClient API is explicit but verbose - you manage partitioning, batching, retries, and offsets yourself. The high-level IggyPublisher and IggyConsumer wrap that surface with fluent builders that handle:"],[4645,"The per-shard verifier evicts stale group-member connections. A link eviction does not itself end the logical session. The metadata liveness process expires its lease and rebalances membership. The session timeout is 1.2 times heartbeat.interval, 36 seconds by default, with periodic checks around that threshold."],[5316,"The pg_replicate backend (based on Supabase's ETL framework) is not implemented: selecting cdc_backend = \"pg_replicate\" fails at startup unless the cdc_pg_replicate build feature is enabled, and the backend returns an error even when it is."],[5306,"The plugin's retries cover transient polling SELECT failures, including connection I/O, pool timeouts, serialization failures and deadlocks. Connection setup, CDC fetches and delete/mark statements do not use that retry loop. A poll error is logged and polling continues without incrementing the runtime's forwarding-error counter. Repeated delivery or checkpoint failures instead receive Nack. The SDK stops after five consecutive Nacks."],[4418,"The policy applies to the completed server operation, not merely to enqueueing a request in a producer or receiving an early dispatch response."],[2023,"The policy changes what a replica must retain before its acknowledgement can count toward commit. It does not replace quorum commit with a primary-only disk write."],[7039,"The poll response does not wait for the auto-commit to finish. The sample processes messages after polling, so auto-commit does not confirm that the application handled each message."],[4029,"The pool has 28 buckets with buffer sizes from 4 KiB to 512 MiB."],[6040,"The PostgreSQL sink connector consumes messages from Iggy topics and stores them in a PostgreSQL table, with optional automatic table creation and configurable payload storage."],[5195,"The PostgreSQL source connector fetches data from PostgreSQL databases and streams it to Iggy topics. It supports incremental table polling and Change Data Capture (CDC) via logical replication."],[254,"The preamble selects a shard and is not authentication. ReplicaHello repeats the peer and link identity and supplies the link count. The acceptor rejects a mismatch. With authentication enabled, placement fields participate in the handshake MAC. Shard counts can differ between nodes."],[900,"The primary value is a configured replica ID, not a roster position. Retain the incarnation and metadata operation when constructing a request. Group ownership adds its own generation."],[931,"The primitives command payloads are built from: names, identifiers, consumers and the other shared encodings."],[3482,"The producer"],[2917,"The producer and consumer below match the getting-started examples in the repository."],[2961,"The producer connects, creates missing resources and sends ten messages to partition zero. Resource creation returns errors for existing names, which this example handles separately from other failures."],[6911,"The producer creates sample-stream and sample-topic (if missing) and sends 5 batches of 10 messages to partition 0. The consumer polls them from offset 0 and exits after 5 batches. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based. These repository examples use numeric stream and topic IDs 0, so create no other streams or topics before running the producer on the fresh server. Run the producer first."],[7290,"The producer creates sample-stream and sample-topic if needed and sends five batches of ten messages. The consumer reads from the first message, stores no offset and exits after five batches. Running it again replays retained messages."],[3504,"The producer creates the stream and topic only if they are missing, so it can be run any number of times. A restarted service resumes from the group's stored offset. To start from nothing, stop the container, and --rm removes it along with its data."],[7161,"The producer is a high-level abstraction that allows you to send messages to the topic. It's quite simple to use and doesn't require you to handle the offsets, partitions or any other low-level details. To begin with, you can simply invoke client.producer() to get the IggyProducerBuilder allowing you to configure the producer. Let's take a look at the basic example:"],[6502,"The project builds with the Bazel version pinned in .bazelversion (BUILD.bazel / MODULE.bazel). Its Rust bridge uses the Cargo and Rust toolchain provisioned by rules_rust, with a separate target directory in the Bazel output tree. Use a compatible Java runtime for Bazel. The pinned Bazel 9.2.0 requires Java 21 or newer."],[3527,"The Prometheus targets page also shows the iggy job as down."],[915,"The protocol version is a semver packed into one u32, 10 bits per component (each must be below 1024):"],[3475,"The Python implementation example shows follow and rebuild loops. The other SDK source guides show their equivalent typed queries."],[7105,"The Python SDK, a PyO3 wrapper around the Rust SDK, and how to install it."],[3251,"The query uses wait_us, min_count, max_bytes and request_timeout_us alongside the normal consumer, partition, strategy and count fields. A positive wait needs at least one second between the wait and request timeout. Configure a proxy timeout long enough to receive the reply."],[4429,"The query value ack=replicated selects the awaited path even when the topic uses persisted. It does not override the topic policy."],[3100,"The QUIC reconnection keys differ from TCP: reconnection_max_retries (not reconnection_retries) and reconnection_reestablish_after (not reestablish_after). Using the TCP names in a QUIC string is a hard error."],[6137,"The Quickwit connector allows you to send data to the Quickwit API using HTTP. At startup it checks readiness, creates a missing index, and appends messages as newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON). Each poll can become multiple requests, each limited to 8 MiB including document newlines."],[6199,"The RabbitMQ sink publishes messages to an AMQP 0.9.1 exchange. It declares the configured exchange, enables publisher confirmations and uses mandatory publishing. Configure a queue and binding that match the routing key before sending messages."],[5324,"The random source connector generates random messages and sends them to Iggy streams. This is useful for testing, benchmarking, and verifying that your connector pipeline is working correctly without needing an external data source."],[6893,"The reader and high-level consumer follow group assignments. A one-shot poll names one partition. They default to manual checkpoint commits. See Deferred polling and the Guide."],[6950,"The reader joins, refreshes assignments and leaves on Close. Its default wait is five seconds. Commit stores a delivered checkpoint after processing. iggcon.AutoCommitPolicy(iggcon.DefaultAutoCommit()) selects automatic commits after processing. See Deferred polling."],[7284,"The reader reserves up to 16 MiB and 16000 messages for buffered and in-flight replies. If the poll count is larger, that count is the default message reservation. Configure prefetch_bytes and prefetch_messages to bound read-ahead independently of each fetch."],[4447,"The reason is optional. An allowed username must resolve to the provisioned account at admission. The server rejects malformed decisions and refuses redirects from the callout endpoint."],[6209,"The Redshift sink serializes batches to Parquet, stages them in S3 and loads an existing Redshift table with COPY. Configure both the Redshift connection and S3 access before starting the runtime."],[5394,"The reference crate is core/connectors/sources/random_source. Use its Cargo.toml from the matching checkout, including [lib] crate-type = [\"cdylib\", \"lib\"] and its workspace dependency features. The Rust blocks in this sample implementation section combine into src/lib.rs. The trait above is an API reference and is not part of that file."],[5398,"The reference manifest supplies these dependencies:"],[6371,"The reference manifest supplies these dependencies:"],[2220,"The release uses the Docker image apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0. Until it is published, build the matching source release candidate."],[2330,"The release uses the Docker image apache/iggy-connect:0.6.0. Until it is published, build the matching source release candidate."],[102,"The release uses the Docker image apache/iggy-mcp:0.6.0. Until it is published, build the matching source release candidate."],[4456,"The releases are published to GitHub and can be found here. The official Docker images can be found here, use docker pull apache/iggy:0.10.0, or apache/iggy:latest for the newest stable release."],[6727,"The rental API returns payload slices backed by a pooled buffer. Dispose each rental and copy any bytes that must outlive it. Do not retain a payload or raw-header reference after disposal. The high-level consumer exposes ReceiveRentedAsync for this path."],[3593,"The repo's driver script builds, starts the server, runs a send and a poll benchmark, and shuts the server down. Use it only in a disposable checkout on an isolated host: it deletes local_data and signals matching iggy-server and iggy-bench processes by name."],[4349,"The repository root carries its own Dockerfile and docker-compose.yml for building from source (a multi-stage build: Rust compilation including the Web UI, then a minimal runtime image):"],[7300,"The repository root contains the Swift Package Manager manifest and exports the Iggy product. For the upcoming release, use the source release candidate. The package dependency below names that release candidate without relying on an unpublished final tag:"],[4361,"The repository-root Dockerfile builds a local image with wildcard transport binds and IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS=localhost. The release image uses core/server/Dockerfile and its /app data path. Keep the advertised address explicit in your deployment so reconnects work from the client network. Do not infer the release image's defaults from the local development Dockerfile."],[4517,"The requested count may not be allocated on a fragmented host. Boot-time reservation is more reliable. Reserved HugeTLB memory is unavailable for ordinary allocations or page cache, even while unused. Check NUMA placement and the actual pool, not just the requested count. See HugeTLB administration."],[852,"The response body:"],[2003,"The response lists the cluster name, nodes, client endpoints, and current roles. cluster m is a shorthand for cluster metadata."],[906,"The response starts with a u32 section count, followed by a table of 56-byte section headers:"],[902,"The response uses the same 40-byte prefix as PollMessages. Waiting requests need a positive count, min_count from 1 through count and a byte budget of at least 344. A positive wait requires at least one second of reply margin. Waits cannot exceed 600 seconds, and request timeouts cannot exceed 630 seconds. The server can shorten waits through its configuration."],[2447,"The retry module ships helpers for connectors talking to flaky external systems: a CircuitBreaker, an HttpRetryMiddleware for reqwest-based clients, and a build_retry_client factory that wraps a reqwest client with the retry middleware (the circuit breaker is wired separately)."],[4666,"The root user cannot be deleted, and its permissions are fixed: it always holds every permission."],[4674,"The root user has full access to everything and bypasses all permission checks. Other users can be assigned specific permissions using the CLI, HTTP API, SDK, or Web UI."],[1999,"The roster ip is the address other replicas dial. It's not the bind interface for the client transports: tcp, quic, http, and websocket bind whatever their own address settings say, and the roster supplies only the port. The defaults listen on loopback, so a cluster spread across hosts needs each transport's address set to 0.0.0.0 or the routable interface. The server warns at startup when a bind cannot serve the advertised ip."],[252,"The roster supplies each node's tcp_replica port and literal IP address. Lower replica IDs dial higher IDs. Each pair can use several links, which distribute traffic across shards. The default frame limit is 64 MiB through message_bus.max_message_size."],[2350,"The runtime appends source_{key} as a URL path segment, preserving any query string. URL query values are redacted from runtime configuration logs and transport errors. Static request headers are passed to the state server. Configure this map in TOML. The other state settings support environment overrides such as IGGY_CONNECTORS_STATE_STORAGE and IGGY_CONNECTORS_STATE_HTTP_URL."],[5727,"The runtime applies stream decoding and configured transforms before the sink formats its requests. To forward opaque message bytes, use schema = \"raw\" with batch_mode = \"raw\" and no transforms. See the SDK format limits when selecting other schemas."],[6488,"The runtime auto-commits while polling, logs/counts plugin callback failures, and continues without replaying the failed batch. The sink retries transaction-conflict errors, connection/timeouts, and HTTP 408, 429, 500, 502, 503 and 504. Delays use capped exponential backoff with jitter. Retry-After is not used. Connection errors trigger reconnection and repeat sign-in/health/optional DDL. A failed reconnect stops that chunk, and retries plus startup requests can exceed query_timeout in total."],[6028,"The runtime auto-commits while polling, records plugin errors and continues without replaying the failed batch. Earlier or later chunks may already be stored, and a timeout can leave the write outcome uncertain. Duplicate tolerance and retries do not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[6321,"The runtime auto-commits while polling. It logs/counts a failed plugin callback and continues without replaying that batch. A successful callback can mean only that messages were buffered, so runtime processed counts do not establish delivery to S3. A crash loses unflushed buffers. There is no write-ahead log or dead-letter queue."],[6204,"The runtime commits Iggy offsets on read and does not replay a failed callback after its retry budget. Publisher confirmations do not provide an end-to-end exactly-once guarantee. See the source reference."],[5434,"The runtime commits offsets on read and does not replay a failed sink callback. A successful processed count does not prove that every row reached BigQuery. Preserve Iggy metadata when downstream deduplication or reconciliation is required. See the source reference for type mappings and row-error handling."],[6244,"The runtime commits offsets on read. A COPY failure that outlasts plugin retries is logged and is not replayed by the runtime. A retry after an uncertain load can duplicate rows. See the source reference."],[2357,"The runtime forwards a source batch to Iggy, saves its optional checkpoint, then acknowledges the batch to the plugin. A failed send or checkpoint save produces a negative acknowledgement. Remote checkpoint durability depends on the state server. Connector lifecycle operations do not delete remote state."],[5410,"The runtime forwards the encoded payload, optional message ID and headers. Although ProducedMessage also accepts checksum, timestamp and origin_timestamp, the current forwarding path does not copy those three fields into the Iggy message."],[2556,"The runtime loads transforms generically by TransformType, so no runtime changes are needed."],[6382,"The runtime logs and counts decoding or transform failures, drops the affected messages and passes the remaining batch to the plugin. A plugin consume() error is counted as a failed batch. The runtime uses auto-commit when polling and does not automatically retry that failed batch. Any destination retry must be implemented by the plugin."],[2283,"The runtime logs via the tracing crate. Merge the runtime configuration snippets on this page into the minimal runtime configuration. The output format is configured in the [logging] section:"],[2345,"The runtime opens two Iggy TCP clients, one for producers and one for consumers. Set credentials matching the broker and a connector configuration provider. Omitted settings use the embedded defaults, including file-based source state storage. Save this example as connectors.toml in the repository root and replace path/to/connectors with your connector configuration directory."],[5885,"The runtime records a plugin error and continues polling with consumer auto-commit. Failed chunks and batches skipped by the circuit breaker are not queued for replay, so retries and the circuit breaker do not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee. Other chunks from the same batch may already have reached InfluxDB."],[2418,"The runtime selects CommitPolicy::OnRead. Offsets can advance before a plugin finishes its callback. A failed sink callback is recorded and does not cause the runtime to replay that batch. Deferred reads change waiting and batching, not this delivery guarantee."],[2358,"The runtime supports two types of configuration providers for managing connector configurations:"],[2325,"The runtime uses a shared Tokio runtime for its connector-management and forwarding tasks. Each loaded plugin library also has an SDK Tokio runtime shared by its instances. Additionally, it has built-in support for logging via tracing crate."],[5502,"The runtime uses consumer auto-commit and does not replay a failed sink batch. See sink guide. End-to-end at-least-once delivery is therefore not guaranteed. Plugin retries resend the same batch without an insert_deduplication_token, so a lost acknowledgement can also produce duplicate rows."],[5605,"The runtime uses consumer auto-commit before the sink finishes. After a chunk fails, the plugin still attempts later chunks and returns the first error. The runtime logs and counts that error, adds no processed messages for the failed batch, and continues polling without replaying it. A crash or exhausted retry budget can therefore lose data. A non-JSON payload aborts the remaining chunks immediately."],[3247,"The Rust automatic policy defaults to every 10,000 messages or five seconds, whichever happens first, after processing. It also stores progress on close, shutdown and partition revocation. Processing counts when the application requests the next item, a handler succeeds, or the reader closes. Marked progress supports applications that finish work concurrently. Do not close a reader as if processing succeeded after a failed handler."],[959,"The Rust binary SDK resolves balanced and key routing client-side and sends kind = 2. The binary server requires explicit partition routing. The HTTP API can resolve the other strategies."],[3260,"The Rust high-level producer supports opt-in idempotence on binary transports:"],[2198,"The Rust producer and Kafka gateway support opt-in idempotent batches. This suppresses retries within bounded producer history. It does not make external side effects atomic with offset commits."],[7125,"The Rust SDK examples in the core repository, and how to start a server to run them against."],[4964,"The Rust SDK exposes the same call as describe_options. The scope is topic, stream, or user."],[3386,"The Rust SDK exposes these operations through traits on IggyClient. Availability and method names vary across foreign SDKs and transports:"],[3329,"The Rust SDK is always the first to receive new features."],[7252,"The Rust SDK is the primary and most feature-complete client for Iggy. It is available on crates.io and the source code is part of the core repository."],[2170,"The Rust SDK needs no feature flags. Depend on the published crate:"],[3420,"The Rust SDK resolves these strategies before sending a binary request. The HTTP API can resolve the strategy on the server. Binary clients route to the selected partition primary."],[7247,"The Rust SDK, the primary and most complete Iggy client, and where its source lives."],[2867,"The Rust SDK's IggyMessageHeader::to_bytes() representation is 64 bytes, with little-endian fields. The diagram and table describe that SDK representation, not Rust struct memory layout. On the wire and on disk, messages travel inside batch records with a compact 48-byte per-message frame header. See the Binary Protocol section for the exact encodings."],[3384,"The Rust snippets assume an async Tokio context. HTTP requires an explicit login after construction. Connection-string credentials are applied automatically only by the binary transports."],[3672,"The RUST_LOG environment variable takes precedence over logging.level. An invalid effective filter stops startup."],[6254,"The S3 sink connector writes messages from Iggy streams to Amazon S3 and S3-compatible object stores (MinIO, Cloudflare R2, DigitalOcean Spaces, Backblaze B2). Messages are buffered in memory and uploaded as files, rotated by size or message count, under configurable path templates with offset ranges in the object names."],[1524,"The same 256-byte header layout carries two traffic planes. The pages below cover both:"],[6970,"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Return to examples/java for the client commands. Their CA path is relative to that directory."],[6920,"The same data and credential prerequisites apply. These certificates are for development only. Run the producer before the consumer with the TLS flags (paths relative to examples/go):"],[7075,"The sample below targets Iggy 0.10.0 on 127.0.0.1:8090. Start the server:"],[5445,"The sample expects a local ClickHouse HTTP endpoint with user default and an empty password. Create a test table with matching columns:"],[7035,"The samples below expect an Iggy server on 127.0.0.1:8090. See Getting started for the server prerequisites:"],[7116,"The samples below target Iggy 0.10.0 on 127.0.0.1:8090:"],[5051,"The saved counters include fetched documents, successful polls (including empty polls), empty polls, serialized payload bytes, errors and the last error text. The average successful-poll duration includes the configured polling delay. Document-ID, scroll-ID and offset fields are retained in state but do not drive polling. These plugin counters are separate from the runtime's sent/error metrics."],[2470,"The schema_registry_url field is reserved and currently not implemented. The SDK never contacts a schema registry, and schemas are loaded only from schema_path or descriptor_set."],[3045,"The scheme picks the transport. Each transport has its own default server port:"],[6935,"The SDK and the server must speak the same wire protocol. Tagged releases up to v0.8.0 predate VSR (and the current API) and pair only with servers of the same era. The current SDK speaks VSR only. This page targets server 0.10.0. Use a compatible SDK release or edge version. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."],[4449,"The SDK can replay a login. The service must accept repeated verification of the same credentials safely. One-time passwords that become invalid after the first verification do not survive this replay model. A failed external service never grants access by default."],[2450,"The SDK includes support for Protocol Buffers (protobuf) format with both encoding and decoding capabilities. Protocol Buffers provide efficient serialization and are particularly useful for high-performance data streaming scenarios."],[6882,"The SDK is built around the IIggyClient interface, which aggregates every feature (publishing, consuming, stream/topic management, users, offsets, consumer groups, and system operations). For the low-level per-call API and the full configuration reference, see the Guide. For the ergonomic, batteries-included producer/consumer abstractions, see the High-level SDK."],[5409,"The SDK permits only one batch in flight. Returning an error from on_batch_result() stops polling. Returning an error from poll() logs the error and continues the polling loop. A source that tracks a cursor or performs destructive upstream work must implement the callback instead of relying on its default no-op."],[7257,"The SDK provides two layers. The snippets below assume an async Tokio context with a Result return type."],[7299,"The SDK requires Swift 6.0 or later. Supported platforms are macOS 13, iOS 16, tvOS 16, watchOS 9, visionOS 1 and Linux. The runnable examples require Swift 6.1 or later."],[1853,"The SDK retains this context with a request. A route refresh does not replace the history of an already uncertain operation. HistoryUnavailable requires an application decision about the new history. Readers expose a seek operation to choose a new start."],[6886,"The SDK supports two transport protocols:"],[6883,"The SDK targets .NET 8 and .NET 10 (net8.0 and net10.0). Version 0.10.0 matches the server 0.10.0 protocol and includes both topic durability options. Version 0.8.0 uses the older TCP framing. When building from source, use the server and SDK from the same checkout."],[2090,"The secret must be at least 32 bytes of CSPRNG output and byte-identical on every node."],[4480,"The seeder logs in as iggy/iggy by default. Pass --username and --password to use other credentials."],[2857,"The segment, being a part of the partition, is the actual physical layer which stores the records in the binary format in a form of the files. Each segment has a soft size limit (1 GiB unless the topic sets its own segment_size). A segment can exceed it by one whole batch before it is sealed and a new segment is created. The segment name is based on the start offset of the first record in the segment and is unique per partition."],[4741,"The segmented append-only log, and how streams, topics, partitions and segments map onto files on disk."],[3939,"The self_signed flag doesn't mean the same thing everywhere:"],[7018,"The separate producer and consumer scripts do not consistently share resources: the envelope and headers consumers create new empty topics, and the tenant producer deletes its streams before exiting. The getting-started and basic producers spread batches over five partitions, while their consumers poll partition 0. Use the self-contained stream-builder command above for a complete send-and-consume walkthrough. The sink-data producer deletes its selected topic and stream when it finishes, including resources that already existed."],[3685,"The server configuration no longer has a [system] wrapper. Its runtime tables live at the root, and the data directory is the root path key. Old IGGY_SYSTEM_* overrides are rejected. Retention, durability, and segment layout are topic creation options, not server-wide defaults."],[4572,"The server doesn't export metrics over OTLP. To bring them into an OpenTelemetry pipeline, scrape /metrics with the collector's Prometheus receiver, using the same bearer token. Server export of logs and traces over OTLP isn't available yet. See [telemetry]."],[3534,"The server exports about forty metrics, including http_requests_total, streams, topics, partitions, segments, messages, users and clients. To list them all:"],[4543,"The server exports Prometheus metrics on its HTTP API at /metrics. Both the HTTP API and the metrics endpoint are on by default. See [http.metrics] to change the path or turn it off."],[3520,"The server flags are explained on Docker & Helm, and metrics are on by default. Grafana is on port 3001 because the server already uses 3000."],[2789,"The server is built on the Viewstamped Replication (VSR) consensus protocol. Metadata operations are committed through the VSR write-ahead log even on a single node, which is the default deployment. Multi-node clustering is enabled via the [cluster] configuration (a node roster plus --replica-id to pick the local entry). See clustering for the details and maturity caveats."],[3610,"The server is thread-per-core and shared-nothing, built on io_uring on Linux (via compio), with shard and CPU pinning configurable under [sharding]. Throughput and latency depend heavily on hardware, transport, and payload shape (messages-per-batch * message-size). Run the benchmarks on your own hardware, or browse current, dated results on the benchmarking platform."],[3655,"The server reads a single TOML file. A copy of core/server/config.toml is embedded into the binary and serves as the default configuration, so the server boots with working defaults even without any file on disk. The embedded file is commented throughout and is the authoritative reference for the exact version you're running."],[3500,"The server rebalances the group. The following example shows svc-b reading partition 2 while svc-a reads 0 and 1. Rebalances and retries can replay messages."],[4610,"The server runs one thread and runtime per selected shard (thread-per-core, shared-nothing), with CPU selection and optional pinning controlled by [sharding]. All listeners (every client transport) bind on shard 0 only. Shard 0's coordinator hands accepted plaintext TCP and WebSocket connections to peer shards round-robin by transferring the file descriptor at accept time. From then on the connection lives entirely on its shard. QUIC, TCP with TLS, WebSocket with TLS, and HTTP terminate on shard 0, because their per-connection state cannot move between threads. Sockets never migrate after accept: an operation that touches a partition owned by another shard rides the internal message bus instead."],[3017,"The server stores each message payload as bytes without interpreting it, so you choose the format: plain text, JSON, Protobuf or anything else. This page shows how to send more than one kind of message to the same topic, so the consumer knows how to decode each one."],[3243,"The server submits the last returned offset as part of the read"],[4458,"The server uses io_uring on Linux, which sets a minimum kernel version."],[1981,"The server uses Linux io_uring. Use a recent Linux kernel and a sufficient locked-memory limit."],[3618,"The server's TOML configuration, how a file is merged over the embedded defaults, and what each section controls."],[3483,"The service"],[3505,"The service is the same consumer shape as the high-level consumer example in the repository, with a JSON handler and signal handling added."],[4016,"The service maps a caller to an existing active, non-root account. Local usernames never use the callout. See External authentication for the JSON contract and failure behavior."],[4445,"The service must return a successful HTTP status and a JSON decision:"],[3252,"The sharding configuration bounds parked requests, per-session requests, disk reads and completion delivery. HTTP requests without a binary session count against their user. Capacity exhaustion can return a retryable error. Keep retries within a bounded application deadline."],[2772,"The sharding system supports multiple allocation modes via the cpu_allocation config:"],[4496,"The shell's limits are not necessarily the service's limits. For a running process, inspect /proc/PID/limits, /proc/PID/status, and its cgroup limits. Replace PID with that process's ID. Record the VM type, vCPU topology, memory limit, disk type and provisioned throughput, filesystem, and network bandwidth with the result."],[4365,"The single-node write path"],[2424,"The Sink and Source traits, the connector macros, and the decoders and encoders used to read and write Iggy streams."],[6192,"The sink calls POST /api/v1/<index_id>/ingest?commit=auto. HTTP success means submission for indexing, not search visibility or acceptance of every document. The sink checks the status and does not inspect per-document rejection counts in the response. See the Quickwit ingest API."],[6177,"The sink does not add Iggy IDs, offsets, timestamps or headers. It serializes the payload after runtime decoding and transforms:"],[6333,"The sink is represented by the single Sink trait, which defines the basic interface for all sink connectors. It provides methods for initializing the sink, writing data to external destination, and closing the sink."],[5806,"The sink logs each skipped message with its offset. It does not expire table snapshots or rewrite manifests. Run table maintenance through the catalog or an engine that supports it, with retention appropriate for your time-travel requirements."],[6203,"The sink pipelines publishes and waits for confirmations. Transient failures retry from the first unconfirmed message. An in-flight tail can already be delivered, so retries can duplicate it. An unroutable mandatory publish fails the batch and forces a new connection before the next poll."],[6322,"The sink retries HTTP 408, 429, 5xx and client errors. Other HTTP statuses stop the upload immediately. Its exponential backoff includes jitter and a 60-second cap, without handling Retry-After. The S3 library also retries transport errors once internally and uses a 60-second request timeout, so max_attempts counts outer PutObject calls rather than individual network requests or a total time budget. The startup probe does not use the sink's upload retry loop."],[2417,"The sink starts processing when the read is ready, its byte budget is reached or the wait ends. Replace the old poll_interval field. A sink that still supplies it fails to start, even when max_wait is also present."],[6126,"The sink uses plain INSERT, with no conflict-ignore or upsert clause. Reusing an ID already in the table rejects the entire chunk, including any new IDs alongside it. There is no transaction covering the complete poll. The runtime auto-commits while polling and does not replay failed chunks. A connection failure can leave the write outcome uncertain. These retries do not provide an end-to-end at-least-once guarantee."],[1065,"The size field at offset 48 is the total frame length in bytes, header included. A body, when present, immediately follows the 256 header bytes and has length size - 256. Readers can decode size before typing the header. That offset is a protocol constant."],[3923,"The size knobs are optional. Leaving one unset keeps the compio-ws (tungstenite) default shown in parentheses. A malformed size string fails config load."],[6503,"The snippet below mirrors the SDK's own end-to-end tests in foreign/cpp/tests/e2e. Start a local server on 127.0.0.1:8090 with the iggy/iggy credentials first. The stream name must not already exist."],[7153,"The snippets assume an async function with a Result return type and the iggy, Tokio and futures-util dependencies. Use the Rust SDK setup and a development server with the sample credentials."],[6939,"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."],[6989,"The snippets below follow the getting-started example. Stream, topic, and partition IDs are 0-based: the first partition of a topic is partition 0."],[5357,"The source is represented by the single Source trait, which defines the basic interface for all source connectors. It provides methods for initializing the source, reading data from it, and closing the source."],[5082,"The source retains a negatively acknowledged batch and retries it with bounded backoff. There is no fixed NACK stop limit by default. Set max_consecutive_nacks only when accepted webhooks can be replayed externally after a restart. A stopped source can leave a buffer that no task drains."],[5184,"The source returns no durable connector checkpoint. Recovery after restart depends on the broker retaining the MQTT session. MQTT packet IDs are temporary and cannot serve as durable deduplication keys. The source does not provide an end-to-end exactly-once guarantee, including when MQTT uses QoS 2."],[2457,"The source sends 100 records, then continues polling without new messages. Stdout logs message offsets and the serialized JSON envelope bytes, containing type_url and base64 value. The sink's raw schema also determines how the plugin receives those transformed bytes."],[2727,"The source tree contains 17 sinks and 6 sources. Sinks include PostgreSQL, MongoDB, Elasticsearch, ClickHouse, InfluxDB, Apache Iceberg, Delta Lake, Quickwit and S3. Sources include PostgreSQL, Elasticsearch, InfluxDB, HTTP, MQTT and random data generation. See the connectors documentation for the full catalog."],[2452,"The source's schema = \"proto\" selects the default protobuf encoder, which wraps each JSON record in a google.protobuf.StringValue inside google.protobuf.Any. The sink reads raw bytes and applies proto_convert to expose the Any envelope as JSON. This does not unpack a custom protobuf message schema."],[3509,"The stack"],[6393,"The stdout sink connector prints messages from Iggy streams to the standard output. This is useful for debugging, development, and verifying that your connector pipeline is working correctly."],[2064,"The storage mechanisms are implemented in the partition persistence worker and prepare journal. The crash-recovery tests exercise acknowledged persisted messages and offsets below ordinary flush thresholds, on both a singleton and a three-replica cluster."],[7274,"The stream builder creates an IggyProducer, an IggyConsumer, or both from configuration objects. It uses the same APIs described in High-level SDK. Stream and topic names identify the resources. The 0.10 API does not take stream or topic IDs in these configuration objects."],[839,"The stream catalog accepts a create-only, write-only encryption_key. An omitted key uses the server creation default. See Stream options."],[5308,"The stream config should use schema = \"json\". Flat-schema sinks such as Iceberg need the unwrap_envelope transform to extract the data field. See the transforms documentation."],[4841,"The stream key travels in the create request. Use a transport with TLS. The server never returns the data key or its wrapped bytes. Reads report encryption_key_source as stream, server or none, and fingerprints for encrypted streams."],[1752,"The stream permission format is STREAM_ID[:STREAM_PERMISSIONS][#TOPIC_ID[:TOPIC_PERMISSIONS]]:"],[7285,"The stream-builder examples cover producer, consumer and combined configurations. The source consumer configuration defines each builder field."],[5653,"The stream-level batch_length governs batching: each consume cycle submits its supported messages in one bulk request. The connector performs no retries of its own. Request, HTTP-status, and response-decoding failures return an error. The runtime logs and counts it, adds no processed messages for that batch, and continues polling. Consumer auto-commit occurs before indexing completes, and the failed poll is not replayed."],[6191,"The strict mapping above accepts only mapped fields and requires timestamp. To use wrapper documents, map their fields or use mode: dynamic, and remove timestamp_field and its dependent retention section unless every document supplies a timestamp. The add_fields transform enriches JSON objects. It does not add timestamps to raw/text wrappers created later by this sink."],[6412,"The SurrealDB sink connector writes messages from Iggy streams into SurrealDB over the HTTP API. Each poll is split into chunks of at most batch_size messages, with one SurrealQL bulk INSERT IGNORE ... RETURN NONE for each chunk containing valid records. Every record gets a deterministic record id derived from stream, topic, partition, offset, and message id, so replaying the same identities leaves existing records untouched. Messages are not buffered across polls."],[7288,"The Swift examples use the SDK from the same checkout. Start the server from the repository root with development credentials and a separate data directory:"],[7298,"The Swift SDK 0.2.0 provides an async TCP client with optional TLS, resource management, producers, partition readers and consumers. It routes partition requests to their primaries and reconnects with heartbeat support."],[5790,"The table declared in the message field cannot be loaded, including missing tables and catalog lookup failures."],[5791,"The table name has no namespace or contains an empty name component."],[5540,"The table schema is captured once at startup and not refreshed, so restart the connector after changing it."],[3041,"The tables below describe the Rust SDK and its Python, C++ and PHP bindings. Native Go, Node.js, C# and Swift clients also accept TCP connection strings. Java supports a smaller subset, described below."],[3722,"The tables below list every section with its shipped defaults. In cluster mode, every enabled transport takes its port from this node's cluster.nodes ports entry. The transport's own address picks only the bind interface."],[5554,"The target database and table must be pre-created. The connector never issues DDL. database and table must match [A-Za-z0-9_]+ or startup fails. Streams must use schema = \"json\". Startup validates the configuration but does not check the table or connection. These are first used when a batch arrives."],[6995,"The TCP builder also accepts TLS and resilience options. Start the TLS development server and run this fragment with examples/java as the working directory:"],[6999,"The TCP builders accept fromConnectionString. Only iggy:// and iggy+tcp://, username/password credentials, tls and tls_ca_file are supported. PAT connection strings and additional options are rejected. The builder also exposes shutdown quiet-period configuration for applications that need prompt close."],[6627,"The TCP client pings the server on its own every HeartbeatInterval while connected, so an idle session survives the server's heartbeat verification and consumer-group memberships and assignments stay fresh. The ping goes through the regular request path: a lost session is repaired by the same reconnect and auto-login any other request would trigger. Heartbeat failures are logged and the loop keeps going until the client is disposed."],[7019,"The TCP/TLS pair needs a TLS-enabled source server. Run this command from the repository root, then the two test:tcp-tls:* scripts from examples/node, where they load ../../core/certs/iggy_ca_cert.pem:"],[4547,"The token can read every metric the server exports, so store it like any other secret: in a Kubernetes Secret or your secrets manager, not in a file you commit. With the Helm chart, set it through server.serviceMonitor.authorization. See Docker & Helm."],[4546,"The token is printed once. The Prometheus scrape config is in [http.metrics]."],[108,"The token value can be either a literal PAT or a file: reference such as token = \"file:/run/secrets/iggy_pat\", in which case the token is read from the given file and surrounding whitespace is removed (~/ is expanded to the home directory). A non-empty token takes precedence over the username and password."],[2850,"The topic is also the logical concept, which is a part of the stream. The topic is identified by its ID, which is unique within its stream. You could think of topic as an entity being responsible for storing the specific type of the records. For example, you could have a topic for the user events, and another topic for the order events, etc."],[862,"The trailing ack byte is mandatory on both write commands. A payload without it fails to decode. Ack levels:"],[3605,"The transport subcommands are tcp, quic, http, and websocket (alias ws). Consumer-group workloads (bcg, bpcg, and e2ecg) require a binary transport: HTTP consumer-group joining returns FeatureUnavailable, even though the benchmark CLI accepts that combination. Each transport subcommand takes --server-address for a non-default server, for example:"],[2459,"The two [[streams]] shapes differ: a source produces to a single topic and can tune batching via batch_length and linger_time, while a sink consumes from a list of topics and can additionally set batch_length, max_wait, min_count, and consumer_group."],[6357,"The two flags at the end are optional and default to false: verbose switches the connector's per-batch logging to info level, and benchmark emits per-batch timing events for performance measurement."],[6509,"The typed IggyBlockingClient also provides resource management, readers, consumers, offsets, user administration and client inspection. Personal access token creation is exposed by the bridge. Use iggy.hpp for typed StreamCreateOptions, TopicCreateOptions, update patches and PollOptions."],[823,"The u32 command codes. Replicated commands are identified on the wire by their operation byte (see Operation discriminants). The code column below is the protocol-level registry and, for non-replicated commands, the value carried in header bytes 196..200."],[5094,"The V2 example requires an existing telemetry bucket in iggy_org, a token with read access, and cpu points with a numeric usage field and a host tag. Save this entry in the runtime's connector directory and start the runtime from the checkout root."],[2052,"The WAL also retains predecessors across message and offset operations. Consequently:"],[3550,"The Web UI is a purely client-side application: every call to the Iggy HTTP API is issued by your browser, not by the Web UI container or server. Two things follow from this:"],[3554,"The Web UI is compiled directly into the Iggy server binary via the iggy-web cargo feature, which is enabled by default (official binaries and Docker images include it). To serve it at the /ui endpoint, flip the web_ui flag (disabled by default):"],[3560,"The Web UI lives in the web/ folder of the repository. Use a Node.js version supported by the frontend toolchain (^20.19.0 || ^22.13.0 || >=24, for example Node.js 22.13 or later in the 22.x series) and npm (pnpm and yarn are not part of the supported workflow):"],[2722,"The Web UI provides a comprehensive dashboard for the Iggy server, built with SvelteKit and TypeScript. It can run in two modes:"],[3562,"The Web UI provides the following pages and functionality:"],[7113,"The wheel bundles the Rust SDK, whose binary transports speak the current Iggy wire protocol without falling back to older formats. Use an SDK release compatible with your server. For unreleased changes, build the SDK and server from the same source checkout. The published wheel can lag source API changes."],[4328,"The wildcard says nothing about where clients reach the container, so the server refuses to start until IGGY_NODE_ADVERTISED_ADDRESS supplies that address. Use localhost when the ports are published to the host, the compose service name when the clients are containers on the same network, and the external hostname or load balancer name when they are further away. The value reaches clients through cluster metadata, where it is the endpoint they reconnect through."],[4326,"The working directory is /app and the iggy-server and iggy binaries are on PATH (/usr/local/bin). The default data directory local_data therefore resolves to /app/local_data - mount your volume there, or set IGGY_PATH and mount that path instead."],[4425,"The write completed under the topic's message policy"],[2968,"Then run the consumer:"],[4507,"There are three distinct controls:"],[2828,"There is an extra level above topics. Every topic lives in a stream, so you create a stream before you create its topics."],[6860,"There is no built-in deserializer counterpart - consumers implement IDeserializer<T> themselves (see Typed consumer)."],[3800,"There is no default secret. When both secrets are empty and cluster authentication supplies no key, each server start mints a random signing key, so issued tokens do not survive a restart and are valid only on the node that issued them. If you configure a secret, set it through the environment (IGGY_HTTP_JWT_ENCODING_SECRET / IGGY_HTTP_JWT_DECODING_SECRET), never commit it, and use a long random value."],[4782,"There is no index caching configuration: the in-memory index cache is an internal per-segment structure the server manages itself."],[4978,"There is no migration from a 0.9.x data directory. Keep a backup and export messages that you need with the old server and compatible clients. Start 0.10.0 with a separate, empty data directory. Import the retained data through a compatible client. Do not point the new server at the old directory."],[1839,"There's no --context flag: the active context is persistent state, switched with iggy context use <name>. Flags passed on the command line override the corresponding values from the active context. Credential fields merge independently: supplying --username does not clear a context token, which still has higher authentication priority."],[7020,"These are repository development certificates."],[2460,"These are SDK configuration fields, not Random or Stdout plugin_config keys. The runtime's schema = \"proto\" uses the default encoder or decoder."],[101,"These are the development credentials configured in that guide. Use your broker's credentials or a PAT when connecting to an existing installation."],[3234,"These are the shared poll defaults. High-level consumers can select a positive wait by default. The SDK derives a request timeout from the wait plus 10 seconds. The wire protocol permits waits up to 600 seconds, but the server caps them at sharding.deferred_poll_max_wait, 30 seconds by default."],[4383,"These are topic creation options, not config.toml settings or server environment overrides. GetTopic reports the effective values. Both policies are create-only. Resource updates cannot change them on an existing topic."],[7138,"These certificates are for local testing. Then run:"],[8,"These docs describe the upcoming Apache Iggy 0.10.0 release. Use the source release candidate until the matching packages and images are published. The upgrade guide covers the coordinated cutover and fresh data directory. The 0.9 docs remain available."],[1791,"These docs target server 0.10.0 and CLI 0.15.0, prepared for the same release. The CLI has its own version number, so use the version that matches your server. Install it from crates.io with:"],[7034,"These docs target server 0.10.0 and Node SDK 0.11.0. The Node package 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. The current SDK speaks only the VSR wire protocol and doesn't fall back to older formats."],[7100,"These examples target Iggy 0.10.0. Start the server:"],[6959,"These examples target server 0.10.0 and speak the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. Build the SDK and server from the same checkout for unreleased changes. The examples log in as iggy/iggy. From the repository root, start a disposable development server with no IGGY_ROOT_USERNAME or IGGY_ROOT_PASSWORD overrides:"],[7014,"These examples target server 0.10.0. See Getting started for prerequisites:"],[3548,"These instructions target Iggy server 0.10.0. Use a matching source checkout for local builds."],[5181,"These plugin limits are separate from the runtime producer's batch_length and linger_time. Configure only one destination [[streams]] entry per source instance. There is no per-message destination routing."],[4325,"These properties of the published image matter for deployment:"],[6898,"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the equivalent low-level, per-call flow, see the Guide."],[6519,"These samples use the High-level SDK - the recommended way to build producers and consumers. For the low-level, per-call equivalents, see the Guide."],[4878,"These settings apply whichever language your tests are in:"],[1938,"These settings live under [cluster.coordinator] and apply when the server has more than one shard:"],[4665,"These variables initialize the root account only when it is first created. Supplied values are still validated on later starts, but they do not replace the stored credentials. Use the password-change API or iggy user password with the current password to change an existing account. The --fresh flag deletes all data. It is not needed for a password change."],[6700,"Things to know:"],[1792,"This builds and installs the iggy binary. With cargo-binstall, you can download a prebuilt binary instead. It builds from source if there isn't one for your platform:"],[7275,"This complete example uses a local server with the development credentials. The producer creates missing resources during initialization. Existing topics keep their configuration."],[2076,"This configuration requires at least three cluster nodes. A topic can override it at creation. Increasing the number of partitions does not change the factor."],[6985,"This documentation targets server 0.10.0 and SDK 0.10.0. The TCP transport speaks the VSR (Viewstamped Replication) wire protocol. The Java artifact 0.8.0 uses the older protocol. When working from source, build the SDK and the server from the same repository checkout."],[2791,"This example shows a fresh partition with the default paths. The stream, topic and partition directories are named after their numeric IDs, assigned from 0. The metadata/journal.wal file is the VSR write-ahead log for metadata operations (stream/topic/user creation, etc.). Its index has 1024 slots by default (journal_slots under [metadata]). Before they run out, the server checkpoints the metadata into metadata/snapshot.bin, records the checkpoint in a superblock under metadata/, and drops the checkpointed operations from the log. Root initialization is recorded through metadata replication, and startup checks that journal or checkpoint state proves initialization. The runtime/current_config.toml file is rewritten at every boot with the configuration the server booted with and the addresses it actually bound. Secrets (the JWT secrets, the encryption key and the cluster shared secret) are left out. The state/log file is empty. The server creates it at boot and does not otherwise use it. Segment files are named by their 20-digit start offset. Recovery can create an empty active segment at a reserved offset beyond the last stored message. Partition superblocks record the recovery frontiers. A superblock is written to two files in turn, superblock.a and superblock.b. A fresh partition has only superblock.a. superblock.b appears at the next superblock write, for example after a later persistence operation. The .index file is created automatically and speeds up searches by keeping track of the offsets and timestamps of the records. The offsets/ directory holds the server-side consumer and consumer group offsets."],[4884,"This example uses testcontainers 0.27 and the iggy crate 0.12, which goes with server 0.10.0."],[4888,"This example uses testcontainers with apache-iggy 0.11.0, the Node.js SDK that goes with server 0.10.0, and the built-in node:test runner. testcontainers needs Node.js 22.19 or newer."],[4886,"This example uses testcontainers, pytest and pytest-asyncio with apache-iggy 0.10.0:"],[2451,"This example uses the Random source and Stdout sink from the matching checkout. Start a server using the getting-started guide, then build the plugins, runtime, and CLI from the repository root:"],[3592,"This flag affects only the first creation of the root user. Existing credentials remain unchanged, and environment variables take precedence. For another server, pass its credentials with iggy-bench -u USER -p PASSWORD before the benchmark kind."],[7306,"This fragment uses an existing connected client. Commit after processing. Automatic commits use .auto(.default), which counts processing and stores every 10,000 messages or five seconds. .onRead stores with the read. Close readers and shut down consumers to finish offset work and leave groups."],[6571,"This guide covers client configuration and the full IIggyClient API surface - the low-level, per-call operations. For the ergonomic producer/consumer abstractions built on top of these, see the High-level SDK."],[2952,"This guide targets server 0.10.0. Use its official Docker image:"],[2652,"This is not yet another extension running on top of existing infrastructure, such as Kafka or SQL database."],[3024,"This is the better choice for Protobuf or any other binary format, because the payload doesn't have to fit inside JSON. See the message-type example."],[7041,"This loop waits for messages until it is stopped. It stores checkpoints after printing. The group has its own offsets, separate from a standalone consumer."],[4253,"This node's own client-facing identity. The address is read only while cluster.enabled = false: in cluster mode the roster answers the same question per node, so the address itself is ignored and the server says so at startup. The key is still validated in every mode, so a malformed value refuses boot even with cluster.enabled = true."],[1790,"This page covers installing the CLI, connecting to a server, authenticating, and managing connection contexts. The full command tree is documented in the command reference."],[2758,"This page covers the internals of the Iggy server: how work is scheduled across CPU cores, how requests are routed, and how data ends up on disk. If you're looking for the domain model (streams, topics, partitions, consumer groups, message format), see concepts first."],[48,"This page covers the problems people most often hit the first time they run Apache Iggy. Each entry shows what you see, what causes it, and how to fix it."],[4544,"This page covers the server's own metrics. The connectors runtime has a separate endpoint with its own metric names. See Connectors observability."],[3002,"This page gets a server running and a message sent and received, in one file. To build separate producer and consumer applications step by step, see Getting started. For other languages, see the SDK section."],[5443,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream clickhouse_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5514,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream delta_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5815,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream influxdb_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5895,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream meilisearch_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[6255,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream s3_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[6413,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference is the upstream surrealdb_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5196,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including payload extraction recipes, custom query parameters, and sink round-trip examples, is the upstream postgres_source README in the apache/iggy repository."],[6041,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including SQL query recipes for each payload format, is the upstream postgres_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5678,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including the full configuration table, deployment patterns, and performance analysis, is the upstream http_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5092,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including the full cursor semantics and stuck-timestamp handling, is the upstream influxdb_source README in the apache/iggy repository."],[5552,"This page is a curated subset of the documentation. The canonical reference, including the label scheme, redirect security model, and operational guidance, is the upstream doris_sink README in the apache/iggy repository."],[1682,"This page lists every top-level iggy command. Examples assume you're already authenticated, for example via iggy login. See installation and authentication. Run iggy <command> --help for the full flag list of any command."],[6501,"This SDK isn't published to any package registry. Consume it from the monorepo and build it from source with Bazel."],[1522,"This section describes binary protocol version 0.12.0. The protocol version is the major.minor.patch of the iggy_binary_protocol crate, ignoring prerelease tags, and it's exchanged and checked during login (see Connection lifecycle). The crate itself is the source of truth: request and response types document their payload layouts, and the server dispatch defines which operations are supported."],[6365,"This sink keeps an in-memory invocation counter. The runtime does not persist sink plugin state. Iggy consumer-group offsets are managed separately."],[4403,"This table describes acknowledged messages on one node, with no later retention or deletion:"],[2946,"This tutorial uses the low-level Rust SDK to show how things work under the hood - creating streams, topics, sending and polling messages step by step. This is great for understanding the fundamentals."],[2916,"This tutorial uses the Python SDK's low-level API (IggyClient) directly, so you can see each step. It needs Python 3.11 or newer. On Linux the server needs kernel 5.19 or newer. MacOS has no kernel requirement. See System requirements."],[2745,"Thread per core (shared nothing) + io_uring"],[2659,"Thread per core shared nothing design